<node id="Phosphogypsum">
  <data key="d0">Phosphogypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphogypsum is a major industrial byproduct and solid waste generated during the wet-process production of phosphoric acid, phosphate fertilizer manufacturing, phosphate ore processing, and rare-earth extraction, yielding approximately five tons per ton of acid and resulting in an annual global production of 100 to 280 million metric tons. Sourced from locations such as the Fuling District in Chongqing, the Jorf Lasfer complex, and Yunnan Province, it is primarily composed of calcium sulfate dihydrate, with calcium and sulfur accounting for over ninety percent of its mass. The material also contains calcium oxide, sulfur trioxide, hemihydrate, anhydrous calcium sulfate, quartz, and calcium hydrogen phosphate dihydrate. Physically, it appears as a damp, powdery, silty, beige, grey, or yellowish-brown soft granular solid with low plasticity, irregular jagged particle morphology, a wide size distribution, and a microstructure of elongated prismatic and needle-like crystals typically under sixty-three micrometers, often exhibiting a homogeneous prismatic piling arrangement and well-defined crystalline structure. It maintains an acidic pH between 1.5 and 5.5, contains over sixty chemical elements, retains residual acids, salts, and organic matter, and harbors trace heavy metals such as lead and cadmium. Its impurity profile depends on the initial phosphate rock and processing method, and it is sometimes categorized into s-PGs and m-PGs.

Historically characterized by low utilization rates, phosphogypsum has led to massive global stockpiling in untreated dry waste storage across regions including southwestern Spain, the Yangtze River basin, Ukraine, Russia, various Chinese provinces, and southern Turkey, with accumulation rates reaching twenty kilotons annually. This excessive accumulation poses severe ecological and radiological hazards through toxic gas emissions, acidic runoff, and groundwater contamination via leaching. The material harbors uranium-series radionuclides including Radium-226, Lead-210, and thorium(IV) phosphate, classifying it as Technologically Enhanced Naturally Occurring Radioactive Material that emits gamma radiation and serves as a Radon-222 source, frequently releasing mobile metals and radionuclides from industrial stacks. To mitigate these risks and enhance its properties, phosphogypsum undergoes extensive scientific characterization using scanning electron microscopy, FTIR spectroscopy, XRD, XPS, mechanical testing, spectral analysis, and thermogravimetry, including DTG plots to analyze thermal decomposition kinetics, often when mixed with coal at a 10:1 mass proportion. Processing strategies include calcination, thermal dehydration, chemical stabilization, leaching, controlled destruction, and high-temperature reduction smelting to remove contaminants, reduce heavy metal content, and improve mechanical properties and water resistance.

Increasingly recognized as a valuable secondary resource within circular economy practices, phosphogypsum is widely applied across multiple sectors. In agriculture, it serves as a soil amendment, biofertilizer, and remediation agent that improves soil structure, stabilizes aggregates, increases ion content, passivates cadmium, and influences soil pH and heavy metal speciation. In construction and geotechnical engineering, it functions as a partial cement replacement, an activator and binder component, and a stabilizing additive when combined with fly ash, lime, or cement for soil modification, road embankments, pavements, backfilling, hydraulic binders, gypsum plaster, cement retarders, gypsum powder and blocks, cement paste, self-leveling mortar, and non-fired bricks. While it consistently prolongs setting time and offers advantages such as reduced density and increased chemical resistance, it can cause drawbacks like decreased workability and mechanical strength, though these are often mitigated through advanced processing. Beyond construction, it acts as a versatile adsorbent for fluorine uptake and heavy metal removal in water treatment, serves as a precursor for synthesizing nano-hydroxyapatite and other nanostructures, and supports advanced applications including carbon dioxide mineralization to produce calcium carbonate, the production of ammonium sulfate, potassium sulfate, and portlandite, rare earth recovery (particularly lanthanum), carbothermic reduction smelting for sulfur recycling and slag wool production, use as a foundational matrix for the PGI system, and in papermaking. Consequently, phosphogypsum is extensively studied in academic and industrial research, focusing on its complex solid-state decomposition, thermal behavior, reaction mechanisms, environmental and radiological impacts, and suitability as a multifunctional industrial resource and construction binding material.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is a calcium sulfate by-product from the phosphoric acid process, investigated as a concrete additive.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is an industrial by-product investigated for use as a cement replacement and aggregate in concrete.&lt;SEP&gt;A material utilized as a replacement component in concrete and mortar experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;A material used in the study where washing significantly improves compressive strength at low replacement percentages.&lt;SEP&gt;A raw material whose chemical composition is analyzed before and after thermal treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is a byproduct material used as a replacement in construction mixes, categorized into M and A types.</data>
