<node id="Gypsum">
  <data key="d0">Gypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Gypsum, chemically defined as calcium sulfate dihydrate (CaSO₄·2H₂O), is a naturally occurring monoclinic mineral whose structure closely resembles brushite. It crystallizes into elongated, prismatic, needle-like, or plate-like forms that aggregate into dense fibrous or agglomerated textures. Its crystallization is modulated by sulfuric acid levels, silicon content, processing additives, and impurities. Thermodynamically, it possesses a standard enthalpy of formation of -1434.5 kJ/mol. Analytically, it is identified by strong elemental signals, XRD peaks near 20°, and infrared absorption bands around 3400 cm⁻¹ and 1000–1100 cm⁻¹, often designated as chemical phase G. While inherently acid-resistant, gypsum dissolves in alkaline treatments like EDTA, incorporates phosphate ions during acidification, and demixes at high phosphate concentrations.

Structurally, gypsum interconverts between dihydrate, hemihydrate, and anhydrite phases via thermal dehydration. During calcination, water is removed from its mineral structure, transforming it to anhydrite at lower temperatures than the anhydrite III to II transition. This dehydration is followed by rehydration, which later hydrates back to improve mortar strength. Gypsum also acts as a cementing compound formed when calcined phosphogypsum hydrates at high temperatures, and its endotherm intensity decreases at higher curing temperatures. It frequently serves as the primary component in analyzed samples and forms during pozzolanic reactions that accelerate mechanical strength development.

Gypsum is the primary component of phosphogypsum, comprising 90 to 96 weight percent of the industrial byproduct, and is consistently found in both washed and unwashed phosphogypsum samples at 170°C. Its needle-shaped crystals host trace elements, rare earth elements, cadmium, and uranium via substitution and adsorption, establishing it as a primary rare earth vector. The material contains hazardous impurities alongside its primary composition. While phosphogypsum often substitutes natural gypsum in construction after beneficiation, natural gypsum remains the target mineral recovered during purification for sulfur salvage and is actively utilized in recycling and novel separation processes.

Industrially, gypsum functions as a versatile binder, a standard retarder in Portland slag cement, an exclusive calcium oxide source, and a reactant in the Merseburg process. During cement hydration, it reacts with tricalcium aluminate to form ettringite and additional dihydrate gypsum, which modulates setting times, enhances road base durability, recrystallizes to improve brick strength, and influences FT-IR peaks. It is widely applied in building materials, carbon dioxide sequestration, composites, plaster, and chalk. Agriculturally, gypsum ameliorates acid soils like acid Andosols, improves tillage and root growth, and reduces heavy metal leachability. Environmentally, it serves as a baseline for impact assessments due to material-saving benefits, though it can release radioactivity comparable to phosphate fertilizers, with radium typically adsorbed or incorporated. Ongoing research focuses on its growth, aggregation, ion incorporation, the utilization of stacked phosphogypsum to reduce natural extraction, and its thermal reduction and carbon reactions in inert atmospheres.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-00