<node id="Calcium Ions">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium ions are positively charged chemical species, frequently represented as 3Ca²⁺ in chemical equations, that serve as fundamental reactive components across aqueous solution chemistry, solid-state crystallography, and various industrial and environmental systems. In aqueous environments, they are mobilized through dissolution and degradation pathways, including the breakdown of calcium-aluminum-silicate phases, hydrolysis reactions, structural degradation of hydroxyapatite, and release from complexes such as Ca-NTA. Once dissolved, they act as essential reactants in chemical transformations like the formation of calcium aluminate hydrate and alkaline precipitation reactions. They are the primary reactive species driving the formation of calcium phosphate compounds, coordinating with phosphate ions to induce supersaturation that leads to amorphous calcium phosphate formation.

In solution, calcium ions exhibit diverse coordination and chelation behaviors. They interact with phosphate species and chelate with glutamic acid to form clusters that eventually crystallize into hydroxyapatite nuclei and drive crystal growth and core-shell formation. During hydrothermal synthesis, they interact with amine groups and organic additives to generate stable chelate complexes. Additionally, excess STMP can chelate these ions, thereby affecting microsphere aggregation. Furthermore, calcium ions bind to the hydrophilic headgroups on the inner surface of micelles through hydrogen bonds, demonstrating their affinity for polar molecular structures.

Within solid mineral frameworks, particularly hydroxyapatite and the broader apatite family, calcium ions function as native lattice and channel components. They reside within and border unconnected channels, forming direct bonds with hydroxyl groups to stabilize the crystal lattice, and remain susceptible to ionic substitution by other metal ions under appropriate geochemical conditions. On hydroxyapatite surfaces, their positive charge facilitates the electrostatic attraction of negatively charged proteins. The concentration of calcium ions significantly modulates material properties; decreasing concentrations correlate with lower points of zero charge, while higher solution concentrations enhance the sorption capacity of hydroxyapatite for selenite.

Industrially and environmentally, calcium ions present a complex profile. They are advantageous for processes such as rare earth element recovery but can simultaneously degrade phosphogypsum quality and contribute to the generation of calcium sulfate waste. Their multifaceted reactivity, structural integration, and surface interactions underscore their critical importance across geochemical, materials science, and chemical engineering applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium ions are chemical ions that form bonds with oxygen ions in MPG.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792301205</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydroxide Ions">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxide Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydroxide ions are negatively charged chemical species that play a multifaceted role across aqueous chemistry, solid-state materials, and spectroscopic analysis. In aqueous environments, they are typically produced during hydrolysis reactions and released into the solution, directly increasing and establishing the alkaline environment. Their concentration serves as a critical driving force in various chemical transformations, most notably promoting the hydrolysis of calcium sulfate into calcium hydroxide.

As essential reactants, hydroxide ions readily combine with calcium and aluminum species to facilitate the formation of calcium aluminate hydrate compounds. Their presence ensures the necessary basic conditions for these mineral and synthetic hydrate syntheses to proceed efficiently.

Beyond solution chemistry, hydroxide ions function as fundamental anionic components within crystalline frameworks, particularly in apatite and hydroxyapatite lattices. Within these solid structures, they exhibit notable substitutional flexibility; they can be replaced by carbonate anions, a modification that enhances the material's acidity, or substituted by chloride ions during specific catalytic reactions.

From a spectroscopic perspective, hydroxide ions are characterized by distinct infrared stretching and bending vibrational modes. The intensity of these characteristic spectral signatures is highly responsive to thermal conditions, increasing significantly upon heat treatment, which provides a reliable analytical method for tracking their presence and structural integration in various materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxide ions are chemical species in the solution that hydrolyze CaSO₄ into Ca(OH)₂ during synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ions that react with STMP to produce phosphate species, influencing shell formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Ions produced by urea hydrolysis that provide an alkaline environment and drive the chemical transformation of STMP.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxide ions are provided by the slow hydrolysis of urea and react with STMP to initiate the synthesis process.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxide ions are ligands that form mono- and di-hydroxide complexes with lead.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-020</data>
