<node id="Carbon Dioxide">
  <data key="d0">Carbon Dioxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbon Dioxide is a gaseous chemical compound and a rapidly increasing greenhouse gas that plays a multifaceted role across environmental science, industrial chemistry, and biological research. Characterized by a distinct spectral peak near 2350 cm^-1 and a molar mass conversion factor of 44.01 g/mol, its atmospheric content is frequently determined via titration with potassium hydroxide. As a prominent atmospheric contaminant, its rising emissions have spurred the development of advanced capture technologies, such as PSC Technology, to mitigate environmental impacts and protect ecosystems and human health. Consequently, it is widely targeted for permanent storage and sequestration through various mineral carbonation processes.

Industrially, Carbon Dioxide is generated as a byproduct across numerous chemical and metallurgical pathways. It is produced during the water-gas shift reaction in lignite gasification, the total oxidation of volatile organic compounds, and the initial reaction stages of various thermal treatments. Notably, it forms during the carbothermal and initial carbon reduction of calcium sulfate, as well as during the reduction of iron(III) oxide, where its diffusion is accelerated by aluminosilicate glass. It is also liberated during the thermal decomposition of calcium carbonate, a process measurable via thermogravimetric analysis weight loss. In aqueous environments, the gas readily dissolves, with solubility increasing in basic media to facilitate carbonate ion absorption and initiate acid formation. It is commonly denoted as CO2↑ to indicate its gaseous state.

A significant focus of Carbon Dioxide management lies in mineral carbonation and sequestration, particularly utilizing backfill materials, mine tailings, and phosphogypsum. During the thermal treatment of phosphogypsum, Carbon Dioxide is released in the third stage of mass loss and can be extracted from slag and hydrolyzed liquid phases. The gas is continuously injected into reactors to react with calcium-rich solutions or calcium hydroxide, facilitating low-cost carbonation reactions that yield stable calcium carbonate and occasionally potassium sulfate. When incorporated into ternary binder systems, this approach reduces Carbon Dioxide release by up to 57%. The gas can also be adsorbed or captured from the atmosphere during chemical precipitation processes.

In material synthesis, Carbon Dioxide exhibits versatile chemical behaviors, particularly in the precipitation synthesis of hydroxyapatite. It dissolves in water during this process, influencing lattice parameters and acting as an atmospheric impurity that partially substitutes phosphate groups without adverse effects, though it typically disappears upon calcination. It also actively reacts with Portlandite to form calcite, a property utilized in feldspar pellet processing and noted by a specific stretching mode band affecting portlandite samples. Additionally, it is employed in controlled atmospheres to synthesize type A apatite from hydroxyapatite, with catalytic process efficiency often evaluated using PG-CuFe2O4 carriers. Beyond industrial applications, Carbon Dioxide is extensively utilized in biological and laboratory contexts, where it is maintained at a 5% concentration in incubators to support cell culture physiology and naturally supplied by plant hairs into micro-basins in specialized botanical setups to facilitate targeted chemical interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon Dioxide is captured from the atmosphere during preparation, leading to the partial substitution of phosphate groups.&lt;SEP&gt;A gas whose partial pressure and seasonal cycles in the soil-atmosphere system are influenced by the CCE.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon Dioxide is a product formed in the reduction reactions involving carbon and oxygen.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon Dioxide is an impurity in phosphogypsum that leaves the length-to-width crystal ratio unchanged.&lt;SEP&gt;An atmospheric gas that reacts with phosphate ions to form carbonate impurities within the hydroxyapatite lattice.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-003</data>
