<node id="Deionized Water">
  <data key="d0">Deionized Water</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Deionized water is a highly purified liquid characterized by the near-complete removal of ionic impurities, making it a critical chemical substance and reagent across analytical, industrial, and materials science applications. As a foundational medium, it serves as an ideal high-purity solvent, diluent, and reaction medium. It is routinely employed to dissolve analytical grade chemicals and reagents for preparing aqueous solutions and simulated body fluids, effectively handling a broad range of substances including Ct, CTAB, and (NH4)2HPO4. In experimental workflows, deionized water functions as a control medium and is utilized in experimental water washing pretreatments to prevent analytical errors. It dilutes digested and dissolved particle samples prior to trace element analysis, dissolves solids before filtration and subsequent testing, and acts as a baseline solvent for measuring the initial solubility of materials such as phosphogypsum and gypsum. Additionally, it is mixed with compost samples for physicochemical evaluation to ensure accurate, contamination-free measurements.

In materials engineering and synthesis, deionized water acts as the essential liquid component that combines with powder constituents to form homogeneous cement pastes and specialized composite bone cements, while having almost no detrimental effect on the filling body's strength or structure. It plays a vital role in the extraction and carbon dioxide mineralization of milled feldspar pellets. Within hydroxyapatite research, it serves as the primary solvent for hydrothermal synthesis, dissolution, and desorption experiments, and is repeatedly used to wash hydroxyapatite precipitates and solid products three times following centrifugation. Furthermore, it is utilized as a rinsing agent to wash precipitates after synthesis, as a solvent in synthesis suspensions such as those supplied by Hydrolab, and as a solvent added to Teflon vessels to replenish sample volume. It is also recognized as a purified water source used as a reagent in standardized testing procedures like SW-846.&lt;SEP&gt;Deionized water is used to wash the precipitates and remove excess ions after the reaction.&lt;SEP&gt;Deionized water is a solvent used to wash the precipitate after filtration during nanosized hydroxyapatite preparation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792318157</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfur Dioxide">
  <data key="d0">Sulfur Dioxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfur Dioxide is a gaseous chemical compound, chemically represented by the formula SO₂ and frequently denoted as 2SO₂↑ in reaction equations. It is primarily generated as a byproduct across various industrial and chemical processes, particularly during the thermal and reductive decomposition or conversion of phosphogypsum, the processing of feldspar pellets, the broader reduction of sulfate compounds, and the reaction between carbon or coke with calcium sulfate. Specifically, it forms alongside calcium oxide at high temperatures during calcium sulfate breakdown, emerges during the self-decomposition and reduction of calcium sulfate, and is produced when calcium sulfide reacts with undecomposed calcium sulfate. The gas also diffuses out of slag during phosphogypsum decomposition and is released during secondary reaction stages and multiple other process phases.

Beyond its role as an industrial byproduct, Sulfur Dioxide serves as a valuable intermediate for further chemical synthesis and is subsequently processed in sulfuric acid production. Environmentally, it is classified as an air pollutant emitted during production, necessitating compliance with industry emission standards. Its emissions can be significantly mitigated through the application of PSC Technology and are commonly treated using bag filters, with strengthened control measures required across various operational scenarios. Despite its industrial importance, the precise decomposition behavior of phosphogypsum and related sulfate materials during its generation remains an area with limited scientific understanding.

In analytical and computational contexts, Sulfur Dioxide is treated as a chemical compound with an equivalent molar mass of 15.02 g/mol, a value utilized in concentration calculations. Its mass fraction is determined using specific analytical formulas, notably Equation 23, and it functions as a correction factor in Equation 22. Furthermore, the compound serves as the target analyte for quantification in Section 14, where it is measured using spectrophotometric methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur Dioxide is a product formed in the reduction reactions of calcium sulfate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792363202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nitrogen">
  <data key="d0">Nitrogen</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Nitrogen is a chemical element that functions as an inert gas and serves diverse roles across industrial, analytical, agricultural, and scientific contexts. Industrially, it is released as a gaseous component during the gasification of lignite and is recovered from waste phosphogypsum leachate through precipitation processes. It also provides a protective inert atmosphere during the calcination of feldspar pellets and is utilized to establish controlled atmospheric conditions for testing environmental effects on thermal treatments.

In analytical chemistry, nitrogen serves as the adsorbate gas in adsorption-desorption isotherm analysis to accurately determine the porosity and surface area of various materials. Its presence is also monitored in quality control protocols, such as when it is undetected in HAp-CTAB-9 samples to confirm the absence of CTAB impurities. Structurally, nitrogen can act as a central atom in cationic structures, carrying a positive charge and bonded to four substituents.

From an agricultural and biological standpoint, nitrogen is an essential plant nutrient that is solubilized by microorganisms and commonly supplied to crops through biofertilizers or compounds like ammonium sulfate. In scientific data documentation, nitrogen is frequently cataloged as a chemical element with specified concentration values recorded in tabular formats.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen serves as the dynamic atmospheric gas during thermogravimetric analysis to control the reaction environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen is used as the dynamic atmosphere with a flow rate of 10 mL/min for kinetic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen is an inert gas used as the atmosphere in the tube reactor experiment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792362890</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weathering">
  <data key="d0">Weathering</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Weathering is a carbonation process applied to the PGA solid phase to produce the PGAW solid phase.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural process that reduces the acidity of stored phosphogypsum over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792455914</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bubbling">
  <data key="d0">Bubbling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Bubbling is a carbonation process applied to the PGA solid phase, yielding the PGAB solid phase and liquid waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790847976</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Milling">
  <data key="d0">Milling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Milling is a mechanical processing step applied to feldspar pellets after calcination.&lt;SEP&gt;Milling is a mechanical post-processing step implemented to break apart aggregates and adjust the particle size of the synthesized material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792427797</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Filtration">
  <data key="d0">Filtration</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Filtration is a fundamental physical separation technique primarily employed to isolate solids from fluids, a process that frequently relies on applied pressure differentials to drive the flow. At its core, the method functions to divide reaction mixtures or slurries into distinct solid cake and liquid filtrate components, effectively separating solid and liquid phases following chemical transformations.

In industrial and chemical processing contexts, filtration serves multiple targeted purposes depending on the specific reaction pathway. During hydrolysis procedures, it is utilized to eliminate slightly soluble and insoluble impurities from the solution. In post-treatment stages, the technique recovers specific solid byproducts, such as crystallized gypsum, solid calcium carbonate, or potassium sulfate, while simultaneously removing unwanted hydroxide precipitates and calcium carbonate-rich slag. By systematically partitioning reaction mixtures, filtration ensures the efficient isolation of desired solid products and the purification of liquid phases across a wide range of chemical manufacturing and processing operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Filtration is a separation technique used to isolate solid crystals from liquid reaction mixtures.&lt;SEP&gt;A separation technique used to isolate the liquid phase from the solid phase after dispersion.&lt;SEP&gt;A process step that follows carbonation and yields the final product calcite.&lt;SEP&gt;Filtration is a processing step where rhombic crystal morphology in Group 3 phosphogypsum is preferred.&lt;SEP&gt;Filtration is a treatment method applied to phosphogypsum to remove impurities and reduce radioactivity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792404058</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Diffusion">
  <data key="d0">Diffusion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Diffusion is a passive transport mechanism for gases moving through soil and water layers.&lt;SEP&gt;Diffusion is a physical mechanism through which methane escapes from flooded soil into the atmosphere.&lt;SEP&gt;Diffusion is a physical process facilitating methane release from flooded soils.&lt;SEP&gt;Diffusion is the transport mechanism facilitated by reduced particle size, allowing solvent to contact extracted substances more quickly.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791790646</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ebullition">
  <data key="d0">Ebullition</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Ebullition is a gas transport mechanism involving the physical bursting of gas bubbles from waterlogged soils.&lt;SEP&gt;Ebullition is a physical mechanism involving bubble release that transports methane from soil to the atmosphere.&lt;SEP&gt;Ebullition is the bubbling release of methane gas from anaerobic soils into the atmosphere.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Floodwater">
  <data key="d0">Floodwater</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Floodwater is the water body situated above the soil surface, acting as a medium for gas transport.&lt;SEP&gt;Floodwater is the uppermost layer in a rice paddy ecosystem, situated above the soil profile.&lt;SEP&gt;Floodwater is the top layer in the rice paddy ecosystem with an oxidation-reduction potential greater than 300 mV.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aerobic Layer">
  <data key="d0">Aerobic Layer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Aerobic Layer is the upper soil zone with high redox potential (Eh &gt; 300 mV) where oxygen is present.&lt;SEP&gt;The aerobic layer is a soil zone in the rice paddy ecosystem characterized by high redox potential (Eh &gt; 300 mV).&lt;SEP&gt;The aerobic layer is a soil stratum in rice paddies with an Eh between 100 and 300 mV.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Facultative Anaerobic Layer">
  <data key="d0">Facultative Anaerobic Layer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The facultative anaerobic layer is a soil zone in the rice paddy ecosystem with a redox potential starting at 100 mV.&lt;SEP&gt;Facultative Anaerobic Layer is a soil zone with intermediate redox potential (Eh 100 to 300 mV) between aerobic and anaerobic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The facultative anaerobic layer hosts rhizosphere methanotrophs that consume methane.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rabaoui, L.">
  <data key="d0">Rabaoui, L.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Rabaoui, L. is a co-author of the 2019 study on radiation hazard assessment in Tunisia.&lt;SEP&gt;Rabaoui, L. is a co-author of the 2019 study on industrial waste radiation hazards in Tunisia.&lt;SEP&gt;Rabaoui, L. is a co-author of the research article on natural radioactivity and radiation hazard assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Rabaoui, L. is a researcher who assessed heavy metal pollution in the Gulf of Gabes using benthic macroinvertebrate communities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-050</data>
