<node id="Hemihydrate">
  <data key="d0">Hemihydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hemihydrate, commonly known as plaster, is a hydrated mineral phase and chemical compound of calcium sulfate that represents an intermediate hydration state, often identifiable through variations in peak positions and intensities. It is primarily formed through the controlled dehydration or calcination of calcium sulfate dihydrate, commonly referred to as gypsum. This process typically involves the precise removal of 1.5 water molecules per formula unit and is generally conducted at temperatures around 125°C. In addition to thermal dehydration, hemihydrate can also be produced through chemical treatment with sodium carbonate. Naturally, it occurs as a mineral phase within evaporite deposits derived from gypsum, while industrially it frequently appears as an alternative crystalline phase in phosphogypsum, where its formation and structural characteristics are heavily influenced by crystallization temperatures.

Structurally, hemihydrate can crystallize into solid solutions that effectively stabilize the sulfate framework. Specific modifications involve stabilization through cerium and sodium solid solutions, which notably slow the recrystallization of the dihydrate phase. However, the presence of co-crystallized species can complicate its behavior, making the setting time of calcined hemihydrate more difficult to control. Owing to its distinct chemical and physical properties, hemihydrate exhibits excellent performance and is widely utilized across various material applications. Notably, it serves as a key component in the preparation of filling cementitious materials, where it significantly influences their final properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemihydrate is a calcined form of phosphogypsum used in cementitious binders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-011</data>
