</node>
<node id="Sulfuric Acid">
  <data key="d0">Sulfuric Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄) is a highly corrosive, versatile, low-cost, strong inorganic mineral acid with a standard enthalpy of formation of -814 kJ/mol. It serves as a fundamental reactant in the wet-process manufacturing of phosphoric acid from mined phosphate rock, including apatite and fluorapatite. In this acid attack process, it digests phosphate minerals and carbonates, reacting with thorium(IV) phosphate using a stoichiometric coefficient of five to yield phosphoric acid and thorium sulfate. This reaction generates phosphogypsum as a major byproduct, with sulfur directly incorporated into its structure. Sulfuric acid is essential for converting phosphate rock into concentrated, plant-available fertilizers and functions as a primary input, an intermediate compound, a final product in sulfur processing, and a byproduct during initial reactions, ammonium hydroxide interactions forming ammonium sulfate, and specific synthesis and drying stages. It is explicitly listed as a key reactant in thermodynamic analyses.

The acid is critical for phosphogypsum treatment, resource recovery, and quality control. It acts as an economical leaching agent and chemical modifier to facilitate acid leaching, purification, pre-treatment, valuable metal extraction, phosphorus pentoxide recovery, and rare earth element (REE) extraction via conventional and double leaching. By adjusting pH to precipitate contaminants, it enhances leaching efficiency and enriches REEs. In slurries, it typically remains in excess, controlling gypsum crystal morphology, texture, and crystallization behavior while governing impurity co-crystallization. Its concentration directly influences these properties; increased levels decrease HPO4 2- incorporation into gypsum, and manipulation allows observation of morphological changes. It is used in diluted forms to wash phosphogypsum for quality assessment and serves in phosphate rock flotation, where it can be partially replaced by acidic phosphogypsum sewage. Additionally, it maintains low pH, mobilizes toxic substances and trace elements like cadmium, and enables radium transfer and radionuclide fractionation under concentrated applications.

Chemically, sulfuric acid exhibits the lowest gypsum solubility among tested acids and is evaluated in diluted mediums to determine solubility constants, making it viable for cyclic leaching and a key component in solubility modeling compared to gluconic acid biolixiviants. It is specified in gypsum analysis standards and used as a 12 mol/L experimental reagent. Recovery and synthesis involve cyclical pathways where it shifts between reactant and product. It can be manufactured from thermally treated phosphogypsum via high-temperature reductive decomposition or derived from calcium sulfate and elemental sulfur through microbiological, thermal, wet decomposition, solid-solid reactions, industrial sulfur dioxide treatment, electrodialysis, chemical looping combustion, and hydrothermal conversion to hydroxyapatite. It is frequently produced as a main, secondary, or co-product alongside cement and slag wool. While traditional methods yield unstable quality due to impurities, advanced recovery technologies like co-production and PSC methods enhance extraction for direct reuse, generating significant revenue. Ultimately, sulfuric acid functions as a highly recoverable resource, a regulated air emission, a listed product, and a primary exported commodity, maintaining its indispensable role in phosphate processing while offering substantial potential for industrial reuse.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfuric acid is a corrosive chemical used to test the acid resistance of construction specimens.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfuric acid is a concentrated acid used in sequential leaching that enhances the solubility of uranium, cadmium, zinc, and copper.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical treatment applied to PG that resulted in the best reduction of cement setting times.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfuric acid is the chemical reagent used in the wet process to react with phosphate rock.</data>
