<node id="Cement">
  <data key="d0">Cement</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Cement is a fundamental binding material and primary construction binder extensively utilized in concrete, mortar, pastes, bricks, asphalt, and soil stabilization. Traditionally composed of calcite and processed compounds, or produced from the residual solid material following the thermal decomposition of phosphogypsum, it features a very fine particle size and moderate loss on ignition. It serves as a baseline reference for hydration and setting properties in geotechnical and materials studies. A significant area of development involves integrating industrial byproducts, particularly phosphogypsum and desulfurization or selenite gypsum, into cementitious systems. In these applications, phosphogypsum functions as a natural gypsum substitute, calcium oxide source, and set regulator, typically substituted at ratios ranging from 0% to 18% and evaluated at 28 days. While phosphogypsum incorporation generally accelerates hydration kinetics, increases matrix compactness, and boosts compressive strength and density, it also modulates setting time, often prolonging it, and can reduce clinker strength if impurities are present. The properties of cement are notably influenced by organic matters and chemical impurities, such as phosphorus and fluorine, found in phosphogypsum, which can degrade product quality and diminish antifreeze and weathering resilience despite cement's inherent water resistance.

Beyond phosphogypsum interactions, cement is routinely blended with fly ash, rice husk ash, quicklime, silica fume, sand, and tropical soil to produce materials for bricks, road bases, asphalt, storage areas, and parking lots. In specialized formulations like phosphogypsum-based self-leveling mortar, cement comprises approximately 7.6% of the mixture, where it regulates hydration reactions, ettringite formation, and overall structural strength, with reduced cement content directly increasing initial fluidity and flow. Industrially, waste phosphogypsum is treated and valorized within cement production to enhance sustainability and reduce costs, though large-scale implementation in regions like India and the Philippines faces constraints related to high decomposition temperatures for sulfuric acid coproduction. Nevertheless, technologies such as PSC successfully recover cement as a co-product. Ultimately, cement remains a versatile, foundational construction material that continuously evolves through the strategic incorporation of industrial byproducts and recycled aggregates to optimize performance, manage setting characteristics, and promote environmentally sustainable building practices.&lt;SEP&gt;A binding material produced by intergrinding treated PG with clinker, evaluated for setting time and mechanical strength.&lt;SEP&gt;Cement is the primary construction material whose setting times and mechanical properties are modified by various additives and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A binding material frequently studied for its interaction with phosphogypsum in construction applications.</data>
