<node id="Calcium Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium Sulfate (CaSO₄) is a versatile chemical and mineral compound that naturally occurs in crystalline forms including gypsum, bassanite, and anhydrite, existing as either a dihydrate or hemihydrate depending on crystallization temperature. It is universally recognized as the primary chemical component and major mineral phase of phosphogypsum, an impure industrial byproduct generated by reacting phosphate ores with sulfuric acid. Within this matrix, it serves as the principal host lattice for foreign ions and coprecipitated radionuclides, is responsible for the material's acidic properties, and is frequently identified in XRD patterns under the name gypsum. Its purity is often compromised by aluminum content, and spectral identification relies on characteristic Calcium-Oxygen bonds, crystalline phase markers aligned with ICDD #072-0916, and verification via green vertical tick marks.

The physicochemical behavior of Calcium Sulfate is heavily dependent on its source and processing methods. It exhibits an inversion temperature between 390°C and 450°C, which decreases to 370–390°C after purification, and undergoes a distinct phase transition from Anhydrite III to Anhydrite II at 428°C. Although it is the main component of raw phosphogypsum targeted for dissolution in hydrochloric acid during purification, its overall solubility in the acid is poor, a limitation that restricts extraction efficiency and rare earth element leaching; conversely, it shows notably high solubility in ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid. During phosphogypsum processing, filtration can be impeded by acicular fluorosilicate crystallization, while dissolution and hydration may be inhibited by calcium phosphate formation or encapsulation by calcium silicate. Additionally, its content increases during extended holding times and elevated temperatures.

Thermally, Calcium Sulfate acts as a starting material that undergoes gradual, temperature-dependent decomposition, characterized by decreasing diffraction peak intensity and initiating at Reaction I. It efficiently decomposes under molten conditions, participates in carbothermal reduction and self-decomposition, and reacts with carbon during decomposition. At high temperatures between 950 and 1200 °C, it decomposes via redox reactions to yield calcium oxide and sulfur dioxide, producing carbon dioxide when coke is introduced. It can be reduced by iron compounds, functions as an effective oxygen carrier, and frequently represents the formula for thermally treated residues in its common anhydrous form. During the thermal decomposition of MPG, it forms alongside hemihydrate calcium sulfate. It also reacts with ammonium carbonate during carbonation, initially transforming back into gypsum.

Chemically, Calcium Sulfate serves as a direct precursor for synthesizing crystalline whiskers with large lattice structures and a primary reactant in conversion equations to produce hydroxyapatite. It also acts as a main participant in the increase of potassium sulphate. Ongoing research focuses on its valorization using potassium hydroxide and carbon dioxide to recover valuable products for agricultural and industrial applications, as well as on extracting rare earth elements like europium, whose stability within the compound is actively analyzed. Beyond materials science, it is extensively utilized in construction and biomedical engineering as a foundational matrix in composite bone cement, where it dissociates to release calcium ions that facilitate hardening and structural development.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate is a compound in phosphogypsum whose filterability is improved by the acicular crystallization of fluorosilicates.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate is a mineral compound whose formation and impurity uptake are influenced by phosphoric acid processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate is the primary chemical component of phosphogypsum, characterized by sulfate ion bending vibrations.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate is the chemical compound that constitutes the primary component of gypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate (CaSO4) is a chemical compound extracted during the recrystallization process in phosphoric acid production, where phosphogypsum serves as the primary raw material.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Sulfate is a compound formed at high anhydrite contents, whose dissolution is expected to reduce mortar strength over time.</data>
