<node id="Rare Earth Elements">
  <data key="d0">Rare Earth Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Rare Earth Elements constitute a group of chemically similar, highly valuable, and strategically critical metallic elements, primarily characterized by ions with a +3 charge. This group encompasses the lanthanides along with scandium and yttrium, including both light and heavy variants, with specific members such as lanthanum, cerium, neodymium, and europium frequently highlighted. Often termed industrial vitamins, these elements are indispensable to modern technology, functional materials, and daily life, with global demand steadily rising, particularly for green and clean energy applications.

Naturally occurring in phosphate rocks, mineral concentrates, and geographically variable deposits like those in the Philippines, Rare Earth Elements are also widely recognized as valuable trace components and impurities in various industrial byproducts and waste streams. In the context of phosphogypsum and associated residues such as phosphoric acid sludge, WBS, TWBS, apatite ore, end-of-life products, shredder feed, synthetic phosphogypsum, marine sediments including the Santos Basin, and fertilizers, they exist as mineral inclusions, isomorphous substitutes, dispersed salts, or co-crystallized components within the gypsum dihydrate structure. Their distribution is non-uniform, with yttrium typically being the most abundant and europium often found on crystal surfaces. Concentrations generally remain below 0.1 wt% or 0.01 wt%, ranging between 344.32 and 349.14 ppm, and represent approximately 70-85% of the Rare Earth Elements originally present in phosphate rock. These elements exhibit low mobility, are primarily trapped in the phosphogypsum matrix, and their leaching is limited by the residue's solubility. Removal efficiency during processing peaks at two hours, slightly increases at four hours, and declines at eight hours. During extraction, they are frequently co-extracted alongside radionuclides, ultimately remaining in the decontaminated product.

Despite their low concentrations, Rare Earth Elements represent a high-value secondary source for critical raw materials and are heavily targeted for recovery through advanced hydrometallurgical, separation, bioleaching, acid leaching, ionic liquid, microwave, and innovative treatment techniques. Extraction methodologies encompass sulfuric acid leaching, mineral acid treatment, resin-in-leach systems, sequential chemical processes, multi-step diffusion and bonding break procedures, raw material beneficiation, digestion, and purification, often leveraging the solubility of calcite in acidic media. Although direct extraction from materials like phosphogypsum is complex and costly, the overall recovery process is considered cost-effective and carries inherent risks. Ultimately, extracting Rare Earth Elements from these secondary resources facilitates environmental remediation and pollution prevention, secures critical supply chains, reduces reliance on primary natural resource extraction, and transforms previously hazardous impurities into economically beneficial resources. Their applications, exploration, and environmental impact are extensively reviewed, particularly regarding sedimentary phosphate deposits, supply constraints, and potential global crises, underscoring their substantial value in sustainable resource management and advanced technological applications.</data>
