  <data key="d0">Carbonation Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Carbonation Process is a chemical transformation method that converts gypsum-derived compounds, specifically phosphogypsum and Portlandite (calcium hydroxide), into stable calcium carbonate using carbon dioxide (CO2). Operating as a distinct phase or second step in broader conversion workflows, the process is typically applied to calcium hydroxide following solid stream inputs. It involves the injection of a CO2 flow into a Portlandite solution, where the gas dissolves and triggers subsequent ion formation. This chemical reaction proceeds rapidly under mild conditions, typically completing in approximately 15 minutes at room temperature and atmospheric pressure.

Beyond its rapid kinetics, the Carbonation Process serves critical environmental and resource recovery functions. It is primarily utilized to sequester CO2 and alter the environmental behavior of phosphogypsum by transforming its dissolved calcium ions into stable calcium carbonate. A notable outcome of this chemical treatment is the concentration of rare earth elements within the resulting byproduct, thereby enhancing the process's utility for sustainable material management and industrial remediation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792120308</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrolysis">
  <data key="d0">Hydrolysis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction process fundamentally characterized by the interaction of water with various substances, leading to their breakdown, dissolution, or transformation into ions and other compounds. In its general mechanism, the process involves the cleavage of chemical bonds by water or hydroxide ions, often occurring concurrently with the dissolution of minerals such as calcium carbonate. This aqueous-driven transformation is essential for converting solid phases into soluble ionic species and other chemical compounds.

In specific industrial and materials science applications, hydrolysis is utilized across a range of distinct chemical systems. It is applied to calcium sulfide to produce an alkaline solution containing free calcium ions, hydrogen sulfide ions, and hydroxide ions, and is similarly employed to break down phosphogypsum decomposition slag into calcium sulfide and various impurities. The process also facilitates the aqueous dissolution of calcium-aluminum-silicate phases. Furthermore, hydrolysis plays a critical role in molecular bond cleavage, such as the breakdown of pyrophosphate bonds by hydroxide ions to yield water and phosphate ions, and is actively used in the synthesis of hydroxyapatite from precursor solutions. Collectively, these varied reactions demonstrate hydrolysis as a versatile chemical mechanism driven by aqueous interaction, essential for both material processing and compound synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrolysis is a chemical process used to synthesize hydroxyapatite from brushite, resulting in decomposition starting around 700 °C.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical reaction process used to generate hydrogen from hydrides.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical process resulting from raw material breakdown that releases SiO2 and facilitates ettringite formation.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical reaction that the stripping process aims to prevent by using faintly acidified water.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical process likely illustrated by the equation, where polyphosphates break down in the presence of hydroxide ions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791985383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CO2 Dissolution">
  <data key="d0">CO2 Dissolution</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CO2 Dissolution is a process describing the equilibrium chain of CO2 transitioning between gas, aqueous, and carbonate forms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790848098</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Precipitation">
  <data key="d0">Precipitation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Precipitation encompasses two distinct but related phenomena: a versatile chemical and physical separation process, and an atmospheric weather event. In industrial, environmental, and materials science applications, precipitation is a multifaceted chemical technique used to separate, immobilize, and transform dissolved substances. It operates by causing dissolved ions to solidify into distinct mineral phases or tangible solids, such as calcium carbonate, aluminum hydroxide, nano-calcium sulfate, or composite nanomaterials. This mechanism is routinely applied to extract target elements like aluminum and rare earth elements from solutions following initial leaching, effectively eliminating slightly soluble and insoluble impurities. It also serves as a conventional wet method for fabricating hydroxyapatite with controlled morphology and is indicated in the bottom left region of chemical phase diagrams. Additionally, precipitation functions as a critical environmental factor in leaching simulations and is employed to immobilize potentially toxic elements within stabilized phosphogypsum, where it facilitates the formation of relatively pure mineral phases from the material's major elements. The process can also yield tangible solid products when high-temperature autoclave mixtures cool to room temperature or when dissolved salts revert to their solid state.

In atmospheric and environmental contexts, precipitation manifests as both meteorological rainfall and a primary removal mechanism for airborne substances. Meteorologically, it is depicted as rainfall in the top left section that falls directly onto phosphogypsum stacks. Chemically, it acts as a dominant atmospheric cleansing process by extracting highly soluble compounds like hydrogen fluoride from the air. It also serves as the main pathway for removing phosphogypsum and its associated particulate matter, facilitating their deposition and reducing environmental dispersion. Together, these diverse applications underscore precipitation as both a fundamental chemical transformation and purification technique and a vital natural process for atmospheric purification and material deposition.&lt;SEP&gt;The chemical process where La, Ce, and Nd ions precipitate out of solution at pH values higher than 6.&lt;SEP&gt;Precipitation is the procedure where oxalic acid is added to the stripping solution to form RE oxalate.&lt;SEP&gt;Precipitation is a chemical synthesis technique involving the formation of solid particles from a solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A retention mechanism resulting in the formation of a new solid phase from a supersaturated liquid species.&lt;SEP&gt;A process contributing to the fate of elements in gypsum by forming pure mineral phases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-056&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792407841</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Industry">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Industry</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Phosphate Industry is the industrial sector dedicated to the mining and processing of phosphate rock. Its core operations are responsible for producing phosphoric acid and generating raw phosphogypsum, which serves as the initial feedstock for subsequent valorization processes. In addition to these primary outputs, the sector produces significant byproducts and residues, notably hazardous phosphogypsum and Naturally Occurring Radioactive Material (NORM) residues.

The substantial volume and potential hazards of these waste streams have made the Phosphate Industry a focal point for environmental and resource management research. Consequently, the sector's byproducts are extensively studied to assess environmental impacts, develop sustainable waste management strategies, and explore efficient resource recovery techniques. These ongoing efforts aim to mitigate ecological risks while enhancing the overall sustainability and circular economy potential of phosphate production.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791592065</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Washing Process">
  <data key="d0">Washing Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Washing Process is a purification step that removes soluble impurities from Raw PG to produce Washed PG.&lt;SEP&gt;A purification step that removes soluble impurities from raw phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;The washing process removes sodium sulfate and facilitates further increases in rare earth element concentration.&lt;SEP&gt;The Washing Process involves hot water and ethanol to remove surfactant residues from the synthesized samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Washing Process is a purification step using hot water and ethanol to remove surfactant residues from the synthesized samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A treatment applied to phosphogypsum that significantly improves compressive strength at low replacement percentages.&lt;SEP&gt;The washing process is applied to phosphogypsum, though it has negligible effects on compressive strength at higher replacement levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792502969</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonation System">
  <data key="d0">Carbonation System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbonation System is the industrial apparatus where Portlandite reacts with CO2 to produce Calcite.&lt;SEP&gt;Industrial equipment where Portlandite reacts with CO2 to form calcite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcite">
  <data key="d0">Calcite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcite is a common, naturally occurring, and industrially produced crystalline mineral and chemical compound composed of calcium carbonate (CaCO3). It exists as a stable carbonate mineral and a primary polymorph of calcium carbonate, characterized by a rhombohedral crystal structure. The mineral forms through carbonation, carbonization, and conversion reactions, frequently serving as the major crystalline phase in carbonation products. Its formation is thermodynamically favorable, possessing an energy state significantly lower than that of CO2.

Calcite is widely distributed across natural and industrial materials, functioning as the primary component of eggshells, a standard constituent in concrete mixes alongside quartz and portlandite, and a detectable phase in sodium bicarbonate-leached materials where it can constitute approximately 21.664% of the composition. Its abundance is influenced by specific material compositions, such as reduced production in cement-phosphogypsum systems incorporating fly ash. In industrial and environmental applications, calcite is primarily utilized as a building material or for lime production. It also serves as a secondary raw material in phosphogypsum recycling, where it competes with cheaper alternatives. Within phosphogypsum processing flowcharts, it represents a key mineral product yielded from the solid path branch, specifically as the final product obtained after filtration. Synthesized calcite exhibits notable environmental properties, including greater solubility than phosphogypsum in acidic environments and the ability to preferentially accumulate radionuclides. It has been evaluated as an adsorbent for environmental remediation, particularly for fluoride removal, though studies indicate it achieves significantly lower adsorption uptake compared to hydroxyapatite.

Analytically, calcite is routinely identified and characterized using X-ray diffraction. In carbonation products and samples containing approximately 12% CaCO3, it is distinctly identified by specific XRD peaks, notably peak 6 represented by a solid circle symbol, exhibiting characteristic diffraction clusters around 29°–30° and throughout the 30°–60° range.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcite is a crystalline phase identified in the XRD patterns and labeled as C.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcite is a mineral compound that precipitates on composite surfaces, forming a protective calcium carbonate coating.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792487165</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="KOH">
  <data key="d0">KOH</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">KOH is a chemical reagent added during the processing step to facilitate the production of Portlandite and Potassium Sulfate.&lt;SEP&gt;KOH, or potassium hydroxide, is a chemical reactant used in the thermostatic jacket to convert washed PG into Portlandite.&lt;SEP&gt;KOH is a chemical reagent used in the transformation of PG to produce calcium sulfate and potassium sulfate.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium hydroxide, a solution tested for its reaction with calcium carbonate.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical compound used to prepare a 200 g/L solution as specified in the standard.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium hydroxide, a chemical compound used in a formula to calculate CO2 concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792067577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Raw PG">
  <data key="d0">Raw PG</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Raw PG, an abbreviation for raw phosphogypsum, is an unprocessed and untreated material extracted directly from the phosphate industry prior to any industrial processing. Physically, it presents as a granular and pulverulent substance characterized by a tabular crystalline morphology. Chemically and geotechnically, it is typically characterized as a baseline material marked by high acidity, poor geotechnical properties, and high sensitivity to water, which generally leads to increased water absorption and a tendency to reduce compressive strength in composite applications. However, some studies note that in specific comparative contexts, raw PG can exhibit higher unconfined compressive strength than certain treated variants, indicating that its mechanical performance may vary depending on the specific treatment methods or testing conditions applied.

In practical and research applications, raw PG is frequently prepared by air-drying and sieving to ensure uniform particle distribution. It is evaluated and utilized across various construction and engineering contexts, including as a component in embankments, road mixes, and general construction layers. Additionally, it serves as a critical reference material in laboratory settings, such as being air-dried and sieved for incorporation into cementitious binder specimens. In academic and technical literature, the term is commonly employed to denote the pre-treatment composition of phosphogypsum, as seen in standardized tables and experimental frameworks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792485464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Washed PG">
  <data key="d0">Washed PG</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Washed PG is the purified phosphogypsum material obtained after the washing process removes soluble impurities.&lt;SEP&gt;Washed PG is phosphogypsum that has undergone a washing process to remove soluble impurities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Methanogenesis">
  <data key="d0">Methanogenesis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Methanogenesis is a biological process occurring in anaerobic layers where methanogens produce methane.&lt;SEP&gt;Methanogenesis is a biological process occurring in the anaerobic layer of rice paddies where methane is produced from organic matter decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;A biochemical process occurring in the anaerobic layer that produces methane.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acetogenesis">
  <data key="d0">Acetogenesis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Acetogenesis is a metabolic pathway in anaerobic conditions that converts substrates into acetate.&lt;SEP&gt;A biochemical process occurring in the anaerobic layer alongside methanogenesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Acetogenesis is a biological process occurring in the anaerobic layer of rice paddies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Decomposition of Soil Organic Matter">
  <data key="d0">Decomposition of Soil Organic Matter</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Decomposition of Soil Organic Matter is the breakdown process of organic material in lower anaerobic soil layers.&lt;SEP&gt;A breakdown process of organic material that occurs in the deep anaerobic layer.&lt;SEP&gt;Decomposition of soil organic matter occurs in the deep anaerobic layer, providing substrates for methanogenesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Methanogens">
  <data key="d0">Methanogens</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Methanogens are microorganisms residing in anaerobic layers that drive methanogenesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Methanogens are microorganisms residing in the anaerobic layer of rice paddies that drive the process of methanogenesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Microorganisms present in the anaerobic layer that drive methanogenesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790942862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rhizosphere">
  <data key="d0">Rhizosphere</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Rhizosphere is the soil region surrounding plant roots, characterized by microbial activity and methane consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhizosphere is the root zone region whose depth determines the appropriate soil horizon for mechanical processing.&lt;SEP&gt;The soil region surrounding plant roots where biological processes maintain a pH close to 7.&lt;SEP&gt;The soil region surrounding plant roots whose development is hindered by standard technology but improved by intra-soil milling.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhizosphere is the root zone region whose depth determines the appropriate soil horizon for mechanical processing.&lt;SEP&gt;The soil region surrounding plant roots where biological processes maintain a pH close to 7.&lt;SEP&gt;The soil region surrounding plant roots whose development is hindered by standard technology but improved by intra-soil milling.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790950059</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anaerobic Layer">
  <data key="d0">Anaerobic Layer</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Anaerobic Layer is the lower soil zone with negative redox potential where methanogenesis and acetogenesis occur.&lt;SEP&gt;A soil zone characterized by negative redox potential containing methanogens and specific biochemical processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
