  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fly Ash">
  <data key="d0">Fly Ash</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fly ash is a fine, aluminate-rich industrial by-product and combustion waste primarily generated from coal burning in thermal power plants. It is characterized by high concentrations of silica, alumina, and magnesium oxide, trace iron oxides, measurable baseline radionuclides, and very low loss on ignition, establishing it as a standard baseline for material property comparisons. Functioning as a supplementary cementitious material, pozzolanic blending agent, and soil stabilizer, fly ash is extensively utilized across construction, geotechnical engineering, and environmental remediation. In geotechnical applications, it stabilizes soil by modifying its plasticity index, reducing optimum moisture content, maximum dry density, and maximum dry unit weight, while significantly enhancing mechanical and unconfined compressive strength. Within concrete and construction matrices, its integration promotes ettringite precipitation and pore filling, which improves compressive strength, long-term durability, and water resistance, often yielding high mullite concentrations. It serves as a partial replacement for cement or clay in various building products, including bricks, marine construction, recycled aggregate concrete, cemented paste backfill, geopolymer cement, PSC or CCM formulations, and slag wool production, though it may increase water absorption in certain brick applications.

A major industrial focus involves the synergistic blending of fly ash with phosphogypsum in raw, treated, or calcined forms, alongside lime, cement clinker, yellow phosphorus slag-based binders, activated carbon, and red mud. This combination is widely employed to manufacture cementitious binders, mortars, stabilizing composites, filling slurries, road bases, pavement layers, and low-cost housing materials. The synergy is largely driven by sulfate ions released from AFt crystal breakdown, which modifies mix properties and enhances overall strength and durability; however, impurities within phosphogypsum can occasionally inhibit the pozzolanic reaction. In specific applications, fly ash is used to modify phosphogypsum as a cement retarder, partially replaced by calcined phosphogypsum to boost geopolymer compressive strength, or studied alongside phosphogypsum to analyze unit weight variations. Additionally, fly ash immobilizes phosphorus, constituting 35% of the CPGFM formulation at the Qingping Phosphate Mine, and is co-processed with red mud to facilitate resource recovery.

Beyond structural applications, fly ash supports diverse environmental and resource recovery initiatives. It is utilized in mechanochemical recycling processes to immobilize heavy metals, serves as an additive to enhance rare earth element transfer, and mitigates greenhouse gas emissions when combined with steel slag or applied to paddy fields. Accelerated carbonation processes employing fly ash are also deployed for brine remediation. To ensure optimal performance across these varied industrial and ecological contexts, fly ash has been rigorously evaluated under extreme conditions, including elevated temperatures, thermal shock cycles, and constant magnetic fields.&lt;SEP&gt;A supplementary material mixed with phosphogypsum and lime to form cementitious binders and hollow blocks.&lt;SEP&gt;Fly ash is an industrial byproduct incorporated into binder formulations to enhance mechanical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;A byproduct material combined with lime and phosphogypsum to create durable binders and activate cement systems.&lt;SEP&gt;An industrial by-product often combined with lime and phosphogypsum to create construction materials like hollow blocks.</data>
