<node id="Gabes">
  <data key="d0">Gabes</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Gabes is a coastal city and region situated in southeastern Tunisia, widely recognized for its concentration of industrial phosphate treatment plants and phosphate fertilizer factories. The area is closely linked to significant industrial byproducts, most notably extensive phosphogypsum stockpiles and designated dumping sites. Due to these operations, the region has become a focal point for scientific assessments regarding industrial waste radioactivity and broader environmental impact.

Environmental and geochemical studies in Gabes frequently evaluate its water quality in comparison to the nearby island of Djerba. Research indicates notably lower pH values in waters adjacent to phosphogypsum disposal zones. Comprehensive scientific characterizations of the region document a water pH range spanning 6.2 to 7.9, alongside distinct marine and geological features such as seafloor nodules and a high abundance of siliceous particles. These combined factors underscore Gabes as a major industrial center where economic activity intersects with ongoing environmental monitoring and geochemical research.&lt;SEP&gt;Gabes is a location in Tunisia where phosphate rock and phosphogypsum from fertilizer factories were studied.&lt;SEP&gt;A coastal city in southeastern Tunisia known for its phosphate fertilizer factories and associated mining activities.&lt;SEP&gt;A coastal city in southeastern Tunisia known for its phosphate fertilizer factories and associated mining activities.</data>
