<node id="SO2">
  <data key="d0">SO2</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">SO2 is a gaseous chemical pollutant and industrial air emission primarily generated through the reductive decomposition of phosphogypsum when processed alongside high-sulfur-concentration coal. The formation of this gas is chemically facilitated by iron (Fe) and further enhanced by nickel (Ni). As a recognized atmospheric contaminant, SO2 is formally cataloged in emissions tables and its output is systematically measured in kilograms across specific industrial frameworks, notably the PBG and PSC process scenarios.

Beyond its industrial origins, SO2 functions as a significant environmental hazard that heavily utilizes the ecological services provided by the atmospheric environment. It is a known contributor to respiratory disorders and is frequently evaluated for emergy loss to quantify its ecological footprint. Due to its hazardous properties, SO2 requires effective treatment and stringent control measures, typically necessitating capture by dedicated emissions control systems to mitigate its release into the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;SO2 is a gas whose concentration increases abruptly when temperatures exceed 750 °C.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792362890</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fe">
  <data key="d0">Fe</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Fe, chemically known as iron, is a chemical element that serves multiple roles across materials science, geochemistry, and analytical spectroscopy. In the context of phosphogypsum processing, Fe is introduced as an additive or catalyst component to investigate the material's thermal decomposition properties under multi-atmosphere control. Its presence specifically functions to reduce the decomposition temperature of phosphogypsum, thereby directly influencing its thermal breakdown behavior.

Beyond its application in thermal processing, Fe is systematically quantified in geochemical studies, where its concentration is measured, reported, and cataloged within geochemical datasets and data tables that specify defined concentration ranges. Furthermore, in metallurgical analysis, Fe refers to iron found in slag samples, with its valence states actively investigated using X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS). Together, these descriptions establish Fe as a critical element for modifying phosphogypsum thermal properties, tracking geochemical concentrations, and analyzing slag composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792097742</data>
