<node id="Water">
  <data key="d0">Water</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Water (H₂O) is a fundamental chemical compound with a standard enthalpy of formation of -285.8 kJ/mol, serving as both a reactant and a byproduct across numerous chemical, industrial, and environmental processes. Spectroscopically, it exhibits characteristic O-H stretching and bending vibrations between 3400 and 3445 cm⁻¹, particularly in materials like biochar, Curve A, calcium carbonate samples, and structural water within hemi-hydrate gypsum. Thermogravimetric analysis distinguishes its presence as surface moisture, adsorbed water, or crystallization water, with the latter two forms notably embedded within gypsum and hydroxyapatite structures. Chemically, Water drives diverse reactions including gasification, hydrolysis of calcium aluminum silicate hydroxide, fluorapatite acidulation, neutralization, and condensation. Conversely, it is generated as a secondary or sole byproduct during the water-gas shift reaction, pyrophosphate hydrolysis, VOC oxidation, hydroxyapatite dissolution and dehydroxylation, and various transformations involving gypsum, phosphogypsum, calcium phosphate, and phosphoric acid.

In material science and industrial applications, Water functions extensively as a solvent, reaction medium, and additive. It dissolves calcium carbonate, modulates the solubility of phosphatic and fluoride compounds (often alongside aqueous citric acid), and facilitates hydrothermal synthesis between 80°C and 400°C. It is integral to slurry preparation, wet phosphoric acid production, phosphogypsum adsorption studies, and dispersion and filtration processes. Water strips rare earth ions from ionic liquids, reduces their viscosity to enhance mass transfer, and migrates with hydrated calcium nitrate. In radiochemistry, it solubilizes Radon-222 in a temperature-dependent manner, reduces recoiling atom energy, and increases emanation coefficients. It also serves as the solvent in desorption studies to evaluate fluoride release, acts as a moisture component in MPG (where content decreases as temperature and holding time are reduced), and is mixed with phosphogypsum and other waste to create a pulp form for soil application. Furthermore, Water is critical for PCR, PBG, and PSC processes, phosphoric acid filtration, and the valorization of phosphogypsum in brick production, where it forms PGEF mixtures and CPGFM slurries to wash impurities and drive the conversion of calcium sulfate hemihydrate to gypsum.

Within construction, geotechnical engineering, and biological contexts, Water operates as a liquid additive mixed with gypsum powder and glass fibre during block formation, and it sets bone cement, though ethanol is required for displacement measurements. It influences pH and ion migration in cementitious materials, adjusts soil liquid limits for softening tests, and exists within hydroxyapatite where nanoscale OH groups influence protein adsorption and spectral bands weaken post-calcination. Environmentally and systemically, Water acts as a final discharge destination for unused biosolids and effluent, serving as a final output directed from the Effluent Box. While it interacts with atmospheric conditions and surface-disposed phosphogypsum and is systematically tracked in impact analyses, water bodies face significant pollution risks from phosphogypsum discharged via rivers, ponds, and landfills. Notably, Water is recognized as a resource with more abundant data compared to phosphorus, underscoring its pivotal role across chemical thermodynamics, industrial synthesis, material processing, and environmental systems.&lt;SEP&gt;A calm body of water in the foreground of the cover that reflects the trees and mountains.&lt;SEP&gt;Water is used extensively in the purification processes for washing, mixing, and slurrying PG samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Water is used as a washing agent for phosphogypsum and as a curing medium for supersulfated cement.&lt;SEP&gt;A component that decreases rapidly as temperature increases, dropping to near zero at higher temperatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Water is a chemical component listed in the chemical composition analysis of phosphogypsum samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-76e07d75a9f5124d07b9dafa2dacfba7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-05