  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphoric Acid">
  <data key="d0">Phosphoric Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphoric Acid is a strategic, water-soluble inorganic compound and a primary chemical product of the phosphate industry. It serves as a key industrial intermediate corresponding to phosphorus pentoxide inventory, forming when phosphorus pentoxide dissolves in water with a standard enthalpy of formation of -1271.7 kJ/mol. The compound is predominantly synthesized from calcium phosphate ores, such as apatite and phosphorite, through wet or dry manufacturing routes, most notably the wet-process acidulation using sulfuric acid. Major production complexes, particularly in Morocco, utilize this method, while alternative thermal, dihydrate, thorium(IV) phosphate, and citric acid extraction processes are also employed. Its production is central to phosphate fertilizer manufacturing, where it acts as both an intermediate and a key ingredient, with its concentration directly influencing phosphogypsum crystallization.

All manufacturing routes generate phosphogypsum as a major solid byproduct at approximately a five-to-one ratio, alongside hydrofluoric acid and recoverable sulfuric acid. The wet process specifically introduces silicon dioxide contamination into this byproduct. Chemically and environmentally, phosphoric acid functions as a partitioning medium and chemical agent that retains and migrates radionuclides, heavy metals, and heavy rare earth elements during dihydrate or hemihydrate processing. It partitions 70% to 85% of uranium and thorium isotopes (including approximately 70% of uranium-238), 20% to 30% of heavy rare earth elements, lead-210, and approximately eighty percent of cadmium content. As a residual interstitial component within phosphogypsum, it persists in pore spaces, dictates waste acidity and composition, and exists primarily as soluble phosphorus ions. It dominates leachates below pH 5, is removed via pure water rinsing, and requires rigorous purification to eliminate impurities for safe, radiologically accurate use. Regional policies often legally link production volumes to phosphogypsum recycling mandates.

Industrially and agriculturally, phosphoric acid is a vital precursor for fertilizers, white phosphorus, fluorine separation, analytical degradation, and monoammonium phosphate production. It supplies phosphate ions for precipitation and neutralization, notably synthesizing hydroxyapatite Type 1 and hybrid nano-hydroxyapatite with calcium hydroxide. It is combined with waste phosphogypsum, HPA, and calcium sulfate dihydrate, and utilized with nitric acid or standalone to leach rare earth elements from ores and phosphogypsum, as well as in lime calcination. When added to phosphogypsum mixtures, it drives synthesis, influences crystal morphology, and affects filtration rates, though it can adsorb onto hemihydrate particles to hinder hydration or react with quicklime to form inert salts. Procured from suppliers like POCh Gliwice for thermodynamic analyses, it holds high global demand and strategic economic value, historically driving Chinese phosphorus chemical initiatives and serving as a conventional soil amendment. In emergy assessments, it accounts for 4.58% of total input in Scenario 1, 4.44% in Scenario 2, 1.49% in Scenario 3, and 4.28% in other specific system evaluations. Its production processes are continuously optimized and evaluated across multiple recovery and processing studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical compound produced by the phosphate fertilizer industry, with phosphogypsum generated per tonne of its production.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical compound produced industrially that generates phosphogypsum as a major byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphoric Acid is a chemical product manufactured from phosphate rock, with production generating phosphogypsum waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphoric Acid is a residual acid contained within Phosphogypsum, contributing to its highly acidic nature with a pH below 3.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphoric acid is an acidic solution used in leaching experiments with calcined phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphoric acid is a water-soluble impurity removed from PG using citric acid solution.</data>
