<node id="Fluorine">
  <data key="d0">Fluorine</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluorine is a non-metallic chemical element (F) that serves as a primary chemical component and persistent impurity within phosphogypsum and related industrial matrices. Originating from fluoride minerals, volcanic activity, manufacturing processes, and phosphate ore, it is retained alongside phosphorus during production and is notably absent in natural gypsum. Within these materials, fluorine exists in soluble and insoluble forms, frequently associating with fluorapatite, hydration products, and rare earth element-enriched phases. It constitutes a major component of the fluorite phase, reaching up to 43.67 weight percent, and is consistently detected with a uniform spatial presence that mirrors chromium distributions and aligns closely with calcium and phosphorus. Reported concentrations in bulk phosphogypsum typically range from 1.17 to 1.39 weight percent, though values span from 0.12% to 35.00 weight percent depending on the sample matrix and analytical methodology. Analytical techniques such as SEM-EDS routinely quantify fluorine as a volatile species, revealing sharp ring structures and tracking its behavior during the acid attack of phosphate ore where it appears in mobile solid or dissolved forms.

As an environmental contaminant, fluorine readily adsorbs to phosphogypsum surfaces and mobilizes into aqueous solutions, with leaching and immobilization heavily governed by pH levels and backfill interactions. It migrates into surrounding soils and aquifer sediments, significantly elevating total and water-soluble concentrations in agricultural zones where phosphogypsum is applied. This contamination disrupts local ecosystems and enters the human body through environmental cycling, potentially inducing dental and skeletal fluorosis. Geochemically, fluorine influences the coprecipitation of calcium and rare earth elements, substitutes oxygen during initial silica dissolution, and migrates across solid and gas phases in processes like chemical looping gasification. Industrially, its presence adversely affects phosphogypsum reuse by reducing crystal interaction forces, altering cement setting times, and diminishing the initial strength of gypsum and filler bodies.

Effective management of fluorine requires balancing its environmental hazards with its industrial utility. During phosphogypsum remediation, it acts as a pollutant that can be retained by N-Al2O3 and subsequently removed by carbide slag, or recovered from waste leachate through precipitation processes. Its concentration decreases significantly in specific hydrated matrices like PBSLM6 compared to raw phosphogypsum, and detection in modified phosphogypsum occurs under specific temperature and time conditions. Standard industrial protocols prioritize removal or stabilization via fluidized calcination, lime and flotation treatment, citric acid application, and advanced solidification techniques that trap the element for secure storage, with residual levels monitored using fluorine ion-selective electrodes. Conversely, when intentionally incorporated into hydroxyapatite matrices, fluorine forms fluorohydroxyapatite ceramics that significantly enhance thermal and chemical stability. It is also deliberately added to alpha-hemihydrate gypsum to improve mechanical properties. In research contexts, fluorine serves as a target adsorbate measured in milligrams per gram of adsorbent during kinetic studies, underscoring the necessity for precise control throughout chemical processing.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element that decreases significantly with increasing temperature, particularly in washed samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorine is a chemical component analyzed in the phosphogypsum samples, with data noted for washed samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-05