<node id="Table 1">
  <data key="d0">Table 1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 1 functions as a recurring, context-dependent data compilation label widely utilized across scientific disciplines rather than representing a single fixed dataset. It serves as a standardized repository that dynamically adapts its structure and content to report key experimental parameters, analytical results, and methodological details specific to each publication. In materials science and chemistry, Table 1 catalogs chemical compositions, reaction conditions, thermodynamic data, and standard enthalpy of formation values. It frequently presents quantitative XRF and EDS analysis results detailing the chemical and oxide compositions of phosphogypsum and nanosized hydroxyapatite derived from it, expressed in weight percentages. Within phosphogypsum research, it organizes sample codes, industrial sources, detailed chemical profiles, and major and trace element concentrations from regions such as Morocco, Hubei, Yunnan, and Guizhou. It also records spectroscopic characterization, nitrate leach liquid analysis, α-Hemihydrate production methods, macro-ion content, Haplic Chernozem soil profiles, heavy metal pollutant levels in phosphogypsum evaluation frameworks, and data quality metrics for phosphorus flow analysis. Additionally, Table 1 compares the radioactivity of phosphogypsum against concrete, ceramic bricks, and natural gypsum, and serves as a reference for oxide compositions across various studies.

In biomedical and biomaterials contexts, Table 1 extensively details hydroxyapatite and bone cement characteristics. It outlines synthesis methods, preparation conditions including molar ratios, pH, temperature, and duration, and resulting morphologies. The table records crystallographic properties, crystallinity degrees, and particle sizes for hydroxyapatite samples prepared with CTAB and P123 surfactants. It specifically summarizes BET surface area, pore volume, mean pore size, and textural characteristics for microspheres synthesized at different hydrothermal times. Furthermore, it documents calcium and phosphorus weight percentages, as well as Ca/P ratios analyzed via ICP-OES for S-HAp and BA samples. It also records physical properties of composite bone cements, such as density, porosity, setting times, and performance across varying sodium chloride concentrations, alongside simulated body fluid reagent specifications. In some studies, it shows volume percentages of hydroxyapatite within different size regions for varying reaction and milling times.

Beyond materials and biomedical applications, Table 1 extends into agricultural, environmental, and industrial research. It compiles physicochemical and geotechnical data for soils, including test soils I and II and selected adobe soil, while supporting microbial fermentation and material decomposition studies. The table provides sieve analysis results, life cycle inventory analyses for technologies like PCR, PBG, and PSC, and adsorption capacities for sorbents targeting formic acid, cadmium, and cobalt ions. It also defines waste composition of functional units and presents the main chemical constituents and percentages of synthetic and commercial potassium sulfate. In methodological and evaluative frameworks, it summarizes evaluation systems by listing indicators, their connotations, units, calculations, and sources, detailing the four dimensions and sixteen indicators used for weight determination, along with specific calculation methods and raw data references. Across all domains, Table 1 consistently delivers a structured, context-specific framework for essential experimental and analytical data.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 1 presents the chemical composition and fineness data for OPC and PPC.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 1 lists the chemical compositions and fineness of the ordinary and pozzolanic Portland cements used.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-01