<node id="Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate is a versatile chemical compound and anionic group characterized by a central atom bonded to four oxygen atoms, commonly existing as PO43- and HPO42- species in aqueous and mineral environments. Functioning as a reactive ion in chemical transformations, such as reactions with sulfuric acid, it is also frequently referenced in spectroscopic measurements. Geologically, phosphate is extracted from phosphate rocks as a primary mineral resource and serves as the essential raw material for phosphoric acid production. It constitutes a fundamental anionic group in apatite and hydroxyapatite minerals, where it can be naturally substituted by carbonate, vanadate, arsenate, and sulfate, or exchanged with selenite during adsorption. Within hydroxyapatite, phosphate interacts with protein amine groups and acts as a chemical precursor that combines with calcium to form hydroxyapatite on FTO substrates.

In industrial and material contexts, phosphate is a primary chemical component of phosphogypsum, existing in crystalline lattice forms within particles smaller than 25 mm and as Ca(PO3)2, which contributes approximately 0.2% to raw phosphogypsum. It is frequently identified as a minor phase impurity and a soluble contaminant that alters gypsum crystal morphology, negatively impacting setting and hardening processes, thereby necessitating its removal prior to plaster manufacturing. Its presence also directly influences the physical properties of cemented backfill. Additionally, phosphate acts as a counterbalancing ion associated with europium within secondary phases and on the surfaces of gypsum crystals.

From a biological and environmental standpoint, phosphate is an essential nutrient for plant growth, though its environmental bioavailability is often limited due to its tendency to fix with various metals. It frequently accumulates at high concentrations in groundwater near phosphogypsum stacks, where it forms mobile complexes with radionuclides. Consequently, phosphate is closely monitored as a chemical discharge and liquid emission, typically reported as phosphorus (P) with concentration limits and determination methods defined by environmental standards. Its leaching potential and presence in groundwater emissions are routinely quantified in kilograms and integrated into environmental analyses and Life Cycle Inventory data associated with phosphogypsum disposal.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate is an impurity present in PG that contributes to the retardation of cement hydration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-003</data>
