<node id="Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfate ($SO_4^{2-}$) is a double-charged chemical anion that functions as a discrete ion or integral component of broader compound groups, such as thorium(IV) sulfate and potassium sulphate. Chemically, it is highly reactive and exhibits strong characteristic stretching vibration bands in the 1000–1100 cm⁻¹ range of the FTIR spectrum. Structurally, sulfate can substitute for phosphate within apatite crystal lattices, enhance the partitioning of uranium into gypsum crystal lattices, and form sludges with radium and barium, which directly affects radium solubility. In aqueous environments, it predominantly exists in a free state, with its molar fraction routinely calculated using the solution's effective ionic strength. It participates in various chemical processes, including reduction by carbon to release sulfur dioxide and carbon dioxide gases, and acts as a reactive anion in speciation modeling where it interacts with cadmium and influences the type of lead precipitate formed during immobilization. Additionally, sulfate forms insoluble precipitates with lead in phosphogypsum and serves as a counterbalancing ion associated with europium on the surface of gypsum crystals and within secondary phases.

Sulfate is a primary constituent of phosphogypsum and gypsum stacks, with phosphogypsum serving as a major sulfate source. It is frequently extracted and measured across different aqueous media, migrating from these stacks into surrounding environmental compartments as a key component of process water and leachate. Groundwater monitoring consistently tracks sulfate concentrations, which are measured as dissolved ions, chemical discharges, and liquid emissions in Life Cycle Inventory data for phosphogypsum disposal, often quantified in kilograms and frequently revealing levels significantly elevated above natural background concentrations. Analytical data tables typically catalog these measurements with specific superscript notation, showing a steady downward trend that can increase during neutralization processes. The environmental fate of sulfate is commonly predicted using GEOCHEM modeling software, and its leaching is routinely estimated in environmental analyses.

From a remediation and regulatory standpoint, sulfate can be effectively removed from process water and leachate through interaction with geological materials, with removal efficiency notably enhanced by iron(III) hydroxide and abundant calcium. During large-scale stockpiled phosphogypsum remediation, sulfate acts as a pollutant and chemical compound retained by N-Al2O3. Despite its prevalence, sulfate is classified as a contaminant whose emissions to soil and water currently lack damage factors in primary impact assessment methods. Consequently, it is subject to established regulatory thresholds, including a general limit of 250 mg/L and a World Health Organization guideline value of 25 mg/L. Collectively, these characteristics underscore sulfate's significance as both a fundamental reactive chemical species and a quantitatively monitored environmental parameter.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate is a dissolved ion in the acid that influences fluoride precipitation and gypsum structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate is an ion whose increasing levels cause crystal size to increase and shape to stretch into needle-like forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate is a chemical group (SO₄²⁻) identified through specific absorption bands in the FTIR spectra.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate is a chemical component whose concentration must be kept below 4.5% for slag to be suitable as concrete aggregate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-001</data>
