<node id="Zhang et al.">
  <data key="d0">Zhang et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhang et al. refers to multiple distinct research groups cited across various scientific domains, with a prominent cluster focusing on environmental science, materials engineering, and industrial waste management. These researchers have extensively investigated the utilization of phosphogypsum and its integration into sustainable construction and carbon capture technologies. Their work includes developing a novel cementitious composite material utilizing phosphogypsum, Portland slag cement, fly ash, and quicklime, as well as examining the mechanical properties of recycled aggregate concrete that incorporates slag, fly ash, and phosphogypsum. Additionally, they have applied molten phosphogypsum to engineer new in-situ-built walls featuring enhanced mechanical strength and thermal insulation. Their investigations into the carbonation rate and reactivity of phosphogypsum, alongside the reaction mechanisms of phosphogypsum decomposition slag for carbon dioxide sequestration, highlight their focus on greenhouse gas reduction and sustainable waste management. Further contributions include assessing phosphogypsum's quality and suitability as a filling material, as well as researching the application of calcium hydroxide to immobilize soluble phosphorus within phosphogypsum.

In the fields of mineral processing and hydrometallurgy, a separate research team cited as Zhang et al. has documented the chemical decomposition of phosphates extracted from monazite ore using caustic soda. This work provides critical insights into alkaline extraction methodologies for efficient phosphate recovery. Concurrently, related studies attributed to this citation have examined the preferred orientation growth of calcium sulfate dihydrate, contributing to a deeper understanding of crystalline formation processes relevant to sulfate-based materials.

A third distinct group referenced as Zhang et al. has contributed to agricultural science and horticulture by applying orthogonal experimental design to systematically optimize fertilizer ratios for blueberry cultivation. Their research aims to enhance crop productivity and nutrient efficiency through structured statistical methodologies, demonstrating the citation's broad interdisciplinary reach across environmental, materials, mineral, and agricultural sciences.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhang et al. are researchers who previously reported the disappearance of sharp endothermic peaks reflecting thermal changes of Hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers cited as a source for land occupation indicator data.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhang et al. are researchers who popularized the method for calculating additional local ecological services.</data>
