<node id="China">
  <data key="d0">China</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">China, a predominant agricultural nation in North Asia with a population exceeding 1.4 billion, serves as a critical geographic, policy, and research hub for global industrial and environmental studies. As the world’s largest producer of phosphate rock, phosphate fertilizer, and phosphoric acid, China generates approximately 70 to 80 million metric tons of phosphogypsum annually, representing roughly 20 percent of global output and holding an estimated 40 percent of the world’s stock. The nation’s geological profile, characterized by calcium phosphate rock with approximately 15 percent silicon dioxide content and domestic sulfur shortages, drives substantial phosphogypsum discharge, particularly from provinces like Yunnan. This waste typically contains sulfur trioxide levels between 34.00 wt% and 53.72 wt%, and calcium oxide from 28.19 wt% to 40.98 wt%. Historically burdened by massive solid waste accumulation since the 1950s, China faces significant land and environmental risks, with environmental analyses in regions such as Chongqing revealing variable contamination profiles, including heavy metals and radioactivity that occasionally exceed regulatory limits. Heavy metal risk assessments have also been conducted on agricultural products like rice.

To mitigate these challenges, the government has implemented strict regulatory frameworks that have shifted the focus from traditional stockpiling toward large-scale recycling and low-value utilization. Consequently, the comprehensive phosphogypsum utilization rate rose to 55.6 percent between 2015 and 2023, with national targets aiming for 75 percent by 2025. Approximately 30 percent of phosphogypsum is currently utilized for cement retarder production, while advanced recycling technologies are being industrialized across dozens of mines in Guizhou, Hubei, and the Yangtze River Basin. Key applications include cemented paste backfill, the commercial deployment of the Merseburg ammonocarbonation process, and innovative treatments for industrial carbon dioxide-containing tail gas. Beyond waste management, China functions as the world’s largest consumer of natural gypsum and a key market for construction materials such as PBSLM. The country also navigates its status as the largest global carbon dioxide emitter through comprehensive carbon neutrality initiatives and energy transition policies, while concurrently addressing historical international scrutiny over phosphate supply monopolies and export quotas. As the primary source for national statistics and environmental accounting data, China’s proactive policy innovations and industrial scale continue to shape international standards for sustainable waste management, ecological protection, and industrial resource optimization. Additionally, China serves as a predominant country in North Asia and a primary destination for Australian agricultural exports.

In scientific and industrial research, China hosts the majority of surveyed recycling companies and serves as the foundational context for evaluating disposal practices, lifecycle inventories, and industrial standards. It is the base for numerous research institutions and authors, leading global scientific inquiry by producing over 800 publications that account for approximately 44 percent of relevant analytical frameworks. Research and operational sites span Guizhou University, Fuzhou for geotechnical studies, and Ordos City for ecological efficiency analyses. The country is frequently referenced in literature concerning environmental accountability, surface water quality, phosphorus cycling, sustainable crop production, fertilizer industry trends, green building emissions, municipal solid waste incineration, new energy vehicle policies, and low-impact development. Furthermore, China is a geographical source of phosphogypsum with documented major element compositions, and it is the location where phosphogypsum samples were analyzed and reported in scientific literature, including studies by Cao et al. and Li and Zhang. These samples have been analyzed for radionuclide content, and the nation is also recognized as a country where unconventional uranium in phosphate rock has been reviewed and documented, as well as a geographic region referenced in the broader context of phosphate rock uranium resources. Additionally, China hosts critical rare earth deposits at Bayan Obo and Longnan, which frequently serve as case studies for life cycle assessments, and is the country where Shanghai Precision &amp; Scientific Instrument Co. Ltd. is located.</data>
