<node id="PG">
  <data key="d0">PG</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphogypsum (PG) is an acidic, damp, powdery, and silty industrial byproduct classified as a Class II solid waste, primarily generated by phosphate chemical plants and the fertilizer industry. Originally formed as large block-like structures before processing, PG is chemically dominated by calcium sulfate dihydrate within a complex matrix containing minor compounds such as calcium fluoride, fluorine, phosphorus, and soluble phosphorus impurities. Physically, it exhibits a specific gravity of 2.89, a coarse particle size distribution, high loss on ignition, and the highest Blaine fineness, and is classified as an A-4 or ML soil. The material is notably enriched with rare earth elements, rare and precious heavy metals, and toxic elements, with their distribution governed by specific ionic properties and the host material's crystalline structure. Additionally, PG exhibits significant radioactive disequilibrium characterized by deviations from natural isotopic ratios, making it a frequently utilized dried sample for radionuclide concentration measurements. Due to its inherent acidity, corrosiveness, and high heavy metal content, PG poses substantial ecological and human health hazards. Contaminants migrate into environmental media through stacking, disposal, rainwater, leaching, and fertilization, with initial leachates containing high phosphorus concentrations.

Consequently, PG requires careful management and pretreatment, and its dual nature as both an environmental challenge and a versatile industrial resource drives research into safe extraction methods, including dissolution, decomposition, crystallization, hydrometallurgical conversion, and hydrothermal treatment. Thermal and chemical processing significantly alters PG's properties; thermal treatment decomposes calcium sulfate, releasing impurities like silicon dioxide and generating calcium oxide, while calcination decreases the crystal aspect ratio, improving internal compactness and fluidity. Purification and calcination processes are commonly applied to mitigate impurities that otherwise affect hydration and crystal growth. In experimental and analytical contexts, PG serves as a baseline material compared against PGI-1.4, appears as a solid blue line in spectral graphs, bears the label PG on XRD graphs for raw material identification, and functions as a quartz-containing starting material. Its reuse in alternative resource management mitigates environmental impacts by avoiding leachate production and reducing ecological services for pollutant dilution, aligning with circular economy considerations discussed in the context of mining sustainability.

Industrially, PG acts as a precursor and calcium source for synthesizing hydroxyapatite (HAp) and nano-hydroxyapatite (nHAp) particles used as adsorbents, though impurities may contaminate particles and elevate calcium-to-phosphorus ratios. In energy conversion, PG operates as an oxygen carrier or foundational support for compound oxygen carriers during the chemical looping gasification of low-rank lignite to produce syngas, leveraging strong oxygen-carrying capacity while silicon dioxide inhibits grain growth. In construction and geotechnical engineering, PG functions as a soil stabilizer, amendment, partial cement replacement, aggregate, filler, mineralizer, and fluxing agent in cement, clinker, mortar, concrete, and brick manufacturing. Its hydration generates crystal formation that dictates strength, while its cohesion affects the fluidity and hardened structure of mixes like PBSLM and PBSLM6. It acts as an activator and filler, reacting with aluminates to produce ettringite and influencing hydration processes, microstructural development, workability, and compressive strength, although impurities can occasionally hinder cementitious hydration. PG serves as a primary component in CPGFM, a base for engineered mixes for embankments and pavements, a key ingredient in CM-PG-SM-HRB mixes, and a clay/sand replacement in brick production. It is also evaluated for road construction with available life cycle inventory data, showing varying effects on material strength depending on concentration. In backfill applications, it temporarily immobilizes fluoride through calcium-fluoride precipitation, though rising pH levels risk secondary release. Throughout these applications, PG is evaluated for its physical and chemical properties and compared against MPG in heavy metal risk assessment models.&lt;SEP&gt;PG is a byproduct investigated for its effects on cement pastes and mortars, including setting time, soundness, and strength.&lt;SEP&gt;PG is a material used to partially replace OPC in mortar mixes, leading to decreased mechanical strengths.&lt;SEP&gt;PG is a replacement material for OPC in mortar mixes, whose percentage replacement inversely affects mortar strength.&lt;SEP&gt;PG (Phosphogypsum) is the industrial byproduct subjected to calcination and chemical analysis.</data>
