<node id="Apatite">
  <data key="d0">Apatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Apatite is a versatile group of phosphate minerals primarily occurring as a natural calcium phosphate ore, frequently manifesting as carbonate apatite within phosphorite formations. It serves as the dominant phosphate mineral in phosphate rock deposits and functions as the principal raw material for phosphoric acid production through sulfuric acid treatment. The apatite crystal structure is highly accommodating to trace elements, which readily substitute for calcium due to similar ionic radii or adsorb onto the lattice. This includes uranium, commonly incorporated in both U(IV) and U(VI) oxidation states, as well as thorium, yttrium, and radium. The substitution of radium for calcium specifically leads to Radon-222 emanation coefficients ranging from 0.5% to 25%. Beyond phosphorus extraction, apatite is recognized as a valuable, non-conventional source for recovering fluorine, strontium, and rare-earth elements. During industrial processing, apatite may sometimes remain unreacted and persist as an insoluble impurity, though complete dissolution releases rare-earth elements that can precipitate as secondary phosphate phases. Consequently, apatite is frequently subjected to dissolution studies to understand rare-earth element release rates across varying pH levels.

In industrial contexts, apatite is subjected to wet chemical attack to generate phosphogypsum, which serves as a primary byproduct. Apatite also acts as a mineral component within the phosphogypsum matrix, where it contains yttrium and uranium. Structurally, apatite functions as a phase where phosphate and fluoride ions associate and reprecipitate. It also operates as a crystalline phase that nucleates and grows on ACP microspheres following a dissolution-reprecipitation mechanism.

In biomedical applications, apatite is essential for bioactivity. When implanted biomaterials or composite bone cements are exposed to physiological environments or Simulated Body Fluid, a bone-like apatite crystal layer precipitates on their surfaces. This precipitation mechanism is fundamental to achieving effective osseointegration and tissue bonding in medical treatments. The structural chemistry and synthesis of apatite underpin its wide-ranging industrial, geochemical, and medical applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-011</data>
