<node id="Hydroxyapatite">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxyapatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydroxyapatite is a naturally occurring and synthetically produced calcium phosphate mineral with the chemical formula Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2, crystallizing in a hexagonal system. It serves as the primary structural component of vertebrate bones, teeth, and enamel, and functions as a biologically relevant material and synthetic bone mineral substitute. Thermodynamically, it has a standard enthalpy of formation of -4120.8 kJ/mol, remains stable in neutral to alkaline pH environments, and corresponds to reference code ICDD #073-1731. Its flexible crystal lattice readily accommodates anionic substitutions, such as carbonate (forming Type B variants) or fluoride (yielding fluorapatite), which alter its crystallographic structure, texture coefficients, and optical properties. The compound exhibits a negatively charged surface, high porosity, structural and thermal stability, modulable acid-base characteristics, exceptional biocompatibility, bioactivity, non-toxicity, and high adsorption and ion-exchange capacities.

Hydroxyapatite is extensively synthesized via co-precipitation, calcination, microwave-driven hydrothermal processing, neutralization, in situ techniques, and single-pot methods. Common precursors include phosphogypsum, flue gas desulfurization gypsum, bovine bone, eggshells, and phosphoric acid, with the target solid typically precipitating under controlled pH conditions or during phase separation. Synthesis parameters, reaction agents like CTAB and various surfactants, and post-treatments such as citrate, AL molecule functionalization, HDTMP modification, and heat processing enable precise control over particle size (often maintained around 22 nm or ranging from 6 to 30 nm for nano-HAp), morphology (including nanoparticles, nanowires, nanorods, flaky structures, microspheres, nanopowders, rod-like, and spherical particles calcined at 900 °C), crystallinity, specific surface area, and doping profiles. The material's particle size distribution, volume fractions, and crystalline grain size are frequently analyzed under different milling conditions. It is rigorously characterized using X-ray diffraction (where it often appears as the likely mineral phase labeled H), Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy for stoichiometric and structural analysis, phosphate peak analysis, zeta potential, particle size analysis, thermal evaluation, density benchmarking, and microscopic techniques.

Due to its versatile physicochemical and biological properties, Hydroxyapatite is deployed across numerous fields. In biomedical and orthopaedic applications, it is utilized for bone tissue engineering, skeletal repair, dental implants, composite bone cements, nanocomposites, medical device coatings, chromatography media, biosensors, and controlled drug delivery systems. It is also extensively studied for its bioactivity and applications in regenerative medicine, serving as a precursor for biomimetic materials. Environmentally, it functions as a non-toxic sorbent and immobilization agent for wastewater and soil remediation, effectively capturing heavy metals, radionuclides, fluoride, selenium, organic dyes, and fulvic acid, frequently in combination with biochar. Industrially and chemically, it serves as a heterogeneous catalyst or support for oxidative and photocatalytic degradation, VOC oxidation, and biodiesel production. Additionally, it is employed as an agricultural fertilizer, a corrosion protection substrate, and a base material for engineered composites, thin coatings, and experimental samples where its specific surface area, pore size, photoelectrochemical behavior, and pore structure properties are evaluated. Structural similarity is often assessed via the Ca/P ratio, and ion exchange with fluoride occurs primarily on the surface rather than in the bulk, making it a highly adaptable compound in both natural and synthetic contexts.</data>
