<node id="Equation 12">
  <data key="d0">Equation 12</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation 12 is a chemical formula presented at the beginning of the text, likely representing a reaction involving ammonium and sulfate.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 12 is a chemical formula presented at the beginning of the text, likely representing a reaction involving ammonium and sulfate.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 12 is a chemical formula presented at the beginning of the text, likely representing a reaction involving ammonium and sulfate.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 12 is a chemical equation excerpted from a scientific paper discussing phosphogypsum chemistry.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 12 describes the chemical reaction forming Ca(H₂PO₄)₂ from CaHPO₄, P₂O₅, and water.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 12 rearranges previous equations to relate peak broadening to crystallite size and strain.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 12 is the mathematical formula used to calculate standard enthalpy and entropy changes from the adsorption equilibrium constant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792317061</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation 13">
  <data key="d0">Equation 13</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation 13 is a generic numerical label applied to multiple distinct mathematical and chemical formulas across various scientific disciplines, each tailored to specific analytical contexts. In rare earth metallurgy and mineral processing, it denotes a chemical formula that summarizes the decomposition of rare earth element phosphates extracted from monazite ore using caustic soda. Within cement chemistry and quicklime analysis, the designation refers to a calculation used to determine the theoretical content of soluble phosphorus pentoxide (P₂O₅) based on quicklime parameters, alongside a separate formulation that describes the chemical hydration reaction of calcium silicate.

In materials science and crystallography, Equation 13 modifies a preceding formula to graphically illustrate material strain and crystallite size. In broader mathematical and thermodynamic frameworks, it serves as a referenced component in the derivation of Equation 17 and functions as a thermodynamic expression defining the standard free energy change of adsorption. Because the identifier Equation 13 is reused across different publications and fields, its precise mathematical structure and chemical meaning must always be evaluated within the specific scientific domain from which it originates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792317751</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation 14">
  <data key="d0">Equation 14</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation 14 refers to multiple distinct formulas and chemical equations used across various scientific and engineering disciplines, rather than representing a single universal definition. In nuclear chemistry and mineral processing, it denotes a chemical formula summarizing the decomposition of thorium phosphate from monazite ore using caustic soda. Within materials science and physics, Equation 14 expresses Hooke's law, mathematically relating mechanical stress to strain via Young's modulus and serving to estimate minimum strain values. In cement chemistry, it describes the specific chemical reaction between Tricalcium Aluminate and Gypsum.

Additionally, Equation 14 is applied in environmental and dental chemistry to characterize the ion exchange reaction between hydroxyapatite and fluoride ions. In certain water treatment and engineering contexts, it functions as a calculation method for determining the theoretical concentration of fluoride ions based on quicklime parameters. These varied applications demonstrate that Equation 14 serves as a contextual label for distinct scientific relationships spanning inorganic chemistry, solid mechanics, and materials engineering.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792317751</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fig. 4">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 4</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 4 is a recurring figure label utilized across numerous independent scientific publications rather than representing a single, unified diagram. Its content varies significantly depending on the specific study, predominantly appearing in research concerning phosphogypsum utilization, materials science, environmental remediation, and engineering applications. In industrial processing and rare earth element recovery, Fig. 4 illustrates primary extraction technologies, procedural steps, leaching efficiency under various acid types and reaction conditions, and functions as a flowchart for nano-hydroxyapatite production via phosphogypsum decomposition with hydrochloric acid. It also displays thermal degradation data showing mass loss variations across heating rates, outlines challenges and solutions for phosphogypsum recycling, maps the distribution of comprehensive phosphogypsum utilization routes in China for 2022, presents the mathematical model of natural convection for PGEF, visualizes Equation 15 for synthesized hydroxyapatite samples, and presents comprehensive utility performance metrics for phosphogypsum recycling technologies.

Within materials science and biomedical engineering, Fig. 4 documents microstructural and compositional analyses. It presents scanning electron microscopy micrographs of composite bone cements at varying magnifications, often supplemented by energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy data, and transmission electron microscopy images comparing hydroxyapatite samples prepared via hydrothermal and microwave methods. The figure tracks hydroxyapatite morphology transformations during calcium and citrate solution stirring, displays X-ray diffraction patterns and attenuated total reflectance spectra, and compares the compressive strength of bricks across studies by Zhou et al., Raut et al., and Azam et al. Furthermore, it visualizes the microstructures of filling bodies at a 28-day curing age under different phosphorus conditions, alongside optical microscopy and backscattered electron images illustrating the morphological textures of phosphogypsum grains. Specific instances include graphical representations of XRD test results and diffraction peaks of phosphogypsum, FTIR spectra of hydroxyapatite synthesized with a four-hour reaction time under different milling conditions, and compressive strength trends for mortars prepared with PPC and NS, as well as the relationship between compressive strength and phosphogypsum replacement percentage for those mortar mixes.

In environmental and geochemical research, Fig. 4 visualizes diverse datasets related to soil chemistry, radiometric analysis, and adsorption performance. It maps macro-ion equilibrium concentrations in soil solutions following phosphogypsum application, catalogs international phosphogypsum chemical compositions in tabular format, and visualizes U-238/Ra-226 and U-238/Pb-210 activity ratios in phosphate rock and phosphogypsum. The label demonstrates adsorption dynamics, illustrating the effects of pH, contact time, doping amounts, initial concentrations, and zeta potentials on the removal of Co2+, Cd2+, Pb(II), Cu(II), and Zn(II) using various adsorbents like BC, HAP-BC, and HPA. It also presents adsorption isotherms, pore width distributions for synthesized powders, and the relationship between TBP-TOPO concentration in kerosene and both phosphogypsum leaching efficiency and contaminant removal rates. Additional applications include a diagram showing phosphogypsum distribution in a Chondrite normalized format, graphical data illustrating the direct relationship between nano-hydroxyapatite dosage and fluoride removal percentage, and illustrations of detailed phosphorus flows into, through, and from wastewater treatment processes.

Beyond these primary domains, Fig. 4 is employed to compare impact categories for soil amendment scenarios and to illustrate California Bearing Ratio test results and Proctor compaction test data for raw phosphogypsum. Specifically, it plots CBR and dry density results as a function of moisture content for raw phosphogypsum, and presents nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms, pore size distributions, particle size distributions, and mean particle diameter histograms. Collectively, these iterations underscore the label's extensive, context-dependent application across analytical, industrial, and environmental scientific domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-00