<node id="Dissolution">
  <data key="d0">Dissolution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dissolution is a chemical and physical process in which solid materials break down and disperse into a liquid medium, such as water or other solvents. Fundamentally, it involves the transformation of solid precipitates, matrices, or compounds into individual ions within an aqueous phase. The progression and efficiency of this process are often accelerated by specific physical conditions, facilitating the transition from a solid state to a dissolved state.

In practical and industrial applications, dissolution is employed to process and extract compounds from various specific materials. It is notably used in the handling of phosphogypsum (PG), where the solid is mixed with caustic liquid waste or chloride solutions to extract compounds and effectively separate liquid and solid phases. During these operations, the gypsum matrix and its surface particles undergo breakdown through leaching. Additionally, the process encompasses the ionization of calcium carbonate within aqueous solutions. Across these diverse contexts, dissolution serves as a fundamental mechanism for material breakdown, phase separation, and chemical extraction.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical process indicated in the top right region of the phase diagram.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-041</data>
