<node id="Phosphorus">
  <data key="d0">Phosphorus</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorus is a fundamental chemical element and a strategic, nonrenewable resource that serves as a vital nutrient for plants and animals, playing a central role in global agricultural productivity, food security, and environmental health. Structurally, it functions as a central atom within tetrahedral anion configurations bonded to four oxygen atoms and forms an integral part of various crystal lattices. In agricultural systems, phosphorus is a primary component of fertilizers, though its soil bioavailability is often low and is increasingly enhanced through microbial solubilization and nano-hydroxyapatite formulations. Its chemical behavior in soils dictates root availability, making its management, import dependency, and recovery rates critical to sustainability. This is tracked via substance flow analysis across sectors, with significant annual imports, exports, and environmental losses averaging 15 kilotonnes per annum from food waste, while manure loads are calculated using mass balance equations.

Industrially, phosphorus is heavily associated with phosphogypsum, a calcium sulfate byproduct of fertilizer production that originates from incomplete phosphate ore decomposition. It naturally occurs as a trace impurity or measurable component within phosphogypsum, with mass fractions ranging from 0.030% to 2.980% and exhibiting geographic variations, such as lower concentrations in Brazilian deposits compared to Moroccan ones. In industrial samples, it can constitute up to 13% by weight. When adsorbed onto cementitious materials, phosphorus acts as a toxic and acidic impurity that hinders hydration, retards setting times, and reduces early strength, though its concentration decreases significantly post-hydration (e.g., by 43% in leached samples or notably in PBSLM6) and correlates with silicon inclusions. Analytically, phosphorus is quantified using automated wet chemistry systems and detected via energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy at approximately 2.0 keV, with X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy identifying P-O bonding and P2O5 associations. It appears in samples at varying concentrations, including trace, undetectable (0.00%), or consistent with elemental maps, and registers as prominent spectral peaks around 214 to 215.5 nm.

Environmentally, uncontrolled phosphorus releases disrupt the global phosphorus cycle and trigger eutrophication in waterways, necessitating rigorous monitoring of leaching behavior and transfer dynamics. To mitigate ecological risks, phosphorus is targeted for removal during pretreatment and can be effectively immobilized through solidification and stabilization techniques, including coprecipitation with aluminum and iron, incorporation into cemented paste backfill via calcium-phosphate species, adsorption onto N-Al2O3, and removal by carbide slag during phosphogypsum modification. It is notably easier to immobilize than fluorine in CPGFM applications, can be efficiently removed via fluidized calcination, and is leached using citric and oxalic acids. Beyond waste management, phosphorus is integral to resource recovery efforts, successfully extracted from incinerated sewage sludge ash and recovered from waste phosphogypsum leachate through precipitation processes. During thermal and chemical treatments like chemical looping gasification, it undergoes sequential phase transitions and ultimately remains in the solid residue, while also being recognized as a harmful element that must be removed during potassium persulfate decomposition. Collectively, these characteristics underscore the necessity for precise analytical monitoring and strategic management of phosphorus across industrial, environmental, and agricultural sectors to balance resource utility with ecological safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorus (P2O5) is an element extracted from Kovdor apatite ore during the production of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphorus is a chemical element whose resource efficiency is discussed in the context of industrial and mining practices.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element whose resource efficiency is a focus of industrial ecology research.</data>
