<node id="Fig. 5">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 5</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 5 is not a singular, unified illustration but rather a generic figure label employed across numerous independent scientific publications, with its specific content varying significantly depending on the source document. Primarily centered on phosphogypsum processing, materials science, environmental chemistry, and microbiology, the label denotes distinct figures tailored to diverse experimental contexts. In materials science and chemical processing studies, Fig. 5 frequently illustrates application mechanisms, syngas generation via chemical looping gasification, and the environmental and economic impacts of utilizing one ton of phosphogypsum. It documents chemical process flowcharts for synthesizing nano-hydroxyapatite using water as a solvent through high-temperature calcination, tracks calcium carbonate shape transformations across varying stirring times, and records mass-loss rate variations under multiple heating rates. The figure also displays mineral phases of phosphogypsum and embedded fillers, the quantitative effect of fluoride addition on filling body porosity, and thermogravimetric analysis results of phosphogypsum. Structural and microscopic characterizations include transmission and scanning electron microscopy images of calcium carbonate transformations, hydroxyapatite layers fabricated at 180 °C, and fracture surfaces of composite and bone cements across compositions from x = 0 to 2.5 wt%. Additional materials data encompass adsorption isotherm-derived properties like BET surface area, cumulative pore volume, and average pore width, alongside dissolution rates and strength coefficients of phosphogypsum cementitious materials under dry-wet cycles. Compressibility curves from oedometric tests and stress-strain fractal behavior analyses are also featured, as well as comparative water softening resistance in phosphogypsum-stabilized adobe and diagrams showing the TBP-TOPO molar ratio’s effect on phosphogypsum leaching.

Within environmental chemistry and ecological assessments, Fig. 5 analyzes chemical speciation, surface properties, and microbial dynamics. It displays zeta potentials of biochar and hydroxyapatite-modified biochar across varying solution pH levels, and depicts the mole fraction of water-soluble cadmium in water extracts following phosphogypsum application, alongside model-calculated ion distributions. For rare earth element investigations, the figure provides elemental mapping and spot characterizations of acid-leached phosphogypsum, highlighting mineral inclusion outlines and their spatial overlap with cerium distribution. It also presents the elemental deportments of rare earth elements, cadmium, uranium, and chromium in phosphogypsum samples, as well as the distribution results of phosphogypsum recycling technologies, including probability density curves and rank variations. In wastewater treatment contexts, Fig. 5 serves as a composite figure illustrating adsorption kinetics, isotherms, dynamics, regeneration performance, practical applications, and material characterization. Ecological and microbiological assessments are supported through single-factor analyses of sample differences and visualizations of microflora networks.

The label further encompasses specific analytical plots and mechanistic diagrams. Fig. 5 appears as a graphical plot visualizing Equation 17 for measuring crystallite dimension and anisotropic density, and as a UDEDM plot for synthesized hydroxyapatite nanoparticles and their variants. It illustrates the formation mechanism of porous hydroxyapatite core-shell microspheres via a one-step hydrothermal method, alongside diagrams displaying the system boundary of the analysis. Graphical data also demonstrates the strong dependency of fluoride adsorption capacity on solution pH, and a diagram displays the radioactivity index of various phosphogypsum types versus strontium. Due to its generic labeling convention, some publications merely reference the figure without detailed captions, collectively confirming that Fig. 5 encompasses a wide array of distinct scientific illustrations rigorously adapted to their specific experimental contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 5 is a figure illustrating size distributions of ex situ unmilled hydroxyapatite with different hydrothermal reaction times.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 5 is a graphical representation showing compressive strength trends for mortars prepared with NS and L.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 5 demonstrates the effect of aggregate type on the compressive strength of mortar mixes prepared using OPC.</data>
