<node id="Fig. 6">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 6</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 6 is a generic figure label employed across numerous distinct scientific publications, with its specific content and purpose varying significantly depending on the research domain. Rather than representing a single fixed entity, it serves as a standard numbering convention that illustrates diverse data, processes, and analyses across multiple fields.

In environmental and materials chemistry research focusing on phosphogypsum, Fig. 6 frequently illustrates toxic impurity solidification and stabilization, specifically depicting cadmium concentration and chemical speciation in soil water extracts following application. It also features elemental mapping images comparing cerium with calcium in leached phosphogypsum to document elemental release and mobility from the gypsum lattice. Within environmental and economic analyses, it presents data on the environmental and economic impact of China’s phosphogypsum disposal in 2021, projecting changes under optimized management scenarios. Additionally, it provides a visual illustration of the step-by-step preparation process for modified phosphogypsum, coupled thermal analysis data including thermogravimetric analysis, differential thermal analysis, and temperature curves for phosphogypsum alone, and X-ray diffraction patterns of flaking material from phosphogypsum cement mortar specimens after deterioration experiments. In chemical engineering contexts, it functions as a flowchart detailing nano-hydroxyapatite production from phosphogypsum via sulfuric acid decomposition and calcination, as well as a diagram illustrating the effect of reaction temperature on phosphogypsum leaching by TBP-TOPO in kerosene.

Across materials science and biomedical engineering studies, Fig. 6 characterizes synthesized compounds and evaluates structural properties. It displays X-ray diffraction patterns and field emission scanning electron microscopy images for bone cement and hydroxyapatite-bone cement composites, alongside high-magnification micrographs revealing fracture surfaces, internal microstructure, void distribution, and porosity. Other materials studies utilize it to present X-ray diffraction patterns of synthesized powders such as Surf, Surf-900C, NoSurf, and NoSurf-900C, illustrate the formation mechanism of hydroxyapatite microcrystals influenced by calcium carbonate shape, outline the hydration progress of hemihydrated gypsum noting HG and DG, and demonstrate the effect of calcium fluoride on the crystal shape of filling bodies. For grain size analysis, it shows grain size evolution under fragmentation for E1, E2, and E3 mixes and presents plots used to measure crystallite size. In nanoparticle synthesis and analytical modification research, it documents scanning electron microscopy images of brick samples processed through various hydration and drying methods, illustrates the SSP technique analysis for hydroxyapatite nanoparticles, and presents attenuated total reflectance spectra, thermogravimetric analysis curves, and thermogravimetric-differential scanning calorimetry patterns for peeled, AL-functionalized, bovine serum albumin-loaded, and lysozyme-loaded hydroxyapatite samples. It also graphically represents X-ray diffraction patterns of materials synthesized with varying Brij-93 surfactant concentrations to evaluate structural effects.

Beyond materials and chemical engineering, Fig. 6 appears in studies focusing on optical and electronic properties, presenting graphical representations of optical absorption curves, band gap determination, and band gap alignment of prepared samples relative to water redox potentials. In rare earth element research, it serves as a visual representation showing concentration changes during leaching processes. In emergy analysis, it illustrates the emergy flow components of Scenario 1. Finally, within microbial ecology, it displays functional prediction results and abundance differences within bacterial communities to provide insights into microbial functional potential and population dynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 6 presents graphical data depicting the kinetic adsorption behavior and equilibrium attainment of fluoride ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 6 is a figure plotting the ratio of volume fractions of region II relative to region III against milling duration.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 6 depicts the effect of purified PG replacement on the compressive strength of mortars with NS after moist curing.</data>
