<node id="Uranium">
  <data key="d0">Uranium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranium is a naturally occurring, polyvalent, heavy, and radioactive chemical element that serves as a significant trace component within phosphate-related industrial and environmental systems. It is predominantly found in phosphate rocks, phosphate ore, phosphogypsum, ferruginous siltstone, and fertilizers, where it frequently co-occurs with rare earth elements, lead, and thorium. As a naturally occurring radionuclide, uranium is extensively tracked across global mining resources and specific regional deposits, such as those in Iraq and China, where it is increasingly evaluated as an unconventional resource. Its presence contributes substantially to the natural radioactivity of phosphorite deposits, with concentrations in phosphogypsum typically ranging from 8.3 to 27.4 ppm.

During wet process phosphoric acid production, uranium exhibits high partitioning efficiency, with approximately 70 to 86 percent transferring from the raw ore into the final phosphoric acid stream. Consequently, it is recognized as a valuable trace element and a potential supply source for supporting nuclear power fleets. Recovery efforts frequently employ solvent extraction methods to isolate uranium from carbonate leach solutions and wet process phosphoric acid. Scientific research extensively characterizes its distribution across phosphogypsum grain fractions, utilizing alpha-particle and gamma spectrometry alongside systematic separation and purification techniques. Analytical studies also leverage uranium as a baseline for calculating the 226Ra/238U activity ratio and model its leaching, dissolution, complex solubility, and stripping characteristics to optimize transport and recovery throughout phosphate production cycles.

Chemically, uranium primarily exists in the U(IV) and U(VI) oxidation states and demonstrates high mobility within phosphogypsum matrices, preferentially leaching over time and adsorbing onto gypsum surfaces as the uranyl ion. This leaching process releases significant quantities of uranium alongside rare earth elements and cadmium, with concentrations reaching up to 17.29 µg/L. Environmentally, uranium migration is well-documented across phosphate materials, groundwater, mine wastewater, and soil-to-produce pathways. The disposal of uranium-enriched phosphogypsum presents notable radiation hazards, elevates uranium levels in marine ecosystems, and necessitates rigorous ecological monitoring of air and soil near storage facilities, alongside occupational safety protocols that track cumulative internal radiation doses for nearby personnel. Conversely, uranium’s interactions with materials like hydroxyapatite are actively investigated for groundwater remediation and nuclear waste disposal, demonstrating superior removal efficiency compared to granulated activated carbon, though removal rates may decline by approximately 53 percent under specific conditions. Ultimately, uranium is recognized as both a valuable industrial resource and a persistent environmental contaminant requiring comprehensive geochemical, radiometric, and safety management.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive element found in phosphogypsum along with its decay products.&lt;SEP&gt;Uranium is a naturally occurring radioactive element that largely ends up in phosphoric acid rather than Phosphogypsum during processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Uranium is a trace element found in phosphogypsum that exhibits varying solubility depending on leaching conditions and salinity.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element studied in stack solutions and leachates from phosphogypsum disposed in coastal areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Uranium is a chemical element whose concentration in phosphogypsum was measured across the sampled locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-00