<node id="Phosphogypsum (PG)">
  <data key="d0">Phosphogypsum (PG)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphogypsum (PG) is a solid industrial byproduct and chemical waste residue generated in massive quantities during the production of phosphate fertilizers, phosphoric acid, and phosphate rock processing. It typically appears as a finely granulated powder, silt, or silty-sand material. Chemically, PG is primarily composed of calcium sulfate dihydrate and silica or quartz, alongside high concentrations of calcium ions, phosphorus, major and trace elements, heavy metals, and valuable rare earth elements. Due to its composition, it is frequently classified as TE-NORM, with radioactive elements and other impurities often concentrated in its finest particles. Consequently, its radioactivity levels frequently exceed regulatory limits, leading to disposal bans and necessitating dedicated waste management strategies. In industrial accounting, PG generation typically marks the end of the accounting boundary, requiring extensive physico-chemical characterization, elemental analysis, and studies on solubility, stability, and treatment methods to ensure safe handling.

The massive production volume, complex composition, and inherent hazards of PG pose significant environmental and health risks, particularly when improperly disposed of or stored in large coastal stockpiles. Practices such as marine dumping or long-term stacking can cause ecological contamination, pH alterations, and broader waste management challenges. These large stockpiles accumulate radioactive waste that poses substantial environmental contamination risks, highlighting the urgent need for sustainable disposal and utilization strategies to mitigate these ecological problems.

Despite these challenges, PG is increasingly valorized as a multifunctional secondary raw material with substantial industrial and economic potential, particularly in regions like Morocco and China. It serves as a primary calcium source and starting material for synthesizing hydroxyapatite, including nanosized hydroxyapatite, through processes such as acid dissolution, pH shift, and hydrometallurgical or hydrothermal synthesis. PG also enables chemical reactivity with atmospheric carbon dioxide to form stable calcite and is employed in thermal decomposition applications. Industrially, it is utilized as a raw material for cement retarders, building gypsum, roadbed construction, cemented backfill aggregates, and as a cement substitute that prolongs the setting time of concrete mixtures. Additionally, PG is used in varying percentages to manufacture bricks and concrete, significantly influencing their mechanical and physical properties, and is employed in construction composites and soil stabilization applications. It also serves as a matrix for recovering rare earth elements and radionuclides (often leached using TBP-TOPO solvent mixtures), functions as an oxygen carrier in gasification processes, and is processed into modified phosphogypsum and self-leveling mortar. Through these recycling and valorization efforts, PG successfully transitions from a problematic waste stream into a valuable resource.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is a byproduct used as a partial replacement for cement in mortar mixes, whose mechanical properties are improved by heat treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-007</data>
