<node id="Magnesium">
  <data key="d0">Magnesium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnesium is a double-charged chemical ion and metallic element that plays significant roles across solution chemistry, industrial materials, geochemical matrices, and agriculture. In aqueous environments, its molar fraction is determined by effective ionic strength, and its concentration is systematically monitored, often exhibiting trends that closely mirror calcium, including a notable decline at the twentieth experimental stage. As a fundamental chemical species, it is frequently analyzed in various material and environmental samples.

In geochemical and industrial contexts, magnesium is a major chemical species and measurable impurity within phosphogypsum, typically constituting mass fractions between 0.040% and 1.500%. It functions as an interfering metal ion in phosphogypsum leach liquor at concentrations around 150 mg/L and significantly influences material properties by altering fluorine precipitation mechanisms. Geochemical analyses also reveal regional distribution variations, with Brazilian phosphogypsum consistently reporting higher magnesium concentrations than Moroccan samples. Beyond phosphogypsum, magnesium serves as the primary base metal of the AZ31B alloy, highlighting its importance in metallurgy.

In biomaterials science and crystallization processes, magnesium substitutes for calcium in hydroxyapatite structures, which reduces crystal size and increases material solubility while simultaneously influencing the transformation of amorphous calcium phosphate during crystallization. Although this substitution can reduce overall structural stability, magnesium is deliberately utilized as a dopant in nano-hydroxyapatite particles; when combined with zinc, it enhances nanoparticle nutrient uptake efficiency, demonstrating its dual utility in modifying structural properties and improving functional performance. Additionally, magnesium is employed as a fertilizer component specifically studied for its effectiveness in improving acid paddy soil conditions. Analytical studies also note its presence in specific samples, such as at pt2, where it is indicated by low-intensity jagged bumps below 200 counts in spectroscopic or diffraction data.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium is a metallic oxide impurity identified in the PG sample.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium changes the fluorine precipitation mechanism and influences the formation of specific fluorosilicates.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnesium is a trace metal ion found in natural hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-000</data>
