<node id="Aluminum">
  <data key="d0">Aluminum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Aluminum is a chemical and metallic element that exhibits diverse chemical behavior and significant relevance across environmental, agricultural, industrial, and materials science domains. In analytical and compositional contexts, it is frequently measured and recorded in data tables, appearing as a major chemical species, a metallic impurity, a trace element, or a minor component depending on the specific sample matrix. Detection methods identify it through weight percentages, such as 11.41% in one analyzed sample and mass fractions ranging from 0.090% to 1.920% in phosphogypsum, as well as through spectral peaks at approximately 226 and 227/237 nm and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy results.

Within industrial and phosphogypsum processing systems, aluminum functions as both a major constituent and an impurity, with concentrations varying by source and tracked as a key parameter in process water. It actively influences fluorine precipitation mechanisms and lowers the decomposition temperature during gasification. The element can be successfully recovered from phosphogypsum and red mud through recycling methods including sintering, leaching, and co-sintering. Additionally, it is detected in MPG samples under specific temperature and time conditions.

In agricultural and soil chemistry applications, aluminum acts as a binding agent for phosphate, precipitating the nutrient into insoluble forms that reduce its bioavailability to plants. The release rates of aluminum are utilized to estimate the efficiency of gypsum applications in soils, with certain materials demonstrating an approximate 92% increase in its removal rate. In construction and advanced material processing, aluminum appears as an ion in cemented paste backfill mixtures and is conventionally represented by a grey circle in technical diagrams. Furthermore, aluminum compounds are added alongside fluorine to enhance the mechanical properties of alpha-hemihydrate gypsum, and the element serves as the primary alloying component in the AZ31B alloy.&lt;SEP&gt;Aluminum is a metallic oxide impurity identified in the PG sample.&lt;SEP&gt;Aluminum tends to form complexes with fluorides and modifies ionic species distribution in the system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-011</data>
