<node id="Chromium">
  <data key="d0">Chromium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Chromium (Cr) is a transition metal and heavy metal element that functions as a significant trace element and impurity across geological, industrial, and materials science contexts. Naturally enriched in phosphorites relative to crustal averages, it frequently acts as a metal dopant within the apatite lattice, shifting hydroxyapatite’s absorption spectrum from the ultraviolet to the visible region. In industrial and environmental settings, chromium is prominently detected in phosphogypsum (PG), modified phosphogypsum (MPG), gypsum, commercial and synthetic potassium sulfate, AZ31B alloys, and PBSLM6. Its concentration varies widely across these matrices, ranging from trace levels in parts per million or micrograms per gram to specific measurements of 31 mg/kg in PG and 6.38% by weight in other contexts, often exhibiting a uniform, low-intensity speckled distribution. The percentage content of chromium in gypsum is specifically determined by Equation 35.

During material processing and environmental interaction, chromium demonstrates high mobility and undergoes significant liquid-solid fractionation. It is typically fixed through adsorption or weak bonding to gypsum surfaces, though comprehensive remediation remains inefficient, with simple water washing achieving only about a 28% removal rate and processing transfer rates between 3% and 26%. Chromium concentrations generally decrease during hydration, thermal treatments (such as at 185°C and 190°C), and the synthesis of potassium sulfate, with commercial potassium sulfate exhibiting higher levels than the synthetic version. However, adsorption dynamics can cause slight variations depending on temperature and duration, such as lower concentrations at 180°C/10 h compared to 195°C/10 h due to crystal property impacts. Analytical quantification is routinely performed using Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass-Spectrometry (ICP-MS), with concentrations tracked in elemental deportment studies and analytical datasets.

As a toxic heavy metal contaminant, chromium poses substantial ecological and public health risks. It readily leaches into surrounding environments, contaminating stockyards and amended soils where levels frequently exceed the GB 15618-2018 risk control standard, resulting in moderate soil pollution. It is actively assessed in leaching toxicity analyses like the Toxicity Characteristic Leaching Procedure (TCLP), with concentrations recorded in milligrams per liter. Regulatory frameworks strictly monitor chromium in surface and irrigation waters, enforcing limits such as 0.05 mg/L, while the US-EPA sets a mixture limit of 5 mg/L. Despite these controls, chromium often appears in mixtures below detection or rare effect thresholds. In industrial Life Cycle Inventory data, particularly for brick production and phosphogypsum disposal, it is tracked as a trace metal emission and chemical discharge into groundwater, sometimes quantified at levels like 8.62 × 10⁻⁴ kilograms. Although occasionally referenced as a compound in emission datasets, chromium is fundamentally an elemental heavy metal that manifests in various chemical forms, necessitating continuous environmental tracking and regulatory oversight to mitigate non-carcinogenic risks from oral ingestion and broader ecological impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromium is a trace metal element measured in the provided data table with concentrations in ppm.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromium is a heavy metal found in phosphogypsum, primarily in fine particle fractions.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromium is a chemical element whose concentration in phosphogypsum was measured across the sampled locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-021</data>
