<node id="Agglomeration">
  <data key="d0">Agglomeration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Agglomeration is a physical phenomenon characterized by the clustering or clumping of small particles and nanoparticles. In phosphogypsum, this process is driven by moisture content, which causes fine particles to aggregate. Across various material systems, agglomeration significantly impacts scientific analysis and measurement techniques. Specifically, particle clustering can interfere with dynamic light scattering measurements, leading to observed size discrepancies and complicating the accurate determination of particle size distributions.

In hydroxyapatite (HAp) systems, agglomeration manifests as a pronounced tendency for nanoparticles and nanorods to cluster together. The severity of this clumping behavior varies significantly depending on formulation factors, particularly surfactant concentrations. For example, slight particle clustering has been specifically observed in HAp-CTAB-3 samples. Conversely, the addition of specific surfactants, such as Brij-93, effectively prevents the clumping of HAp nanorods. Overall, agglomeration represents a critical physical behavior that influences both the structural properties of materials and the reliability of their analytical characterization.&lt;SEP&gt;Agglomeration refers to the slight clustering of particles observed in the HAp-CTAB-3 sample.&lt;SEP&gt;Agglomeration refers to the clumping of HAp particles, which varies in severity across different surfactant concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;Agglomeration is the clumping of hydroxyapatite particles, which milling aims to break apart.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792427797</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dust">
  <data key="d0">Dust</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Dust is a particulate air pollutant emitted during industrial production that requires effective treatment to minimize its environmental impact. It is strongly associated with respiratory disorders and has been identified as the most significant contributor to emergy loss across all evaluated scenarios.

In specific industrial applications, such as phosphogypsum processing, dust generation is naturally mitigated by moisture-induced particle agglomeration. Despite this reduction, particulate matter remains a visible concern, frequently observed blowing away from the tops of phosphogypsum stacks. To ensure comprehensive emission control, the pollutant is systematically captured using gas-collecting hoods and bag dusters before it can disperse into the surrounding environment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-056&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-024</data>
