<node id="Morocco">
  <data key="d0">Morocco</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Morocco is a sovereign North African country and geographic region that serves as a global powerhouse in the phosphate industry, controlling approximately 70% to three-quarters of the world’s phosphate rock reserves. As a leading industrial hub, it operates an extensive chemical sector dedicated to phosphoric acid manufacturing, anchored by major facilities like the Jorf Lasfar industrial complex. The nation’s vast sedimentary and igneous phosphate rock reserves, particularly the Bou Craa deposit which contains rare earth elements between 0.14% and 0.16%, underpin its economic and industrial significance. This massive extraction and processing activity results in the annual production of roughly 15 million tonnes of acid phosphogypsum residues.

Historically, Morocco has managed this substantial waste output by storing or discharging the majority of phosphogypsum into the Atlantic Ocean, a practice that has generated significant environmental concerns. The disposal of this underutilized material has led to heavy metal pollution, soil cadmium accumulation, ecological degradation, and scale deposits associated with regional desalination activities. Currently, the phosphogypsum is concentrated in stacks and ponds. In response, the country is actively advancing circular economy strategies and sustainable waste management practices. These initiatives prioritize processing phosphogypsum to recover valuable compounds like calcium and sodium, while also investigating its application in construction projects such as rural road development and embankments. The use of this material in infrastructure is regulated by specific local building codes, geotechnical standards, and settlement limits, with ongoing development of radium removal technologies to effectively mitigate its inherent radioactivity.

Beyond its industrial role, Morocco functions as a critical geographic reference and sampling site for extensive geochemical, radiological, and industrial research. The region, which includes locations such as Fez and hosts numerous suppliers, provides phosphogypsum, phosphate rocks, and natural water samples that are frequently analyzed for their chemical compositions, elemental profiles, thermal behaviors, and natural radioactivity levels. These samples are characterized by elevated radionuclide concentrations, relatively low sulfur trioxide and calcium oxide content, and significant impurities, including a high unlisted component value of 32.16, with specific analytical datasets representing the country at approximately 43% by weight. Phosphogypsum K-10 activity concentrations have been measured at 560 Bq kg−1, and phosphate rocks, phosphogypsum, and fertilizers have been systematically assessed for radiological hazard impacts. Research in the region, heavily supported by prominent academic institutions including Mohammed VI Polytechnic University, the Geology &amp; Sustainable Mining Institute, and the University Hassan II of Casablanca, investigates methods to improve filterability and reduce radioactivity during phosphoric acid production, as well as the behavior of heavy metals during phosphogypsum carbonation. These scientific inquiries are prominently featured in studies by Abouloifa et al. (2023) and Bouargane et al. (2023), as well as regional academic forums like the 2018 National PhD Students Days conference.&lt;SEP&gt;A region included in a dataset detailing phosphate rock characteristics and activity concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;Morocco is a geographical source of Phosphogypsum with documented major element composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Morocco is a region where researchers investigated radium solubility from phosphogypsum in aqueous solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Morocco is the origin location for phosphogypsum samples analyzed by Borrego et al.&lt;SEP&gt;A country where natural radioactivity in phosphates and phosphogypsum was studied.&lt;SEP&gt;Morocco is a geographical location where phosphogypsum samples were analyzed, showing high Silicon Dioxide levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-00