<node id="Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluoride is a negatively charged chemical species derived from fluorine that functions as both an element and a compound across environmental, industrial, and agricultural contexts. It occurs naturally as a trace impurity in phosphogypsum, constituting an average mass fraction of approximately 1.27%, where it exists in both soluble and insoluble forms. Industrially, fluoride is released as gaseous hydrogen fluoride and silicon tetrafluoride during phosphate processing, acting as an air pollutant that contributes significantly to emergy loss alongside dust and sulfur dioxide. Environmentally, fluoride exhibits high diffusive mobility in soil, readily adsorbing to topsoil and accumulating in plants before entering the food chain. It frequently leaches into groundwater, with concentrations often surpassing the 4.0 mg/L regulatory limit and the 1.5 mg/L World Health Organization guideline, occasionally reaching up to 32 mg/L. In aqueous systems, it influences lead precipitate formation and complexes with uranous ions under low pH and reduced conditions, with its environmental fate commonly modeled using GEOCHEM software and tracked in Life Cycle Inventory data, which records specific chemical discharges such as 0.164 kilograms into groundwater.

As a toxic trace element and primary characteristic pollutant in phosphogypsum, fluoride poses substantial ecological and health risks. Elevated exposure causes fluorosis in livestock and respiratory issues in humans through oral ingestion or dust inhalation, while also threatening aquatic ecosystems. Consequently, strict immobilization and leaching control are required, particularly in phosphogypsum-based backfill and soil amendment applications. Remediation strategies primarily focus on adsorption, with nanosized hydroxyapatite (nHAp) derived from waste phosphogypsum proving highly effective, achieving over 90% adsorption efficiency that depends on particle size and increases with temperature. Experimental analyses have extensively tracked fluoride uptake, desorption, and equilibrium across various conditions, confirming its role as the primary adsorbate ion in concentration measurements and adsorption experiments. Alternative removal methods include chemical precipitation to form stable compounds like fluorite, aluminum fluoride complexes, and calcium fluorophosphate, alongside the application of various geological materials. Collectively, these scientific and engineering efforts aim to mitigate fluoride's environmental mobility and toxicity, ensuring agricultural and ecological safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride precipitates in various crystalline forms depending on the concentrations of other dissolved ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride is a naturally prevalent ionic substitution typically lacking in synthetic hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;An impurity present in phosphogypsum that can cause atmospheric and groundwater pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride is a water-soluble impurity in phosphogypsum that can be reduced through washing processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-010</data>
