<node id="XRF">
  <data key="d0">XRF</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">X-ray Fluorescence (XRF) is an analytical characterization and measurement technique primarily utilized for elemental analysis and chemical composition determination. It is extensively applied to study phosphogypsum (PG) waste, often employed in conjunction with complementary methods such as X-ray Diffraction (XRD) and Infrared Spectroscopy (IR) to ascertain phase composition and the elemental makeup of carbonation products.

Within phosphogypsum research, XRF serves multiple specific analytical functions. It identifies calcium and sulfur as the predominant elements, quantifies radionuclide proportions, and measures calcium concentrations to calculate theoretical carbonate amounts. Additionally, the technique is used to verify the elemental composition of synthesized calcium carbonate and to confirm the presence of impurities in potassium sulphate. Through these diverse applications, XRF provides comprehensive insights into the chemical and structural properties of phosphogypsum and its derived materials.&lt;SEP&gt;An elemental composition analysis method using X-ray fluorescence spectroscopy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-004</data>
