<node id="Sulfur Trioxide">
  <data key="d0">Sulfur Trioxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfur trioxide (SO3) is a chemical oxide compound that serves as a vital intermediate in industrial chemistry, most notably in the conversion of sulfur dioxide to sulfuric acid. In industrial processing, its concentration within slag is routinely analyzed to calculate decomposition and desulfurization rates. Beyond its role as a process intermediate, sulfur trioxide is a major chemical constituent across a variety of industrial and construction materials, including raw phosphogypsum, potassium sulfate, cement, fly ash, and clinker. It is also routinely identified in materials such as PBSLM6 through X-ray fluorescence (XRF) analysis.

Within phosphogypsum, sulfur trioxide is consistently the highest chemical component, typically comprising between 37.60% and 58% of the material, with many sources noting it exceeds 44%. Its concentration varies significantly depending on the geographic origin of the samples, with analytical measurements recording values such as 41.1 wt%, 48.12 ± 9.04 wt%, 51.48 wt%, and 53.5 wt% within the gypsum phase. Following calcination pre-treatment, the measured concentration generally increases. Due to its dominant presence and variability, sulfur trioxide is the primary compound routinely quantified in analytical procedures to accurately determine the chemical makeup of phosphogypsum.

In other material systems, sulfur trioxide levels are similarly tracked and measured. For instance, it is present at approximately 2% content in clinker produced using phosphogypsum and reagent gypsum. Its concentration is also actively measured in cement and potassium sulfate samples, underscoring its widespread relevance across multiple industrial sectors and material science applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur Trioxide is a major oxide in phosphogypsum that is not significantly lost during heat treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical component that notably increases after calcination, reaching values between 53% and 54.67%.&lt;SEP&gt;A major component whose relative percentage increases as water and volatiles are lost during calcination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-010</data>
