<node id="Silicon Dioxide">
  <data key="d0">Silicon Dioxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Silicon Dioxide (SiO₂), also known as silica, is a chemically stable, acid-insoluble oxide compound that naturally occurs in vitreous glass, quartz, and gypsum, typically at higher concentrations in natural gypsum than in phosphogypsum. It is a fundamental chemical component across various materials, including cement, fly ash (FA), and phosphogypsum (PG). Within wet-process phosphoric acid production, silicon dioxide enters phosphogypsum as a component of calcium phosphate rock, functioning as a secondary chemical compound in PPG precursor materials and a minor or major component depending on the sample context, such as in PBSLM6. Its quantitative presence varies regionally, notably appearing at higher levels in Hubei and Yunnan, with specific concentrations reported at 2.27 wt% overall and 0.79 wt% in upper sections, though it decreases following calcination pre-treatment. Analytically, it is routinely monitored via X-ray fluorescence (XRF) and X-ray diffraction (XRD), where it is identified by a characteristic diffraction peak labeled Q at approximately 20 degrees. Its mass ratio is utilized in calculating oxide ratios and pH values, and it is explicitly mapped within phase diagrams relevant to the phosphogypsum system.

Chemically, silicon dioxide influences the thermal decomposition and phase behavior of phosphogypsum, reacting with generated calcium oxide, iron oxide, and calcium sulfide to form calcium silicate. Depending on the application, it can serve beneficial roles, such as enhancing sintering resistance when used as an oxygen carrier, participating in syngas production, forming eutectic mixtures that reduce melting points while remaining unfused, or being added to raw mixtures for slag wool production. It is also integrated into specialized experimental systems, including solid-state reactions for synthesizing lanthanum silicate, combinations with bovine serum albumin for thermal property analysis, and as a material susceptible to hydrofluoric acid-based etching. Conversely, it is often treated as a major acid-insoluble impurity requiring systematic elimination during standard beneficiation and synthesis operations, where it is removed via filtration, flotation, initial separation stages, or high-concentration agitation. Throughout these diverse industrial, analytical, and experimental contexts, silicon dioxide remains a critical compound referenced in material calculations, phase mappings, and synthesis protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon Dioxide is a primary chemical component of Phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon Dioxide exhibits significant variation in phosphogypsum samples, ranging from 0.29% to 18% depending on the location.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon Dioxide is a major oxide in phosphogypsum that does not volatilize significantly during heat treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical component that generally increases with calcination temperature, peaking around 4.14%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-007</data>
