<node id="Sodium Oxide">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Oxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium Oxide (Na2O) is a chemical compound that functions as a measurable constituent across several industrial and natural materials, most notably phosphogypsum, natural gypsum, cement, and fly ash. Within phosphogypsum, it is consistently documented during compositional analysis, with reported concentrations varying according to sample origin, stratification, and processing history. Bulk measurements typically range from 0.14 wt% to 0.17 ± 0.01, while other analyses record concentrations between 2358.36 and 2372.60 ppm. In contrast, the top section of phosphogypsum composition tables shows a significantly lower concentration of 0.01 wt%. When compared to phosphogypsum, sodium oxide is present at higher levels in natural gypsum, though it remains a minor component in both materials. The compound is also identified as part of the chemical composition of cement and fly ash.

The distribution and concentration of sodium oxide are highly sensitive to physical and industrial factors. In Moroccan phosphogypsum, the compound exhibits a distinct spatial pattern, becoming highly concentrated specifically within particle size fractions larger than 170 mm. Industrial processing methods also markedly influence its levels; concentrations decrease significantly in washed beneficiated concentrates, a reduction directly caused by the elimination of sodium sulfate during the washing phase. Beyond its role in material characterization, the mass and molar ratios of sodium oxide are utilized in analytical equations within related studies. Collectively, these characteristics establish sodium oxide as a process-sensitive, quantifiable component whose presence and distribution are systematically tracked to evaluate material composition, processing outcomes, and industrial byproduct management.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium oxide is a chemical specification metric for phosphogypsum, required to be less than 0.5 percent.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium Oxide is a chemical component that generally decreases or stays low, mostly below 0.11%, after calcination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-009</data>
