<node id="J. Environ. Radioact.">
  <data key="d0">J. Environ. Radioact.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d2">J. Environ. Radioact. is a scientific and academic journal dedicated to publishing research on environmental radiochemistry and radioactivity measurements. The publication serves as a key resource for studies examining the radiological properties, behavior, and environmental impacts of various industrial and agricultural materials.

The journal features extensive research on phosphogypsum, including studies on its radiochemistry, the dissolution of radium-226 (226Ra), and the distribution of radionuclides and trace elements within the material. Additional topics covered in the journal include uranium uptake in synthetic gypsum utilized for mine tailings, the radiological impacts associated with phosphate rock processing, and the radiological consequences of applying phosphogypsum in agricultural settings. Furthermore, the journal publishes findings on radon exhalation rates resulting from phosphogypsum recycling processes. Collectively, these publications underscore the journal's comprehensive coverage of environmental radioactivity and radiological safety across diverse material applications.&lt;SEP&gt;J. Environ. Radioact. is a scientific journal that published research on uranium chemistry in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;J. Environ. Radioact. is a scientific journal that published research on radionuclide partitioning in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792402587</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cadmium Ion">
  <data key="d0">Cadmium Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Cadmium Ion (Cd²⁺) is a heavy metal cation that functions as a significant environmental contaminant, particularly within soil and aqueous systems. In soil environments, it primarily exists as a free ion within soil solutions, where its molar fraction remains lower than the total cadmium content. Its equilibrium concentration and activity are routinely calculated and analyzed, with its overall activity notably reduced through complexation with other ions and hydroxo complexes. These interactions are often quantified using specific association coefficient equations. Furthermore, the environmental mobility of the Cadmium Ion and its subsequent transfer to plants are heavily dependent on soil pH levels and the presence of soil carbonates.

Beyond soil systems, the Cadmium Ion is frequently targeted for removal from wastewater and aqueous solutions due to its environmental impact. Extensive sorption studies have investigated its adsorption behavior and removal efficiency using various adsorbents, including hydroxyapatite (HAP), 20HPA-HAP, and HPA-modified hydroxyapatite. Research indicates that the Cadmium Ion exhibits stronger covalent bonding and a higher adsorption capacity on HPA-modified hydroxyapatite compared to cobalt ions, demonstrating high removal efficiency when processed with tested composite materials.

In geological and mineralogical contexts, the Cadmium Ion is commonly present in materials such as phosphogypsum, where it actively participates in substitutional solid solution formation. Because the ionic radius difference between the Cadmium Ion and calcium ions is less than 15 percent, it can readily replace calcium to form displacement solid solutions. These combined chemical, environmental, and structural characteristics define the behavior, mobility, and remediation strategies associated with the Cadmium Ion across natural and engineered systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792300665</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydroxo Complex">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxo Complex</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Hydroxo Complex (CdOH+) is a form of cadmium that binds prematurely in the soil solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical complex formed by cadmium ions that influences their activity and speciation in soil solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary form of cadmium in soil solution that drives biogeochemical passivation and reduces cadmium penetration into plants.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hydroxo Complex (CdOH+) is a form of cadmium that binds prematurely in the soil solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical complex formed by cadmium ions that influences their activity and speciation in soil solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary form of cadmium in soil solution that drives biogeochemical passivation and reduces cadmium penetration into plants.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical forms of lead (PbOH⁺, Pb(OH)⁰) that serve as primary agents for reducing lead activity and promoting its removal from soil.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792342794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonate Form">
  <data key="d0">Carbonate Form</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Carbonate Form (CdCO3^0) is a chemical species of cadmium present in lower concentrations than the hydroxo complex.&lt;SEP&gt;The Carbonate Form (CdCO3^0) is a chemical species of cadmium present in lower concentrations than the hydroxo complex.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonate form is the initial state of Cerium in phosphogypsum with an oxidation state of +4.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791427277</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bicarbonate Form">
  <data key="d0">Bicarbonate Form</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Bicarbonate Form (CdHCO3+) is a chemical species of cadmium present in lower concentrations than the hydroxo complex.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bicarbonate Form (CdHCO3+) is a chemical species of cadmium present in lower concentrations than the hydroxo complex.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790951494</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium Ion">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Calcium Ion (Ca²⁺) is a positively charged, exchangeable macro-ion that exists as a free species across environmental, geological, and experimental settings. Its concentration is routinely monitored to track chemical dynamics and quantify chemical discharge into groundwater, typically measured in kilograms. In soil science, it functions as a key exchangeable cation within Haplic Chernozem soils, residing in the soil solution where it forms associates and actively drives carbonate deposition. The ion is naturally released into aqueous environments during calcium carbonate dissolution and is industrially extracted from phosphogypsum, where it is subsequently liberated by calcium sulfate to drive pozzolanic and hydration reactions. In these applications, its concentration and molar fraction are frequently measured across varying phosphogypsum doses, with baseline control values recorded at 0.75 and concentrations increasing proportionally with higher doses.

Chemically and mineralogically, the Calcium Ion is a fundamental structural component of several crystalline lattices, most notably gypsum and hydroxyapatite. In hydroxyapatite, it represents the characteristic oxidation state of calcium, identifiable via an XPS peak at 352.8 eV and translational vibrational modes detectable in the 200–350 cm⁻¹ range of Raman spectra. The ion ensures geochemical stability and reactivity while remaining substitutable by uranyl ions. It readily reacts with soluble phosphorus or phosphate ions to precipitate as insoluble calcium phosphate. Within simulated body fluid systems, it binds to negative surface charges on hydroxyapatite to nucleate calcium-rich amorphous calcium phosphate, a critical mechanism for biomimetic mineralization. It further supports hydroxyapatite crystallization and growth following calcium citrate decomposition and can be adsorbed onto CTAB monomers during crystal precipitation. Additionally, it facilitates fluoride removal in aqueous solutions and preferentially forms clusters on micellar surfaces.

In advanced material and cementitious systems, the Calcium Ion is highly abundant within the cementitious matrix and calcium silicate hydrate phases. It is rapidly generated when cement dissolves in water, promoting chemical equilibrium shifts and complex formation with retarders. The ion also reacts with arsenic compounds during hydration processes. Due to its exchangeable nature, it frequently participates in ion exchange processes within apatite structures, cementitious networks, and calcium sulfate compounds, where it can be readily substituted by other metal ions, including toxic heavy metals with similar ionic radii, forming substitutional solid solutions. This versatile reactivity, combined with its structural, catalytic, and geochemical roles across natural cycles and engineered material applications, underscores the Calcium Ion's significance in both scientific research and industrial practice.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792344045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Magnesium Ion">
  <data key="d0">Magnesium Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Magnesium Ion (Mg²⁺) is a divalent cation and macro-ion that serves multiple functional roles across soil science, chemical processing, and materials research. In environmental and agricultural contexts, it acts as an exchangeable cation within Haplic Chernozem soils and exists as a soluble macro-ion in the soil solution, where it readily forms associates. Its behavior is frequently monitored in experimental settings, with researchers measuring its concentration and molar fraction across varying phosphogypsum application doses, which directly influences its levels in the solution.

Beyond soil chemistry, the Magnesium Ion is treated as a standard chemical parameter and species tracked across diverse experimental conditions, often quantified and explicitly cataloged in scientific data tables under the Mg²⁺ designation. In laboratory and industrial purification workflows, it is identified as an impurity that must be extracted from the filtrate during the initial purification stage. Furthermore, in materials science, the ion is utilized as a key chemical species for the modification of hydroxyapatite. Collectively, these descriptions characterize the Magnesium Ion as a versatile, measurable cation that functions as a soil nutrient, a process impurity, and a functional modifier in advanced material synthesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792344220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sulfate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Sulfate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Sulfate Ion (SO₄²⁻) is a polyatomic, negatively charged inorganic sulfur compound that functions as an anionic macro-ion across environmental, chemical, and industrial systems. Chemically, it acts as a reactant in various equations and as a spectator ion during stabilization processes, while also undergoing anionic substitution for phosphate ions. Spectrally, the ion is characterized by strong absorption bands and distinct vibrational peaks around 1100 cm⁻¹ and 600 cm⁻¹, which are routinely utilized for its identification via infrared and FTIR spectroscopy, including its detection in MPG. In aqueous and soil environments, the Sulfate Ion readily forms associates and complexes with heavy metals, specifically binding with cadmium and lead ions. Its equilibrium concentration is factored into lead association coefficients, and it is known to increase in concentration with higher application doses. The ion is highly soluble and is typically removed as an impurity from final nano-hydroxyapatite products through washing.

Industrially and environmentally, the Sulfate Ion is a prominent chemical component and characteristic pollutant within phosphogypsum. Released from calcium sulfate, it participates in pozzolanic and hydration reactions, drives conversion processes, and is subsequently removed during the phase transition of calcium sulfate in hydrothermal treatments. When present in leachate from phosphogypsum stockpiles, it serves as a notable environmental contaminant. In soil applications, it exists as a macroion within the soil solution, where its concentration and molar fraction are routinely quantified across varying phosphogypsum doses. Furthermore, the Sulfate Ion exhibits strong hygroscopic behavior in atmospheric contexts, where it competes for moisture with hydrogen fluoride. This competitive interaction significantly prolongs the atmospheric residence time of phosphogypsum and associated atmospheric mixtures. Additionally, it reacts directly with potassium ions to form potassium sulfate, and its concentration has been specifically recorded at 0.58 for control doses in scientific studies.&lt;SEP&gt;The sulfate ion is a chemical species that coexists with phosphate ions in the solid solution gypsum phase.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate ion is an ion substituted by hydrogen phosphate ions in the gypsum phase, with absorption bands difficult to determine in the brushite lattice.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate ion is a chemical group whose bending vibrations produce characteristic absorption peaks in the 500–700 cm⁻¹ range.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfate Ion ($SO_4^{2-}$) is the chemical formula representing the sulfate group.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792403444</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Carbonate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Carbonate Ion (CO3^2-) is a negatively charged chemical species and macroion that plays a multifaceted role in geochemical, aqueous, and environmental systems. In soil solutions, it frequently forms associates and directly governs the deposition and dissolution dynamics of calcium carbonate. Its concentration and molar fraction are routinely monitored, often alongside bicarbonate levels, to track solution chemistry across varying experimental conditions, such as different phosphogypsum doses. The ion exhibits significant reactivity with various metals in aqueous environments. It actively binds with lead (Pb^2+) to form complexes that influence solubility calculations and serve as equilibrium parameters in association coefficient equations. Additionally, it competes with other ligands for cadmium binding sites and readily precipitates with rare earth elements.

In mineral crystallography, the Carbonate Ion functions as a critical substituent within hydroxyapatite matrices. By adsorbing into the structure and replacing hydroxyl and phosphate ions, it forms distinct carbonated apatite variants that substantially modify the host mineral's physical and chemical properties. This incorporation is analytically verified through Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy, where it appears as a functional group in carbonated hydroxyapatite. Its presence and adsorption within the mineral matrix are specifically confirmed by characteristic C 1s spectral peaks, providing a reliable method for structural analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792344518</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bicarbonate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Bicarbonate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Bicarbonate Ion (HCO3-) is a negatively charged macroion and macroelement that plays a multifaceted role in environmental geochemistry and biomineralization. Produced primarily through the hydrolysis of calcium carbonate, this anion is a fundamental chemical species routinely tracked as a key parameter in experimental data and measured solution parameters. In soil solutions, it functions as a macroion whose concentration and molar fraction are systematically quantified across varying conditions, such as different phosphogypsum doses. Within these terrestrial environments, the Bicarbonate Ion actively forms associates and participates directly in calcium carbonate deposition processes.

Beyond its geochemical functions, the ion engages in significant metal complexation dynamics. It forms complexes with cadmium ions, which directly influences adsorption calculations, and binds with lead ions (Pb^2+) to contribute to lead association and complexation modeling. In materials science and biomineralization contexts, the Bicarbonate Ion serves as a critical chemical factor where its concentration directly affects carbonate substitution during the precipitation of hydroxyapatite. Consequently, it is extensively investigated for its specific effects on this mineralogical transformation, highlighting its versatility as a reactive anion in both natural ecosystems and controlled experimental systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792344518</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chloride Ion">
  <data key="d0">Chloride Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Chloride Ion (Cl⁻) is a negatively charged macro-ion that exhibits significant chemical and environmental versatility. In soil science, it functions as a macro-ion within the soil solution, where it readily forms associates. In aqueous and mineral contexts, the chloride ion acts as a competing anion for cadmium binding and possesses the ability to substitute for hydroxyl ions within apatite structures. These properties underscore its active role in geochemical interactions and mineral formation processes.

Beyond its environmental behavior, the chloride ion plays specific roles in surface chemistry and laboratory analysis. When sodium chloride accumulates on surfaces, the release of chloride ions helps attract calcium ions, effectively creating nucleation sites. In synthetic procedures, chloride ions may persist as impurities prior to washing when calcium chloride is utilized as a reactant. Analytically, the chloride ion is a quantifiable chemical species whose concentration is routinely measured across experimental conditions. Its standard quantification method involves silver nitrate titration, which applies a conversion factor of 35.45 g/mol to determine its presence and abundance.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloride Ion is a highly soluble impurity ion removed from the final nHAp product by washing.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloride Ion is a macroion present in the soil solution whose concentration and molar fraction are measured across different phosphogypsum doses.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloride ion (Cl^-) is a chemical species involved in binding with Pb^2+ in the soil solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A macroelement ion whose equilibrium concentration is included in the lead association coefficient formula.&lt;SEP&gt;An anion present in the solution, with concentrations varying across different treatment doses.&lt;SEP&gt;Chloride Ion is a chemical parameter measured in the scientific study, corresponding to the Cl⁻ column in the data table.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792344045</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydroxide Ion">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxide Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Hydroxide Ion (OH-) is a negatively charged anion and macro-ion that serves as a fundamental indicator of basic or alkaline chemical environments. It is frequently represented as OH- in chemical equations and is notably released during the dissolution of calcium carbonate, a process that directly signals alkalinity in aqueous systems.

In chemical reactions, the Hydroxide Ion acts as a key reactant that facilitates hydrolysis processes. Specifically, it breaks pyrophosphate bonds and reacts with pyrophosphate during hydrolysis, while also facilitating the hydrolysis of triphosphate. Its presence drives these bond-breaking and transformation reactions in alkaline conditions.

Within soil solutions, the Hydroxide Ion functions as a macro-ion that forms associates and significantly influences geochemical behavior. It participates in reactions with toxic metal ions to form insoluble precipitates, thereby affecting their mobility. Furthermore, it plays a critical role in pH-dependent leaching behavior and forms stable complexes with cadmium ions, directly impacting their stability constants.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxide ions (OH−) are generated alongside calcium ions during cement dissolution, contributing to the alkaline environment affecting retarder stability.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxide Ion is a macroion present in the soil solution whose concentration and molar fraction are measured across different phosphogypsum doses.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxide ion (OH^-) is a chemical species involved in binding with Pb^2+ in the soil solution.&lt;SEP&gt;A macroelement ion whose equilibrium concentration and squared concentration are used in lead association calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;An anion involved in lead complexation, appearing in the solubility model terms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792344518</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CCE System">
  <data key="d0">CCE System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The CCE System refers to the current state of equilibrium that governs ion binding, adsorption, and salt transfer in the soil.&lt;SEP&gt;The CCE System refers to the current state of equilibrium that governs ion binding, adsorption, and salt transfer in the soil.&lt;SEP&gt;A carbonate-calcium equilibrium framework that governs ion migration, accumulation, and salt transfer in soil.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792342794</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soil Profile">
  <data key="d0">Soil Profile</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The Soil Profile is the vertical section of soil through which carbonate forms and salts migrate and accumulate.&lt;SEP&gt;The Soil Profile is the vertical section of soil through which carbonate forms and salts migrate and accumulate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790951494</data>
