<node id="Biogeochemical Barrier">
  <data key="d0">Biogeochemical Barrier</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Biogeochemical Barrier is a natural soil mechanism situated at the soil-plant interface, specifically functioning at the soil-root boundary. Its primary role is to act as a protective filter that limits, restricts, and reduces the penetration of heavy metals, pollutants, and other contaminants from the surrounding soil into plant roots. By establishing a stabilized zone at this critical junction, the barrier effectively controls the mobility of harmful substances, preventing their excessive uptake by vegetation.

In addition to restricting contaminant movement, the Biogeochemical Barrier actively regulates chemical consumption and pollutant intake by plants. This regulatory function is closely tied to its stabilization process, which is notably maintained at a neutral pH of 7. Through this pH-dependent stabilization, the barrier ensures that the soil-root interface remains effective in mitigating environmental pollution and safeguarding plant health from toxic soilborne elements.&lt;SEP&gt;A root-soil interface that becomes weak under high moisture, allowing hazardous substance penetration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-008</data>
