<node id="Water Extract">
  <data key="d0">Water Extract</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The liquid component from soil samples used to analyze macro-ion content and cadmium forms.&lt;SEP&gt;A sample medium used to analyze macro-ion content and chemical parameters in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;The liquid component from soil samples used to analyze macro-ion content and cadmium forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Water Extract is the sample medium analyzed in Table 1 to determine macro-ion content and pH levels.&lt;SEP&gt;A liquid sample obtained from soil used to measure macroion analytical content and lead forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Water Extract is the aqueous sample from soil used to measure Pb^2+ concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792345598</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium">
  <data key="d0">Calcium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium is a chemical element and alkaline earth metal that typically exists as a double-charged cation (Ca2+). In analytical and diagrammatic representations, it is depicted as a blue circle and functions as an elemental component where ten atoms are balanced on both sides of a reaction equation. It serves as a fundamental constituent of various synthesized materials, notably forming the crystal lattice of hydroxyapatite (HAp) and apatite, where it functions as a critical structural ion and primary elemental component detected via XPS analysis. In these compounds, calcium forms various bonds and phases, participates in electrostatic interactions with proteins, and acts as a key binding target for AL molecules. Although often deficient in biological apatites—a condition that impacts material stability and solubility—calcium is routinely utilized as a chemical precursor in specific molar ratios with phosphate to synthesize bulk and nano-hydroxyapatite nanoparticles. These nanoparticles release calcium through dissolution-precipitation mechanisms and are routinely identified via EDX, XPS, and XRD analyses. Calcium also shares significant chemical and biological similarities with radium, influencing radium's environmental behavior, while its ionic radius closely matches that of cerium, facilitating ionic substitution in crystal structures.

Analytically, calcium is consistently identified across multiple spectroscopic and diffraction techniques. It is detected via EDX/EDS through prominent spectral peaks near 0.5 keV and 3.7 keV, alongside XRD, XPS, and optical peaks at approximately 210, 220, 221, and 227 nm. Quantitative analyses reveal varying concentrations depending on the matrix, with calcium detected at weight percentages such as 12.19% and 21.56% in specific samples, and consistently identified as a predominant element via XRF. Its presence is confirmed through particle surface distribution, EGTA volumetric methods, and mathematical formulas based on effective ionic strength. In materials science, calcium is a primary constituent of portlandite and various calcium phosphate compounds, often appearing alongside sulfur and phosphorus in analytical spectra.

Within phosphogypsum (PG), calcium forms the core gypsum matrix alongside sulfur and phosphorus, denoting a major elemental component widely distributed throughout the matrix. It comprises approximately 23% by weight in specific samples, with XRF concentrations ranging from 2.6% to 28.98% (averaging 38.14 ± 1.70), accounting for over 90% when considering the calcium sulfate matrix and 38 wt% of the fluorite phase. Calcium is a key element in PG-derived compounds, including calcium selenate, calcium fluorosilicate, and calcium fluoride. Within the PG matrix, calcium ions can undergo isomorph substitution, being replaced by rare earth elements, uranium (including U(IV)), thorium, titanium, and strontium, with titanium substitution notably creating oxygen vacancies. The relative proportion of calcium in PG increases when treated with oxalic acid, sodium carbonate, and sodium bicarbonate, and its presence is considered beneficial for generating hydration elements. During acid leaching or acid rain exposure, calcium dissolves from the PG matrix, showing stable initial concentrations followed by a delayed decline that reflects dissolution and recrystallization, with concentrations decreasing by 22% in leached samples compared to raw samples. It can be recovered through vertical-flow electrophoresis or converted into high-value compounds like calcium carbonate and calcium oxide, though it also acts as an interfering metal ion in PG leach liquor, measured at concentrations of 15.45 g/L. Additionally, calcium serves as a source for carbon dioxide mineral sequestration and acts as the precursor for carbonate formation, constituting 41.26 wt% of synthesized calcium carbonate. In other industrial contexts, calcium is present in fly ash, with varying amounts distinguishing Class F and Class C fly ash, while its overall content can remain low in certain matrices due to silicon aggregation and the thermal decomposition of calcium sulfate.

From an agricultural and environmental perspective, calcium functions as a vital plant nutrient and is utilized as a fertilizer component to improve acid paddy soil. When phosphogypsum is applied as a soil amendment, calcium acts as a chemically activated element that adsorbs to the soil complex, reinforcing aggregate structures and functioning as a cation exchange agent. It displaces sodium in sodic soils and hydrogen or aluminum in acidic soils, thereby promoting soil flocculation. Calcium derived from phosphogypsum specifically improves SAC ion content and reinforces soil structure. However, calcium also interacts directly with soil phosphate, precipitating it into insoluble forms that reduce phosphate bioavailability, though abundant calcium forms complexes with phosphate that effectively mitigate competition with sulfate for adsorption sites. In environmental monitoring and life cycle assessment contexts, calcium is measured as a dissolved chemical ion in groundwater analysis and is recorded as a chemical compound in groundwater emissions, as well as a liquid emission in LCI data for PG disposal. Collectively, these chemical, structural, and ecological properties define calcium's measurable behavior and environmental impact across laboratory and field applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is a primary component of phosphogypsum whose leachability is enhanced by increased salinity.</data>
