<node id="Carbonate">
  <data key="d0">Carbonate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbonate (CO3 2-) is a chemical anion and functional group recognized by distinct absorption peaks in the 1400–1500 cm⁻¹ range of the FTIR spectrum. In aqueous and liquid-phase systems, it typically forms as the final ion prior to reacting with calcium. It is also carried by phosphate rock, where it reacts with sulfuric acid to release carbon dioxide. Within chemical speciation models, carbonate interacts with cadmium and governs the specific type of lead precipitate generated during immobilization processes, notably forming insoluble precipitates with lead in phosphogypsum.

Structurally, carbonate serves as the primary substituent in biological hydroxyapatite, generally constituting 5 to 8 wt% of the material. It substitutes for phosphate ions within both apatite and hydroxyapatite crystal lattices, either incorporating directly into the structure or adsorbing onto the surface. This incorporation is known to diminish crystalline stability and increase solubility. Additionally, carbonate within hydroxyapatite is believed to influence protein adsorption behaviors.

Experimentally, carbonate concentrations are frequently monitored, typically beginning at low initial levels and exhibiting a consistent, steady decline over time. Analytical observations further indicate that the molar fraction percentage of carbonate decreases when exposed to elevated phosphogypsum doses relative to standard analytical baselines. Beyond its role in synthetic and biological mineral matrices, carbonate also occurs naturally as a compound that functions as a cementing and cohesive agent, contributing to mechanical improvements in geological and material contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical ion ($CO_3^{2-}$) involved in forming lead complexes and indicating equilibrium with atmospheric CO2 or soil carbonates.</data>
