<node id="CCE">
  <data key="d0">CCE</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">CCE is a chemical equilibrium system and soil parameter primarily utilized to calculate calcium carbonate solubility and its relationship with liquid phases. It functions by determining the proportion of molar fractions between free and associated ions within the soil solution, thereby playing a critical role in establishing and influencing thermodynamic equilibrium. The behavior and state of CCE are dynamically governed by several interacting factors, including ion association, pH levels, redox potential (Eh), and carbonate dynamics.

Beyond its fundamental geochemical functions, CCE serves as a key chemical and physical factor in soil environments. Alongside ion association, it significantly contributes to the passivation of heavy metals within the soil solution. This passivation process reduces the bioavailability and mobility of toxic elements, highlighting CCE's importance in soil chemistry and environmental contexts. By integrating these roles, CCE operates as a central mechanism in understanding ion distribution, equilibrium states, and metal immobilization in aqueous soil systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-011</data>
