<node id="Sodium">
  <data key="d0">Sodium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium is a metallic alkali chemical element and ion that exhibits distinct behaviors across scientific, environmental, and industrial contexts. In analytical settings, it is consistently identified as a present constituent in various samples, with its weight percentages routinely recorded through techniques such as SEM-EDS and EDS. While analytical reports may classify sodium as a major chemical species, a trace element, or a metallic impurity depending on the specific sample matrix and detection thresholds, its measured mass fraction in phosphogypsum consistently ranges from 0.300% to 1.210%, aligning with standard profiles. In experimental settings, sodium ion concentration levels are frequently monitored across multiple stages, where data indicates that its levels remain highly stable, consistently tracking near the 100 mark throughout the duration of the studies.

Within phosphogypsum, sodium functions as a significant component that actively participates in the formation of secondary minerals such as ralstonite and cryolite. Chemically, it acts as a metallic alkali element that forms complexes with phosphates and sulfates. From a mineralogical standpoint, sodium plays a crucial structural role by substituting for calcium within apatite crystals to maintain electrical neutrality in the mineral lattice. It is also found in carbonate-rich hydroxyapatite, where its presence modifies the compound's chemical formula and physical properties.

In agricultural and soil science applications, sodium behaves as a highly hydrated cation that accumulates on the exchange complex of sodic soils. High concentrations of this cation are known to induce clay dispersion, which degrades soil structure and fertility. However, the application of phosphogypsum to these affected soils introduces calcium that effectively displaces the excess sodium, mitigating its adverse effects and demonstrating a practical synergy between the element and soil remediation techniques. Beyond environmental management, sodium is also recognized as a valuable metal that can be recovered from industrial solid waste alongside aluminum through synergistic treatment processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium is a component in various precipitates like cryolite and ralstonite within the phosphogypsum system.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium is an ion that is not completely eliminated during the washing of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium is a naturally prevalent ionic substitution typically lacking in synthetic hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792425629</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chloride">
  <data key="d0">Chloride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Chloride ($Cl^-$) is a fundamental chemical anion and ion that serves as a major component in aqueous solutions, typically quantified in milligrams per liter (mg/L). In experimental settings, its concentration levels are systematically tracked across various stages in charts and tables, often maintaining the most stable concentration near the 100 mark throughout the duration of the experiment. As a key chemical specification metric, it is routinely measured to monitor solution composition and experimental progression.

Within chemical speciation models and solution chemistry, chloride plays a critical interactive role. It actively engages with cadmium, influencing metal speciation dynamics. Furthermore, chloride readily forms lead complexes in modeled solutions and directly influences the type of lead precipitate that forms during immobilization processes, highlighting its significant impact on metal behavior and reaction pathways in aqueous environments.

Beyond laboratory modeling, chloride is recognized as a notable impurity in phosphogypsum (PG), where it must be effectively separated through preliminary screening processes. Its concentration is strictly regulated due to environmental and quality concerns. Regulatory frameworks establish a maximum permissible limit of 250 mg/L, while the World Health Organization (WHO) recommends a stricter guideline value of 25 mg/L. Additionally, in the context of phosphogypsum production, chloride content is mandated to remain below 0.01 percent to meet industrial specification standards.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792406334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
