<node id="South Africa">
  <data key="d0">South Africa</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">South Africa is a country and geographic region that serves as a primary global source of phosphogypsum and hosts the Phalaborwa phosphate rock deposit, recognized as the largest igneous phosphate deposit in Africa. The nation contains a substantial domestic phosphogypsum industry alongside phosphoric acid manufacturing plants, particularly in the Lowveld region, where phosphogypsum samples are frequently extracted and extensively analyzed in scientific literature. Geochemical studies have consistently identified South African phosphogypsum as containing significantly elevated concentrations of trace elements, including yttrium, zirconium, copper, barium, and nickel, with notably high zirconium levels. Additionally, the material is characterized by its content of specific radioactive isotopes, has been systematically recorded in comparative data tables, and has been thoroughly evaluated for its cement set retardation properties.

Leveraging these unique geochemical and industrial characteristics, South Africa has developed advanced methods for the co-production of sulfuric acid and cement using phosphogypsum. Despite these industrial applications and research advancements, the widespread disposal of phosphogypsum remains a significant environmental and industrial challenge within the country, largely driven by the material's historically underutilized value.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792480197</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carbonates">
  <data key="d0">Carbonates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbonates are a versatile class of chemical compounds examined across environmental, nuclear, and materials science disciplines. In soil environments, they significantly affect the mobility and plant uptake of cadmium. Regarding rare earth element chemistry, carbonates act as dispersed, soluble salts, although their dissolution can be restricted by gypsum coatings. In phosphogypsum processing, carbonates bind a portion of the contained uranium and can be refined into high-purity forms for various industrial applications.

In materials research, carbonates interact notably with hydroxyapatite. They are efficiently absorbed by the material, achieving optimal uptake at a pH of 9. Conversely, when carbonates remain as synthesis residues, they decompose during thermal treatment, contributing to mass losses in hydroxyapatite. Furthermore, researchers frequently analyze carbonates for their characteristic calcite crystal structures and distinct spectral properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineral classes highlighted by some authors as potential main rare earth element-bearing minerals in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonates are chemical species (PbCO^0, Pb(CO3)2^2-) that bind with lead, though present in lower amounts than hydroxo complexes.&lt;SEP&gt;Compounds whose dissolution, migration, and precipitation influence the CCE system in the soil profile.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbonates are compounds that can lead to the formation of rapidcreekite during industrial processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405227</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
