<node id="Sodium Ion">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium Ion (Na⁺) is a chemical species and exchangeable cation that functions as a measurable parameter across diverse scientific and environmental studies. In soil science, it acts as a macroion within the soil solution and is specifically identified as an exchangeable cation in Haplic Chernozem soils. Researchers routinely monitor its concentration and molar fraction under varying experimental conditions, such as different phosphogypsum application rates, where the ion typically exhibits only slight concentration fluctuations.

Beyond its environmental presence, the Sodium Ion plays distinct roles in materials chemistry and surface processes. It can be released from sodium chloride (NaCl) accumulations on surfaces, where it attracts phosphate ions to facilitate the formation of nucleation sites. Conversely, during the production of nHAp materials, it frequently appears as a highly soluble impurity that must be removed from the final product through washing procedures. Across all these contexts, the Sodium Ion is consistently tracked as a standard chemical parameter, with experimental data typically recorded in scientific tables under the Na⁺ designation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792344518</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
