<node id="Sulfates">
  <data key="d0">Sulfates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulfates are chemical compounds and mineral classes that serve important functions in environmental analysis and industrial resource processing, particularly concerning phosphogypsum. In soil analysis, sulfates are routinely examined in soil samples, where their presence is typically detected via barium sulfate (BaSO4) sedimentation and their concentrations are measured using gravimetric techniques.

In the context of phosphogypsum, sulfates present a dual nature that reflects both their natural occurrence and industrial utility. They are commonly described as chemical impurities and insoluble compounds located in the residual phase of phosphogypsum, where they also form complexes with metallic elements such as potassium and sodium. While initially characterized as impurities, sulfates can alternatively be processed into high-purity chemical compounds synthesized from phosphogypsum for diverse industrial applications. Additionally, specific mineral classes of sulfates have been identified by researchers as potential primary carriers of rare earth elements within phosphogypsum, highlighting their significance in both waste management and critical mineral recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792039862</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Na+">
  <data key="d0">Na+</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Na+ represents sodium ions whose content is measured using flame photometric detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Na+ represents sodium ions whose content is measured using flame photometric detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Na+ is a monovalent cation that can substitute for divalent cations in apatite structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247568</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cd">
  <data key="d0">Cd</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cadmium (Cd) is a chemical element classified as a heavy metal and heavy metal ion, widely recognized for its hazardous properties and significant environmental and health impacts. As a persistent pollutant, Cd is known to cause cancers, impair the immune system, and pose substantial environmental safety risks, particularly regarding cancer risk under oral intake. It frequently coexists with other heavy metals in environmental matrices and is commonly detected in soil, terrestrial materials, and phosphogypsum (PG) waste. In PG, Cd levels often exceed regulatory standards, which can significantly hinder the safe utilization of the waste material; however, its concentration is effectively reduced to safe levels in modified phosphogypsum (MPG). The element is highly mobile in aquatic environments, as it can migrate with leachate and contaminate water sources, though it has also been recorded at 0.0 mg/L in PBSLM.

Due to its toxicity and persistence, Cd is routinely monitored as a trace element across various scientific, geochemical, and analytical datasets, typically measured in parts per million. Its total and water-soluble concentrations in soil are quantified using analytical techniques such as X-ray fluorescence (XRF) and atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS). Comprehensive concentration data is systematically recorded in tabular formats to support environmental assessments, geochemical studies, and spike recovery tests. Furthermore, reference tables provide critical toxicological parameters for Cd, including Reference Dose (RFD) and Slope Factor (SF) values, which are essential for evaluating its health risks and guiding regulatory compliance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792295269</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X-ray Fluorescence">
  <data key="d0">X-ray Fluorescence</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">X-ray Fluorescence is a versatile analytical and spectroscopic technique primarily employed to determine the elemental and chemical composition of various materials. It is capable of identifying major, trace, and micro-elemental contents, as well as assessing semi-quantitative chemical profiles and detecting impurities. The method provides detailed insights into both bulk and trace constituents, making it a critical tool for material characterization.

The technique is widely applied across diverse sample types, including solid samples, raw materials, and synthesized products. Specific applications include measuring total cadmium content in soil, analyzing trace and semi-quantitative elemental compositions in phosphogypsum or PG waste, and determining major elements and compounds within synthesized materials. By accommodating a broad range of matrices, X-ray Fluorescence supports comprehensive quality assessment and compositional analysis across environmental, industrial, and research contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-002</data>
