<node id="Lead">
  <data key="d0">Lead</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Lead (Pb) is a heavy metal chemical element that predominantly exists in the +2 oxidation state. Naturally occurring as a trace element, it is notably enriched in phosphorites, where it substitutes for calcium within the apatite crystal structure. It is also present in trace amounts in phosphogypsum, a byproduct of the phosphate industry, with documented concentrations ranging from 4.70 to 204.29 μg/g, specific measurements such as 6.2 ± 1.7 and 55.98 mg kg⁻¹ SDW, substantially exceeding the Earth's crust average. Approximately 27% of lead transfers from phosphate rock during wet processing. In environmental and industrial systems, lead leaches from geological and industrial sources, migrates through various compartments, precipitates as lead sulfide (PbS), or adsorbs onto clays and organic materials. It tends to concentrate in coarser grain size fractions due to polonium decay and organic matter retention, and in certain contexts, it is also characterized as a radioactive element or radionuclide. As a toxic element in phosphogypsum, it accumulates preferentially in calcite during conversion, with regional variations in presence.

The environmental solubility and mobility of lead are primarily governed by lead carbonate and lead sulfate, with its soluble form content in water extracts influenced by free anions and macroion pair equilibrium. In phosphogypsum, lead is categorized as a trace metal with intermediate mobility, often present as insoluble precipitates with sulfates or carbonates, resulting in low release rates and limited leaching (e.g., reaching only 0.24 µg/L under specific conditions). Its equilibrium content and total versus water-soluble forms are frequently quantified in soil and soil solution studies. Regulatory frameworks commonly enforce a maximum concentration limit of 0.05 mg/L in water quality standards, while the US-EPA sets a limit of 5 mg/L for mixtures. Lead concentrations are actively monitored against such limits and frequently measured in milligrams per liter during Toxicity Characteristic Leaching Procedure (TCLP) tests. Its concentrations in phosphogypsum typically remain lower than the rare effect concentration, and it is routinely listed in tabular data for phosphogypsum analysis, sometimes found in mixtures below detection limits. Lead is also monitored in fertilizers according to the Chinese standard GB/T23349.

Due to its environmental persistence and inherent toxicity, lead poses considerable hazards to ecological systems and human health. As a divalent heavy metal ion, it is readily absorbed by plants from contaminated soils, initiating bioaccumulation pathways that introduce substantial food safety risks. Its phytoavailability and bioaccessibility have been extensively studied in vegetables exposed to pollution, and it is frequently investigated alongside cadmium and nickel regarding phytotoxicity. However, when present in low water-soluble and free-form contents in soil, lead poses minimal risk to plants and soil biota. Its presence varies regionally, with contamination extending to soils amended with phosphogypsum, notably in the Mississippi Delta and New Orleans regions.

To mitigate these risks, remediation and environmental management strategies frequently employ apatite-based materials, including natural and synthetic apatite as well as nanocrystalline hydroxyapatite. These phosphate-based sorbents rapidly and effectively remove lead ions from aqueous solutions and contaminated matrices through dissolution-precipitation mechanisms. Additionally, lead can be immobilized in contaminated soils using phosphogypsum slag-based cementitious materials, facilitating in situ stabilization, significantly reducing bioavailability, and minimizing environmental dispersion. Its mobility and toxicity in solution are actively modeled to inform these management practices.&lt;SEP&gt;A heavy metal classified as toxic, commonly found in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Lead is a trace metal element measured in the provided data table with concentrations in ppm.&lt;SEP&gt;Lead is a chemical element whose concentration in phosphogypsum was measured across the sampled locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-021</data>
