<node id="Portlandite">
  <data key="d0">Portlandite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Portlandite, an alternative mineral name for the solid, crystalline compound calcium hydroxide (Ca(OH)2), is a significant material phase encountered across material science, environmental remediation, and geochemical modeling contexts. It naturally forms as a primary hydration product of clinker phases and appears as a lamellar hydration product within autoclaved aerated concrete when exposed to highly alkaline environments. In industrial and experimental settings, Portlandite is frequently synthesized or generated through the alkaline dissolution, dispersion, or hydrometallurgical conversion of phosphogypsum, sometimes utilizing potassium hydroxide. It often serves as a high-purity intermediate or precursor in chemical conversion and carbonation reactions.

Chemically, Portlandite is highly alkaline and readily undergoes rapid carbonation, reacting with carbon dioxide to produce calcium carbonate (calcite). This dissolution and carbonation behavior makes it highly valuable for environmental applications, where it contributes to material stability, fluoride immobilization, and effective carbon dioxide capture even under harsh conditions. Furthermore, it is incorporated into geochemical models to accurately simulate the alkalinity of the CPGFM assemblage.

Analytically, Portlandite is readily identified and characterized using multiple techniques. X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis reveals specific diffraction peaks, including a prominent peak 3 around 47° in PG1 samples (often marked by a solid diamond symbol in graphs), with its presence being particularly notable in samples containing 12% calcium carbonate. The abundance of Portlandite is compositionally sensitive, as its diffraction peaks progressively decrease and eventually vanish as phosphogypsum content increases. Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) further characterizes the compound by revealing characteristic absorption bands associated with hydroxide and water interactions. Additionally, commercial samples and experimental materials related to Portlandite have been subjected to X-ray fluorescence (XRF) analysis for calcium oxide and potassium content, as well as trace element analysis, with the compound frequently categorized in trace element concentration comparison tables.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-018</data>
