<node id="Phosphate Rock">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Rock</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate Rock is a naturally occurring, finite geological mineral resource and ore, primarily composed of calcium phosphate and fluorapatite, that serves as the essential primary source of phosphorus for global supply chains. Geologically manifesting as sedimentary, igneous, or metamorphic deposits distributed worldwide, it contains valuable trace elements, including rare earth elements, lanthanides, and uranium. Historically sourced from locations such as Nauru and Morocco, current commercial production reached 258 million metric tons in 2018, with substantial reserves concentrated in China’s Yangtze River Basin and North Africa. Due to geopolitical concentration and an annual depletion rate of 352 kilotons, the resource is increasingly scarce and expensive. Mining operations face strategic regulation, such as China’s 14th Five-Year Plan restrictions, while Australia domestically mines the resource in Queensland and supplements supply through imports from Morocco and Western Sahara. Its cost fluctuations substantially influence emergy evaluation results, consistently accounting for nearly half to over half of total emergy input, and its trade, production, and reserves are systematically tracked within phosphorus flows models and phosphate budget analyses.

Industrially, Phosphate Rock functions as the foundational feedstock for phosphate manufacturing, phosphorus chemicals, and fertilizers. The material is typically concentrated via flotation and subjected to acidification with concentrated sulfuric acid in the wet acid process to generate phosphoric acid. During this reaction, carbon dioxide and hydrogen fluoride are released, yielding phosphoric acid and a major solid byproduct known as phosphogypsum. The ore’s inherent composition, attack time, and aluminum content directly dictate phosphate incorporation rates and gypsum formation processes, while also determining the trace element concentrations and morphology of the resulting phosphogypsum. The waste stream inherently contains aluminum, titanium, heavy metals, cadmium, and naturally occurring radioactive materials, including uranium, thorium, and radium-226 in radioactive equilibrium, with uranium typically partitioning into the acid stream.

To mitigate environmental impact and enhance resource efficiency, industrial operations frequently leach phosphogypsum to recover residual sulfuric acid and employ hydrometallurgical processes to extract rare earth elements and lanthanides. Radiologically, Phosphate Rock establishes a standard comparative baseline for radionuclide analysis and is closely studied alongside fertilizer facilities. Environmentally, it is utilized and compared to hydroxyapatite for its capacity to reduce metal bioavailability in soil, though its acid decomposition significantly contributes to carbon emissions. Ultimately, the resource is intrinsically linked to radiological profiles, waste management dynamics, and the migration mechanisms of associated harmful substances, underscoring its status as a complex geological asset critical to both industrial and environmental systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate Rock is a natural mineral resource processed to produce phosphoric acid, naturally containing radioactive elements.&lt;SEP&gt;The natural mineral source from which PG is derived, containing varying levels of radioactive isotopes.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate Rock is the natural geological source material from which Phosphogypsum is derived.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate rock is the raw material from which metals are transferred to phosphogypsum waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate rock is the raw material from which phosphogypsum is derived and originally contains radionuclides like polonium, radium, and uranium.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate rock is the raw starting material for industrial phosphorus production pathways.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-01