  <data key="d5">1791722536</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quartz">
  <data key="d0">Quartz</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Quartz is a chemically stable silicate mineral composed of silicon dioxide (SiO2) that serves as a standard reference mineral in geological contexts and is frequently concentrated in sediments such as those found in Gabes. In industrial and geological settings, it commonly functions as a typical gangue mineral in phosphate ores, comprising approximately 10% of phosphorite compositions, and is identified across various raw materials. Within the phosphogypsum matrix, Quartz exhibits variable abundance, ranging from a minor or trace mineralogical component co-occurring with calcium sulfate dihydrate to occasionally serving as a primary crystalline phase in specific samples. It is formally classified as a non-soluble silica component in phosphogypsum specifications and is closely associated with cadmium distribution within the matrix. Due to its high chemical stability and insolubility under standard processing conditions, Quartz co-occurs with aluminosilicates, remains retained in residual Beneficiated Solids, and is closely linked to silicon. Its persistent presence necessitates active removal during processing to mitigate erosion-corrosion damage to industrial reactors, although it does exhibit distinct solubility and solvation behaviors in dilute hydrothermal solutions at temperatures exceeding 150 °C.

Beyond its role as an industrial impurity, Quartz demonstrates versatile applications in construction and materials science, frequently utilized in formulations such as phosphogypsum-containing bricks, cement mortar, concrete mixes, and calcium carbonate composites. Analytically, Quartz is readily identified via X-ray diffraction across diverse materials, including phosphogypsum waste, synthetic hydroxyapatite particles, and various composites. In graphical X-ray diffraction plots, it is conventionally represented by an open circle symbol and is specifically characterized by peak 5, which generates a prominent signal around 26 degrees and typically dominates sample compositions. The prominence of this crystal phase peak directly correlates with the SiO2 to Al2O3 ratio, increasing as the ratio rises, and remains clearly visible in phosphogypsum-based mortars. While often detected with low intensity in X-ray diffraction patterns, Quartz also appears as a contaminant in hydroxyapatite samples and spectra, attributable to starting materials and identifiable through a specific absorption band.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartz is a mineralogical component found in phosphate ore.&lt;SEP&gt;A trace mineral compound consistently detected in unwashed PG samples across temperatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartz is a mineral compound present in the mortar system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-004</data>
