<node id="Aluminosilicates">
  <data key="d0">Aluminosilicates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Aluminosilicates are impurities in phosphogypsum that undergo transformation and breakdown in water.&lt;SEP&gt;A crystalline phase present in the soil material sample alongside silicates.&lt;SEP&gt;A main crystalline phase constituting 65 wt% of the soil material alongside silicates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791938112</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Groundwater">
  <data key="d0">Groundwater</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Groundwater is a critical subsurface aquifer system and natural environmental medium that functions as a vital ecological and water resource. As an underlying water source, it naturally contains dissolved ions and radioactive components, and it frequently serves as a receiving medium for various liquid chemical emissions. However, its geochemical dynamics are profoundly influenced by interactions with solid waste and engineered filling bodies, such as cemented paste backfill in mining operations, as well as phosphogypsum storage facilities, landfills, and tailings stacks. These hydrogeological contacts often induce acidic conditions, prolonged soaking, and contaminant migration, which can compromise the structural stability of engineered fills while simultaneously facilitating the leaching and redistribution of inorganic pollutants.

Due to its permeability and flow characteristics, groundwater is highly susceptible to contamination from hazardous spills, leakages, and the mobilization of toxic elements. Key pollutants frequently leaching into these subsurface systems include phosphorus, fluorides, heavy metals, and mobile anions originating from soil and industrial waste. Radiochemical and hydrochemical analyses of affected groundwater consistently reveal distinct contaminant signatures, notably an enrichment of Uranium-234 relative to Uranium-238, alongside detections of Polonium-210 and Radium-226. The aqueous concentration of Radium-226 is further modulated by environmental factors such as ionic strength and total dissolved solids. As these contaminants migrate, they often form extensive plumes that travel toward coastal areas, posing significant risks to aquatic ecosystems and human health, particularly through the contamination of drinking water supplies.

Given its vulnerability and critical role in environmental and public health, groundwater requires stringent management and careful engineering design when industrial waste interacts with subsurface water systems. To mitigate pollution risks, it is regularly monitored as a standard component of remedial actions, particularly for phosphogypsum tailings ponds and landfills. The complex interplay between hydrogeological transport, chemical leaching, and industrial byproducts underscores the necessity of robust leachate control and continuous environmental assessment to preserve groundwater as a safe and sustainable natural resource.</data>
