</node>
<node id="Oxygen">
  <data key="d0">Oxygen</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Oxygen is a fundamental chemical element that exists as a diatomic molecule in its standard state and can function as an ion in specific mixtures. It is a primary constituent of crystal lattice structures and oxide or sulfate matrices, actively driving oxidation reactions while maintaining strict atomic balance in chemical stoichiometry to ensure mass conservation, as demonstrated in instances where 44 atoms are balanced on both sides. Structurally, oxygen atoms surround the central phosphorus atom in the orthophosphate ion and serve as a primary constituent of quartz, where it undergoes substitution by fluorine during the rate-determining step of certain mineralogical reactions. It is also explicitly depicted in the molecular diagrams of PTEs and interacts with surface-disposed phosphogypsum under atmospheric conditions.

Analytically, oxygen is consistently identified as a dominant element with high signal intensity across various spectroscopic techniques. In energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS/EDX) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), it typically produces the tallest peak, often accompanied by a medium-height peak at approximately 0.5 to 0.52 keV, and can exhibit a dominant peak with nearly 2000 intensity at specific sample points such as pt2. These characteristics establish oxygen as a definitive analytical marker, with its weight percentages routinely recorded in SEM-EDS analyses.

Oxygen is highly abundant and uniformly distributed throughout numerous geological and industrial materials. It constitutes between 52.01% and 58.02% by weight of dried phosphogypsum alongside sulfur and calcium, is a major component of portlandite and potassium sulphate, and accounts for 65.29 wt% of synthesized calcium carbonate. In hydroxyapatite and BC nanoparticles, oxygen serves as a primary component, though its weight percentage decreases as surfactant concentration increases. Functionally within hydroxyapatite, it acts as an electron acceptor from material vacancies or trapping mechanisms, facilitating radical formation that oxidizes organic compounds. Industrially, oxygen extracted from or utilized within phosphogypsum is strategically deployed as an oxygen carrier in chemical looping gasification processes. Furthermore, during the thermal breakdown of calcium sulfate, oxygen is released as a gaseous product alongside sulfur dioxide, a behavior observed during both self-decomposition and high-temperature decomposition stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxygen (O) is a chemical element detected in the sample with a weight percentage of 46.25%.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxygen is a chemical element measured in the provided composition data.&lt;SEP&gt;A dominant element in the sample, showing high weight and atomic percentages.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxygen acts as a reactant in the reduction environment.</data>
