<node id="pH Level">
  <data key="d0">pH Level</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">pH level is a fundamental chemical parameter that quantifies the acidity or alkalinity of a substance. As a critical environmental and chemical property, it governs the chemical speciation, precipitation, and immobilization efficiency of key elements such as phosphorus and fluorine. The value of the pH level directly influences phase stability; a decrease in pH typically promotes the dissolution of phases and facilitates the release of ions into the aqueous phase.

In materials science and environmental engineering, pH level serves as a key indicator of chemical changes induced by specific treatments. It is recorded across different phosphogypsum treatment doses to assess efficacy and functions as a chemical property of bricks that is notably affected by the incorporation of PG. These applications highlight its role in monitoring how additive materials alter the chemical environment of constructed or treated systems.

In controlled laboratory settings, pH level is frequently manipulated or monitored as a precise experimental condition. It is commonly maintained at specific values to standardize reactions, such as being held at 2.5 during extraction experiments to control the aqueous phase conditions, or controlled at 7.4 using PBS buffer. When plotted in experimental data, it often serves as a Y-axis parameter measuring acidity or alkalinity, with observed ranges typically spanning from 6.5 to 8.5 depending on the specific system under investigation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792318634</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
