<node id="Scale Bar">
  <data key="d0">Scale Bar</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The Scale Bar is a visual reference tool and interface element designed to indicate physical dimensions, distance, and magnification across cartographic and scientific imaging applications. In cartography, it functions as a standardized map interface element, typically positioned in the bottom right corner, to represent specific geographic distances such as 100 kilometers, enabling users to accurately gauge spatial relationships and real-world measurements directly from the map layout. In scientific imaging, particularly within scanning electron microscopy and other microscopic panels, the Scale Bar serves as a technical reference marker for size calibration and estimating particle or nanostructure dimensions. These microscopy scale bars are consistently placed in the corners or bottom right of image panels, adapt to required magnification levels, and confirm the microscopic or nanoscopic scale of observed features.

The Scale Bar manifests in various visual formats and specific labeled measurements tailored to different analytical needs. Common implementations include a white horizontal line or bar indicating lengths such as 20 micrometers or 1 micrometer, a black horizontal reference marker denoting 0.5 micrometers or labeled "40 nm," and a red double-headed arrow accompanied by a ruler marked 5.00µm or labeled 5µm. Additional specific markers include references labeled 10 micrometers to indicate magnification and field of view, a 50 micrometer reference for particle analysis, and a 50 nm marker used to estimate the total dimensions of nanostructure clusters. Despite their distinct environments and varied visual presentations, all iterations of the Scale Bar provide essential, standardized visual metrics to ensure accurate spatial interpretation, size calibration, and measurement across both macroscopic and microscopic domains.&lt;SEP&gt;The scale bar on the map is marked "0 25 50 Km" and indicates distance.&lt;SEP&gt;A technical annotation on the micrograph indicating a real-world length of 20 micrometers.&lt;SEP&gt;A scale bar representing 50 µm is used to estimate particle sizes in the micrograph.&lt;SEP&gt;A visual reference present in each panel indicating physical dimensions for accurate size estimation.&lt;SEP&gt;Scale Bar is a technical parameter indicating a physical length of one micrometer on the SEM image.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792483381</data>
