<node id="Turkey">
  <data key="d0">Turkey</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d2">Turkey is a country in Asia that functions as a prominent global industrial hub and a critical center for scientific research, particularly regarding industrial byproducts and environmental management. As a major producer of phosphogypsum, the nation generates approximately 3.0 million metric tons annually, alongside substantial volumes of fly ash. These materials are often discarded in landfills or rivers, which has spurred extensive environmental and radiological investigations. Researchers, many of whom are based in and affiliated with institutions across Turkey, have systematically analyzed the chemical compositions and natural radionuclide content of this phosphogypsum. Notable efforts include a 2008 study by Degirmenci, alongside broader assessments of radiological characteristics, public radiation doses, and municipal solid waste management practices evaluated through multi-criteria decision-making frameworks.

Geographically, Turkey encompasses key locations such as Istanbul, Mersin, Ankara, Balikesir, and Manisa, which serve as focal points for material sampling, PG procurement, and industrial operations like the Bagfas Fertilizer Factory. The country’s phosphogypsum is frequently featured in scientific literature and data visualizations, often appearing in comparative tables and charts that detail material properties and chemical compositions. These datasets highlight Turkey’s phosphogypsum as a documented geographical source with major elemental profiles, sometimes associated with a weight percentage of approximately 43%, and frequently referenced alongside research citations. Beyond industrial and environmental studies, Turkey’s architectural practices reflect adaptation to local climatic and social conditions, particularly through ongoing engineering improvements to traditional adobe construction. Collectively, these attributes underscore Turkey’s dual significance as a leading producer of gypsum and phosphogypsum and a vital hub for material science research, environmental monitoring, and sustainable engineering development.</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792480807</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
