  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CaO">
  <data key="d0">CaO</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium oxide (CaO) is an alkaline chemical compound that serves as a major constituent across multiple industrial and construction materials, including cement clinker, granulated slag, fly ash, and phosphogypsum. It is routinely quantified in weight percentage and functions as a representative chemical indicator in compositional analyses, particularly for phosphogypsum. Across various studies and analytical datasets, CaO concentrations typically range between 30% and 50%, with specific documented values spanning 29.05% to 38.39%. In phosphogypsum, content fluctuates notably between 28.68% and 36.9%, while Soma fly ash records concentrations of 15.34%. CaO is consistently identified as a distinct chemical phase within mixtures and reference patterns. Additionally, the production of CaO is directly influenced by feedstock characteristics, increasing with larger coal sizes.

In industrial and chemical applications, CaO plays multifaceted roles. Within slag systems, it contributes to quaternary acidity and phase formation. During high-temperature processing of phosphogypsum, CaO exhibits dynamic thermodynamic behavior; it is generated through the oxidation of calcium sulfide (CaS) and actively competes with CaS throughout the decomposition pathway, necessitating thermodynamic modeling to track reaction kinetics. Beyond thermal processes, CaO acts as a critical alkaline agent. It is utilized in base-washing procedures to adjust the pH of phosphogypsum systems and is incorporated into alkaline activators to stimulate sulphate activation. This activation directly enhances the mechanical strength of cement, underscoring CaO's vital importance in optimizing cementitious performance, managing material composition, and controlling thermal decomposition dynamics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792455412</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Al2O3">
  <data key="d0">Al2O3</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Aluminum oxide (Al2O3) is a fundamental chemical compound widely recognized across geological, industrial, and environmental contexts. It serves as a major elemental constituent in various materials, including cement clinker, granulated slag, commercial calcium carbonate, Soma fly ash, and multiple variants of phosphogypsum, where it is frequently quantified as a mass fraction or percentage. In quantitative analyses, Al2O3 concentrations vary across samples, with documented averages reaching approximately 4536.63 ppm in specific datasets. Its presence and concentration are routinely measured using titration methods and calculated via specific titration formulas.

In industrial and materials science applications, Al2O3 plays a critical role in phase formation and chemical behavior. Within slag systems, it influences quaternary acidity and phase development, where reductions in its concentration can inhibit the formation of ettringite and diminish characteristic phase peaks. Additionally, Al2O3 functions as an additive in gypsum decomposition processes, with its transmission carefully monitored throughout the reaction. Beyond construction and waste materials, it is utilized as a traditional catalyst support in environmental catalysis, frequently benchmarked against apatite carriers to evaluate DeNOx activity performance. Collectively, these attributes underscore Al2O3's versatility and significance in both compositional analysis and applied chemical engineering.&lt;SEP&gt;Aluminum oxide, one of the primary oxides measured in the chemical composition of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical compound recorded with fluctuating values in the compositional analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792503973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fe2O3">
  <data key="d0">Fe2O3</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fe2O3, chemically identified as iron(III) oxide or iron oxide, is a significant chemical compound utilized across various industrial, analytical, and energy applications. In the energy sector, it functions as a primary iron oxide component within mixed oxygen carriers designed for chemical looping combustion. Additionally, Fe2O3 serves as an additive that modifies melting points and eutectic behavior, and it is incorporated into gypsum decomposition processes where its chemical transformation is actively monitored.

In material science and industrial byproduct analysis, Fe2O3 is consistently identified as a key constituent in cement clinker and phosphogypsum. Within phosphogypsum matrices, it is classified as both a major species and a notable impurity, appearing as a trace element compound in low concentrations derived from raw materials. Quantitative evaluations document its presence across specific numerical ranges, reporting an average measured concentration of 990.65 ppm and a distinct impurity level of 0.13 wt %. These analytical findings are frequently tabulated with associated numerical values, highlighting its prevalent occurrence and critical role in compositional profiling and industrial material characterization.

Analytically, the concentration and mass fraction of Fe2O3 are systematically quantified and calculated using established titration methods and corresponding mathematical formulas. These standardized procedures ensure precise measurement of the compound across the diverse industrial matrices and applications detailed in the provided records.&lt;SEP&gt;Fe2O3 is a chemical compound contributing to the total composition of Soma fly ash.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron(III) oxide is a major chemical component measured in percentage.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron oxide, one of the primary oxides measured in the chemical composition of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron(III) oxide, a chemical compound listed with varying numerical values in the dataset.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical compound tracked with varying concentrations in the compositional dataset.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792503973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MgO">
  <data key="d0">MgO</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnesium oxide (MgO) is a chemical compound widely distributed across various industrial and construction materials, including cement clinker, cement, fly ash, granulated slag, slag systems, phosphogypsum (including phosphogypsum brut), Portlandite, and synthetic potassium sulphate. It is routinely listed as a distinct constituent in composition tables. Depending on the specific material matrix and analytical context, MgO is characterized as both a major chemical component and a trace element. Analytical data tracks its concentration across different samples, reporting values in both weight percent and parts per million (ppm). The dataset records generally low concentration values, with a documented average of 834.86 ppm. These measurements are compiled in concentration and data tables featuring numerical ranges, and the specific values are derived using a defined calculation formula. Reporting consistency varies across research efforts, as the compound was not measured or reported in some initial studies.

For laboratory analysis, MgO samples undergo calcination at 950 °C ± 25 °C to prepare them for accurate measurement. Beyond its analytical presence, MgO serves several functional and chemical roles. It acts as an alkaline activator in binder mixtures utilized for phosphogypsum remediation and is added in analytically pure form to phosphogypsum to satisfy empirical parameters required for slag wool production. Furthermore, within slag systems, MgO contributes to quaternary acidity and influences phase formation. These combined characteristics highlight its dual role as both a commonly monitored compositional variable and a functionally active material in industrial applications.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical compound measured across multiple samples in the compositional analysis table.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792503973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SO3">
  <data key="d0">SO3</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">SO3, chemically known as sulfur trioxide, is a significant chemical compound that is measured in both cement clinker and phosphogypsum (PG). It is frequently noted as a potential constituent in phosphogypsum samples, with compositional analyses consistently identifying it as a major component. Data tables routinely list SO3 alongside associated numerical measurements, and it has been specifically recorded as a chemical component present in phosphogypsum at a concentration of 38.74 wt %.

Beyond its compositional prevalence, SO3 plays a critical functional role in cementitious systems. When combined with calcium oxide (CaO), it significantly affects the mechanical characteristics of phosphogypsum slag cement. Furthermore, SO3 is generated as a chemical byproduct during the thermal decomposition of phosphogypsum, where it is subsequently utilized in the industrial production of sulfuric acid. Collectively, these attributes establish SO3 as a key chemical component that is routinely quantified and utilized across cement and phosphogypsum processing contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;A major constituent of potassium sulphate, comprising 15.77% of its composition.&lt;SEP&gt;SO3 is a chemical compound measured at 0.99% in Soma fly ash.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur trioxide is a major chemical component measured in percentage.&lt;SEP&gt;SO3 is a chemical component of phosphogypsum, with content fluctuating between 44% and 50.3%.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulfur trioxide, a chemical compound showing significant variation in the provided data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-000</data>
