<node id="K2O">
  <data key="d0">K2O</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Potassium oxide, chemically represented as K2O, is a compound frequently analyzed across various industrial and material science contexts. In analytical datasets, it is cataloged with numerical concentration ranges and quantified in weight percent. The measurement process relies on precise weighing and drying techniques, with concentration values typically derived through specific calculation formulas. While recorded concentrations often remain low in certain sample matrices, the compound maintains a defined presence in standardized data tables.

The substance serves as a functional component in multiple material systems. It acts as a minor chemical constituent within cement clinker and is routinely quantified in phosphogypsum samples during compositional analysis. Furthermore, K2O is a major constituent of potassium sulphate, accounting for exactly 24.96% of its chemical composition. These varied analytical and compositional roles underscore its significance in both laboratory measurements and industrial material characterization.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium oxide is a major chemical component measured in percentage.&lt;SEP&gt;K2O is a chemical compound whose concentration is recorded in the compositional analysis data.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical compound present in trace amounts throughout the compositional measurements.</data>
