<node id="Phosphates">
  <data key="d0">Phosphates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphates are chemical compounds that manifest in two distinct contexts: as undesirable contaminants within industrial byproducts and as stable components in natural geological environments. In industrial settings, particularly within phosphogypsum waste and solids, phosphates function as primary potentially toxic impurities distributed throughout the crystals, associated water, and residual phases. Their concentration and variation are heavily dependent on the specific wet production process employed. During phosphogypsum purification, phosphates are systematically targeted for removal; however, they frequently adhere to gypsum crystals, substitute directly into the crystal lattice, and dominate the undissolved particles in leaching residue. They are insoluble compounds found in the residual phase and are adsorbed, incorporated, and transferred to the solution during treatment, necessitating their removal for industrial use.

The chemical behavior of phosphates in phosphogypsum is complex. Their dissolution is significantly hindered by surrounding gypsum coatings, though their solubility has been explicitly determined in scientific studies. Under certain conditions, these phosphogypsum-bound phosphates undergo microbial reduction to generate phosphine gas. Additionally, they form complexes with metallic elements such as potassium, sodium, and thorium, with the latter interaction reducing thorium's mobility. They can be effectively immobilized at rates exceeding 90% through the addition of calcium carbonate. From an industrial application standpoint, these phosphate impurities are known to delay cement setting time and reduce early strength development. Furthermore, certain mineral classes containing phosphates have been highlighted by researchers as potential primary carriers of rare earth elements within phosphogypsum.

In a separate natural context, phosphates refer to specific green minerals embedded within soil profiles. These naturally occurring phosphate minerals are notably composed of magnesium and potassium, representing a stable, metal-rich mineral component. This dual characterization highlights the entity's varied presence across both industrial waste management and natural geological environments, where it manifests either as a chemically reactive impurity requiring remediation or as a stable geological mineral.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
