  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fluorides">
  <data key="d0">Fluorides</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluorides are a class of chemical compounds, predominantly sodium- and calcium-based, that function as significant impurities and chemical residues within phosphogypsum, a byproduct generated during the wet-process phosphate rock industry. Common species include CaF, Na2SiF6, Na3AlF6, and NaF. Their concentrations are intrinsically tied to the source rock and governed by anion substitution mechanisms, frequently occurring alongside phosphates, acid residues, and heavy metals. In certain contexts, fluorides exist as dispersed soluble rare earth element salts, although their dissolution can be hindered by gypsum coatings, and some authors highlight them as potential main rare earth element-bearing minerals in phosphogypsum. When subjected to leaching with sodium carbonate and sodium bicarbonate, these fluorides are exposed as impurities, whereas they remain absent in raw and acid-treated samples. As a chemical parameter, they are routinely measured in phosphogypsum samples for leaching toxicity analysis.

Classified as primary potentially toxic elements, fluorides pose substantial ecological pollution risks and public health hazards. Due to their high solubility, they readily migrate into surrounding soils and groundwater aquifers, creating a persistent risk of secondary release during groundwater leaching. Consequently, their sorption behavior and mineral stability in leachate-affected soils are actively examined, and they are strictly monitored as environmental emissions generated during the production process, as well as during brick firing and soil application. To mitigate these environmental and health risks, fluorides are systematically targeted for removal during phosphogypsum purification and can be effectively immobilized at rates exceeding 99% through treatment with calcium carbonate. Additionally, fluorides can form insoluble complexes with thorium, thereby limiting the mobility of this element.

Beyond environmental protection, precise control and removal of fluorides are critical for preserving the engineering viability of phosphogypsum when repurposed as construction backfill. Elevated fluoride concentrations undermine material performance by forming inactive protective coats on cement particle surfaces, which contributes to a retarder effect that extends setting times, degrades pore structure formation, and inhibits hydration product precipitation within cemented paste backfill matrices. This directly correlates with marked declines in material workability, mechanical performance, and overall structural strength. Therefore, managing fluoride content is essential not only for preventing ecological contamination but also for ensuring the functional reliability of phosphogypsum-based construction applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorides are impurities in phosphogypsum that delay cement setting time and reduce early strength development.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorides are impurities in PG whose concentration decreases after purification treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorides are impurity compounds in phosphogypsum that are significantly reduced through thermal calcination processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
