<node id="Table 2">
  <data key="d0">Table 2</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 2 functions as a recurring, generic reference label rather than a single unified dataset, with its specific content strictly determined by the experimental design, research focus, and material selection of individual scientific studies. Consequently, its documented data spans multiple disciplines and material types. In materials science and engineering contexts, it catalogs the chemical composition percentages and elementary components of high-sulfur-concentration coal samples, as well as volume fractions across different size regions following dry milling post-processing. It also details the advantages and shortcomings of utilizing phosphogypsum, alongside trace metal contents across various phosphogypsum sources. For construction and cementitious applications, Table 2 records expansion values for different paste mixes, setting times for pastes with varying phosphogypsum or gypsum replacement percentages, and autoclave expansion percentages.

In chemical and mineralogical analyses, the table documents the average chemical and elemental composition of phosphogypsum from various industrial production processes, including major species, trace elements, heavy metals, and rare earth elements such as Strontium Oxide and Yttrium Oxide. This data incorporates global and regional Chinese samples from Hubei, Yunnan, and Guizhou provinces, utilizing analytical techniques like X-ray fluorescence, inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry, and inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry. It classifies impurities by type, chemical form, and solubility, provides fractionation and leaching data, and records radiological assessments involving gamma spectrometry, Lead-210 levels, and activity ratios.

Within materials synthesis and characterization, Table 2 serves as a repository for synthesized powders, composites, and construction materials. It outlines physical, chemical, and strength properties of cement, fly ash, and phosphogypsum blends, alongside quantitative phase compositions for bone cement and brick properties integrated with Life Cycle Inventory data. For hydroxyapatite variants, it details microstructural and crystallographic parameters derived from X-ray diffraction models, including crystallite dimensions, lattice parameters, d-spacing, crystallinity, Ca/P ratios, and optical band gap energies. It records particle sizes across synthesis temperatures of 150, 165, and 180 °C, thermal analysis metrics, FTIR adsorption bands, oxygen concentrations, calculated compositions for Mk systems based on alumina and magnesium oxide dosages, and precise mix proportions and water-binder ratios for experimental PBSLM samples.

In agricultural and environmental engineering, the table documents phosphogypsum's efficacy as a biofertilizer, noting positive impacts on crop growth and yield for species like ryegrass and pakchoi. It supports environmental modeling and fuel quality assessments by reporting biodiesel parameters such as density, viscosity, and flash point, comparing hydroxyapatite-derived biodiesel against standards. It provides Langmuir and Freundlich isotherm constants for ion adsorption kinetics on biochar and hydroxyapatite-biochar substrates, illustrates the influence of soil solution ionic strength on macroion association, and presents macroion equilibrium form calculations, ion binding percentages, and various chemical parameter calculations.

Finally, Table 2 compiles thermodynamic data, specifically standard enthalpies of formation for reactants and products at 298.15 °C. In methodological and kinetic contexts, it lists balanced weighting results for four evaluation dimensions, presents weights assigned by different methods, summarizes phosphorus losses and recovery rates by sector, and lists kinetic parameters for pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order models at various temperatures, alongside corresponding model parameters and normalized standard deviation values. Collectively, these diverse instances confirm that Table 2 operates as a flexible, discipline-specific placeholder for distinct analytical results, dynamically adapted to each study's unique requirements.</data>
