<node id="Distilled Water">
  <data key="d0">Distilled Water</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Distilled Water is a highly purified form of water that functions primarily as a solvent and washing agent across various chemical, material processing, and laboratory applications. Its exceptional purity makes it ideal for precise scientific procedures, such as solubility determinations, where contamination must be strictly avoided. In synthesis and material preparation workflows, it serves as the primary medium for dissolving chemical precursors, including calcium nitrate tetrahydrate in specialized routes like the NoSurf synthesis method, and is utilized for all chemical and hydrothermal syntheses in relevant studies. In certain chemical reactions, it is also introduced directly as a reagent or generated as a byproduct.

Beyond its role as a dissolution medium, Distilled Water is extensively employed as a washing agent to purify solid materials and eliminate unwanted components. It is used to rinse residual solid phases, beneficiated solids, synthesized powders, specific substrates such as BS material and chicken eggshells, and raw phosphogypsum samples during preparation. The washing process, which occasionally incorporates boiling mixtures, effectively removes water-soluble impurities, dirt, undesirable phases, and specific compounds like nardite. Notable applications include its use by Haridasan for leaching radium-226 from phosphogypsum waste, as well as its role in the synthesis and purification of hydroxyapatite (HAp). By thoroughly rinsing these materials, Distilled Water facilitates the isolation of clean solids, ensuring the removal of residual contaminants and significantly enhancing the overall purity of the final products.&lt;SEP&gt;Distilled water serves as a neutral control medium in desorption experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Distilled water is the solvent used to prepare the 5% solution for pH measurement.&lt;SEP&gt;Distilled water is a purified washing medium used in phosphogypsum treatment methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Distilled water is a solvent used in leaching experiments to measure radium release from phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Distilled water is used in batch and continuous leaching experiments to measure the dissolution rates of trace elements in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792485464</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Phosphate Ion is a negatively charged polyatomic anion that plays a significant role in various chemical and materials science applications. It is typically represented by the formula PO4 3-, though some descriptions note it as PO²⁻. This ion is generated during the hydrolysis of pyrophosphate ions and can manifest as two orthophosphate ions in reaction products. In chemical environments, it readily reacts with calcium ions to form insoluble calcium phosphate. Additionally, it participates in surface reactions on cement particles, where it actively influences retardation mechanisms. During the precipitation of hydroxyapatite crystals, the ions are known to adsorb onto CTAB monomers.

Structurally, the Phosphate Ion serves as a fundamental sub-lattice component within hydroxyapatite and the broader apatite crystal lattice, where it is subject to anionic substitution. Its presence in these crystalline frameworks dictates specific vibrational characteristics, with translational modes detectable in Raman spectroscopy within the 200–350 cm⁻¹ range. Together, these properties underscore the ion’s importance in driving precipitation reactions, modifying material surfaces, and defining the structural and spectroscopic profiles of phosphate-based systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The phosphate ion is a product formed from the hydrolysis of the triphosphate ion.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate Ion is a chemical component fundamental to hydroxyapatite structure.&lt;SEP&gt;An ion whose concentration variations dictate the sequential appearance and coexistence of ardealite and brushite phases.&lt;SEP&gt;The phosphate ion is a chemical species that substitutes into the solid solution and influences phase composition and spectral properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792404732</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pyrophosphate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Pyrophosphate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Pyrophosphate Ion is a polyphosphate species found in Phosphogypsum that undergoes hydrolysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The reactant ion that undergoes hydrolysis in the described chemical equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790961463</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
