<node id="India">
  <data key="d0">India</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">India is a South Asian country that plays a substantial role in the research, production, and application of phosphogypsum, while also providing broader context for electronic waste urban mining and circular economy transitions. The nation accounts for 6.35% of global phosphogypsum production and reserves, with significant domestic output that supports both academic and industrial advancements. Scientific contributions from India are prominent in this domain, highlighted by data visualizations indicating a weight percentage of approximately 47%. The country serves as a geographic origin for phosphogypsum samples and is frequently referenced in data tables regarding material properties.

Research and institutional efforts in India are spearheaded by organizations such as the Central Building Research Institute, which operates under Indian Standards and has conducted extensive analysis and tabulation of phosphogypsum’s chemical compositions. This institutional work aligns with academic studies, including a 2023 investigation by Pratap et al. that examined the material's chemical components and geographic properties. These research initiatives underpin the nation's practical applications of phosphogypsum, which include the commercial use of the Merseburg ammonocarbonation process and the reclamation of sodic soils.

In terms of manufacturing and industrial utilization, India predominantly directs phosphogypsum toward cement production, with smaller quantities allocated to agriculture and fertilizer manufacturing. The material is also extensively incorporated into the production of various construction products, including gypsum board, blocks, and bricks. Through these diverse applications and robust research frameworks, India continues to advance both its industrial capabilities and scientific understanding of phosphogypsum utilization.&lt;SEP&gt;India is a geographical source of Phosphogypsum with documented major element composition.&lt;SEP&gt;India is a location where phosphogypsum samples were analyzed for radionuclide content.&lt;SEP&gt;India is the country where Kochi is located, serving as the source region for phosphogypsum samples in leaching studies.&lt;SEP&gt;A country whose phosphogypsum samples were included in the chemical composition dataset.&lt;SEP&gt;India is a geographical location where phosphogypsum samples were analyzed, showing low Silicon Dioxide levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792480370</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Citric Acid">
  <data key="d0">Citric Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Citric Acid is a water-soluble, weak organic acid naturally produced by microorganisms, widely utilized across industrial, environmental, and materials science applications. In phosphogypsum processing, it serves as a highly effective aqueous leaching and treatment reagent, typically applied via soaking at concentrations between 2.0% and 5.0%, with optimal levels identified at 3.0% for HDS samples and 4.0% for DA samples. Chemically, it reacts with soluble fluorine, phosphorus, and phosphate impurities, converting them into water-soluble compounds that are easily washed away, while also lowering the pH during bioleaching processes. It is recognized as the most effective, cost-efficient, and environmentally friendly agent for reducing hazardous constituents in phosphogypsum, specifically decreasing radionuclide levels by approximately 22% to about 0.34 wt%, which represents the lowest radionuclide content among tested methods. Additionally, it transforms calcium sulfate into gypsum and functions as a selective leaching agent for recovering rare earth elements from phosphogypsum and coal fly ash, although sulfuric acid is occasionally noted to provide superior leaching efficiency for this specific purpose. Citric Acid also serves as a reference solution in solubility charts to evaluate calcium phosphate dissolution behavior.

Beyond waste remediation, Citric Acid plays a critical role in materials science and biomineralization as a surfactant and morphological control agent. Under hydrothermal conditions, it suppresses the nucleation and growth of hydroxyapatite, effectively reduces particle size, and directs crystal formation into specific structures, notably yielding rod-like hydroxyapatite morphologies. It similarly alters the crystalline phase shape of gypsum and facilitates the breakdown of Plaster of Paris into flakes. In construction and environmental management, it is employed as a pretreatment agent to enhance the performance of phosphogypsum-based cemented paste backfill and to control fluoride pollution within the mixture. Despite its significant improvements in impurity removal and process efficiency, the application of Citric Acid is noted to substantially increase overall operational costs.&lt;SEP&gt;Citric acid is a dilute acid solution used in washing treatments to reduce soluble phosphorus pentoxide in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Citric acid is an organic acid solution used to shake and wash phosphogypsum to eliminate impurities.&lt;SEP&gt;Citric acid is an alternative chemical solution tested to remove impurities from phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-008</data>
