<node id="Solubility">
  <data key="d0">Solubility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Solubility is a fundamental physicochemical and chemical property that defines the capacity or ability of a substance to dissolve in a given solvent. As a quantitative measure, it indicates the maximum amount of a solute that can be dissolved per unit volume, commonly expressed in grams per liter or per 100 milliliters of solution. This property is heavily influenced by environmental and chemical factors, including pH levels, redox conditions (Eh), compound formation, and the specific nature of the solvent or reagents used. It directly determines the concentration of elements in solution and serves as a key metric for understanding dissolution dynamics.

In specialized industrial and mineralogical research, solubility is frequently measured across various solvents and tested reagents to optimize extraction and processing methods. It is particularly critical when evaluating phosphatic and fluoride compounds, calcium phosphate, gypsum, calcium sulfate, and phosphogypsum. These assessments often focus on how much of these specific compounds dissolves in water or aqueous citric acid solutions. By quantifying dissolution behavior under controlled conditions, researchers can accurately track element concentrations and refine chemical processing techniques for these materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Solubility is a property of substances in phosphogypsum that is determined by hydrothermal process parameters and mineralizers.&lt;SEP&gt;Solubility is a chemical property that limits the leaching efficiency of rare earth elements in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Solubility is the limiting factor for rare earth element release, as elements become trapped in undissolved particles once the limit is reached.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792036549</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chemical Composition">
  <data key="d0">Chemical Composition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Chemical Composition refers to the quantitative breakdown and elemental makeup of materials, particularly phosphogypsum and raw materials utilized in industrial applications. It encompasses the percentage distribution of specific chemical constituents, including P₂O₅, F, organic matter, Na₂O + K₂O, CaO, SiO₂, and SO₃, alongside physical and chemical parameters such as pH, moisture content, and heavy metal concentrations. This material property is systematically verified and analyzed using analytical techniques like Energy Dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy (EDS) and X-ray Fluorescence (XRF) spectroscopy.

As a critical quality attribute, Chemical Composition in phosphogypsum is inherently variable and fluctuates across samples, which necessitates data normalization for accurate assessment. This variability directly impacts the generalizability of backfill applications and serves as a fundamental determinant of the material's performance. Furthermore, the chemical makeup significantly influences the setting duration of cement, underscoring its importance in construction material engineering. Overall, Chemical Composition represents a comprehensive measure of a substance's internal makeup, with direct implications for its processing, quality control, and industrial utility.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical composition refers to the elemental breakdown of Phosphogypsum, including CaO, sulphates, SiO2, and Al2O3.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical composition refers to the elemental breakdown of OPC and PPC listed in Table 1.&lt;SEP&gt;Structured data detailing the weight percentages of various oxides and elements in phosphogypsum samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792502969</data>
