<node id="Sulphuric Acid">
  <data key="d0">Sulphuric Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sulphuric acid is a key industrial chemical and versatile reagent primarily utilized in phosphate processing and environmental management. It serves as the fundamental chemical agent in the wet process, where it attacks and acidifies both phosphate rocks and phosphate concentrates to synthesize phosphoric acid. This large-scale industrial application inherently generates phosphogypsum as a major byproduct. The chemical is also recognized as a central component of the sulphuric process, underscoring its foundational role in modern phosphate manufacturing and related industrial workflows.

Beyond primary acid production, sulphuric acid is employed to acidify rock phosphate for the synthesis of various fluoride and phosphate compounds. It also features in alternative treatment methodologies for phosphogypsum, where it reacts with the material to form bassanite and effectively reduces residual phosphate and fluoride concentrations. In analytical and regulatory contexts, the compound functions as a standard reagent within the SW-846 testing procedure. Furthermore, specific industrial records document its generation through the PSC process, with tracked production quantities reaching 408.00 kg. Collectively, these applications highlight sulphuric acid's critical function across extraction, byproduct remediation, and standardized testing protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical compound produced from industrial by-product gypsum through new technological developments.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulphuric acid is a strong acid used to stir and treat phosphogypsum, often combined with thermal processes or milk of lime.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical reagent combined with silica for phosphogypsum purification.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulphuric acid is used in purification and treatment processes for phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical reagent used in the treatment process of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Sulphuric Acid is a residual acid contained within Phosphogypsum, contributing to its acidic properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792487517</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium Citrate">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Citrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium citrate is a water-soluble compound formed when fluoride impurities react with aqueous citric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium citrate is a compound believed to perform the same role as citric acid by suppressing HAp nucleation and growth under hydrothermal conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium citrate is an additive used in hydrothermal synthesis to create hierarchical morphology with porosity.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium citrate is used as an additive in hydrothermal synthesis to produce HAP with hierarchical morphology.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium citrate is a water-soluble compound formed when citric acid converts phosphate impurities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720679</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrofluoric Acid">
  <data key="d0">Hydrofluoric Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrofluoric acid is a highly reactive, water-soluble aqueous solution that functions as both a significant industrial byproduct and a versatile chemical reagent. It is primarily generated during the wet-process manufacturing of phosphoric acid, where fluorine derived from apatite and phosphate rock reacts with silicate impurities upon digestion with sulfuric acid, often releasing the compound as a volatile or gaseous byproduct. The substance can also be produced when citric acid converts fluoride impurities, and it frequently interacts with silica during these formation processes to yield hexafluorosilicic acid. Leveraging its strong chemical reactivity, hydrofluoric acid is widely utilized as a precision etching agent for glass and silicon-based materials, as well as a processing reagent designed to remove quartz phases and convert specific components within industrial materials.

Within phosphogypsum, hydrofluoric acid persists as a harmful, soluble free acid impurity that directly contributes to the material's highly corrosive and acidic properties. Recognized as one of the initial chemicals alongside other acids in phosphogypsum processing, it is systematically managed to mitigate its hazards. This is typically achieved through chemical reactions, such as neutralizing the acid with quicklime to form inert salts, or by extracting it as a soluble impurity through subsequent washing procedures. Its presence and controlled removal are essential for ensuring the safe handling and environmental compliance of processed phosphate byproducts.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrofluoric Acid is a residual acid contained within Phosphogypsum, contributing to its acidic properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrofluoric acid is a water-soluble impurity removed from PG during purification.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-058&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-001</data>
