<node id="Calcium Dihydrogen Phosphate Monohydrate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Dihydrogen Phosphate Monohydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium dihydrogen phosphate monohydrate is a phosphatic compound that exhibits maximum solubility in aqueous citric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium dihydrogen phosphate monohydrate is a chemical compound possibly precipitated on the surface of phosphogypsum crystals.&lt;SEP&gt;A water-soluble compound potentially assigned to precipitation in the studied samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A phosphor impurity in PG composition that contributes to increased setting times.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium dihydrogen phosphate monohydrate is a chemical phase that was not detected by XRD analysis in the studied samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792404947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium fluoride (NaF) is a water-soluble fluoride compound formed by the combination of fluorine with sodium cations. It is frequently detected in various studied samples and exhibits distinct chemical and physical behaviors depending on environmental conditions. In aqueous environments, it demonstrates maximum solubility in citric acid, while at elevated temperatures, it transitions into a gas phase compound that begins to appear between 1200 °C and 1300 °C and increases in concentration as temperatures rise.

The compound serves multiple industrial and chemical applications. It is utilized as a chemical additive to influence the synthesis of hydroxyapatite, particularly through the gel method, and functions as a key component in fluoride solutions designed to convert phosphogypsum. However, its presence in cemented paste fill materials has notable structural impacts. Increased concentrations of sodium fluoride lead to higher porosity, coarsened crystal structures, and a significant deterioration of the filling body's pore architecture, ultimately resulting in decreased mechanical strength.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium fluoride is a chemical compound dissolved in distilled water to prepare the fluoride stock solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium Fluoride is a water-soluble fluoride impurity found in Phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792452838</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Purification">
  <data key="d0">Purification</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Purification is a downstream processing operation and treatment method primarily applied to phosphogypsum (PG) and S-HAp particles to remove impurities and render the materials suitable for various industrial applications, safe disposal, or reuse. This process is frequently studied as a critical operational category within phosphogypsum utilization frameworks, addressing inherent contaminants and, in the case of S-HAp particles, irregular morphology that would otherwise limit their practical utility.

The purification process employs improved methodologies and novel cleaning techniques, such as the application of aqueous citric acid, to dissolve and extract contaminants by forming water-soluble compounds. Key targets for removal include phosphorus pentoxide (P2O5), alkalis, and radioactive substances. By effectively reducing these impurities and lowering overall radioactivity levels, purification significantly enhances material performance, notably by shortening setting time and augmenting mechanical strength.

Beyond general material upgrading, purification serves specific functional roles, such as facilitating the isolation of rare earth elements following their extraction from phosphogypsum. While essential for improving material quality and enabling safe handling, the implementation of these purification steps may introduce additional production costs. Consequently, it remains a vital, albeit economically impactful, stage in the comprehensive processing and valorization of phosphogypsum and related synthetic hydroxyapatite particles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792453597</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fig. 1">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 1 functions as a generic, context-dependent figure label rather than a standardized scientific illustration, appearing across numerous independent studies to represent distinct entities, datasets, and experimental frameworks. In phosphate chemistry and phosphogypsum research, it has been utilized to depict the solubility of phosphates in aqueous citric acid at 30°C, present ArcGIS-generated geographical maps of company locations and production or recycling volumes, and visualize rare earth element distributions, system boundaries for disposal methods, fractional and cumulative grain size distributions, thermogravimetric and derived curves for JorfLasfar phosphogypsum, and X-ray diffraction spectra of phosphogypsum samples. It also serves as a reference for the moist gray appearance of phosphogypsum, illustrates the overview and mass balance of phosphoric acid and phosphogypsum production, and outlines the technological process of Scenario 1. Within materials science, Fig. 1 is frequently applied to characterize hydroxyapatite, showcasing its structural framework, scanning electron microscopy images under varying hydrothermal and thermal treatments, X-ray diffraction patterns comparing mesoporous particles with standard samples or synthesized at different temperatures, FT-IR spectra of samples prepared at different hydrothermal times, and schematic diagrams detailing the preparation process for nanorod or nanosized hydroxyapatite particles derived from waste phosphogypsum.

Environmental and agricultural literature employs the label for diverse analytical and preparatory diagrams, including schematics tracking temperature and water content fluctuations in compost piles during biofertilizer preparation, radioanalytical procedures for uranium separation and radon measurement, visual representations of prepared cubic and prismatic adobe samples, microscopic morphologies of experimental raw materials, diagrams illustrating material flows and system boundaries, and visualizations of soil compaction characteristics resulting from cement and cement-phosphogypsum mixtures. Additionally, Fig. 1 is used to illustrate the decision hierarchy structure and steps in the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP), as well as the flowchart of an integrated evaluation process. Collectively, these applications confirm that Fig. 1 serves as a flexible, study-specific identifier tailored to distinct experimental contexts rather than a unified scientific figure.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 1 is a graphical representation showing the effect of PG replacement on mortar compressive strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792502258</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fig. 2">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 2</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 2 functions as a standard, recurring figure label applied across numerous independent scientific publications rather than representing a single unified scientific entity. In each instance, it denotes a distinct visualization tailored to a specific research context, serving as a conventional labeling convention for diverse datasets, comparative analyses, and distribution metrics across multiple independent research domains.

In materials science and chemical characterization, Fig. 2 is frequently employed to display X-ray diffraction patterns, crystallographic peaks of synthesized hydroxyapatite, and the solubility of fluorides in aqueous citric acid at 30°C. It also serves as a composite figure integrating Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, X-ray diffraction, and Brunauer–Emmett–Teller analysis results, alongside graphical representations of adsorption-desorption isotherms for hydroxyapatite and 20HPA-hydroxyapatite composites. Additionally, it documents the morphological structures of hydroxyapatite samples synthesized with varying CTAB concentrations, presents FESEM and TEM micrographs with schematic illustrations of sample morphologies and formation processes, and illustrates the morphological evolution of porous HA microspheres at various hydrothermal times.

Within environmental and resource management research, particularly concerning phosphogypsum, Fig. 2 illustrates a wide array of analytical and assessment data. These include X-ray diffraction patterns of raw phosphogypsum, comparative X-ray diffraction analysis of commercial gypsum, phosphogypsum, washed phosphogypsum, and portlandite, thermogravimetric analysis under both open-system and nitrogen atmosphere conditions, infrared spectra of phosphogypsum derived from Sebaiya ore, and alpha-spectra of uranium isotopes measured in phosphogypsum waste. The label is also utilized for practical and distributional analyses, such as demonstrating the use of phosphogypsum in secondary road construction, presenting particle size curves and stress-strain examination results for raw phosphogypsum, and showing the evolution of grading curves under different stress levels. Furthermore, it supports comparative and distributional assessments, including Life Cycle Assessment results evaluating various disposal methods across environmental impact units, graphical representations of publication trends, geographical distribution, and primary research topics, as well as visualizations of phosphogypsum recycling distribution across different applications in surveyed regions.

In agricultural and soil science contexts, Fig. 2 has been utilized to compare the physical and chemical properties of soil amended with biofertilizer against unamended control soil, as well as to illustrate the impact of fly ash and fly ash-phosphogypsum on maximum dry unit weight and optimum moisture content, and the variation of compaction characteristics for soils stabilized with these materials. In methodological and analytical frameworks, it displays the weights of dimensions and indicators computed using specific weighting methods, alongside comparisons of indicator weights across different weighting methodologies. Collectively, these varied applications confirm that Fig. 2 operates as a ubiquitous, context-dependent figure designation rather than a fixed scientific object, consistently adapted to present domain-specific visualizations, analytical results, and comparative metrics across independent studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 2 illustrates the technological process of Scenario 2.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 2 displays the XRD diffraction peaks, highlighting the sharpness of the (0 0 2) peak relative to others.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 2 is a figure displaying Scanning Electron Microscopy images of Phosphogypsum at different magnifications.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 2 is a graphical representation showing the effect of PG replacement on mortar flexural strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792502258</data>
