<node id="Table 6">
  <data key="d0">Table 6</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 6 is a recurring label utilized across numerous independent scientific publications to organize specialized experimental data, environmental assessments, and material formulations. Rather than representing a single unified dataset, it functions as a contextual identifier that varies by research domain. In materials science and chemical engineering, Table 6 documents the physical properties of gypsum plaster, including setting time, bulk density, and compressive strength, while also detailing the distinct characteristics and intended purposes of various gypsum forms based on their crystal morphology. It further catalogs the physical traits and industrial applications of downstream products derived from phosphogypsum calcination, outlines recommended percentage-based formulations for CPGFM (comprising phosphor slag, phosphogypsum, an activator, a water reducer, and specific concentrations), and records conversion yields and process metrics for fluoride medium transformations. Additionally, one iteration examines the conversion of phosphogypsum into valuable compounds under varied experimental conditions, providing calculated radium equivalent activity and total absorbed dose rate values.

In environmental and public health research, Table 6 serves as a reference for drinking water guidelines concerning Radium-226 and presents a comparative analysis of water quality parameters, specifically highlighting pH ranges between the Gabes and Djerba regions. It also exhibits RFD and SF values for heavy metals. Within waste management and geotechnical engineering contexts, the table ranks four phosphogypsum waste management methods based on aggregated scores from three distinct perspectives. Concurrently, it provides geotechnical data by predicting consolidation settlements in centimeters for phosphogypsum embankments at various heights and height fractions under operating loads. Finally, in biological and materials research, Table 6 compares crystallite sizes across various natural apatites, such as bones and teeth. Collectively, these varied applications demonstrate how the designation Table 6 is adaptively employed across disparate scientific fields to structure and communicate highly specific empirical findings.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-006</data>
