<node id="Dihydrate Process">
  <data key="d0">Dihydrate Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Dihydrate Process is a specific pathway and the most widely adopted variant of the wet phosphoric acid production method. It serves as the most common industrial technique for manufacturing phosphoric acid from apatite ore. Its widespread implementation is primarily attributed to its mature technology and consistently stable operation.

A defining characteristic of the Dihydrate Process is its generation of phosphogypsum as a primary byproduct. This material is dominated by calcium sulfate dihydrate and typically forms filter cakes that contain notable impurities. Furthermore, the process directly influences the adjustment and crystal shapes of the resulting phosphogypsum, making it a critical factor in the overall production and handling of the byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;The dihydrate process is a single-stage wet process widely used for its simplicity and flexibility, operating at 70 to 80 degrees Celsius.&lt;SEP&gt;A manufacturing method where heavy rare earth elements migrate into phosphoric acid.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dihydrate Process is an industrial method used for the production of phosphoric acid.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792406659</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hemihydrate Process">
  <data key="d0">Hemihydrate Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Hemihydrate Process is a widely utilized industrial and chemical method for manufacturing phosphoric acid, primarily from calcareous phosphorites. While some sources describe it as a two-step method, it is operationally defined as a single-stage wet process that functions at temperatures between 90 and 110 degrees Celsius. This configuration requires less grinding than alternative methods and yields phosphoric acid with a higher P2O5 concentration and greater purity, though it achieves a slightly lower overall recovery rate.

A significant byproduct of this method is phosphogypsum, whose crystal structure, formation, and adjustment are directly influenced by the process conditions. This phosphogypsum typically exhibits relatively high impurity levels and distinct crystal shapes, making it a focal point for source reduction technologies aimed at improving recycling rates and material quality. Regarding trace element migration, the process behaves similarly to the dihydrate process, particularly concerning rare earth elements. Additionally, while the phosphogypsum produced contains elevated uranium concentrations (5–100 μg/g) due to its specific crystal lattice, the Hemihydrate Process itself lacks inherent uranium extraction capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792403134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ammonium Hydroxide">
  <data key="d0">Ammonium Hydroxide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ammonium Hydroxide (NH4OH) is a basic aqueous solution and alkaline hydroxide reagent widely utilized across industrial and chemical processing applications. Characterized by a standard enthalpy of formation of -361.2 kJ/mol, it functions primarily as a reactant and pH modifier introduced into reactors or conversion vessels. Its alkaline nature allows it to effectively shift precursor mixtures into basic conditions, with specific pH targets tailored to the process requirements, including maintaining levels above 9, achieving a pH of 10 when paired with ammonium chloride, or adjusting to pH 11.

A major application of Ammonium Hydroxide lies in phosphogypsum conversion experiments and processing. In these procedures, it serves as a critical reactant that adjusts the reaction environment and subsequently reacts with sulfuric acid to facilitate the production of ammonium sulfate. The compound is also integral to various synthesis methodologies, where it is employed to control reaction conditions during hydrothermal synthesis, precipitation, and microwave synthesis procedures.

Beyond chemical synthesis, Ammonium Hydroxide plays a vital role in environmental remediation and materials engineering. It is extensively used to treat phosphogypsum waste and phosphogypsum-based cemented paste backfill. In these remediation and material preparation processes, it is frequently combined with citric acid to effectively lower phosphate and fluoride concentrations. Furthermore, when applied to cemented paste backfill, it mitigates set retardation, thereby improving the material's curing properties. Through these diverse functions, Ammonium Hydroxide operates as a versatile agent for precise pH control, chemical waste remediation, and reaction facilitation.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium hydroxide is a chemical reagent fed to the reactor to shift the pH to an alkaline region and facilitate precipitation.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium hydroxide is a chemical reagent added during the conversion process to facilitate compound synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium Hydroxide is a chemical compound listed as a reactant in the thermodynamic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ammonium hydroxide is a chemical solution tested to reduce phosphate and fluoride impurities in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792487165</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ammonium Sulphate">
