<node id="Radium-226">
  <data key="d0">Radium-226</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radium-226 is a naturally occurring radioactive isotope and the most abundant form of radium, functioning as a key member of the uranium-238 decay series generated through the alpha decay of thorium-230. Chemically analogous to calcium, it readily integrates into environmental and biological systems, migrating through groundwater and accumulating in soils, particularly following phosphogypsum applications. Primarily sourced from sedimentary phosphate rocks and uranium mill waste, it is notably concentrated in Florida phosphate materials and global geological formations. During phosphoric acid production, Radium-226 is primarily transferred into phosphogypsum, where it coprecipitates with calcium sulfate and barium, retaining approximately 80–90% within the gypsum phase through surface adsorption and carbonation binding. It also concentrates in elemental phosphorus and phosphoric acid scale, serving as the primary contributor and major source of radioactivity in phosphogypsum.

Under stable geological conditions, Radium-226 typically exists in secular equilibrium with uranium-238 and lead-210. Its concentration varies significantly across regions and samples, typically averaging around 22 Bq/kg but reaching up to 482 ± 13.2 Bq/kg in specific instances, with activity levels frequently matching or exceeding uranium-238 and occasionally violating regulatory standards. Due to its environmental mobility, it readily leaches into water and soil, posing significant public health and ecological risks. Consequently, it is rigorously monitored across drinking water, soil, crops, aquatic systems, stream sediments, and groundwater, with specific measurements recorded in contexts such as high population areas (e.g., 2106 Bq) and groundwater (e.g., 41.7 kBq). As a gamma emitter with a characteristic energy of 186.2 keV, it is analyzed via gamma spectrometry, often alongside decay products such as radon-222, lead-214, and bismuth-214. It serves as a standard reference material for efficiency determination and is directly correlated with radon exhalation values, while its dissolution characteristics and transport modeling remain challenging due to incomplete thermodynamic data.

In remediation and waste management, Radium-226 is frequently targeted for removal from phosphogypsum to comply with safety standards and is tracked in Life Cycle Inventory data for disposal. Removal and extraction efficiencies depend heavily on methodology and sample composition, with peak stripping efficiencies of approximately 51% at 12 minutes and peak extraction rates of 60% after 2 hours, though specific samples have achieved efficiencies as high as 92.1% and 90.7%. Generally, removal efficiency increases with agitation time up to approximately 2 hours before slightly declining. Notably, Radium-226 consistently demonstrates the highest extraction efficiency among tested species and can be significantly reduced through organic extractants and topochemical reactions. Its specific activity concentrations are critical parameters in radiation safety assessments, directly influencing total absorbed dose rates and gamma exposure, underscoring its prominence in phosphogypsum waste treatment protocols and radiological safety frameworks.</data>
