<node id="WBS">
  <data key="d0">WBS</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">WBS, which stands for Washed Beneficiated Solid, is a processed residual solid matrix derived from phosphogypsum (PG) following solubilization procedures. Initially functioning as a raw material sample, it undergoes a treatment process where quartz and other mineral phases are removed. The material is subjected to a permutations protocol for residual gangue removal, and soluble sodium sulfates are systematically eliminated. Chemically, WBS primarily consists of calcium fluoride and quartz, forming a distinct solid matrix that acts as a washed beneficiated concentrate with an increased concentration of rare earth elements (REEs). These REEs are specifically enriched through coprecipitation with phosphate and fluorine.

As a specialized sample type, WBS is routinely analyzed to determine the precise concentrations of REEs and various main metal elements. The material represents a targeted byproduct or solution phase where valuable components are concentrated, making it a critical subject for elemental assessment and recovery optimization. Its comprehensive processing ensures that gangue materials are minimized while preserving the enriched metallic and rare earth fractions for further evaluation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790983599</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marine Environment">
  <data key="d0">Marine Environment</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The marine environment is an alternative disposal site for untreated phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;The aquatic ecosystem impacted by phosphogypsum dumping, suffering from lowered pH, heavy metal pollution, and disrupted biological growth.&lt;SEP&gt;The Marine Environment is an ecological system impacted by the discharge of phosphogypsum and its toxic contaminants.&lt;SEP&gt;Marine environment refers to the saltwater and tidal conditions used to test the durability of construction composites.&lt;SEP&gt;The marine environment is the testing condition used to evaluate the durability and saltwater intrusion resistance of composites.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792486405</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dehydration">
  <data key="d0">Dehydration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dehydration is a primary physico-chemical and thermal transformation process applied to phosphogypsum and related minerals such as ardealite. It is fundamentally characterized by the elimination of hydration water molecules from the sample, which results in significant mass reduction. This thermal event is a critical component of the material's overall decomposition and manifests through multi-stage mass loss, distinct endothermic peaks, and specific weight loss intervals.

The process occurs across distinct temperature ranges, beginning with a notable second weight loss at 109.65°C and continuing alongside thermal decomposition as temperatures approach 900°C. These sequential thermal events facilitate the structural breakdown of the sample, allowing for the controlled release of components that were previously bound within the mineral matrix.

In the specific context of phosphogypsum, dehydration serves several functional and chemical purposes. The removal of water not only releases rare earth elements that are otherwise locked within the material's structure but also modifies its physical and electrical properties. Specifically, this thermal treatment effectively reduces the setting time of the phosphogypsum and reverses its conductometric curve behavior, making it a vital step in both the chemical processing and practical application of the material.&lt;SEP&gt;A process indicated by the significant drop in water and loss on ignition during calcination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792503863</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="R1">
  <data key="d0">R1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">R1 refers to multiple distinct concepts across different technical and analytical domains. In chemical engineering and phosphate processing, R1 denotes a chemical reaction equation that describes the decomposition of phosphate rock during the wet process. This reaction is specifically involved in the formation and transformation of calcium sulfate (CaSO4) complex salts, particularly during phosphogypsum (PG) impregnation. It also represents the dehydration reaction of dihydrated calcium sulfate into hemihydrate.

In resource management contexts, R1 functions as an indicator designed to measure the consumption of raw and auxiliary materials within the resource dimension.

In emergy analysis and production modeling, R1 represents a renewable emergy input category and flow component that is primarily sourced from the air across various production stages. Within monoammonium phosphate (MAP) production, this emergy flow accounts for 2.79% of the total input. Its proportional share demonstrates scenario-dependent variations, decreasing in Scenario 3 while increasing in Scenario 2, a shift driven by the integration of air and natural gas inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792224994</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EDTA">
  <data key="d0">EDTA</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid, commonly referred to as EDTA, is a carboxylate chemical compound that functions primarily as a pH-dependent chelating agent. Its defining characteristic is the ability to bind metal ions to assess metal complexation, notably forming the [EDTA-Pr]⁻ complex with praseodymium. In analytical chemistry, EDTA is the standard primary titrant for complexometric titrations, utilized to accurately determine metal ion concentrations and metal oxide content. Standardized protocols frequently reference its application, including specific procedures outlined in Section 16, often employing measured volumes ranging from 10.00 mL to 15.00 mL. Additionally, EDTA is routinely analyzed via mass spectrometry alongside calcium sulfate dihydrate to support precise analytical measurements.

Beyond laboratory titrations, EDTA serves critical roles in industrial processing and environmental assessment. It is deployed in both alkaline and acidic treatments to solubilize gypsum compounds and facilitate their elimination, while simultaneously concentrating rare earth elements. In environmental science, EDTA is applied in leaching experiments to evaluate the environmental availability of metals and radionuclides within phosphogypsum-amended soils. Its versatility extends to materials research, where it has been utilized as a comparative agent in hydrothermal deposition studies of HAP on metal substrates. Collectively, these applications establish EDTA as a multifunctional chemical reagent bridging analytical measurement, industrial extraction, and environmental monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;EDTA is a chelating agent mentioned in the context of calcium ion interaction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792261913</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
