<node id="Sodium Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Sodium Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium sulfate (Na2SO4) is a versatile sulfate compound and solid salt that serves as both an intermediate byproduct and a valuable industrial material. It is primarily synthesized from phosphogypsum, including deposits sourced from Morocco, or from gypsum, through aqueous conversion, chloride hydrometallurgy, and various chemical transformations involving sodium-based reagents such as sodium carbonate, sodium hydroxide, or sodium chloride. During these processes, the compound is frequently co-produced alongside calcium silicate and can be isolated via evaporation or specific washing operations, which notably reduce sodium oxide concentration. The recovered sodium sulfate can undergo further reactions to form glaserite. Additionally, it functions as a raw material input in the kiln phosphoric acid process, where it is combined with phosphate ore, and it naturally adheres to the surface of gypsum crystals. In certain industrial contexts, particularly within phosphogypsum generated by the kiln process, it acts as an impurity that can trigger alkali flooding.

Beyond its production and geological interactions, sodium sulfate exhibits distinct physical and chemical behaviors across various applications. In construction and filling materials, it is known to cause microcrack expansion and reduce hydration products within the filling body. Industrially, it is widely utilized in detergent and paper manufacturing. In experimental and chemical processing settings, it has been evaluated as a chemical additive in leaching processes, where it is introduced alongside sulfuric acid into cycled aqueous solutions to mitigate drops in leaching efficiency, although trials showed it did not improve rare earth element extraction. Furthermore, sodium sulfate is incorporated into simulated body fluid preparations as a solid salt component at a concentration of 0.50 mmol L⁻¹, and it is also employed as a chemical solution for curing bricks to test sulfate resistance. Despite specific experimental limitations in certain leaching trials, it remains a recognized and recoverable product from phosphogypsum and gypsum treatment, maintaining established utility across multiple sectors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-012</data>
