<node id="Cerium">
  <data key="d0">Cerium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cerium is a light rare earth element (LREE) and a specific, excessive rare earth element (RRE) according to Seredin’s categorization. Alongside lanthanum and neodymium, it accounts for approximately 80% of the total rare earth content in phosphate rock. Although it does not show enrichment in phosphorites relative to shale, Cerium naturally occurs as an impurity in phosphate rock and exhibits a 56% transfer rate to phosphogypsum (PG) during wet processing. It becomes highly prevalent and dominant in dried PG and its beneficiated concentrates, where it is homogeneously dispersed, forms distinct clusters overlapping with RRE mineral inclusions, and is analytically identified by characteristic Energy Dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy (EDS) peaks at approximately 1.0 keV and 4.8 keV. Geochemically, Cerium concentrations vary regionally, with significantly higher levels in Brazilian PG compared to Florida PG, and a mean concentration of 36 mg/kg recorded in Idaho PG. Throughout mineral and waste processing, its enrichment is systematically measured across various sample types, including WBS and TWBS, and it is actively tracked during leaching to monitor real-time concentration shifts. It is also utilized as a metal element screened and loaded onto PG to form a compound oxygen carrier, while routinely being quantified in analytical data tables.

In recovery and extraction operations, Cerium demonstrates high leaching selectivity of 88.2% when treated with a 1.0 mol/L citric acid solution. Various extraction methods have been employed, including a 36.7% recovery rate for targeted extraction, a 60% extraction rate via two-step bioleaching, and isolation during the solvent extraction phase. It is frequently extracted from nitrate leach solutions or media using ionic liquids, where it forms pentakis anionic complexes and is co-extracted with lanthanum and neodymium. The extraction efficiency of Cerium using ionic liquids has been extensively evaluated under varying stirring rates and ionic liquid concentrations, remaining constant at 97% across pH values ranging from 1 to 6. The presence of ammonium nitrate has also been studied for its impact on extraction efficiency. Following extraction into the ionic liquid phase, Cerium is subsequently stripped using faintly acidified water, with stripping efficiency monitored across varying aqueous-to-organic (A/O) phase ratios. These analytical, geochemical, and processing characteristics establish Cerium as a key indicator and primary target for elemental analysis, recovery, and industrial applications in phosphate-related systems.&lt;SEP&gt;A detected radionuclide in phosphogypsum that can be successfully reduced through citric acid leaching.&lt;SEP&gt;A rare earth element detected in phosphogypsum phases, including 59.5 ppm and 56.9 ppm in fluorite.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerium is a rare earth element occurring mostly in gypsum and fluorite.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerium is a rare earth element whose elemental deportment is depicted in Fig. 5.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerium is a lanthanide element that preferentially retards specific gypsum crystal faces and forms solid solutions during crystallization.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerium is a rare earth element measured in the provided data table with concentrations in ppm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-010</data>
