  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dysprosium">
  <data key="d0">Dysprosium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Dysprosium is a chemical element formally classified as a critical rare earth element, a designation recognized by the European Commission and further categorized by Seredin. This element is notably present in phosphogypsum, where it constitutes a considerable proportion, particularly within dried phosphogypsum samples. In analytical and industrial contexts, the concentration and enrichment levels of Dysprosium are routinely measured across various material types, including WBS and TWBS samples.

The element is frequently targeted in hydrometallurgical recovery processes, specifically within leaching and solvent extraction studies. During these procedures, Dysprosium is selectively isolated during the solvent extraction phase, with its recovery efficiency closely monitored. Experimental data have demonstrated that optimized extraction protocols can achieve a reported recovery rate of 86.1%, highlighting its significant potential for recovery from secondary sources.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-057</data>
