<node id="Silicon">
  <data key="d0">Silicon</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Silicon is a chemical element that exhibits highly variable concentrations and diverse physical distributions across geological, industrial, and materials science contexts. In phosphogypsum, a calcium sulfate dihydrate byproduct, silicon functions as both a trace component and a major chemical species, with measured mass fractions ranging from less than 1% to as high as 31.000% depending on sample preparation, geographic origin, and analytical methodology. Brazilian phosphogypsum consistently demonstrates higher silicon concentrations than Moroccan samples. Within this matrix, silicon is intrinsically linked to silicate mineralogy, commonly associating with quartz, silicates, and feldspars. It typically manifests as distinct, scattered spots or inclusions rather than a continuous matrix, and tends to aggregate at melting interfaces, which can result in relatively low calcium content. Analytically, silicon is routinely quantified using Atomic Absorption Spectroscopy and detected via EDX and EDS techniques, where it characteristically appears around 1.74 keV, often attributed to quartz impurities. It is also identified by spectral peaks at approximately 213, 222, and 223 nm. In processed or leached phosphogypsum samples, silicon remains a major element but exhibits a slight decrease in concentration. Additionally, silicon is recognized as a trace element in other materials, such as the AZ31B alloy.

Beyond its geological and analytical characteristics, silicon plays multifaceted functional roles in industrial and agricultural applications. Industrially, while silicon can act as an impurity that negatively impacts machinery and product purity, it serves a beneficial functional role when phosphogypsum is repurposed as an oxygen carrier, where it actively enhances anti-agglomeration capabilities and sintering resistance. In agricultural settings, silicon is utilized as a fertilizer component specifically studied for its capacity to improve acid paddy soil conditions. Collectively, silicon remains a chemically significant, geographically variable, and functionally relevant element across both industrial and agricultural contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;A minor element detected in the sample.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon increases gypsum crystal growth along the c-axis and prevents aggregate formation by dissolving fluorine.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon is an impurity found in phosphogypsum in association with its primary components.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26bca3d4f742299ce2dbcd6381558f89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-000</data>
