<node id="Wet Processing">
  <data key="d0">Wet Processing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Wet Processing is the industrial technique used to produce phosphoric acid from phosphate rock, generating PG waste as a byproduct.&lt;SEP&gt;Wet processing is the industrial procedure used to treat phosphate rock, which leads to the formation of phosphogypsum waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Wet processing is the industrial method used to produce phosphoric acid from phosphate rock, generating phosphogypsum waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Wet Processing is an industrial technique that causes the selective separation and concentration of naturally occurring radioactive elements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792487352</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphogypsum Waste Samples">
  <data key="d0">Phosphogypsum Waste Samples</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphogypsum waste samples are industrial byproducts analyzed for radiation hazard indices, showing significantly elevated absorbed gamma dose rates compared to international safety standards.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995590</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Rock Samples">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Rock Samples</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate rock samples are raw materials analyzed alongside phosphogypsum waste for uranium isotope ratios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
