<node id="Uranium-235">
  <data key="d0">Uranium-235</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranium-235 is a less prevalent radioactive isotope of uranium that, despite having minor direct importance, plays a vital role in radiometric analysis. Its decay series radiation is generally not considered a major concern. In analytical contexts, Uranium-235 is routinely measured to calculate isotopic ratios with Uranium-238. Because of potential breakdowns in radioactive equilibrium, it is also employed to estimate the activity concentration of Uranium-238. Additionally, the isotope is quantified using its characteristic 143.8 keV gamma line, which serves as a standard energy reference in the dataset. This specific gamma emission facilitates a two-way analytical approach for determining Radium-226 activity concentrations.

Within the provided dataset, the activity concentration of Uranium-235 is actively measured and reported. When examined in phosphogypsum samples, Uranium-235 demonstrates a distinct distribution pattern, exhibiting significantly higher activity concentrations in coarse fractions. These characteristics underscore its utility as a reference radionuclide and its measurable presence across various environmental and industrial matrices.&lt;SEP&gt;Uranium-235 is a naturally occurring uranium radioisotope mentioned in the context of natural uranium composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791915077</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thorium-232">
  <data key="d0">Thorium-232</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Thorium-232 (232Th) is a naturally occurring radioactive isotope and radionuclide that forms the basis of the thorium decay series. As a gamma-emitting nuclide, its activity concentration is typically determined by measuring the gamma emissions of its decay daughters, specifically Lead-212 (Pb-212) and Actinium-228 (Ac-228). For radiological assessment purposes, the gamma radiation dose rate emitted by Thorium-232 is often converted into an equivalent Radium value, and it contributes to the total absorbed dose rate in materials where it is present.

Geologically, Thorium-232 is found in appreciable amounts within igneous phosphates and acts as a minor radionuclide in various environmental and industrial matrices. Its geochemical behavior includes co-precipitation with iron and manganese oxides, as well as the ability to substitute for calcium in apatite. In specific environmental settings, such as Saskatchewan soils, it has been measured with a specific activity of 24 Bq kg⁻¹.

Industrially, Thorium-232 is a notable component of phosphogypsum waste and its derived compounds or reaction products. Its distribution across phosphogypsum fractions has been analyzed in previous studies, and its concentration is routinely monitored. In chemically modified materials, the radioactive equilibrium between Thorium-232 and its daughter isotopes is frequently disrupted. Consequently, the isotope is regularly assessed for its release and mobility, particularly concerning its application and environmental impact in soil amendment practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Thorium-232 is a radioactive isotope whose content in typical PG was measured across multiple countries and regulated by standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive isotope measured in phosphogypsum at 1.0 Bq/kg.&lt;SEP&gt;Thorium-232 is a specific radioactive isotope monitored within the phosphogypsum analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Thorium-232 is a naturally occurring radioactive isotope contributing to the radioactivity of phosphate ores.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792482756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Potassium-40">
  <data key="d0">Potassium-40</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Potassium-40 is a naturally occurring, gamma-emitting radioactive isotope primarily utilized to determine potassium content in various samples through its characteristic 1461 keV gamma radiation emission. As a radionuclide, it is routinely monitored for its activity concentration and spatial distribution across different material fractions. In phosphogypsum waste environments and associated reaction products, Potassium-40 is classified as a minor radionuclide, exhibiting lower activity levels than dominant isotopes such as Radium-226 and Lead-210, with its extraction trend closely following that of Radium-226 but at slightly lower values. Despite its relatively lower abundance, it significantly contributes to the total absorbed dose rate in these materials. To standardize radiological assessments, the gamma radiation dose rate attributed to Potassium-40 is routinely converted into an equivalent Radium value, and its concentration is subject to established regulatory standards, underscoring its importance in environmental monitoring and radiological safety evaluations. Measured activity concentrations in phosphogypsum vary across studies, with reported values including 11 Bq/kg and 72 ± 4.9 Bq/kg.

In phosphogypsum processing, Potassium-40 is a key radionuclide targeted for removal during leaching processes to meet safety standards. Its concentration is quantified in solid residues to evaluate leaching performance, and it demonstrates significant removal rates when subjected to developed leaching and stripping procedures. The removal efficiency of Potassium-40 is highly dependent on agitation time, initially starting with the highest stripping efficiency, peaking at approximately 57.5% after 2 hours of agitation, and subsequently decreasing. The process also shows a plateau effect around 48% stripping efficiency. Specific extraction efficiencies have been recorded at 94.6% and 92.1% for PG-Se and PG-AT samples, respectively. Overall, Potassium-40's behavior in phosphogypsum is closely tracked to optimize radiological safety and processing efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium-40 is a specific radioactive isotope monitored within the phosphogypsum analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium-40 is a naturally occurring radionuclide measured in phosphogypsum samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium-40 (40K) is a radionuclide that makes a minor contribution to the radioactivity of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive isotope present in phosphogypsum that can be removed using organic extractants.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium-40 is a radioactive isotope that can be removed from phosphogypsum using organic extractants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792455914</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radon-222">
  <data key="d0">Radon-222</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radon-222 is a naturally occurring radioactive noble gas and isotope generated through the alpha decay of radium-226. Characterized by high mobility, it readily diffuses and escapes from sealed samples, construction materials, housing panels, and during mechanical operations, a behavior that frequently disrupts radioactive equilibrium with its decay progenies. The isotope’s emanation fraction typically ranges from 18% to 25%, while its exhalation rate notably decreases in coarse phosphogypsum fractions due to reduced surface area. Quantitative assessment of Radon-222 is essential and commonly employs gamma-ray spectrometry, which necessitates establishing equilibrium with its progenies. Researchers often utilize lower activity concentration values derived from its decay products to evaluate diffusion and escape patterns, with measurements frequently recorded in kilobecquerels within inventories and LCI data for phosphogypsum disposal. Specific indoor environmental measurements have documented levels at 371 Bq.

Environmentally, Radon-222 is predominantly associated with phosphogypsum waste and stacks, where it undergoes natural-convective transport and exhalation into the open atmosphere. It also emanates from various building materials and serves as a critical tracer for characterizing the redistribution of Technologically Enhanced Naturally Occurring Radioactive Material (TE-NORM). Research extensively analyzes its exhalation rates and atmospheric concentrations near phosphogypsum stacks, frequently comparing these measurements to natural background levels, as emission rates vary significantly across different sources and environmental conditions. The exhalation and emanation rates from phosphogypsum piles and housing panels are routinely measured in multiple studies to assess radiological hazards.

From a public health and regulatory standpoint, Radon-222 poses documented inhalation risks to humans in both indoor and outdoor environments. Due to its environmental persistence and significant health implications, the atmospheric release of Radon-222 from sources such as gypsum stacks is strictly regulated by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA). This regulatory framework necessitates continuous environmental monitoring to mitigate human exposure, ensure compliance, and accurately evaluate the radiological hazards associated with its widespread presence.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792484906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radon-220">
  <data key="d0">Radon-220</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radon-220 is a short-lived radon gas isotope compared to Radon-222 in terms of half-life.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoron isotope with a short half-life, less likely to escape during processing compared to Radon-222.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-011</data>
