<node id="Equation 2">
  <data key="d0">Equation 2</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation 2 is a contextual label used across multiple scientific and engineering disciplines to denote specific mathematical formulas or chemical reaction steps. Because the designation is locally assigned within different publications, it refers to several distinct entities and processes.

In nuclear waste management and radiation physics, Equation 2 is a mathematical formula used to calculate the total absorbed dose rate values originating from gamma emissions in phosphogypsum waste samples. In materials science and particle characterization, it serves as a mathematical formula to determine the average particle diameter based on specific surface area and material density. Within oxide material research, the same label defines the mathematical calculation for the oxide molar ratio metric, known as M_eta.

In chemical synthesis and environmental remediation, Equation 2 represents different reaction sequences. In one context, it denotes the second step of a proposed two-step reaction sequence involving ammonium hydroxide and sulfuric acid, specifically describing their chemical reaction to form ammonium sulfate and water. In another environmental application, it describes the formation of insoluble calcium phosphate (Ca3(PO4)2), a process highlighted for its excellent phosphorus immobilization properties.

Finally, in ecological engineering and life-cycle assessment, Equation 2 defines the mathematical calculation for the emergy of ecological services required for diluting air emissions. Each usage operates independently within its respective field, sharing only the generic sequential label.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 2 defines the mathematical formula for calculating percent removal of fluoride.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 2 is a mathematical formula used in the Kissinger method to calculate reaction active energy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792364185</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation 3">
  <data key="d0">Equation 3</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation 3 is a mathematical formula used to calculate the radon emanation fraction to quantify radon gas release.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 3 portrays the dissolution step of hydroxyapatite in the dissolution-precipitation mechanism.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 3 represents the overall combined reaction derived from Equation 1 and Equation 2.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 3 is a mathematical formula used to calculate the pH value of the slag.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 3 represents the overall reaction derived from combining Equations 1 and 2.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 3 defines the mathematical calculation for the emergy of ecological services needed for diluting water emissions.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 3 is a mathematical formula used in the Flynn Wall Ozawa method to calculate reaction active energy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792364185</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Half-life">
  <data key="d0">Half-life</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Half-life is the time required for a radioactive isotope to decay to half its initial amount, a key property for evaluating radon gas behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;A temporal property of radioactive isotopes determining decay rates and ingrowth of products.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016</data>
