<node id="Potassium">
  <data key="d0">Potassium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Potassium is a metallic alkali chemical element that serves critical functions across geological, industrial, agricultural, and analytical domains. Naturally occurring as a radionuclide, it is frequently characterized as a radioelement, with its concentration (C_K) commonly applied in equivalent radium calculations. In industrial contexts, particularly within phosphogypsum, potassium acts as a major chemical species and primary component, typically constituting mass fractions between 0.040% and 1.200%. Within this matrix, it forms complexes with phosphates and sulfates and actively participates in the mineralogical formation of cryolite and ralstonite. The precise quantification of potassium in such materials is routinely achieved through gamma spectrometry, SEM-EDS analysis for weight percentage recording, and the classical sodium tetraphenylboron method. Additionally, potassium can be industrially extracted from K-feldspar using thermal decomposition processes that incorporate flue gas desulfurization gypsum and calcium carbonate.

Beyond industrial applications, potassium is a vital agricultural and biological resource. It functions as an essential plant nutrient and a critical soil parameter, frequently utilized as a fertilizer component to improve acid paddy soil. In natural ecosystems, its soil concentration is notably elevated through microbial activity during fermentation and humification processes. Furthermore, plant uptake of potassium is significantly enhanced by synergistic interactions between biofertilizers and microorganisms. Chemically, potassium also serves as a major elemental component of potassium sulphate, comprising 41.71 wt% of its composition. Collectively, these attributes underscore potassium’s multifaceted importance in both natural ecosystems and industrial resource management.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium is a radioactive element contributing to the radioactivity index and is reduced during phosphogypsum treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium is an ion that remains in phosphogypsum after washing procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Potassium is a chemical element used in the calculation of the Radioactivity Index.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-055&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405316</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Industrial By-products">
  <data key="d0">Industrial By-products</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Industrial by-products, such as phosphogypsum, are analyzed using Ra-Eq to measure radionuclide activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1790995715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bismuth-214">
  <data key="d0">Bismuth-214</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Bismuth-214 is a short-lived radioactive isotope and radionuclide that functions as a decay daughter of both Radium-226 and Radon-222 within the uranium decay series. As a key member of this decay chain, it is frequently measured alongside Lead-214 to assess radioactive equilibrium and to determine or estimate the activity concentrations of Radium-226. In scientific datasets and analytical protocols, Bismuth-214 is consistently associated with Radium-226, serving as a reliable indicator for tracking radiometric decay processes.

In practical applications, Bismuth-214 is incorporated into standard reference materials to facilitate detector efficiency determination and is widely utilized in radiometric measurements. Its presence is particularly significant in industrial materials such as phosphogypsum, where it contributes substantially to the sample's overall radioactivity. Consequently, Bismuth-214 is routinely quantified in these matrices to accurately evaluate Radium-226 levels and characterize the radiological properties of environmental and industrial samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791915353</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lead-214">
  <data key="d0">Lead-214</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Lead-214 is a short-lived radioactive isotope and radionuclide that serves as a decay product within the uranium decay series, functioning as a direct descendant of Radon-222 and a downstream daughter of Radium-226. Because of its predictable decay characteristics, it is routinely measured alongside Radium-226 and is extensively utilized in gamma spectrometry and radiometric analysis to determine Radium-226 activity concentrations. Lead-214 is also a standard component in reference materials employed for detector efficiency determination and is commonly applied to assess radioactive equilibrium in environmental and laboratory samples.

When Lead-214 is used to derive Radium-226 activity, measured values may occasionally appear lower than actual concentrations due to the potential escape of Radon-222 gas from the sample matrix. Despite this analytical consideration, Lead-214 remains a critical indicator in radiometric datasets and is frequently quantified in industrial materials such as phosphogypsum, where it substantially contributes to the overall radioactivity. Its consistent presence and measurable correlation with Radium-226 underscore its utility as a reliable proxy for tracking radium-related radiological properties in both research and regulatory contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791915353</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Activated Carbon">
  <data key="d0">Activated Carbon</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Activated Carbon is a porous, carbon-based adsorbent material widely utilized as a conventional filtration and purification medium. It can be derived from various organic precursors, such as Van apple pulp, and is frequently engineered through chemical modifications, including functionalization with oxygen-containing groups to enhance its affinity for specific ions. The material is extensively deployed in environmental remediation and industrial processes, notably for the purification of yellow phosphorous tail gas and the general removal of pollutants from various matrices.

Specific research applications highlight its role in heavy metal adsorption, with studies such as those by Depci et al. demonstrating its efficacy in capturing lead (Pb(II)) and zinc (Zn(II)), as well as its use in competitive heavy metal adsorption and cadmium ion removal. It also serves specialized analytical functions, such as being mixed with PG waste samples to facilitate radon emanation measurements. Despite these versatile applications, Activated Carbon presents notable limitations; it has been investigated for fluoride removal but ultimately deemed unsuitable due to high maintenance costs and suboptimal performance. Furthermore, when benchmarked against advanced alternatives like nanocrystalline hydroxyapatite for heavy metal capture, it is consistently characterized as a traditional adsorbent with inherent constraints relative to newer, more efficient materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Activated carbon is a material combined with phosphogypsum to prepare cementitious materials at optimized operating temperatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Activated Carbon is a conventional adsorbent compared against the synthesized hydroxyapatite for fluoride removal.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792318953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infrared Spectroscopy">
  <data key="d0">Infrared Spectroscopy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Infrared Spectroscopy is an analytical and spectroscopic technique designed to record and interpret the infrared absorption and transmittance spectra of various materials. Its primary function is to identify and characterize chemical bonds, functional groups, chemical species, microstructures, and specific chemical changes within a sample. Methodologically, the technique often involves preparing samples by mixing them with potassium bromide to facilitate accurate spectral acquisition, generating transmittance spectra, and comparing the resulting absorption bands against reference materials.

The method is widely applied across diverse material types to determine chemical composition, structural properties, and crystallinity. Notable applications include the analysis of phosphogypsum and associated calcium sulfate or gypsum compounds, where it is particularly valuable for characterizing water molecules of crystallization and sulphate groupings. In these contexts, it is frequently utilized alongside other analytical techniques. Infrared Spectroscopy is also employed to examine the chemical composition and crystallinity of hydroxyapatite materials, study the structural properties of synthesized products, and analyze specific minerals such as ardealite from particular cave localities. By generating detailed spectral data, the technique provides essential insights into the chemical and structural characteristics of both natural and synthetic substances.&lt;SEP&gt;Infrared spectroscopy is an analytical technique used to evaluate impurity levels in Phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-001</data>
