<node id="Hydroxyl Group">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxyl Group</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Hydroxyl Group is a fundamental chemical functional group characterized by an –OH moiety that significantly influences the structural integrity and chemical behavior of various compounds. Fundamentally, it participates in weak intramolecular hydrogen bonding and serves as an electron donor, thereby shaping the reactivity and properties of the molecules it comprises. In hydroxyapatite (HA or HAp), the hydroxyl group functions as both an integral lattice component and a surface feature. Within the crystal lattice, it is identified by distinct infrared absorption bands, with its stretching vibration specifically appearing at 3576 cm⁻¹ and as a weaker FTIR band at 3540 cm⁻¹. On the hydroxyapatite surface, these groups generate negative surface charges that are essential for initiating the apatite formation mechanism. When present in composite materials like the HAP-AL sample, the hydroxyl groups contribute to a broad spectral range spanning 3000 to 3200 cm⁻¹.

Beyond its role in hydroxyapatite systems, the hydroxyl group acts as a key structural component within the AL molecule, directly contributing to its distinct chemical properties. In industrial and waste contexts, such as PG waste, hydration bonds corresponding to the hydroxyl group are routinely detected and analyzed through infrared spectroscopy. Across all these diverse applications, the hydroxyl group remains a defining functional unit whose vibrational signatures and chemical interactions are consistently utilized to characterize material composition and behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hydroxyl Group is a functional group present in HAp, identified by FTIR spectra.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hydroxyl Group resides within the Hydroxyapatite crystal structure and exhibits specific vibrational and stretching modes.&lt;SEP&gt;The hydroxyl group is a functional moiety present in water molecules and the hydroxyapatite crystal structure.&lt;SEP&gt;The Hydroxyl Group resides within the Hydroxyapatite crystal structure and exhibits specific vibrational and stretching modes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-008</data>
