<node id="Berish">
  <data key="d0">Berish</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Berish is a researcher and author whose work primarily focuses on the environmental and health implications of phosphogypsum. Cited extensively for their analysis of phosphogypsum composition, Berish has identified several key contaminants of concern, including acidic process water, fluoride, radionuclides, and heavy metals. Their research addresses radiation exposure concerns, particularly by summarizing National Emission Standards for Hazardous Air Pollutants (NESHAPS) findings regarding Radon-222 exhalation rates. In assessing the health risks associated with phosphogypsum stacks, Berish noted that irradiation levels decrease exponentially with distance from the source.

Beyond contamination analysis, Berish has contributed to the management and remediation of phosphogypsum storage sites by discussing effective mitigation and closure strategies. This includes practical operational measures, such as utilizing crust formation or moisture to mitigate wind erosion on stacks. Additionally, Berish summarized a groundwater study conducted in Florida that calculated contaminant attenuation distances, further informing risk assessment and environmental monitoring efforts related to phosphogypsum disposal and storage.&lt;SEP&gt;Berish is a researcher who studied the leachability of hazardous elements from phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792485749</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
