<node id="International Atomic Energy Agency">
  <data key="d0">International Atomic Energy Agency</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The International Atomic Energy Agency is an international government organization headquartered in Vienna, Austria. It serves as a central authority for radiometric standards by supplying reference materials with known activity concentrations for detector efficiency and radiometric calibration. The agency also conducts and publishes detailed reports on intercomparison runs specifically designed to measure radionuclides in environmental samples.

In the context of industrial safety and radiation protection, the International Atomic Energy Agency has published comprehensive safety reports addressing the management of NORM residues within the phosphate industry. The agency has been extensively involved in phosphogypsum research, conducting studies on radiation exposure levels associated with its application in construction. Additionally, it is frequently cited as a primary source of empirical data regarding phosphogypsum properties, including its density, moisture content, and radioactivity levels. Through these scientific and regulatory initiatives, the International Atomic Energy Agency continues to advance global standards for radiation safety and environmental monitoring.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792455244</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
