<node id="Brazil">
  <data key="d0">Brazil</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Brazil is a South American nation that functions as a major global hub for the production, research, and utilization of phosphogypsum and related phosphate materials. The country generates approximately 5.4 million tons of phosphogypsum annually, representing roughly 10.62 percent of global production and reserves. Brazilian phosphogypsum, particularly igneous varieties, is characterized by lower concentrations of iron, titanium, and phosphorus alongside elevated magnesium and silicon, and typically exhibits a smaller particle size compared to material from China. The nation actively repurposes a significant portion of this output for agricultural applications and construction, including its use as a setting retarder in Portland cement hydration.

Extensive scientific research has been conducted across Brazil to evaluate the geochemical, radiochemical, and environmental profiles of phosphogypsum. Investigations have systematically analyzed the chemical composition, impurities, and radionuclide activity concentrations of samples sourced from locations such as Imbituba, with notable studies by Costa et al. (2021), Saueia et al. (2013), Geraldo et al., and researchers behind a 2010 characterization study. These efforts have thoroughly mapped radionuclide distribution throughout the material lifecycle and documented samples containing calcium sulphate hemihydrate. Importantly, phosphogypsum in Brazil exhibits significantly lower radioactivity levels that comply with national standards.

Furthermore, Brazil serves as a critical geographic region for evaluating the environmental performance of phosphate fertilizer production routes and conducting microstructural analyses. Consequently, the country stands as a focal point for ongoing geochemical and radiological research, providing essential data on the environmental and chemical availability of phosphogypsum and phosphate fertilizers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-024</data>
