<node id="Radon">
  <data key="d0">Radon</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radon is a radioactive, chemically inert gas that belongs to the uranium decay series and serves as a prominent contributor to natural background radiation. It is primarily generated through the radioactive decay of Radium-226 within soil, from which it naturally emanates into the surrounding environment. Beyond natural soil sources, Radon is also released from Naturally Occurring Radioactive Material (NORM), various building materials, petroleum samples, and notably from phosphogypsum, including its stacks, bricks, and fine particles, which exhibit higher concentrations than larger fractions. The exhalation rate of Radon from phosphogypsum decreases in coarse fractions due to reduced surface area, and its emission from constructed bricks over their lifecycle has been estimated to show no significant change compared to conventional materials. Additionally, Radon can be produced from radium chloride and has been utilized in cancer treatment.

Due to its radioactive emissions and the hazards posed by its decay progenies, Radon presents significant health, environmental, and residential risks, primarily through internal exposure via inhalation, which can lead to excessive radiation exposure. Consequently, it remains a central subject in radiation hazard and exposure assessments. Accurate environmental monitoring and measurement protocols require that Radon establishes equilibrium with its decay progenies over a period of at least seven weeks. Monitoring efforts in environments utilizing phosphogypsum, such as rooms constructed with phosphogypsum bricks, industrial phosphogypsum stacks, and areas near large phosphogypsum storage sites, indicate that Radon concentrations typically align with standard indoor levels and remain significantly below recommended or remedial action limits. Despite these generally acceptable levels, the pervasive presence of Radon across various natural and industrial contexts continues to warrant careful, ongoing monitoring and comprehensive risk evaluation to ensure public and environmental safety.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792485322</data>
