<node id="Rn-222">
  <data key="d0">Rn-222</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-80bac46661a7d25358c897f5feccea99-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d2">Rn-220 is comparable to Rn-222 as a short-lived radon gas.&lt;SEP&gt;A radon gas that may diffuse out of phosphate rock, reducing the activity of subsequent radionuclides in the decay series.&lt;SEP&gt;Radon-222 is a radioactive noble gas produced by the alpha decay of Ra-226, known for its high mobility and environmental significance.&lt;SEP&gt;A radon isotope that escapes from solid phases and decays from Ra-226, with a 3.82-day half-life.&lt;SEP&gt;Rn-222 is the specific radioactive isotope of radon gas that is the primary focus of exposure studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Rn-222 is a gaseous radionuclide produced by the alpha-decay of Ra-226, contributing to atmospheric contamination.</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791428172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rice Husk">
  <data key="d0">Rice Husk</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Rice Husk is an agricultural byproduct mixed with phosphogypsum to produce sustainable construction bricks.&lt;SEP&gt;Rice husk is an agricultural waste material combined with phosphogypsum to develop sustainable bricks.&lt;SEP&gt;Rice husk is utilized alongside phosphogypsum to develop sustainable bricks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-030</data>
