<node id="Ordinary Portland Cement">
  <data key="d0">Ordinary Portland Cement</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Ordinary Portland Cement is a conventional and standard cementitious binder material primarily manufactured from limestone and clay. Chemically, its hydration process generates calcium-silicate-hydrate (C-S-H) gel and facilitates reactions with gypsum to form AFt phases. As a foundational construction material, it serves as the primary binding agent in various formulations, including composite mixtures, mortar, and Cemented Paste Backfill (CPB). The precise proportion of Ordinary Portland Cement in these mixtures directly governs their mechanical performance, allowing engineers to balance structural safety with sustainability goals. Due to its widespread industrial production, the material generates substantial greenhouse gas emissions and a significant carbon footprint, establishing it as a standard baseline against which modified or alternative materials are evaluated, particularly regarding properties like fast setting and high early strength.

To mitigate environmental impacts and manage industrial by-products, Ordinary Portland Cement is frequently blended with or partially to fully replaced by ground granulated blast furnace slag and phosphogypsum. These combinations are actively employed in phosphogypsum remediation processes, with modified phosphogypsum sometimes integrated into construction materials, and raw phosphogypsum can even serve as a manufacturing additive. Additionally, Ordinary Portland Cement functions as an effective cementitious treatment agent when added to leachates, where it rapidly reduces phosphorus concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;Ordinary Portland cement is a standard cement type used in mortar mixes alongside phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792503973</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium-phosphate complexes">
  <data key="d0">Calcium-phosphate complexes</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Compounds formed when dissolved phosphates precipitate after initial hydration, coating cement particles.</data>
