<node id="Workability">
  <data key="d0">Workability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Workability is a fundamental physical property and practical utility parameter of construction materials, encompassing concrete, mortar, and cemented paste backfill (CPB) mixtures. It defines the ease with which a material can be mixed, handled, and placed, and is primarily governed by particle interactions and specific mix proportions. As a critical characteristic in materials science and civil engineering, workability is routinely evaluated in experimental studies, particularly those investigating the performance of modified building matrices.

A consistent observation across multiple investigations is that workability generally declines when phosphogypsum (PG) or Plaster Of Paris (POP) is incorporated into the construction matrix. This reduction is largely driven by the increased specific surface area introduced by these additives, which alters inter-particle dynamics and mixture consistency. As workability diminishes with higher PG or POP concentrations, the material becomes more difficult to process and introduces a heightened risk of deformation during application. Therefore, maintaining optimal workability remains a crucial consideration when formulating construction mixes that utilize phosphogypsum or similar supplementary materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792454775</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
