<node id="Ettringite">
  <data key="d0">Ettringite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ettringite is a crystalline calcium-aluminum double sulfate mineral compound, frequently designated as the AFt structure in mortar. It is a primary hydration product generated through the chemical interaction of calcium and sulfate ions, most notably from the reaction of tricalcium aluminate and gypsum, as well as from calcined phosphogypsum, slag, or fluorgypsum. Its development occurs during early hydration and is highly sensitive to compositional and environmental conditions. The formation is actively stimulated by acidic and alkaline solutions, phosphogypsum, and Plaster of Paris (yielding robust needle-column frameworks at 20% content), whereas decreased aluminum oxide levels inhibit its creation. Additionally, its formation is linked to increased soundness expansion in phosphogypsum-containing matrices, and its thermal endotherm intensity increases at higher curing temperatures.

Morphologically, ettringite typically manifests as needle-like, needle-column, or fibrous crystals. These structures surround phosphogypsum particles, interlace with C-S-H and C-A-S-H gels, and bond to form agglomerates that actively fill pores within cement paste, mortar, and cemented paste backfill systems. This pore-filling action creates a denser microstructure, which significantly improves consolidation, mechanical strength, and chemical resistance. While it generally enhances the strength behavior of solidified materials and accelerates binder hardening, ettringite can limit further cement hydration when present at high concentrations.

Functionally, ettringite governs the hydration kinetics of calcium oxide and fluorgypsum-activated binders, accelerating early strength evolution and enhancing overall durability. Its structural impact is matrix-dependent and can occasionally disrupt geopolymer architectures. Analytically, it is identified through distinct X-ray diffraction patterns, including a characteristic peak near 9° and a star with a dot inside marker, with peak intensities varying according to phosphogypsum concentration, curing duration, and sample ratios. Beyond cementitious applications, ettringite is studied for its functional group chemistry and arsenate coordination, and it facilitates environmental remediation through fluoride immobilization and phosphate removal. Overall, it serves as a critical phase in both sustainable industrial waste utilization and advanced binder performance.&lt;SEP&gt;A hydration product formed during curing that contributes to the strength and durability of the binders.&lt;SEP&gt;Ettringite is a hydration product that forms through chemical reactions and causes material degradation in certain composites.&lt;SEP&gt;Ettringite is a major hydration product formed in early ages, influenced by pH.&lt;SEP&gt;Ettringite is a major hydration product formed in the early stages of supersulphated slag cement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792503863</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fluorite">
  <data key="d0">Fluorite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluorite is a sparingly soluble fluoride mineral that frequently occurs as a minor accessory component within various geological formations, including phosphate rock and general rock strata. Its presence in industrial byproducts such as phosphogypsum has been confirmed through advanced analytical techniques, including scanning electron microscopy and electron probe micro-analysis, which identify it as a distinct mineral phase. Characterized by its low solubility, fluorite typically persists as an insoluble impurity during material processing and represents a compound to which fluoride ions readily precipitate, particularly in cemented paste backfill mixtures.

In environmental and geochemical contexts, fluorite exhibits a dual behavior: it acts as a host phase that restricts the mobility of rare earth elements, yet simultaneously contributes to fluorine leaching within phosphogypsum. From a mineral processing standpoint, fluorite can be effectively isolated from associated minerals like scheelite by utilizing sodium silicate as a depressant in solutions containing calcium and gypsum. Collectively, these characteristics define fluorite as a low-solubility mineral with significant implications for resource extraction, waste management, and geochemical element migration.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorite is a mineral form of fluorine whose proportion decreases during long-term leaching, accompanied by rising fluoride concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorite is a mineral component of the phosphogypsum matrix containing yttrium, lanthane, cerium, cadmium, and uranium.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorite is a solid that may control the solubility potentials of fluorine in phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792407732</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geochemical Modeling">
  <data key="d0">Geochemical Modeling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Geochemical Modeling is a computational tool used to estimate pollutant release and explain PTEs retention mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;A computational technique used to predict mineral solubilities and trace element controls in mining area waters.&lt;SEP&gt;Geochemical modeling is a computational approach used to simulate radionuclide release pathways and elution mechanisms from Phosphogypsum stacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Geochemical modeling is an analytical technique used to simulate radionuclide complexation and mobility in stack fluids.&lt;SEP&gt;Geochemical modeling is a computational approach used to simulate brine remediation processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-015</data>
