<node id="Wastewater">
  <data key="d0">Wastewater</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Wastewater is a complex aqueous byproduct and liquid waste generated in substantial volumes during industrial operations and production facility processes, including leaching, water-washing, MAP production, and phosphoric acid manufacturing. Characteristically, it is often acidic and calcium sulfate-saturated, containing high concentrations of dissolved minerals, heavy metal ions such as lead, and nutrients like total phosphorus. Due to its high volume, acidity, and contaminant load, wastewater poses significant environmental challenges and is typically linked to sewage treatment stations, necessitating pretreatment, secondary treatment, active minimization, and further processing. Depending on the specific industrial framework, it can also be managed through complete recovery systems that achieve 100% reuse.

In terms of remediation and resource management, wastewater serves as a primary medium for discharging total phosphorus, which can be effectively reduced through precipitation processes, PG reuse methods, and broader resource utilization strategies. It is also utilized to remove heavy metals, particularly lead ions, using specialized materials like hydroxyapatite nanoparticles and HAP-BC nanocomposites. Beyond its role as a challenging industrial effluent requiring careful management, wastewater functions as a critical real-world environmental matrix in scientific and environmental research. It is specifically employed to evaluate and validate the removal efficiency of advanced remediation technologies, highlighting its dual significance as both a hazardous discharge requiring treatment and a vital testing medium for developing sustainable extraction and purification solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;A key sector involving human excreta and detergents as inputs into the wastewater treatment system.&lt;SEP&gt;A byproduct of human and industrial activity, tracked for phosphorus removal and recycling rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Wastewater is a sector involving treatment processes, effluent, and biosolids, characterized by significant phosphorus losses and partial recycling.&lt;SEP&gt;Wastewater is a potential source for phosphorus recovery, with feasibility dependent on bioavailability and toxicity.&lt;SEP&gt;A sector managing phosphorus in effluent and biosolids, which are either lost to water, landfill, non-soil, or recycled to agriculture.&lt;SEP&gt;Wastewater is the environmental medium polluted with fluoride that nanosized hydroxyapatite aims to treat.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1b5f95e85d7bfa3b7aab581f3c82b33-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1b5f95e85d7bfa3b7aab581f3c82b33-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1b5f95e85d7bfa3b7aab581f3c82b33-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1b5f95e85d7bfa3b7aab581f3c82b33-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1b5f95e85d7bfa3b7aab581f3c82b33-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-001</data>
