  <data key="d5">1791722976</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcination Temperature">
  <data key="d0">Calcination Temperature</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcination temperature is a critical thermal processing and experimental parameter that significantly influences the structural, chemical, and performance characteristics of various materials across different scientific applications. In the context of phosphogypsum-derived materials, it serves as a key determinant for the properties of modified phosphogypsum, supersulfated cements, and gypsum plaster. Specifically, it positively affects water consumption, compressive strength, and the softening coefficient of modified phosphogypsum, while also governing the preferred orientation growth of calcium sulfate dihydrate. Furthermore, calcination temperature exhibits a negative correlation with dihydrate content and a positive correlation with hemihydrate content, directly impacting the phase composition and overall performance of these gypsum-based products.

Beyond gypsum applications, calcination temperature plays a vital role in the synthesis and optimization of hydroxyapatite catalysts. In biodiesel production, the temperature parameter directly influences catalyst efficiency and overall biodiesel yield. Experimental protocols frequently apply specific thermal regimes to hydroxyapatite powder, such as maintaining conditions at 600 °C and 900 °C for two hours, to achieve desired catalytic properties. Additionally, in broader sample preparation and structural analysis contexts, calcination temperature encompasses various targeted heat treatments, including ranges from 650 °C to 700 °C, as well as discrete treatments at 800 °C and 1000 °C. These varied thermal conditions are systematically employed to analyze sample structures, optimize material synthesis, and tailor chemical compositions for specific industrial and research outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcination Temperature is a process parameter studied for its effect on recycling phosphogypsum into gypsum plaster.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcination temperature is the thermal treatment parameter optimized at 600 °C for PG processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcination temperature is a critical processing parameter that influences the compressive strength enhancement of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcination temperature is a variable that inversely affects the total porosity of PG cement.&lt;SEP&gt;The thermal parameter ranging from 170°C to 950°C that influences the chemical composition of phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792503183</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supersulfated Cements">
  <data key="d0">Supersulfated Cements</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Supersulfated cements are construction materials whose performance is influenced by the calcination temperature of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Supersulfated cements are specialized binding materials whose performance is influenced by phosphogypsum calcination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-031</data>
