<node id="Secondary Pollution">
  <data key="d0">Secondary Pollution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Secondary Pollution refers to an unintended environmental contamination risk that arises during the treatment, recycling, and valorization of phosphogypsum. This phenomenon manifests as a potential negative environmental outcome through several distinct mechanisms, including the leaching of impurities, chemical transformations of the material, and the introduction of added substances during processing. Specific industrial procedures, such as the wet sieve cyclone treatment method, are particularly associated with generating these contaminants, necessitating careful environmental assessment whenever phosphogypsum is reused or repurposed.

The occurrence of Secondary Pollution poses a significant barrier to the industrial advancement of phosphogypsum management. It represents a critical challenge that has historically prevented the establishment of large-scale, high-value-added resource utilization routes for this byproduct. However, specific mitigation strategies exist to address this risk; for instance, the high-temperature calcination method has been identified as an effective approach to avoid the contamination typically associated with added substances. Consequently, managing Secondary Pollution remains a focal point in developing sustainable and economically viable phosphogypsum recycling frameworks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-008</data>
