<node id="United States">
  <data key="d0">United States</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The United States is a developed nation and a major global hub for the production, consumption, and export of phosphate rock. It functions as a primary international destination for Australian food and agricultural exports and was featured in a 2019 comparative policy evaluation of NEV development. As the second-largest annual producer of phosphogypsum, generating approximately 30.0 megatonnes yearly, the country predominantly manages this industrial byproduct through large-scale stockpiling, with only limited agricultural application. This disposal approach is driven by regulatory restrictions and anticipated future demand, alongside a 1990 prohibition on incorporating phosphogypsum into building materials due to impurity concerns.

The phosphogypsum produced in the United States is scientifically notable for its elevated radionuclide levels, a characteristic extensively documented in peer-reviewed literature, including studies by Luther et al. (1993) and Rutherford et al. (1995). These radioactivity constraints are particularly pronounced in Florida. To mitigate potential health risks, the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency classifies phosphogypsum as valueless waste but has initiated research into its potential utilization, building upon early Japanese studies. Concurrently, the country enforces a drinking water standard that sets a combined concentration limit of 0.19 Bq/L for radium-226 and radium-228. Historically, the United States also practiced industrial uranium recovery from wet-process phosphoric acid during the 1980s. Furthermore, to address supply chain vulnerabilities, the nation has formally designated rare earth elements as strategic resources. These regulatory, scientific, and industrial measures collectively define the United States' comprehensive management of its phosphate sector and related resource challenges.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1b5f95e85d7bfa3b7aab581f3c82b33-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1b5f95e85d7bfa3b7aab581f3c82b33-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-008</data>
