<node id="Hydrogen Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Hydrogen Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrogen fluoride (HF) is a toxic, gaseous chemical compound that primarily functions as a hazardous byproduct of industrial phosphate processing and phosphoric acid production. It is initially released during the acidulation and digestion of fluorine-containing phosphate rocks, such as fluorapatite, typically through reactions with sulfuric acid. The compound also emerges during the high-temperature decomposition of fluorine-containing acids and is frequently emitted throughout the wet process reaction and subsequent chemical synthesis stages. In industrial settings, hydrogen fluoride is often managed as a trace contaminant that is eliminated via volatilization during the calcination process.

Physically, hydrogen fluoride typically manifests in the gas phase at temperatures reaching approximately 600 °C, with its gaseous concentration increasing as temperatures rise. In molecular diagrams, it is conventionally depicted as a light blue sphere bonded to a grey or white sphere. Beyond its initial generation during acidulation and digestion, the compound continues to be released as a volatile gas from phosphogypsum, a major co-product formed alongside phosphoric acid. Consequently, hydrogen fluoride is frequently quantified as an air emission from phosphogypsum stacks and disposal sites, with its release often measured in grams within life cycle inventory data.

Due to its persistent release during phosphate manufacturing and from phosphogypsum storage facilities, hydrogen fluoride poses significant environmental and public health risks. As a highly toxic and hazardous gas, its emissions contribute to local air pollution and broader ecological contamination. Human and ecological exposure is known to cause cellular damage, firmly establishing hydrogen fluoride as a dangerous industrial emission that necessitates rigorous monitoring, precise quantification, and careful management to mitigate its biological and environmental hazards.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
