<node id="Wet Sieving">
  <data key="d0">Wet Sieving</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Wet sieving is a physical and gravity-based separation technique designed to classify and concentrate materials by passing them through fine mesh or sieves. The process primarily separates particles based on differences in size and density, effectively dividing input material into distinct fine and coarse fractions. By leveraging fluid dynamics and mechanical filtration, the method isolates specific particle ranges while washing away finer contaminants or lighter components.

In practical applications, wet sieving serves as a highly effective concentration method for rare earth elements, successfully enriching their content from 309 to 1071 ppm within the recovered fine particle fraction. The technique is also extensively utilized in industrial waste management as a pre-treatment and purification step for phosphogypsum. By filtering raw phosphogypsum through sieves, wet sieving removes impurities and separates particle sizes, rendering the material harmless and suitable for secondary applications. Overall, wet sieving functions as a versatile physical treatment method that enhances material purity and recovery rates across both valuable mineral extraction and industrial byproduct processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-008</data>
