<node id="Leaching Efficiency">
  <data key="d0">Leaching Efficiency</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Leaching Efficiency is a quantitative performance metric used to evaluate the effectiveness of acid extraction processes applied to raw phosphogypsum. It calculates the proportion or percentage of rare earth elements successfully transferred from the solid phosphogypsum matrix into the leaching solution. This metric serves as a critical indicator of extraction success under varying experimental and specific operational conditions, providing a standardized way to assess how well acid treatments recover valuable materials from industrial byproducts.

The metric is typically measured during individual leaching cycles to track the recovery rate of rare earth elements throughout the process. A defining characteristic of Leaching Efficiency is its tendency to decrease significantly as the number of leaching cycles increases, a decline primarily attributed to the saturation of gypsum within the system. By quantifying the yield of rare earth elements across different treatment parameters, Leaching Efficiency provides essential insights into the optimization and sustainability of phosphogypsum processing for rare earth element recovery.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791427876</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
