<node id="Strontium">
  <data key="d0">Strontium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Strontium is an alkaline earth chemical element that occurs naturally as a trace component and metallic impurity, primarily originating from phosphate rocks. It exhibits a heterogeneous distribution within phosphorite deposits and phosphogypsum by-products, where it is notably enriched and typically concentrated in the fine fraction. Its concentration in phosphogypsum is routinely measured in milligrams per kilogram or parts per million, with typical mass contents ranging from 47.00 to 469.71 μg/g (approximately 0.49 to 0.54 wt%), and specific measurements recorded at 709 ± 115. The element is routinely characterized using Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass-Spectrometry and spectroscopically identified by a small peak around 1.5 keV. Due to its ionic radius similarity to calcium, strontium readily substitutes for calcium within the apatite crystal structure in phosphorite and into the calcium sulfate dihydrate lattice in phosphogypsum, directly linking it to the gypsum matrix. It is also found in fluorite and quartz phases and is categorized as having intermediate mobility in phosphogypsum, with a release rate that decreases over leaching time. Despite its significant concentration, strontium is highly retained within the phosphogypsum matrix and exhibits poor solubility, requiring strong chemical dissolution for release. It can be leached by citric acid, though its relative proportion increases when other reagents are utilized. In phosphogypsum leach liquor, it acts as an interfering metal ion, often recorded at concentrations such as 17 mg/L, and is present as a Sr2+ ion in synthetic solutions used for extraction experiments.

Geochemically, strontium shares properties with radium and barium, tending to co-precipitate and co-crystallize with them, particularly as sulfate compounds and solid solutions, which is critical for understanding radium mobility in industrial waste systems. While the element itself is stable, its radioactive isotopes are recognized as radionuclides that can be efficiently removed from contaminated environments by hydroxyapatite. Conversely, when strontium acts as a naturally prevalent ionic substitution in hydroxyapatite, it can reduce the material's stability by increasing its solubility, a characteristic typically lacking in synthetic hydroxyapatite. Strontium-substituted hydroxyapatite is also utilized to remove divalent strontium cations from aqueous solutions. The heterogeneous distribution and concentration of strontium in phosphogypsum are analyzed to differentiate between s-PGs and m-PGs. The environmental behavior, mobility, and leaching characteristics of strontium under simulated acid rain conditions are actively studied, particularly regarding the impact of phosphogypsum leachates on soil systems. Overall, strontium's geochemical mobility, substitution patterns, and interaction with mineral matrices make it a significant focus in environmental and industrial waste management research.&lt;SEP&gt;Strontium is a trace metal found in Phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A water-soluble metal released from phosphogypsum into the estuary, estimated at 54,219 tons.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element present in phosphogypsum, often studied alongside radionuclides for environmental impact assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Strontium is a chemical element whose concentration in phosphogypsum was measured, showing high variability between Idaho and Tunisian samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-03