<node id="Drying Process">
  <data key="d0">Drying Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Drying Process involves heating dried PG at 70 °C for over 24 hours to determine free water content.&lt;SEP&gt;A thermal process stage in PSC technology that accounts for nearly 40% of coal use, particularly for high-moisture raw materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Drying process involves heating PG at specific temperatures, which influences its effect on compressive strength.&lt;SEP&gt;A processing step that breaks down larger crystals into smaller, jagged fragments.&lt;SEP&gt;The Drying Process is a treatment method applied to wet Phosphogypsum sludge to remove moisture, resulting in physical cracking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791734744</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Table 3">
  <data key="d0">Table 3</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 3 functions as a generic data presentation label utilized across numerous independent scientific studies, with its specific content and purpose varying significantly depending on the research context. Consequently, the compiled descriptions refer to multiple distinct instances of Table 3 from different publications, which can be comprehensively synthesized by research domain.

In phosphogypsum and mineral processing research, Table 3 documents major element mass fractions, operating conditions, and rare earth element extraction efficiencies and contents in dihydrate phosphogypsum. It presents radionuclide activity concentrations, including Radium-226 deviations and isotope enrichment factors benchmarked against terrestrial materials, alongside P2O5 and fluorine content in samples from China and other nations. The table compiles global phosphogypsum generation, disposal, and utilization data, outlines sector-specific benefits and environmental risks, records transformation temperatures and impurity forms, details compositional changes before and after treatment with various leaching reagents, and lists national policies on phosphogypsum utilization since 2018. Additionally, it provides Life Cycle Inventory data for phosphogypsum as a soil amendment, lists chemical species examined via LA-ICP-MS, presents trace element composition data of phosphogypsum minerals, compares trace element concentrations in phosphogypsum and its derived products to typical soil, and lists measured utility values and coefficients for individual indicators.

In construction and materials science, Table 3 summarizes design criteria and quality monitoring parameters for mixes, alongside experimental results from compaction and Atterberg limit tests for cement and cement-phosphogypsum stabilized soils. It details mortar setting times under varying phosphogypsum replacement levels and substitution materials, and provides comprehensive data on composite cement formulations treated with sodium chloride, including average grain size, mechanical properties, water absorption, microstructural characteristics, and performance across different concentrations. Quantitative results from shrinkage, water softening, and dry unit weight tests are also recorded, specifically highlighting 28-day drying shrinkage outcomes. Furthermore, the table presents TGA results, including decomposition times and mass loss percentages for different phosphogypsum to coal mass ratios.

Beyond materials and chemical analysis, Table 3 serves as a reference for spectroscopic, biological, and environmental data. It lists distinct infrared vibrations for PVA-peeled and AL-functionalized hydroxyapatite, records Ca/P molar ratios determined by ICP-OES for hydroxyapatite Types 1 through 6, presents optical band gap values for synthesized samples, and details the resulting volume fractions of hydroxyapatite samples calculated by integrating across size regions. In agricultural and botanical studies, it documents chlorophyll and vitamin C content across plant specimens subjected to various treatments. Across environmental and soil chemistry contexts, the table presents total and water-soluble lead content in soil across different phosphogypsum doses, explicitly comparing water-soluble lead content to total lead content.

In broader policy and modeling applications, Table 3 outlines sustainable phosphorus measures and their alignment with goals for phosphorus security in Australia. It also presents calculated isotherm parameters and normalized standard deviations for different models at varying temperatures. Across all disciplines, the table functions as a standardized reference for empirical data, literature comparisons, and experimental outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 3 presents the chemical analysis percentages of PG oxides before and after calcination at different temperatures.</data>
