<node id="Radionuclides">
  <data key="d0">Radionuclides</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radionuclides are radioactive isotopes and elements, including radium, thorium, uranium, and members of the uranium-238 decay series, that occur naturally or are generated through industrial processes. Formally classified as a distinct regulatory category of substances, they are characterized by heterogeneous distribution and complex migration pathways. They frequently concentrate in phosphate-derived materials such as phosphate rocks, fertilizers, and industrial byproducts like phosphogypsum, where they constitute approximately 0.522 wt% of the raw material and function as radioactive impurities with solubility properties distinct from natural gypsum. Notably, higher concentrations are documented in materials sourced from Morocco, the United States, and Spain. Beyond phosphogypsum, radionuclides are commonly found in agricultural soils, plants, industrial wastes, and marine sediments, often co-occurring with heavy metals and fluorine.

The environmental behavior of radionuclides involves leaching from industrial stacks, permeating water bodies, and transporting via nanoparticles, which facilitates accumulation in contaminated soils and agrolandscapes. This mobility enables uptake by crops and subsequent transfer through the food chain, posing significant ecological and public health risks to humans and wildlife. Consequently, their presence in construction materials, including phosphogypsum mortars, is strictly regulated under safety standards such as GB 6566, which establish permissible exposure limits. Continuous environmental monitoring is essential, as analytical detection challenges persist; minimum detectable activity values sometimes fall below actual concentrations, underscoring the need for refined quantification techniques and strict quality control.

In industrial and remediation contexts, radionuclides behave as chemical phases under both acidic and basic conditions, with their concentrations and relative proportions significantly affected by various chemical treatments. For instance, their relative proportion increases in oxalic acid, sodium carbonate, and sodium bicarbonate solutions, with the latter containing approximately 2.037% radioactive substances. While leaching and chemical extraction methods are actively employed to target their removal during phosphogypsum treatment, radionuclides often persist as contaminants in treated products. Scientific research extensively investigates their partitioning and migration, such as their movement toward calcium carbonate in the Merseburg ammonocarbonation process, as well as their targeted adsorption and removal from decommissioned nuclear power plant sites. Remediation strategies frequently utilize materials like hydroxyapatite for stable sequestration, reflecting ongoing efforts to mitigate their environmental and health impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Radionuclides are radioactive isotopes whose distribution and partitioning in phosphogypsum are studied.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive isotopes contained in phosphogypsum that pose environmental and health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive isotopes found heterogeneously distributed in phosphogypsum, posing potential radiological risks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-76e07d75a9f5124d07b9dafa2dacfba7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-053&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-050&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-01