<node id="Radium">
  <data key="d0">Radium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radium is a naturally occurring radioactive chemical element and radionuclide, with the isotope 226Ra being particularly notable. It is found in nature alongside uranium and thorium within phosphate rock, phosphate materials, and uranium mine wastes. Due to its large ionic radius, radium cannot integrate into the apatite crystal lattice of phosphorite deposits; instead, it co-precipitates or co-crystallizes with barium and strontium.

During industrial phosphate processing, radium becomes heavily concentrated in phosphogypsum, a major byproduct and waste stream. Approximately 80% of the radium migrates into the processing stream, with roughly 89% ultimately retained within the phosphogypsum crystal structure. Within this matrix, radium preferentially incorporates into sulfate minerals such as gypsum, accumulates in smaller grain-size fractions, and exhibits a slight preference for retention over lead. It also demonstrates lower extraction efficiency compared to barium, a high transfer factor into calcite, and has measurable stripping and extraction efficiencies in processing contexts.

Despite being largely trapped in the phosphogypsum matrix, radium retains the potential to leach into surrounding soil systems and leachates, creating documented risks of water contamination and ecological harm. Consequently, phosphogypsum stockpiles containing radium require careful monitoring and effective removal processes to mitigate environmental impacts. Historically, radium was utilized in paints and medical applications. In modern industrial contexts, it serves as a key radioactive element used in calculating the Radioactivity Index for phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-001</data>
