<node id="Figure 8">
  <data key="d0">Figure 8</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 8 functions as a descriptive label across multiple distinct scientific contexts, each presenting specialized experimental data and visualizations. In the context of phosphogypsum processing and leaching, Figure 8 illustrates the elemental mapping images of phosphogypsum after the complete dissolution of its gypsum matrix using hydrochloric acid. It also depicts the liquid and solid fractionation of chemical species resulting from phosphogypsum leaching. Additionally, this figure presents experimental data on the decomposition rates and X-ray diffraction patterns of both phosphogypsum and PGI-1.4 under varying conditions and at different temperatures.

When applied to hydroxyapatite characterization, Figure 8 displays a dark-field transmission electron microscopy image alongside a particle size distribution histogram for Type 3 hydroxyapatite. This visualization effectively highlights the diverse morphological shapes and size variations inherent to the Type 3 hydroxyapatite particles.

In spectroscopic and adsorption studies, Figure 8 presents the Raman spectra of synthesized powders across specific wavenumber ranges to analyze molecular structures. It also provides a graphical representation of fluoride adsorption isotherms measured at various experimental temperatures, detailing the equilibrium adsorption behavior under different thermal conditions. Collectively, these varied applications demonstrate that Figure 8 is utilized across different research domains to convey analytical results ranging from elemental distribution and thermal decomposition to particle morphology, vibrational spectroscopy, and adsorption thermodynamics.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure showing TGA and DTG curves of phosphogypsum decomposition with coal.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792364653</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 9">
  <data key="d0">Figure 9</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 9 is a scientific illustration label that appears across multiple distinct research contexts, each presenting different analytical data and findings. In the context of phosphogypsum processing, Figure 9 outlines strategies for recovering rare earth elements based on their occurrence states and provides detailed information regarding the distribution of trace and radioactive elements within phosphogypsum. 

Regarding hydroxyapatite characterization, Figure 9 presents Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy spectra for analyzing functional groups, displays a dark-field transmission electron microscopy image alongside a particle size distribution histogram for HAp Type 4, and includes TEM characterizations that confirm the influence of the Brij-93 surfactant on HAp nanorod crystal size. Additionally, an X-ray diffraction pattern within this context demonstrates that hydroxyapatite remains the primary phase following adsorption processes. 

In materials science and metallurgy applications, Figure 9 displays X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy analysis of PGI-1.4 reduced at 1450 °C, specifically highlighting the binding energies associated with different element valence states in slag samples. Collectively, these varied representations of Figure 9 serve as critical visual data supports across environmental recovery, nanomaterial synthesis, and industrial waste characterization studies.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure presenting plots for the Kissinger method and FWO method.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 9 is a series of X-ray diffraction patterns documenting the calcination process of brushite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405113</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 10">
  <data key="d0">Figure 10</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 10 is a generic figure identifier that appears across multiple distinct scientific studies, each utilizing the label to present independent experimental data and analytical results. In the context of phosphogypsum and cement research, Figure 10 illustrates the leaching efficiency of rare earth elements extracted from phosphogypsum over four cycles using hydrochloric acid. It also depicts the effects of varying phosphogypsum and natural gypsum contents on cement setting time and compressive strength, alongside a plot recording the continuous change in solution pH over contact time.

In materials characterization, Figure 10 presents a dark field transmission electron microscopy image accompanied by a particle size distribution histogram for HAp Type 5. Within metallurgical and slag analysis, Figure 10 provides scanning electron microscopy with energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy analysis results of a PGI-1.4 sample reduced at 1250 °C, further detailing the microstructure and chemical composition distribution along the longitudinal section of the resulting slag.

Additionally, in phase analysis research, Figure 10 functions as a phase diagram identifying the phases present in a calcium sulfate system heated to 150°C, with phase determination conducted via X-ray diffraction. These separate applications demonstrate how the identifier Figure 10 is independently applied across different publications to visualize chemical leaching, cement properties, pH dynamics, nanoparticle morphology, slag microstructure, and high-temperature phase behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405113</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 11">
  <data key="d0">Figure 11</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 11 demonstrates the effect of sulfuric acid concentration on the leaching efficiency of rare earth elements from phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A graphical representation showing nitrous oxide conversion rates for various RhO/M-P-O catalysts.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure illustrating the mechanism of phosphogypsum acting as a soil conditioner in saline-alkali land.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 11 displays the surface morphology of the five synthesized HAp sample variants.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 11 presents a dark field TEM image and a particle size distribution histogram for HAp Type 6.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 11 presents SEM-EDS analysis of PGI-1.4 samples with a C/S molar ratio of 0.5 reduced at 1350 and 1450 °C.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 11 is a series of X-ray diffraction patterns documenting the calcination process of ardealite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405113</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 12">
  <data key="d0">Figure 12</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 12 documents the recrystallization phenomenon observed during the partial acid cycle leaching process.&lt;SEP&gt;A graphical representation showing the percentage of initial catalytic activity of ruthenium catalysts after reuse.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure illustrating the mechanism of phosphogypsum acting as an acid soil conditioner.&lt;SEP&gt;A graphical representation illustrating the decreasing order of total water content across different hydroxyapatite types.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 12 presents SEM-EDS analysis of the PGI-1.4 sample with a C/S molar ratio of 2.0 reduced at 1450 °C.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 12 is an infrared absorption spectrum illustrating changes as a function of calcium hydrogen phosphate and calcium sulfate weight contents.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-016</data>
