</node>
<node id="Hydroxyapatite (HAP)">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxyapatite (HAP)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydroxyapatite is a calcium phosphate-based biomaterial and major component of bone inorganic matrix, widely used for bone augmentation and tissue engineering.&lt;SEP&gt;A base material component of the composite bone cement.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydroxyapatite is a calcium phosphate biomaterial studied for its biocompatibility and surface modification potential.&lt;SEP&gt;A calcium phosphate material synthesized on FTO glass via hydrothermal methods.&lt;SEP&gt;An inorganic compound serving as the base material for hybrid nano-adsorbents, often modified with organo-phosphonates.&lt;SEP&gt;A crystalline material whose diffraction peaks decrease and eventually disappear upon HPA incorporation, transforming into an amorphous state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791654123</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Porosity">
  <data key="d0">Porosity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Porosity is a fundamental physical and structural property that quantifies the percentage or volume fraction of void space within a material. In materials science and powder evaluation, it is routinely assessed alongside surface area and analyzed using gas adsorption-desorption isotherms. This property serves as a critical determinant of a material's physical profile and functional behavior across diverse applications.

In cement and composite bone cements, porosity represents the proportion of empty spaces within the hardened matrix and is directly modulated by additives such as sodium chloride. Increasing sodium chloride concentration elevates porosity, which reduces overall density and exerts an inverse, exponential effect on bending strength. Despite this mechanical trade-off, the enhanced void structure is functionally advantageous in biomedical contexts, as it facilitates apatite crystal precipitation, thereby promoting effective bone integration. Similarly, in cemented paste fill materials and filling bodies, porosity acts as a structural feature that increases with fluoride content and inversely correlates with mechanical strength, while conversely decreasing with higher calcium hydroxide content, which also influences overall structural integrity.

In the context of hydroxyapatite (HA) and hydroxyapatite phosphate (HAP) crystals, porosity is recognized as a key structural property and feature intrinsically linked to their three-dimensional architecture. Furthermore, in soil science, porosity is defined as the ratio of void volume to total soil volume and exhibits dynamic responses to agricultural amendments. Specifically, the application of biofertilizers significantly enhances soil porosity through calcium-induced flocculation processes, which reorganize soil particles into stable aggregates. This structural improvement yields a more open matrix that enhances overall soil structure and aeration, demonstrating how porosity universally governs material performance and environmental interaction.&lt;SEP&gt;Porosity is a physical property of raw phosphogypsum that decreases with increased compaction energy, leading to higher density.&lt;SEP&gt;Porosity refers to the voids or gaps between particles in the phosphogypsum sample, indicating a porous structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Porosity is a physical property of the hardened body that affects PG strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792301341</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Water Absorption">
  <data key="d0">Water Absorption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Water absorption is a fundamental physical and functional property that describes how materials interact with and take in aqueous media, with its specific behaviors and influencing factors varying across distinct material systems. In composite bone cement, this property is analyzed to evaluate the material's interaction with aqueous environments. It functions as a key performance indicator that notably increases when the cement is exposed to higher concentrations of sodium chloride.

In construction applications, particularly within phosphogypsum-based systems, water absorption operates as a critical physical property and process. In phosphogypsum cement matrices (PGCM), it involves aggregates and quicklime absorbing water, which subsequently triggers volume changes. The property is heavily governed by the type and percentage of phosphogypsum incorporated. Generally, water absorption in bricks and phosphogypsum matrices declines as phosphogypsum content rises, whether in calcined form or when it substitutes natural sand. Additionally, increasing superplasticizer content in these phosphogypsum matrices further reduces water absorption. However, the effect of phosphogypsum is formulation-dependent: while adding phosphogypsum alone reduces water absorption in certain brick formulations, combining phosphogypsum with fly ash reverses this trend, causing absorption to increase. Ultimately, water absorption remains a defining matrix characteristic that dictates the dimensional stability, durability, and aqueous compatibility of both biomedical cements and phosphogypsum-derived construction materials.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical property measured to assess the durability and quality of the flooring tiles.&lt;SEP&gt;Water absorption is a material property measured to assess durability, with raw phosphogypsum showing higher values than calcined variants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792485322</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
