<node id="Setting Reaction">
  <data key="d0">Setting Reaction</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The setting reaction is a multi-step chemical process involving dissolution, saturation, precipitation, and crystal growth.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061208</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biological Compatibility">
  <data key="d0">Biological Compatibility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Biological compatibility is a property enhanced by gypsum and brushite cements, improving interaction with living tissues.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061208</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrogen Phosphate Ion">
  <data key="d0">Hydrogen Phosphate Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrogen phosphate ion is a chemical species that contributes to the formation of brushite as a by-product.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrogen Phosphate Ion is an anion that substitutes for phosphate ions in the apatite structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrogen phosphate ion is an anion that substitutes sulfate ions in the gypsum crystal lattice.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrogen phosphate ion is an ion syncrystallized in the solid solution, associated with specific absorption bands and peak heights.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792406487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Secondary Nucleation">
  <data key="d0">Secondary Nucleation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Secondary nucleation is a crystal growth mechanism where nuclei develop into larger crystals during cement hardening.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061208</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supersaturation">
  <data key="d0">Supersaturation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supersaturation is a chemical condition achieved in the solution that triggers the precipitation of calcium sulfate dihydrate.&lt;SEP&gt;Supersaturation is a condition in hydrothermal systems that influences the solubility and crystal growth of phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720679</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aqua Solution">
  <data key="d0">Aqua Solution</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Aqua solution is the aqueous medium surrounding cement particles where ion saturation and dissolution occur.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061208</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Previous Works">
  <data key="d0">Previous Works</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Previous works are cited literature that validate the observed biological compatibility and binding properties of the cement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061208</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equation 6">
  <data key="d0">Equation 6</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation 6 represents the chemical reaction transforming calcium sulfate hemihydrate and water into calcium sulfate dihydrate.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 6 is a mathematical formula used to calculate the purity of calcium carbonate based on weight loss and molecular weights.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 6 calculates annual gross emergy value per total population (τ_H) using REM, GDP, and population data.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 6 is a mathematical formula calculating the normalized standard deviation for model fitting.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 6 is a mathematical formula representing lead equilibrium content and its associates in soil solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation 6 is the mathematical representation defining the apparent solubility product of lead.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-25198f8665e133385fd54e5a03e28a44-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792343567</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Initial Setting Time">
  <data key="d0">Initial Setting Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The duration required for the cement to begin hardening, measured in minutes.&lt;SEP&gt;Initial setting time is a metric measuring the percentage improvement in mortar setting times corresponding to Phosphogypsum replacement levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Initial setting time is the first phase of gypsum hardening measured in minutes, analyzed against varying gypsum content percentages.&lt;SEP&gt;A measured parameter tracking the time required for gypsum to begin setting.&lt;SEP&gt;A property of cement and concrete mixtures that is prolonged with increasing PG content.&lt;SEP&gt;Initial setting time is a cement property measured to evaluate the effectiveness of chemical treatments on PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Initial setting time is a measured parameter that ranges from 100 to 560 minutes depending on PG replacement levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792503863</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Final Setting Time">
  <data key="d0">Final Setting Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The duration required for the cement to fully harden, measured in minutes.&lt;SEP&gt;Final setting time is a metric measuring the percentage improvement in mortar setting times corresponding to Phosphogypsum replacement levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Final setting time is the second phase of gypsum hardening measured in minutes, compared between natural and phosphogypsum sources.&lt;SEP&gt;A measured parameter tracking the time required for gypsum to complete setting.&lt;SEP&gt;A property of cement and concrete mixtures that is prolonged with increasing PG content.&lt;SEP&gt;Final setting time is a cement property measured to evaluate the effectiveness of chemical treatments on PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Final setting time is a measured parameter that exceeds 1440 minutes at 100% PG replacement of OPC.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792503863</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Khairoun et al.">
  <data key="d0">Khairoun et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Researchers who established clinical standards for cement setting times in medical applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
