<node id="Calcium Sulfide">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Sulfide</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium Sulfide ($CaS$) is a chemical compound that primarily functions as a key intermediate and solid product formed during the thermal, sulfur, carbon, and carbon monoxide reduction processes of calcium sulfate and phosphogypsum. Its generation typically occurs under reducing or oxidizing atmospheres, often at temperatures below 1000°C, though it also manifests as a distinct intermediate phase at 1100°C and 1200°C before disappearing at 1300°C. The compound's formation can be facilitated by an iron catalyst and involves partial reduction reactions, including redox interactions with calcium sulfate. Structurally, Calcium Sulfide possesses a defined crystal lattice arrangement, frequently illustrated in oxidation process diagrams as a network of yellow and green spheres. During processing, it can yield sulfide residues in slag and react with iron(II) oxide and silicon dioxide to produce iron(II) sulfide. Additionally, the hydrolysis of phosphogypsum decomposition slag containing this compound produces an alkaline solution rich in free calcium ions.

Beyond its role in industrial reduction and waste valorization, Calcium Sulfide is investigated for surface chemical reactions under electron beam irradiation. Its practical applications are diverse, serving as a primary structural component in composite bone cement matrices, a functional material in carbon capture technologies, and a crucial precursor for synthesizing calcium carbonate and hydrogen sulfide. Through these multifaceted roles, Calcium Sulfide effectively bridges environmental remediation, advanced material synthesis, and industrial chemical processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Sulfide is an intermediate product formed during the decomposition of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Sulfide is a product formed in the reduction reactions of calcium sulfate.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Sulfide is an intermediate production that reacts with calcium sulfate in Reaction III.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Sulfide is a chemical compound identified in the XRD pattern of the sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792362329</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Amorphous Calcium Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Amorphous Calcium Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Amorphous Calcium Phosphate is a transient, amorphous precursor phase that serves as a critical intermediate in the precipitation and transformation into crystalline hydroxyapatite and bone-like apatite. It typically emerges during the initial stages of apatite formation, often under conditions of high supersaturation or low temperature, and can function as either a necessary biological intermediate or an undesirable byproduct in controlled synthesis. In solution-mediated and hydrothermal processes, the compound frequently manifests as initial smooth microspheres that gradually evolve into hydroxyapatite, with the primary structural transition typically occurring around the 0.5-hour mark of hydrothermal synthesis.

Beyond its fundamental role in mineralization, Amorphous Calcium Phosphate has been engineered as an inorganic-organic hybrid composite, notably synthesized from phosphogypsum waste to effectively remove heavy metal ions from aqueous environments. Due to its dynamic phase transformation and versatile material properties, the compound is extensively characterized using a variety of analytical methods to monitor its structural evolution and optimize its performance in both biomedical and environmental applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-008</data>
