<node id="Calcium-Rich ACP">
  <data key="d0">Calcium-Rich ACP</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium-rich amorphous calcium phosphate is an intermediate compound formed when negative ions on the hydroxyapatite surface interact with calcium ions in SBF.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium-Poor ACP">
  <data key="d0">Calcium-Poor ACP</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium-poor amorphous calcium phosphate is formed when calcium-rich ACP interacts with phosphate ions in SBF, eventually stabilizing into apatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Group">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Group</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The Phosphate Group (PO4^3-), formally identified as an orthophosphate ion, is an anionic, tetrahedral chemical moiety defined by its phosphorus-oxygen (P-O) bonding framework. It contains oxygen atoms that actively facilitate electrophilic interactions with heavy metal ions. Within hydroxyapatite (HAp) systems, the Phosphate Group serves as a fundamental structural unit and primary lattice constituent. Functioning as both an integral component of the crystal lattice and a prominent surface feature, it generates negative surface charges and actively coordinates with calcium ions to facilitate the formation of intermediate compounds. This structural integration is essential for establishing the material's baseline chemical stability and electrostatic properties.

The presence and vibrational characteristics of the Phosphate Group are routinely verified through spectroscopic analysis, particularly Raman and FTIR techniques, which detect its specific vibrational modes and P-O bonding networks. Spectroscopic identification typically reveals strong absorption signals spanning 920–1200 cm⁻¹. Depending on the specific composite environment or analytical conditions, distinct characteristic peaks are frequently observed at 933 cm⁻¹, 962 cm⁻¹, and 1054 cm⁻¹, with the 1054 cm⁻¹ peak specifically documented in HAP-BC composites.

From an electronic and photochemical perspective, the Phosphate Group plays a pivotal role in the molecular behavior of HAp. In the HPA molecule, the Highest Occupied Molecular Orbital (HOMO) is primarily concentrated within this group. Furthermore, it exhibits notable photochemical reactivity, as its electronic state undergoes measurable alterations during photocatalytic activation under UV irradiation. Collectively, these structural, spectroscopic, and reactive attributes underscore the critical importance of the Phosphate Group in determining the functional, electrostatic, and catalytic performance of hydroxyapatite-based materials.&lt;SEP&gt;The phosphate group is a fundamental structural component of hydroxyapatite that can be substituted by carbonate ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate Group is a fundamental chemical component of HAP, exhibiting characteristic absorption bands in the spectra.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate Group is a chemical component of HAp, identified by a characteristic peak at 962 cm^-1.&lt;SEP&gt;The Phosphate Group is a fundamental structural component of the Hydroxyapatite lattice, exhibiting specific vibrational modes.&lt;SEP&gt;The Phosphate Group (PO4^3-) is a fundamental chemical structure in HAp, identified through P-O bonding and vibrational modes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792188650</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biocompatibility">
  <data key="d0">Biocompatibility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Biocompatibility is a key property exhibited by the x = 2.5 sample, attributed to its porous structure and surface characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biocompatibility is a key characteristic of calcium phosphates that makes them suitable for biomedical and environmental use.&lt;SEP&gt;Biocompatibility is a property of the prepared Hydroxyapatite indicated by the presence of carbonates similar to those in human bone.&lt;SEP&gt;Biocompatibility is a material property indicated by the presence of carbonate groups in the prepared HAp, similar to human bone composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Biocompatibility denotes the ability of a material to perform with an appropriate host response without causing adverse effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792427797</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scaffold Grains">
  <data key="d0">Scaffold Grains</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Scaffold grains are the structural components of the bone cement matrix where increased viscosity and stickiness occur between them.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Flake Apatite">
  <data key="d0">Flake Apatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Flake apatite refers to the tiny, small apatite deposition initially observed on the sample surface after 1 day of SBF immersion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Needle-Like Apatite">
  <data key="d0">Needle-Like Apatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Needle-like apatite crystals form and elongate over time, becoming more prominent with higher NaCl content.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b8bd3776c87a0bcab21842e8e2bb2e06-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791061334</data>
