<node id="Figure 4">
  <data key="d0">Figure 4</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 4 serves as a scientific illustration whose specific content varies across distinct research contexts due to the generic nature of figure numbering in academic publications. In materials science and characterization studies, Figure 4 presents comprehensive analytical data, including X-ray diffraction patterns for samples S2–S4 alongside their thermal treatments, as well as X-ray diffraction patterns for materials synthesized at 200 °C under varying pH and time conditions. It also facilitates the characterization of PG through scanning electron microscopy and X-ray diffraction, while providing thermogravimetric and derivative thermogravimetric curves that detail the thermal distributions of hydroxyapatites. Within this same context, Figure 4 displays the distribution of crystallite sizes determined from the X-ray diffraction patterns, specifically covering HAp Types 1 through 6.

In process and reactor engineering applications, Figure 4 functions as a schematic illustration outlining the experimental procedure and reactor setup. It also acts as a visual representation that maps the dependencies between observed activity ratios and average grain diameter, providing insights into process performance and material behavior.

In fluid dynamics and computational modeling research, Figure 4 presents a mathematical model describing natural convection in PGEF. Because these descriptions originate from independent studies, Figure 4 refers to separate experimental setups, material characterizations, and theoretical models rather than a single unified dataset. Each variation is accurately represented within its respective disciplinary framework, ensuring that all reported analytical, procedural, and modeling aspects are comprehensively accounted for.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4 is a visual representation showing the XRD pattern of the prepared ammonium sulfate sample.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4 presents phase diagrams calculated by FactSage Software for multi-component oxide systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4 provides a schematic illustration of the experimental procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4 shows the concentration change of SO2 with reaction temperature.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure showing the percentage of research on the effect of treated/calcined PG on the mechanical strength of the matrix.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792454158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 5">
  <data key="d0">Figure 5</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 5 is a non-unique identifier that appears across multiple independent scientific studies, each utilizing the label to represent distinct experimental data and visualizations. Consequently, the descriptions encompass several separate contexts, primarily focusing on phosphogypsum-related materials, hydroxyapatite composites, and decomposition product analyses.

In studies involving phosphogypsum and related compounds, Figure 5 illustrates the utilization of oxygen resources within phosphogypsum. It also displays the infrared spectrum and mineral phases of PGEF. Additionally, a graphical representation labeled Figure 5 shows the ash fusion temperatures of phosphogypsum samples subjected to varying quaternary acidity coefficients.

Within the context of hydroxyapatite research, Figure 5 serves multiple analytical purposes. It presents scanning electron microscopy micrographs of Type 1 through Type 6 hydroxyapatite powders and provides an energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy spectrum of as-prepared hydroxyapatite. Structural refinement is depicted through X-ray diffraction data of a hydroxyapatite sample synthesized at 200 degrees Celsius, pH 11, for 15 hours. Furthermore, it illustrates how bovine serum albumin adsorption alters the thermal stability of hydroxyapatites.

Regarding decomposition and solid production analyses, Figure 5 presents X-ray diffraction characterization of solid production. It visually displays X-ray diffraction analysis results used to identify decomposition products, specifically highlighting variations in decomposition production across different coal sizes.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure showing the percentage of research on the effect of PG on water absorption and permeability of the matrix.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792454158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 6">
  <data key="d0">Figure 6</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 6 is a non-unique identifier utilized across multiple independent scientific studies, with each instance representing a distinct visual data presentation tailored to its specific research context. In material science and hydroxyapatite characterization, Figure 6 displays the FTIR spectra of hydroxyapatite powders (designated HAP1, HAP2, and HAP3) both before and after heat treatment, as well as alongside samples with adsorbed protein. It also presents bright field TEM images illustrating the morphology of Type 1 HAp structures, XRD patterns of hydrothermally synthesized samples prepared with and without the Brij-93 surfactant, and kinetics curves for fluoride adsorption onto nHAp at varying temperatures. These kinetic studies are further detailed through non-linear fitted curves of kinetic models compared directly with experimental data.

In environmental and leaching research, Figure 6 provides a graphical representation plotting the relationship between temperature and the leachability of radioactive materials and rare earth elements. It also illustrates the evolution of trace element leaching concentrations over varying time and temperature conditions. Regarding chemical bonding and electronic properties, a distinct instance of Figure 6 depicts covalent bond characteristics, electron localization, and bond order density specifically for O-Cd and O-Co bonds.

In metallurgical and reaction engineering contexts, Figure 6 illustrates desulfurization rates and slag properties, including slag sulfur content, under different reaction temperatures and times. It also shows the effects of various reaction factors on the composition of syngas, as well as CO concentration changes over reaction time across different coal sizes. Additionally, in broader material analysis, Figure 6 serves as a visual representation detailing the main chemical composition of phosphogypsum. Collectively, these separate instances of Figure 6 encompass a broad spectrum of analytical techniques, spanning gas composition dynamics, thermal leachability trends, electronic bond properties, spectroscopic material analysis, chemical composition mapping, and microscopic structural imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure presenting the relative research number for the effect of PG on each property.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792454158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 7">
  <data key="d0">Figure 7</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 7 serves as a recurring label across multiple independent scientific studies, with its specific content and analytical purpose varying significantly depending on the research domain. In investigations involving phosphogypsum, this figure illustrates common modification techniques applied to the material and graphically demonstrates how successive leaching steps affect the removal efficiency of target elements. Within the specific application of chemical looping gasification of low-rank lignite using phosphogypsum, Figure 7 depicts the resulting solid-phase products.

In analytical and materials characterization contexts, the figure presents Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy data used to verify the removal of surfactant residues and identify chemical functional groups. It also displays laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy (LIBS) results that map the identified spectral line wavelengths for various elements. From a computational chemistry perspective, Figure 7 visualizes bond order density and natural adaptive orbital analysis to elucidate underlying interaction mechanisms.

Finally, in nanomaterials research focusing on Type 2 hydroxyapatite (HAp), the figure provides a dark-field transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image paired with a particle size distribution histogram that quantifies the nanoparticle dimensions. Collectively, these distinct representations highlight how the same figure label is utilized across diverse experimental, computational, and analytical frameworks to convey domain-specific data.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 7 is a graphical representation showing thermal analysis curves for PGI-1.4 across different C/S molar ratios.&lt;SEP&gt;A plot demonstrating the multi-linear curves of the intraparticle diffusion model for fluoride adsorption onto the adsorbent.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure displaying microstructures of solid production at different coal sizes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792364653</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium sulfate dihydrate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium sulfate dihydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium sulfate dihydrate is the primary chemical compound composing the PG sample used in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate dihydrate is the primary chemical compound composing the PG sample before the gasification process.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate dihydrate is the specific chemical compound that constitutes the majority of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium sulfate dihydrate is the main chemical component of phosphogypsum that participates in hydration reactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516547</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adsorption-diffusion-desorption process">
  <data key="d0">Adsorption-diffusion-desorption process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Adsorption-diffusion-desorption process is the mechanism through which PG releases oxygen during the gasification of lignite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
