<node id="Natural Gypsum">
  <data key="d0">Natural Gypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Natural Gypsum is a naturally occurring, non-renewable mineral resource that serves as a foundational and traditional raw material across multiple industrial sectors, with extensive consumption in China for construction applications. Historically and currently, it functions as a primary input for the co-production of sulfuric acid and cement, the manufacturing of plaster, wallboard, β-building gypsum powder, various binders, and calcium sulfate whiskers. It is also widely utilized as a standard cement additive, a conventional building material, and a traditional stabilization agent that enhances the physical and mechanical properties of adobe soil.

In both research and industrial contexts, Natural Gypsum operates as a critical benchmark and baseline for evaluating conventional production models and alternative materials. It is systematically referenced to compare setting times, curing effects, mechanical and compressive strength, dehydration behavior during milling, solubility, chemical composition, and cement retardation effects. Notably, it is frequently compared against phosphogypsum, an industrial byproduct that requires purification for use in cement retarders and construction. While phosphogypsum is increasingly promoted as a substitute to conserve dwindling Natural Gypsum reserves, Natural Gypsum maintains a distinct advantage due to its superior physical and chemical characteristics and lack of industrial impurities. Consequently, it remains the standard against which phosphogypsum-based products are measured, often placing the latter at a competitive disadvantage in regional markets. Additionally, conventional hemihydrate gypsum production aims to replace it as a standard raw material, and it is routinely evaluated alongside phosphogypsum in radioactivity assessments.

From a resource and environmental standpoint, the extraction and crushing of Natural Gypsum are highly resource-intensive processes contributing to declining global reserves. To mitigate ecological impacts and conserve the mineral, recycling technologies have been implemented to significantly reduce fossil resource consumption. The strategic substitution of Natural Gypsum with phosphogypsum in binder and cement manufacturing is actively encouraged as a conservation measure. Furthermore, environmental assessments continuously compare Natural Gypsum processing against advanced methods like PCR technology, which generates fewer particulate emissions, reflecting ongoing industrial efforts to optimize material efficiency and minimize ecological footprints.&lt;SEP&gt;A reference material used in cement formulations to compare against PG-treated samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural gypsum is a mineral that phosphogypsum replaces in certain industrial applications.</data>
