<node id="Impurities">
  <data key="d0">Impurities</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d2">Impurities are foreign substances, contaminants, and unwanted elements or compounds that are inherently present in raw materials such as phosphogypsum (PG) and can be transferred to derived products like potassium sulphate. These contaminants typically consist of minor concentrations of elements including silicon, aluminum, sodium, iron, magnesium, strontium, potassium, fluorine, calcium oxide, trace metals, and phosphate-derived compounds, exhibiting varying degrees of solubility from slightly soluble to insoluble. Physically and chemically, they tend to decompose upon heating, leach alongside rare earth elements during processing, and can alter spectral peak characteristics in analytical samples.

In industrial and construction applications, impurities significantly degrade material performance and process efficiency. Specifically, they drastically delay cement setting times, retard hydration reactions, reduce early strength development, deteriorate the hydration properties of cementitious materials, and negatively impact the conductivity of PG. These adverse effects restrict the allowable PG content in construction mixtures and degrade the performance of specialized composites like calcium phosphogypsum fiber-mixed materials (CPGFM), necessitating the use of additional treatment protocols and supplementary materials.

To mitigate these issues, impurities are actively targeted for removal through various chemical and physical treatments. Common separation strategies include precipitation during hydrolysis to isolate slightly soluble and insoluble fractions, as well as leveraging processes like nano-hydroxyapatite (nHAp) synthesis, which effectively filters out raw PG contaminants. Careful management and separation of these impurities remain critical steps in industrial processing to ensure material utility and product quality.</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792502638</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
