<node id="Room Temperature">
  <data key="d0">Room Temperature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Room Temperature functions as a critical environmental parameter and standardized ambient condition across diverse scientific, industrial, and agricultural applications. In laboratory and chemical contexts, it serves as a controlled baseline and low-temperature condition that ensures procedural consistency and provides a stable thermal environment for various processes. It is the optimal thermal condition for re-extraction processes and acts as a reference point for comparing the catalytic effects of elevated temperatures on leaching efficiency. Specifically, Room Temperature governs the initial chemical reaction between phosphogypsum and phosphoric acid, the combination of potassium dihydrogen phosphate with phosphogypsum, and the aging phase within chemical precipitation methods. It also establishes the baseline for the 24-hour treatment of hydroxyapatite (HAP) with AL and the stirring phase of protein adsorption studies.

In materials synthesis, Room Temperature is the ambient condition for precipitation methods that yield hydroxyapatite with higher carbonate content, including the synthesis of HAp Type 1 and fully crystalline hydroxyapatite without a reactor, often facilitating rapid reactions. It similarly serves as the baseline experimental condition under which phosphogypsum samples generate specific daily release rates of trace elements. For leaching and stripping experiments, Room Temperature provides both the initial baseline and the controlled low-temperature environment necessary for these chemical processes.

In specialized agricultural and waste management contexts, Room Temperature carries a distinct operational definition. It refers specifically to the final temperature recorded on the 40th day following the completion of compost fermentation. This metric is utilized to monitor the cooling progression and assess the maturity of composting materials. Collectively, these applications establish Room Temperature as a fundamental controlled variable for chemical and biological laboratory procedures, a key parameter in materials synthesis and environmental monitoring, and a specific temporal endpoint indicator in composting science.&lt;SEP&gt;Room temperature is the target temperature to which samples are rapidly cooled in air after melting.&lt;SEP&gt;Room temperature corresponds to the 25°C condition in the second figure's experimental design.&lt;SEP&gt;Room Temperature is the target cooling state for the reaction vessel, which safely reduces internal pressure.&lt;SEP&gt;Room temperature is the baseline curing condition used for comparison with elevated curing temperatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e290dd6268316238694f70c30a367426-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792452510</data>
