<node id="Shale">
  <data key="d0">Shale</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Shale is a common marine and surficial rock used as the primary baseline for comparing trace element enrichment and depletion in phosphorites.&lt;SEP&gt;Shale is a terrestrial reference material used to compare the elemental composition of phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Shale is a terrestrial material included in the table for comparative trace element analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Shale is a sedimentary rock type used as a terrestrial reference material for chemical concentration comparison.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791186032</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Francolite">
  <data key="d0">Francolite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Francolite is the amorphous form of apatite found in sedimentary phosphorites, exhibiting extensive elemental substitutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Francolite is the amorphous form of apatite found within sedimentary phosphorites.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791186032</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Silver">
  <data key="d0">Silver</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Silver is a chemical element that functions as a trace metal and is frequently categorized as a heavy metal in environmental and industrial contexts. Recognized as a toxic element by the United States Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), it is naturally enriched in phosphorites, where it commonly associates with organic matter through complexation or adsorption. The element is also consistently present in phosphogypsum, with scientific studies actively measuring its concentration across various sampled locations. Due to its environmental impact, silver in aqueous systems is subject to strict regulatory thresholds, including a standard limit of 0.05 mg/L and a World Health Organization (WHO) guideline value of 0.01 mg/L.

In water treatment and material science, silver ions are frequently targeted for removal, such as through the application of Dowex 50W-x8 resin. However, certain treatment materials may exhibit a notable decline in performance, with silver removal rates dropping to approximately 30%. Conversely, silver is also leveraged for beneficial applications; when doped into hydroxyapatite, it creates a highly efficient adsorbent specifically engineered for dye removal processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792481783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Selenium">
  <data key="d0">Selenium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Selenium is a chemical trace element that is both biologically essential and potentially hazardous. It is frequently associated with organic matter and is notably enriched in phosphorites. In environmental and soil science, selenium is often examined alongside heavy metals due to its significant ecological impact. Its geochemical behavior is distinct; similar to arsenic, its concentration does not decline substantially as pH levels rise. Furthermore, its mobile anionic form, selenite, demonstrates a high affinity for hydroxyapatite, resulting in efficient adsorption.

Industrially and environmentally, selenium is commonly detected in phosphogypsum, where it may occur as calcium selenate dihydrate, with measured concentrations consistently recorded at less than 1.75. Given its potential toxicity and mobility in ecosystems, it poses a notable environmental risk. Consequently, multiple regulatory frameworks have established a strict permissible limit of 0.01 mg/L to mitigate its adverse effects on human health and the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element measured in milligrams per liter during the TCLP test.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element measured in the mixtures with a concentration of 0.26 mg/l in PG=100.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element found in mixtures below 1 mg/l, meeting the US-EPA limit.&lt;SEP&gt;Selenium is a toxic element classified by the United States EPA, present in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Selenium is a trace metal found in Phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Selenium is a chemical element whose concentration in phosphogypsum was measured across the sampled locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792482047</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Molybdenum">
  <data key="d0">Molybdenum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Molybdenum is a trace element enriched in phosphorites, often linked to organic matter or formed as metallic sulfide precipitates.&lt;SEP&gt;Molybdenum is a transition metal for which the material shows a removal rate of 0%.&lt;SEP&gt;Molybdenum is a heavy metal whose solidification and stabilization are evaluated using alternative cementitious binders.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element measured in milligrams per liter during the TCLP test.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element measured in the mixtures, with no specific US-EPA limit listed in this context.&lt;SEP&gt;A chemical element with no specific US-EPA limit listed in this context.&lt;SEP&gt;Molybdenum is a chemical element whose concentration in phosphogypsum was measured across the sampled locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792487165</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lithium">
  <data key="d0">Lithium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Lithium is a trace element depleted in phosphorites relative to shale.&lt;SEP&gt;Lithium is a trace metal that exhibits an increasing leaching rate as leaching time increases.&lt;SEP&gt;Lithium is a trace element released at 0.29 µg/L during the leaching experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Lithium is present in phosphogypsum and exhibits high liquid/solid fractionation during leaching, fixed via adsorption or weak bonding to gypsum surfaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Lithium is an element that exhibits high liquid/solid fractionation and is weakly bound to gypsum surfaces.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792035295</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boron">
  <data key="d0">Boron</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Boron is a trace element depleted in phosphorites relative to shale.&lt;SEP&gt;Boron is a chemical element measured in phosphogypsum samples across multiple scientific studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Boron is an element for which the material shows a low removal rate of approximately 5-10%.&lt;SEP&gt;Boron is a trace alloying element added to AZ31B.&lt;SEP&gt;Boron is a chemical element whose concentration in phosphogypsum was measured across the sampled locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792487165</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gallium">
  <data key="d0">Gallium</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Gallium is a trace element depleted in phosphorites relative to shale.&lt;SEP&gt;Gallium is an element for which the material shows a removal rate of 100%.&lt;SEP&gt;Gallium is a trace chemical element present in phosphogypsum with a measured concentration of 1 ± 0.2.&lt;SEP&gt;Gallium is one of the most abundant elements in the gypsum phase of phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792041755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cobalt">
  <data key="d0">Cobalt</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cobalt is a transition and heavy metal element characterized by distinct geochemical, environmental, and catalytic properties. As a trace element, it is naturally depleted in phosphorites relative to shale, and its mobility within natural aquatic seepage waters is significantly reduced by buffering processes. In industrial and environmental monitoring contexts, cobalt is tracked as a toxic heavy metal, with its concentration in solutions such as phosphogypsum carefully measured against regulatory standards. While some assessments note that cobalt concentrations in mixtures align with US-EPA limits, other contexts indicate that no specific US-EPA limit is listed, reflecting variable regulatory frameworks. During toxicity characteristic leaching procedure testing, cobalt levels are quantified in milligrams per liter to evaluate environmental leaching risks.

In environmental remediation and advanced materials science, cobalt is recognized as a radionuclide that is efficiently removed from contaminated environments, often targeted for adsorption and immobilization using various nanocomposites. Its removal efficiency is notably enhanced through hydroxyapatite integration, which achieves a spiked removal rate of approximately 82% and improves adsorption performance when doped up to 20 mol%. Cobalt can be directly substituted into calcium hydroxyapatite nanopowders, a modification that deliberately alters the material’s crystal structure and functional properties, and it is also utilized to promote both calcium-deficient and stoichiometric hydroxyapatite formulations. When supported on hydroxyapatite, cobalt functions as a versatile heterogeneous catalyst for critical industrial reactions, including the oxidation of 1,2-dichloroethane and methanol, the catalytic removal of oxygenated volatile organic compounds, and hydrogen generation.

Furthermore, cobalt frequently occurs alongside rare earth elements in source materials, necessitating dedicated separation processes during extraction. Specific ionic liquid extraction methods are employed to isolate cobalt from these rare earth mixtures. Through these diverse geochemical, remediation, and catalytic applications, cobalt remains a critical element in both natural environmental cycles and advanced industrial systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Cobalt is a trace element measured in phosphogypsum minerals using LA-ICP-MS.&lt;SEP&gt;Cobalt refers to divalent cations removed from aqueous solutions using hydroxyapatite adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Cobalt is a trace metal element measured in the provided data table with concentrations in ppm.&lt;SEP&gt;Cobalt is a heavy metal found in phosphogypsum, primarily in fine particle fractions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cobalt is a chemical element whose concentration in phosphogypsum was measured across the sampled locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-021</data>
