<node id="Vanadium">
  <data key="d0">Vanadium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Vanadium is a chemical element that functions as a trace component across various geological and industrial contexts. Geologically, it is notably enriched in the Phosphoria Formation relative to surrounding shale deposits. In industrial settings, particularly during phosphogypsum processing, vanadium is detected in variable concentrations and exhibits a transfer rate ranging from 1 to 100 percent, with the processing material demonstrating an approximate 53 percent drop in removal efficiency. During this processing, vanadium primarily transfers to portlandite. Analytically, it is quantified in phosphogypsum minerals and other material samples using techniques such as Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass-Spectrometry and Laser Ablation ICP-MS, with concentrations measured in milligrams per liter, parts per million, and specific values like 4.75 ± 0.63. Chemical extraction methods utilizing 8-hydroxyquinoline are also employed for its determination.

In environmental and regulatory contexts, vanadium acts as a heavy metal contaminant that can be discharged into the atmosphere and accumulates in soils amended with phosphogypsum, causing slight pollution. Its environmental mobility and presence make it a key factor in assessing terrestrial ecotoxicity potential. Notably, vanadium can be eliminated through simple water washing, and its levels vary across different material samples, with commercial versions of potassium sulphate and calcium carbonate showing higher concentrations than their synthetic counterparts. While it is frequently measured in mixtures and listed in analytical tables with associated numerical values, no specific US-EPA limit is currently established for vanadium in this context.&lt;SEP&gt;Vanadium is a trace metal element measured in the provided data table with concentrations in ppm.&lt;SEP&gt;Vanadium is a heavy metal found in phosphogypsum, primarily in fine particle fractions.&lt;SEP&gt;Vanadium is a chemical element whose concentration in phosphogypsum was measured across the sampled locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
