<node id="Cr">
  <data key="d0">Cr</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cr, also known as chromium, is a chemical element classified as both a trace element and a heavy metal. It is routinely measured and reported in geochemical datasets and analytical data tables, with concentrations typically quantified in parts per million or milligrams per liter. Analytical determination of the element is commonly performed using inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry, and it is actively monitored during spike recovery tests to validate measurement accuracy. The substance is detected across various environmental and industrial matrices, including terrestrial materials, phosphogypsum, magnesium phosphogypsum, and PBSLM.

In phosphogypsum samples, Cr exhibits the highest concentration among all measured elements and is classified as a hazardous heavy metal. Its presence in phosphogypsum and magnesium phosphogypsum poses significant health concerns, with exposure risks surpassing United States Environmental Protection Agency standards. Specifically, the cancer risk associated with oral intake of Cr has been explicitly measured and documented. Conversely, when detected in PBSLM, the concentration is recorded at 0.1 milligrams per liter, which remains well below established regulatory limits. Due to its hazardous profile, Cr is also cataloged in reference tables that provide Reference Dose values for comprehensive risk assessment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792295116</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cu">
  <data key="d0">Cu</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Copper (Cu) is a chemical element and heavy metal ion that is routinely analyzed as a trace component across various environmental, geological, and industrial datasets. Its concentration is systematically measured and reported in parts per million within geochemical datasets, analytical records, and tabular formats containing specified numerical values. In mining and processing contexts, the concentration of Cu is noted to increase as the particle size of beneficiated phosphate ore decreases. Additionally, it is commonly identified and quantified in phosphogypsum and terrestrial materials, where it appears in data tables alongside associated measurement values.

From an environmental and public health standpoint, copper functions as a critical toxic substance that is discharged into freshwater ecosystems and the atmosphere, particularly during electricity production for PSC technology. As a heavy metal ion, it frequently coexists with other heavy metals in environmental matrices. Exposure to copper poses significant health risks, as it is known to damage the immune system and has been linked to carcinogenic effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
