<node id="Zn">
  <data key="d0">Zn</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Zn is a chemical element classified as a heavy metal ion and a trace element. It is routinely documented in scientific and industrial datasets, where it appears in tabular formats alongside specified concentration ranges and associated numerical measurements. In analytical contexts, its presence is typically quantified in parts per million (ppm). The concentration of Zn is closely linked to geological and processing variables; specifically, its levels increase as the particle size of beneficiated phosphate ore decreases.

From an environmental and public health standpoint, Zn is recognized as a critical toxic substance. As a heavy metal ion, it is known to cause cancers and damage the immune system, and it frequently coexists with other heavy metals in contaminated environments. Its release into ecosystems poses significant risks, including discharge into freshwater bodies during electricity production for PSC technology. Furthermore, leaching tests have demonstrated that Zn concentrations can slightly exceed the safety limits established by the United States Environmental Protection Agency (USEPA), highlighting the necessity for rigorous monitoring and environmental management.&lt;SEP&gt;Zinc is a heavy metal found in phosphogypsum that can migrate with leachate and contaminate water.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-276f29345df37143fc224a22d0f6089b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
