</node>
<node id="Radium-228">
  <data key="d0">Radium-228</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radium-228 is a radioactive isotope of radium and a daughter radionuclide within the Thorium-232 decay series. Recognized as the second most abundant radium isotope, it is typically present at very low concentrations and contributes negligibly to total radioactivity, generally following the geochemical behavior of Radium-226. In environmental and public health contexts, it is routinely monitored in drinking water to ensure radiological safety.

Within industrial phosphogypsum production, Radium-228 coprecipitates with calcium sulfate and demonstrates a distinct propensity to accumulate preferentially in the smaller grain size fractions, even though it remains relatively rare in the bulk material. Its radioactivity is consistently measured and reported, frequently alongside its decay product Actinium-228. For analytical purposes, it is paired with Thorium-228 in gamma-ray spectrometry, and the resulting Thorium-228 to Radium-228 activity ratio serves as a critical metric for evaluating preferential accumulation patterns and assessing specific phosphogypsum fractions.&lt;SEP&gt;A radioactive isotope in phosphogypsum, evaluated for its environmental availability using EDTA leaching tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791419248</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Th-230">
  <data key="d0">Th-230</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Th-230 is a radioactive isotope measured in Florida with a recorded value of 113.&lt;SEP&gt;Th-230 is a decay product in the U-238 series that undergoes alpha decay to form Radium-226.&lt;SEP&gt;Thorium-230 is a radioactive isotope that undergoes alpha decay to form Radium-226 within the U-238 decay series.&lt;SEP&gt;Th-230 is a radioactive isotope measured at AMAX, USSAC, Plant A, Plant B, and Plant C.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188710</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radium Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Radium Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Radium Sulfate is a highly insoluble compound that precipitates early during phosphoric acid production and co-precipitates with Barium Sulfate.&lt;SEP&gt;Radium sulfate precipitates from radium-228 and crystallizes in an orthorhombic system.&lt;SEP&gt;Radium sulfate is a less soluble compound formed when radium co-precipitates with barium and strontium.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421004</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Barium Sulfate">
  <data key="d0">Barium Sulfate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Barium Sulfate is a highly insoluble compound that co-precipitates with Radium Sulfate and shares a rhombohedral crystal system.&lt;SEP&gt;Barium sulfate forms isomorphous crystals with radium sulfate, facilitating radium incorporation.&lt;SEP&gt;Barium sulfate is an insoluble phase formed by barium that precipitates within the phosphogypsum crystal lattice.&lt;SEP&gt;Barium sulfate is a less soluble compound that co-precipitates with radium and forms rhombohedral crystals.&lt;SEP&gt;Barium sulfate is an insoluble chemical phase in which barium is primarily precipitated within phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792037229</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alpha Decay">
  <data key="d0">Alpha Decay</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Alpha Decay is the radioactive decay process by which Th-230 transforms into Radium-226.&lt;SEP&gt;Alpha decay is the radioactive decay process by which Ra-226 transforms into Radon-222 and Radon-222 transforms into Polonium-218.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188710</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wet Phosphoric Process">
  <data key="d0">Wet Phosphoric Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Wet Phosphoric Process is an industrial method for producing phosphoric acid that generates phosphogypsum containing radium.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188710</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bone">
  <data key="d0">Bone</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Bone is a natural biological tissue and human calcified structure that serves as the primary location in the body for radium accumulation, following biological pathways analogous to those of calcium. Composed primarily of hydroxyapatite as its main mineral component, this tissue provides essential structural hardness, rigidity, and strength. Within this biological matrix, citrate plays a critical role in regulating the nucleation and stabilization of hydroxyapatite crystallites. The specific dimensions of these naturally occurring hydroxyapatite crystals have become a key focus for researchers, who actively aim to replicate them using synthesized hydroxyapatite materials.

In a distinct context, Bone is also the title of a scientific journal. This publication has been responsible for disseminating clinical research, including notable studies focused on drug-induced osteopetrosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone is a human hard tissue where synthetic hydroxyapatite is commonly used for implants due to chemical similarity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792001737</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marine Sediments">
  <data key="d0">Marine Sediments</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Marine sediments are environments where the Th-232 decay series is scarce, making Radium-228 rare.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188710</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranium Waste Waters">
  <data key="d0">Uranium Waste Waters</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranium waste waters are environments where Ra and Ba predominantly exist as Ba(Ra)SO4 particulate forms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188710</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RaSO4 Ion Pair">
  <data key="d0">RaSO4 Ion Pair</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The RaSO4 ion pair is a soluble sulfate species that radium may form in groundwater before precipitation occurs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791188710</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Solid Solutions">
  <data key="d0">Solid Solutions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Solid solutions involving radium, strontium, and barium control radium concentration in brines and phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Solid solutions are a chemical phenomenon where major elements in gypsum form mixed crystal structures during phase formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425671</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Brines">
  <data key="d0">Brines</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Brines in phosphogypsum systems are undersaturated with respect to pure RaSO4, with radium controlled by solid solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Brines from phosphogypsum deposits undergo geochemical evolution with significant environmental implications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-045</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791428038</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moisset">
  <data key="d0">Moisset</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Moisset is a researcher who reported on radium association with phosphogypsum grains and crystallization systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Moisset is a researcher who found distinct crystals rich in co-crystallized Ra-226 within phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-033</data>
