<node id="Hg">
  <data key="d0">Hg</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hg, universally recognized as mercury, is a chemical element classified as both a trace element and a hazardous heavy metal. Its concentration is systematically measured and reported across various analytical frameworks, including comprehensive geochemical datasets. Measurements are typically quantified in micrograms per kilogram, with the element being tracked in diverse matrices such as terrestrial materials and specific industrial byproducts.

Within phosphogypsum (PG), mercury is identified as a hazardous heavy metal that poses notable environmental safety risks, although its concentrations are documented to decrease significantly following processing. Analytical protocols also include monitoring Hg during spike recovery tests to validate measurement accuracy, while established reference dose (RFD) values for the element are cataloged in regulatory reference tables. Furthermore, mercury has been specifically detected in PBSLM, where it was recorded at a concentration of 0.0 mg/L. Collectively, Hg is a critical heavy metal element monitored for its environmental impact, regulatory thresholds, and behavior across different material matrices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792483381</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
