<node id="Sand">
  <data key="d0">Sand</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Sand is a natural, granular geological material and fine aggregate characterized by a particle size ranging from 0 to 0.6 mm. It is widely recognized as a traditional and conventional component in construction and industrial applications, serving as a primary aggregate in reinforced concrete and various construction materials when mixed with cement and phosphogypsum (PG). In brick manufacturing, sand is extensively utilized alongside clay, autoclaved PG, fly ash, and lime, and it functions as a key soil component in adobe mixtures, constituting exactly 18.00% of the grain size distribution. Its physical properties, particularly its density relative to PG, directly influence the workability of these construction mixes.

In specific industrial formulations, sand accounts for 14.34% of raw material consumption in PBSLM at a cost of 28.69 Yuan per ton, while serving as a minor component comprising 4.73% of desulfurization gypsum-based self-leveling mortar. Chemically, sand introduces SiO2 peaks in the XRD analysis of mortar samples and can act as a source of silica impurities within PG, typically originating from process water supplies. Conversely, it demonstrates notable adsorption capabilities, effectively removing small quantities of fluoride from aqueous solutions through anion adsorption mechanisms.

Sand is frequently employed as a standard baseline material in brick production, where it is often partially or completely substituted by PG waste to conserve natural reserves. While this substitution supports industrial valorization of PG, it is noted that replacing sand with phosphogypsum increases the overall environmental footprint of the production cycle. Collectively, these characteristics define sand as a versatile material with significant applications in structural engineering, material substitution, and environmental chemistry.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792453181</data>
