<node id="Dihydrate Method">
  <data key="d0">Dihydrate Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The dihydrate method is a wet phosphoric acid process resulting in a uranium content of 5-25 μg/g in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific wet phosphoric acid process that results in phosphogypsum with high impurity content and poor quality.&lt;SEP&gt;The dihydrate method is a production process for phosphogypsum that results in a form unsuitable for direct construction use.&lt;SEP&gt;The dihydrate method is a production process for phosphogypsum that results in material unsuitable for raw construction use.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791916543</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranous Ion">
  <data key="d0">Uranous Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The uranous ion (U4+) forms complexes with fluoride under reduced conditions and low pH.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranyl Ion">
  <data key="d0">Uranyl Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The uranyl ion is the chemical form of U(VI) adsorbed onto apatite crystal surfaces.&lt;SEP&gt;The uranyl ion (UO2 2+) dominates at pH below 5 and may adsorb onto gypsum surfaces or substitute calcium ions.&lt;SEP&gt;The uranyl ion is the U(VI) form of uranium (UO2^2+) that can adsorb onto crystal surfaces or form chemical complexes.&lt;SEP&gt;Uranyl ion is a chemical form of uranium adsorbed on gypsum crystal surfaces that facilitates preferential leaching.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792037229</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Iron(II)">
  <data key="d0">Iron(II)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Iron(II) is a chemical species that reduces U(VI) to U(IV) during acidulation, facilitating uranium transfer to phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ferric Hydroxides">
  <data key="d0">Ferric Hydroxides</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ferric hydroxides are environmental minerals that sorb uranium, following organics in sorption affinity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clays">
  <data key="d0">Clays</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Clays are environmental minerals that sorb uranium, exhibiting lower sorption affinity than ferric hydroxides and oxyhydroxides.&lt;SEP&gt;Clays are materials that adsorb lead, keeping solution concentrations low.&lt;SEP&gt;Clays are natural materials that can adsorb lead and other contaminants in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Clays are materials modified with surfactants, used to draw parallels with BSA-modified hydroxyapatite surfaces.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791748680</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pore Water">
  <data key="d0">Pore Water</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Pore water is a medium where ejected Th-234 atoms accumulate during radioactive decay processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Pore water is the water residing within the pore spaces of mineral structures, affecting Rn-222 transport and energy.&lt;SEP&gt;Pore water is the water contained within the internal pore structure of the mortar.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792502638</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranyl Fluoride">
  <data key="d0">Uranyl Fluoride</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranyl fluoride is a chemical species that dominates at pH below 5.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uranyl Carbonate">
  <data key="d0">Uranyl Carbonate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uranyl carbonate species become prevalent at higher pH levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="UO2HPO4">
  <data key="d0">UO2HPO4</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">UO2HPO4 is a chemical compound formed when uranium(VI) adsorbs onto the surface of gypsum crystals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="UO2(HPO4)2 2+">
  <data key="d0">UO2(HPO4)2 2+</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">UO2(HPO4)2 2+ is a chemical species that becomes prevalent between pH 4 and 7.5.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphorites">
  <data key="d0">Phosphorites</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphorites are sedimentary phosphate deposits that represent approximately 85% of known phosphate rock reserves.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791189571</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Africa">
  <data key="d0">Africa</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Africa is a geographic region where the hemihydrate process for phosphoric acid production is relatively widely used.&lt;SEP&gt;Africa is a geographic region experiencing rapid expansion in the phosphorus chemical sector and subsequent phosphogypsum waste generation.&lt;SEP&gt;Africa is a geographic region characterized by widespread soil potassium deficiency and limited soluble potassium resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Africa is a significant region for phosphate rock production, consumption, and export.&lt;SEP&gt;A global region containing phosphogypsum-producing nations such as Morocco and Egypt.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791720679</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Goethite">
  <data key="d0">Goethite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Goethite is an iron oxide identified as one of the most common Fe-Al oxides in sedimentary phosphate rock.&lt;SEP&gt;Goethite is a mineralogical component found in phosphate ore.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792482756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
