<node id="Crystal Morphology">
  <data key="d0">Crystal Morphology</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Crystal Morphology refers to the physical shape, internal structure, size distribution, and spatial arrangement of crystalline particles or monocrystals. This characteristic is highly material-dependent and is prominently observed in both phosphogypsum and hydroxyapatite (HAp) systems. In phosphogypsum applications, crystal morphology typically manifests as elongated, rod-like structures that frequently form agglomerated arrangements. These features are directly governed by synthesis parameters, including temperature, sulfuric acid concentration, overall production process conditions, and the composition of raw materials.

In hydroxyapatite synthesis, crystal morphology describes the physical form of the particles, which often appear as uniform rod-like monocrystals. The structural characteristics of HAp crystals are notably sensitive to surfactant concentration, as well as general temperature and concentration variables during the synthesis process. Across all contexts, crystal morphology serves as a critical physical indicator that reflects and responds to specific chemical, thermal, and procedural conditions during material formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
