<node id="Phosphate Ions">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate ions are negatively charged chemical species, typically represented as PO₄³⁻ (or 2PO₄³⁻ in specific reaction stoichiometries), that function as fundamental anionic components in various inorganic materials. As a distinct chemical group, they exhibit four characteristic vibrational modes that are routinely analyzed using Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy. Within hydroxyapatite (HAP) systems, phosphate ions constitute the primary anionic framework and can be substituted by carbonate ions during synthesis. They play a critical role in material formation by coordinating with released calcium ions to initiate HAP nucleation, and their concentration is commonly quantified in the supernatant following the heat treatment of hydroxyapatite particles.

In gypsum-based materials, phosphate ions act as both structural modifiers and surface-active agents. During manufacturing, they substitute for sulfate ions within the gypsum crystal lattice. Concurrently, they physically adsorb onto gypsum crystal surfaces, a process that inhibits axial crystal development and alters overall morphology. These surface interactions are dynamic, involving continuous adsorption and desorption processes that directly modulate the material's zeta potential. Together, these properties underscore the multifaceted role of phosphate ions in crystal growth regulation, lattice substitution, and colloidal behavior across calcium phosphate and calcium sulfate matrices.&lt;SEP&gt;Ions whose insufficient amount damages the hydroxyapatite structure and promotes the formation of calcium hydroxide.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate Ions form the phosphate groups within the Hydroxyapatite lattice that undergo partial substitution by carbonate ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Ions generated from STMP hydrolysis that react with calcium ions to reach supersaturation and form clusters.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate ions are produced from the reaction of STMP with hydroxide ions and serve as the phosphorus source for the core formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate Ions form the phosphate groups within the Hydroxyapatite lattice that undergo partial substitution by carbonate ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate ions are chemical species that substitute into the gypsum network during acidification and can form syncrystallized structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405113</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ionic Radius">
  <data key="d0">Ionic Radius</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Ionic Radius is a physical property of metal ions that influences their preferential occupancy of specific calcium sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Ionic radius is a structural property that plays a secondary role compared to charge density in ion exchange.&lt;SEP&gt;Ionic Radius is an atomic property that determines elemental substitution capabilities and influences crystal growth retardation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792407430</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Electronegativity">
  <data key="d0">Electronegativity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Electronegativity is a chemical property that, alongside ionic radius, determines cation affinity for Ca(I) or Ca(II) sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Electronegativity is a chemical property that influences surface complexation and adsorption affinity on hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Electronegativity of the semiconductor is a parameter used in band energy calculations, with HAp having a value of 5.08 eV.&lt;SEP&gt;Electronegativity of the semiconductor is a parameter used in band energy calculations, with HAp having a value of 5.08 eV.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792190654</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Charge Density">
  <data key="d0">Charge Density</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Charge Density is a factor that influences ion-exchange facility, with higher density facilitating easier displacement of calcium.&lt;SEP&gt;Charge density is a physicochemical property that primarily governs cation exchange efficiency in apatites.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247038</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ca(I) Sites">
  <data key="d0">Ca(I) Sites</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ca(I) Sites are one of two nonequivalent calcium positions in the apatite lattice, preferentially occupied by smaller ions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247038</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ca(II) Sites">
  <data key="d0">Ca(II) Sites</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ca(II) Sites are one of two nonequivalent calcium positions in the apatite lattice, preferentially occupied by ions with larger radius or electronegativity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247038</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zhu et al.">
  <data key="d0">Zhu et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhu et al. are researchers who established that ionic radius and electronegativity primarily govern preferential metal ion occupancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhu et al. cited research on hydroxyapatite's ability to retain metals and anions in water treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers who reported that BSA alters its conformation during adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhu et al. proposed that the leaching behavior of fluoride ions is controlled by fluorite and fluorapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wakamura et al.">
  <data key="d0">Wakamura et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wakamura et al. are researchers who determined that ion-exchange facility depends more on cation charge density than ionic radius.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247038</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pollution Control">
  <data key="d0">Pollution Control</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pollution Control is an application field where ion-exchange materials are increasingly used for waste minimization and management.&lt;SEP&gt;Pollution control is a key component contributing to the overall synergistic effect of the production system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792226716</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kandori et al.">
  <data key="d0">Kandori et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Kandori et al. are researchers who conducted studies on hydroxyapatite particles and heat treatment temperatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Kandori et al. are researchers who studied the relationship between protein adsorption and HAp particle morphology.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791485610</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lamonier et al.">
  <data key="d0">Lamonier et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Lamonier et al. are researchers who investigated the effect of sodium ions on the basicity of hydroxyapatite materials.</data>
