<node id="Chen et al.">
  <data key="d0">Chen et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Chen et al. is a research group specializing in environmental engineering and materials science, with a primary focus on soil remediation, cementitious backfill technologies, and the recycling of industrial by-products. Their work extensively evaluates the performance of hydroxyapatite in treating metal-contaminated soils, including comparative analyses against other phosphate materials and detailed examinations of how pH levels influence the removal efficiency of cadmium and zinc. In parallel, the researchers have investigated chemical immobilization strategies, specifically analyzing how sodium hydroxide and aluminate materials affect the retention of fluorine and phosphorus.

A significant portion of their research addresses the environmental and chemical implications of recycling phosphogypsum within cemented paste backfill applications. Chen et al. have provided comprehensive chemical composition data for phosphogypsum samples sourced from China and documented the temporal variation of fluoride content in leachates across various backfill materials. Their studies further reveal that elevated temperatures accelerate cement hydration processes while simultaneously diminishing the retarding effect typically exerted by phosphogypsum. To optimize these cementitious systems, the group has also proposed the combined activation of carbide slag and potassium sulfate. Collectively, their contributions advance the understanding of sustainable waste utilization, heavy metal immobilization, and the chemical dynamics of engineered backfill materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers who combined ball milling with acetic acid to decrease impurities and increase phosphogypsum purity.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers who cited the technique for order of preference by similarity to ideal solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers cited for the transformity value of river water.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers cited for providing the energy conversion factor of steam.&lt;SEP&gt;Chen et al. are researchers cited for the threshold value of the softening coefficient indicating effective water resistance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-007</data>
