<node id="Fluoride Ions">
  <data key="d0">Fluoride Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluoride ions are negatively charged anions that exist in soluble forms and readily combine with alkaline elements or ammonium. In materials science, these ions can be incorporated into apatite crystal lattices, where they serve to modify crystal properties and enhance structural stability.

From an environmental and industrial perspective, fluoride ions are widely recognized as aqueous chemical contaminants that are frequently targeted for removal through adsorption processes. Numerous studies focus on their elimination from drinking water and other aqueous environments using hydroxyapatite, particularly synthetic hydroxyapatite, which facilitates defluoridation chemistry. Additionally, in industrial waste management, fluoride ions are present as undesirable contaminants within phosphogypsum. During the treatment of this material, these ions, which may initially be adsorbed within the phosphogypsum matrix, are often transferred into the surrounding solution. Consequently, fluoride ions represent a significant focus in both environmental remediation and materials engineering, serving as a key target for adsorption-based purification technologies while also functioning as a structural modifier in crystalline compounds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792318283</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Catalyst Lifetime">
  <data key="d0">Catalyst Lifetime</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Catalyst Lifetime is the operational duration of a catalyst, significantly impacted by thermal sintering and stability.</data>
