  <data key="d2">Calmagite is a dye decomposed photocatalytically by hydroxyapatite under UV irradiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Calmagite is an azo dye compound whose photocatalytic degradation is studied using hydroxyapatite suspensions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247343</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Azo Dye">
  <data key="d0">Azo Dye</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Azo dyes are synthetic colorants frequently targeted for degradation in aqueous environments using photocatalytic methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Azo dye is a synthetic pollutant adsorbed onto industrial waste-transformed hydroxyapatite adsorbents.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791655882</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lysozyme">
  <data key="d0">Lysozyme</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Lysozyme is a basic protein that exhibits high specificity for adsorption onto c-oriented hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Lysozyme is a model protein used alongside bovine serum albumin to test drug carrier performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Lysozyme is a protein model selected alongside bovine serum albumin to investigate adsorption and release behavior on hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Lysozyme is a model protein used alongside Bovine Serum Albumin to evaluate protein loading and desorption on hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791485362</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Myoglobin">
  <data key="d0">Myoglobin</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Myoglobin is a neutral protein whose adsorption behavior differs from acidic and basic proteins on hydroxyapatite surfaces.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247343</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="β-Tricalcium Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">β-Tricalcium Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">β-Tricalcium Phosphate is formed during the sintering of hydroxyapatite and releases calcium and phosphate ions that enhance protein adsorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247343</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chromatographic Columns">
  <data key="d0">Chromatographic Columns</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Chromatographic Columns utilize hydroxyapatite's adsorption properties for the separation of proteins, nucleic acids, and viruses.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247343</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C Sites">
  <data key="d0">C Sites</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">C Sites are positively charged binding sites on the hydroxyapatite surface consisting of calcium ions that preferentially adsorb acid molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;C sites are positively charged surface binding sites on HAp particles that attract acidic groups in proteins.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791485610</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="P Sites">
  <data key="d0">P Sites</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">P Sites are negatively charged binding sites on the hydroxyapatite surface consisting of phosphate groups that preferentially adsorb basic molecules.&lt;SEP&gt;P sites are negatively charged surface binding sites on HAp particles that attract basic groups in proteins.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791485610</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="A(B)-Planes">
  <data key="d0">A(B)-Planes</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">A(B)-Planes are crystal planes of hydroxyapatite that primarily expose positively charged calcium ions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247343</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C-Planes">
  <data key="d0">C-Planes</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">C-Planes are crystal planes of hydroxyapatite that expose negatively charged phosphate and hydroxyl groups.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791247343</data>
