<node id="Ca/P Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Ca/P Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The Ca/P Ratio is a chemical and stoichiometric metric that quantifies the atomic, molar, or weight percentage proportion of calcium to phosphorus within hydroxyapatite and related biological or synthetic materials. During synthesis and sample preparation, this ratio is typically set to a theoretical value of 1.67 in the starting materials and solutions to ensure proper stoichiometry and optimal material formation. The actual ratio is commonly determined using Inductively Coupled Plasma Optical Emission Spectroscopy (ICP-OES) and can vary depending on synthesis time, environmental conditions, and the specific composition of the materials being analyzed.

Empirical measurements frequently deviate from the theoretical target, with reported values of approximately 2.46 for synthetic hydroxyapatite and 2.78 for bone ash. Despite these numerical differences, the ratios are generally considered comparable in terms of structural similarity. The Ca/P Ratio serves as a critical indicator for evaluating sample purity against established bone hydroxyapatite standards, assessing structural integrity, acid-base properties, and industrial applicability. Furthermore, it influences the adsorption of amino acids and proteins, making it a vital parameter for characterizing both synthetic and naturally derived calcium phosphate materials and evaluating their structural resemblance to natural bone mineral.&lt;SEP&gt;Ca/P Ratio is a stoichiometric parameter measured in the study, with a value of 1.67 noted.&lt;SEP&gt;A stoichiometric ratio of 1:1 targeted for the calcium phosphate compound in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Ca/P Ratio is a compositional metric represented as Ca/P in the material study.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ca/P Ratio represents the molar proportion of calcium to phosphorus measured across different samples.&lt;SEP&gt;The Ca/P ratio is a key compositional parameter, with a value of 1.67 targeted in the synthesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792261046</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Brönsted Acid Sites">
  <data key="d0">Brönsted Acid Sites</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Brönsted Acid Sites are a type of acid site in hydroxyapatite derived from HPO4 2- species.&lt;SEP&gt;Brönsted Acid Sites are surface locations on apatites associated with hydroxyl vacancies where adsorption occurs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-006</data>
