<node id="Carbonated Hydroxyapatite">
  <data key="d0">Carbonated Hydroxyapatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbonated Hydroxyapatite is a chemically modified, highly crystalline variant of hydroxyapatite characterized by the incorporation of carbonate (CO3 2-) groups within its structure. This compound occurs naturally as a chemically distinct component of bone, where it forms as an impurity when phosphate reacts with atmospheric carbon dioxide. It is also recognized as a biologically derived material that exhibits carbonate release capabilities.

The material is frequently synthesized using wet chemical or hydrothermal methods, which allow for precise control over its composition and processing. These techniques can produce highly crystalline structures, such as nanorods, and the resulting material is often subjected to sintering. Following sintering, Carbonated Hydroxyapatite can be clearly identified in X-ray diffraction (XRD) data as a distinct peak. Its structural modifications and carbonate content are routinely evaluated using spectroscopic techniques, including Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and XRD.

Due to its modified composition, Carbonated Hydroxyapatite possesses enhanced properties compared to standard hydroxyapatite. These favorable characteristics, combined with its biological compatibility, make it a widely utilized bone substitute in biomedical and regenerative applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-011</data>
