<node id="Crystallinity">
  <data key="d0">Crystallinity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Crystallinity is a fundamental structural and material property that quantifies the degree of structural order within a substance, specifically representing the proportion of its crystalline phase relative to the amorphous phase. In the context of hydroxyapatite and related samples, it serves as a critical parameter for evaluating synthesis, characterization, and functional performance. The property is quantitatively assessed using analytical techniques such as X-ray diffraction, Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, and fast Fourier transform analysis. In spectroscopic data, it is indicated by sharp, well-defined peaks, hydroxyl group peak intensity, and varying depths of spectral dips, with specific samples like the "(Not shaken)" sample or synthesized hydroxyapatite exhibiting values as high as 90.4%. FFT patterns further confirm the presence and degree of structural order in prepared materials.

The degree of crystallinity significantly influences the physicochemical characteristics of the material. Higher crystallinity correlates with increased ion adsorption capacity, enhanced photocatalytic activity under ultraviolet irradiation, slower ion release rates, and greater regularity in particle shape. Conversely, it demonstrates an inverse relationship with specific surface area and overall sorption capacity. Comparative analyses, such as those between s-HAp and BA samples, further illustrate how crystallinity levels vary across different material formulations, with s-HAp particles typically displaying lower crystallinity than BA samples.

This structural property is highly sensitive to compositional and processing variables. It generally increases with longer reaction durations, hydrothermal reaction conditions, heat treatment, elevated calcination temperatures, phase breakdown conditions, decreasing citrate concentrations, and specific synthesis and milling parameters. However, the incorporation of carbonate, increased a/c ratios, and the presence of foreign phases typically reduce crystallinity. The impact of surfactant addition presents conflicting observations; while some studies report an increase in structural order, others indicate a reduction, suggesting that the outcome depends heavily on specific experimental conditions or surfactant types. Overall, crystallinity remains an essential metric for understanding and optimizing the behavior of hydroxyapatite-based and related synthesized materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-005</data>
