<node id="Hydrothermal Treatment">
  <data key="d0">Hydrothermal Treatment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrothermal treatment is a chemical-processing and synthesis technique that operates under high temperature and pressure conditions within aqueous solutions, typically utilizing an autoclave. This method functions as a versatile preparation route designed to facilitate controlled crystallization and material formation. It is predominantly applied to synthesize hydroxyapatite and related composites, including hydroxyapatite/titanium dioxide compounds, across diverse structural forms such as nanocrystals, nanoparticles, superstructures, microspheres, and rod-shaped architectures.

The specific operational parameters of hydrothermal treatment vary depending on the targeted material properties and desired output. Standard protocols commonly involve maintaining a reaction temperature of 120 °C for a duration of 12 hours to produce hydroxyapatite powder, bulk samples, or rod-shaped crystals. Alternative optimized procedures may shorten the synthesis timeframe to 4 hours. To further refine particle morphology and enhance reaction kinetics, the process is frequently integrated with mechanical assistance, including continuous agitation, shaking, and the use of milling media. Collectively, these adaptable conditions allow hydrothermal treatment to serve as a highly tunable chemical route for tailoring hydroxyapatite-based materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-006</data>
