<node id="TiO2">
  <data key="d0">TiO2</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0a5563c80c4fd1f68b4442f73621a281-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d2">Titanium dioxide (TiO2) is a chemical compound that is extensively analyzed and quantified across various material datasets. In compositional analysis data, its concentration is systematically recorded with associated numerical ranges, appearing as both a major chemical component measured in percentage and, in other contexts, as a trace element compound. The presence of TiO2 has been documented across multiple samples, including phosphogypsum variants, Portlandite, and potassium sulphate, where it is routinely measured in phosphogypsum samples.

Beyond its role in compositional tracking, TiO2 is investigated in specialized material applications, notably for its interaction with alendronate in dental implant coatings. However, in certain catalytic or material systems, TiO2 demonstrates a notable limitation characterized by rapid deactivation due to gold crystal growth. This susceptibility has prompted the exploration of highly durable alternatives, such as hydroxyapatite. Overall, TiO2 is a widely monitored chemical compound whose abundance and analytical treatment vary depending on the specific sample matrix or application context.</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792454671</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
