  <data key="d5">1791259997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intermolecular Hydrogen Bond">
  <data key="d0">Intermolecular Hydrogen Bond</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Intermolecular Hydrogen Bond is the force that allows citrate molecules to self-assemble and promote orderly aggregation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791259997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FT-IR">
  <data key="d0">FT-IR</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FT-IR) is a fundamental analytical and spectroscopic technique employed to characterize the chemical composition and molecular structure of various materials. The method operates by identifying and analyzing functional groups, chemical groups, molecular bonds, and chemical vibration bands within a given sample. By detecting these specific vibrational modes, FT-IR provides precise insights into the chemical makeup of synthesized materials and solid products, serving as a critical tool for structural verification in chemical and materials research.

In practical applications, FT-IR is utilized to obtain spectral patterns that track phase evolution and monitor changes in samples, including comparative analyses before and after thermal treatments such as calcining. The technique is also specifically applied to confirm the successful loading of compounds like hydroxyapatite (HAP) onto biochar (BC), as well as to characterize the chemical groups and bonds present in the hydration products of PGEF. Through these diverse uses, FT-IR remains an essential method for validating molecular structures, tracking chemical transformations, and ensuring the quality of modified and synthesized substances.&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier Transform Infrared spectroscopy is used to determine stoichiometric calcium-to-phosphorus ratios and verify chemical composition.&lt;SEP&gt;FT-IR is an analytical technique used to confirm the stoichiometric ratios of hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792425519</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sample Ratios">
  <data key="d0">Sample Ratios</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Sample Ratios refer to the varying proportions of citrate to calcium (e.g., 5/1, 4/1, 3/1) used in the prepared samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791259997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surface Charge">
  <data key="d0">Surface Charge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Surface Charge is a property of crystals that increases with high citrate concentration, affecting aggregation.&lt;SEP&gt;Surface charge is a property of HAP and 20HPA-HAP that changes with solution pH, influencing adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Surface charge influences nanoparticle aggregation, where larger particles exhibit stronger repulsive interactions and smaller particles facilitate easier adhesion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791835490</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unstable Colloid Nanoparticle Clusters">
  <data key="d0">Unstable Colloid Nanoparticle Clusters</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Unstable Colloid Nanoparticle Clusters are generated when citrate chelates calcium ions, eventually aggregating to form a muddy solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791259997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dipole Field">
  <data key="d0">Dipole Field</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dipole Field is a field that weakens when citrate concentration decreases, affecting the assembly mode of building blocks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791259997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="XRD Analysis">
  <data key="d0">XRD Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">XRD Analysis is a comprehensive analytical, crystallographic, and mineralogical characterization technique primarily utilized to examine the crystalline structures and determine the phase composition of various solid materials. By systematically evaluating diffraction patterns and identifying characteristic diffraction peaks, the method enables the precise identification of crystalline and mineral phases, assesses the overall degree of crystallinity, and quantifies specific structural parameters such as lattice parameters, crystallite sizes, peak widths, and particle sizes. The technique is fundamentally designed to study the phase and crystalline structure of prepared and synthesized materials, providing critical insights into their compositional and structural properties.

In practical applications, XRD Analysis is extensively employed across geology, chemistry, materials science, and engineering for material characterization, quality control, and fundamental research. It is frequently utilized to identify the crystalline structure and main components of raw phosphogypsum, as well as to determine the phase composition and crystallization states of MPG. The method is also instrumental in investigating the microstructure of MPG powder and PBSLM samples, often utilized alongside XRF. Furthermore, XRD Analysis effectively characterizes commercial and synthetic calcium carbonate, and examines PG-based mortar and concrete samples by identifying the diffraction peaks of both hydrated and unhydrated compounds to pinpoint specific mineral phases.

The technique also excels in tracking crystalline phase changes and elucidating dissolution-precipitation mechanisms. It is specifically applied to confirm the presence of pure-phase hydroxyapatite, and when comparing synthetic hydroxyapatite with natural biological samples, it effectively indicates low crystalline structures while detecting specific phases such as β-TCP and quartz. Through its systematic diffraction pattern evaluation, XRD Analysis remains an indispensable tool for accurately determining crystal structure, phase composition, and crystallinity across diverse scientific and industrial contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;XRD Analysis is an analytical technique used to identify decomposition products and check for elemental sulfur.&lt;SEP&gt;An analytical technique used to examine the crystal structure and compounds involved in the decomposition process.&lt;SEP&gt;An analytical technique used to investigate crystal structures and differentiate between mineral phases like brushite and gypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792404732</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FT-IR Analysis">
  <data key="d0">FT-IR Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">FT-IR analysis is a spectroscopic method used to identify chemical groups such as phosphate and carbonate in the samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A spectroscopic method used to identify functional groups and detect absorbed water within the sample structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Spectroscopic method used to verify the presence of characteristic functional groups in the samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A spectroscopic technique used to identify functional groups in synthetic gypsum and HAp samples.&lt;SEP&gt;FT-IR Analysis is a spectroscopic method used to identify functional groups and chemical bonds within the S-HAp structure.&lt;SEP&gt;An analytical technique used to verify the presence of characteristic functional groups in the samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A spectroscopic method used to identify characteristic chemical bonds and functional groups in the sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792297191</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SEM Imaging">
  <data key="d0">SEM Imaging</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">SEM imaging is a microscopic technique used to visualize the morphology of the products, revealing microspheres and microflakes.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM imaging is used to observe and document the morphology of as-prepared HAP samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Microscopy technique used to visualize the material, where darker areas indicate lower elevations or voids.&lt;SEP&gt;A microscopic imaging technique for which the sample is mounted using conductive materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrothermal Process">
  <data key="d0">Hydrothermal Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Hydrothermal Process is a synthesis technique and experimental condition that utilizes elevated temperature and pressure, typically within aqueous solutions, to produce hydroxyapatite and its various structural forms. This method is frequently employed to synthesize hydroxyapatite particles, nanocrystals, microspheres, and mesoporous rod-shaped structures, with operating temperatures commonly ranging between 120 and 180 °C. By precisely controlling key parameters such as temperature, pressure, reagent concentrations, and pH, the process enables the targeted fabrication of nanocrystalline hydroxyapatite with tailored size and morphology.

Beyond basic synthesis, the application of heat and pressure serves multiple functional roles within this technique. It significantly enhances the stability and crystallinity of the resulting material while facilitating the cleavage of Ca-NTA bonds to release calcium ions. Additionally, the controlled environment allows surfactants to effectively influence the final morphological characteristics of the product. Overall, the Hydrothermal Process provides a versatile and highly regulated method for producing hydroxyapatite-based materials with optimized physicochemical properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrothermal process is a synthesis technique frequently used to prepare nanocrystalline Hydroxyapatite with controlled size and morphology.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrothermal process is the synthesis technique where surfactants influence HAp morphology.&lt;SEP&gt;The hydrothermal process involves heating the reactor to 180°C for 15 hours to synthesize the material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792189472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Microspheres">
  <data key="d0">Microspheres</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Microspheres are spherical structures approximately 50 μm in size composed of microflakes perpendicular to the surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Microspheres are the synthesized spherical particles that transition from solid ACP to core-shell HA structures based on hydrothermal duration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791985846</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acetonedicarboxylate Complex">
  <data key="d0">Acetonedicarboxylate Complex</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Acetonedicarboxylate complex is a decomposition product of citrate under high temperature that participates in HAp crystallization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791259997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PO4 3- Groups">
  <data key="d0">PO4 3- Groups</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">PO4 3- groups are phosphate ions identified in all samples through FT-IR and XRD analysis, exhibiting characteristic stretching and bending modes.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphate functional groups identified through strong stretching vibration bands in the FT-IR spectrum.&lt;SEP&gt;PO4 3- Groups are phosphate functional groups exhibiting strong stretching vibration bands in the S-HAp structure.&lt;SEP&gt;PO4 3- groups are identified at 1011, 567, and 473 cm^-1 in the sample spectrum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792177218</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nucleation">
  <data key="d0">Nucleation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nucleation is a crystallization process suppressed by citrate during the hydrothermal synthesis of HAp.&lt;SEP&gt;Nucleation is the initial process in crystal formation that is regulated by the CTAB template.&lt;SEP&gt;A process regulated by CTAB during the synthesis of hydroxyapatite nanoparticles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791664401</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acetone">
  <data key="d0">Acetone</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Acetone is a decomposition product of citrate under high temperature and pressure during the hydrothermal process.&lt;SEP&gt;A solvent used for ultrasonic cleaning of the FTO substrate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791470403</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ultrasonic Dispersing Treatment">
  <data key="d0">Ultrasonic Dispersing Treatment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
