  <data key="d0">Adsorbed Water</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Adsorbed water is a chemical component identified by a broad absorption dip around 3400 cm⁻¹ in the spectra.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorbed water is linked to broad bands appearing at 3450 and 1636 cm^-1 in the IR spectra.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorbed water is water molecules present on the sample surface, identified by a specific bending mode peak.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorbed Water is identified by a broad infrared absorption band in the 3100–3500 cm⁻¹ range.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorbed Water is identified by a broad FTIR absorption band at 3100–3500 cm⁻¹ in the synthesized samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorbed water is present in the hydroxyapatite sample, indicated by a broad FTIR band between 3100 and 3500 cm^-1.&lt;SEP&gt;Water molecules indicated by the broad absorption band at ~3352.90 cm⁻¹.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792123905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wavenumber">
  <data key="d0">Wavenumber</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Wavenumber is a fundamental spectral measurement parameter and unit, expressed in inverse centimeters (cm⁻¹), that serves as the standard horizontal axis metric for infrared (IR) and Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopic graphs. In these analytical techniques, it is plotted along the x-axis to represent the frequency of infrared light and corresponding spectral ranges. While the standard operational range typically spans from 500 to 4000 cm⁻¹, specific applications or graphing conventions may utilize narrower scales, such as 600 to 1800 cm⁻¹, or display the axis in a descending format from 4000 to 500 cm⁻¹, often decreasing in increments of 500. In IR transmittance spectra, it specifically functions as the measured data variable used to indicate absorption peaks.

Within molecular spectroscopy, wavenumber serves as a critical analytical tool for identifying specific molecular vibrations and mapping the precise locations of infrared absorption bands. By correlating these measured values with distinct vibrational modes, it enables the accurate characterization of chemical compounds and functional groups. The consistent application of the cm⁻¹ unit across spectral data ensures standardized interpretation, making wavenumber an indispensable parameter in analytical chemistry and molecular structure determination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792427006</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Absorption Bands">
  <data key="d0">Absorption Bands</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Absorption bands are the characteristic dips in the spectral curves corresponding to specific chemical vibrations.&lt;SEP&gt;Absorption bands in the 1000–1100 cm⁻¹ range are characteristic spectral features indicating the presence of sulfate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791486417</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transmittance">
  <data key="d0">Transmittance</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Transmittance is a measured data parameter and vertical axis metric utilized in infrared spectroscopy, most notably within Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy. It is plotted on the y-axis of the spectrum graph to quantify the proportion of infrared light that successfully passes through a given sample. As a relative measurement, it serves as the primary intensity scale for the spectroscopic plot, functioning as a critical analytical variable for interpreting spectral data.

The metric is typically expressed as a percentage ranging from 0 to 100 percent, though it may also be recorded in arbitrary units depending on the specific instrumentation and calibration. Transmittance values are inversely related to the sample's interaction with infrared radiation: lower transmittance percentages indicate higher levels of infrared light absorption by the material, while higher percentages signify greater light transmission. This characteristic makes it an essential parameter for characterizing molecular composition and analyzing how samples attenuate or transmit infrared radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Transmittance is a percentage measurement on the y-axis of the infrared spectrum indicating infrared light absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Transmittance is a vertical axis metric measured in percentage, where lower values indicate higher infrared light absorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f2534f7a997df3f80305323317b15be9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792403444</data>
