<node id="Drying">
  <data key="d0">Drying</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Drying is a thermal and physical processing step primarily employed to remove moisture from various materials following synthesis, washing, or filtration. It functions as a critical downstream and final preparation operation designed to solidify, stabilize, and prepare the resulting products for subsequent use. The fundamental purpose of this procedure remains consistent across applications, serving as an essential phase in material processing workflows.

The apparent variations in temperature and duration do not represent conflicting definitions but rather reflect the application of drying to distinct materials and synthesis pathways. For example, when processing a cake or general synthesized materials, the process is typically conducted at 80 °C, occasionally extending for 24 hours as a controlled synthesis condition. In the recovery of calcium carbonate after filtration, drying is applied at 100 °C, whereas phosphogypsum requires a physical preparation step at 110 °C for two hours. Additionally, the technique is frequently utilized as a final step to solidify and prepare hydroxyapatite (HAp) particles or powder. The exact procedural approach and thermal conditions can vary significantly across different methodologies, notably differing from established protocols such as the one described by Smolen et al., which highlights the adaptable nature of drying across diverse chemical and material processing contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Drying is the final post-processing step that follows washing to complete the material preparation.&lt;SEP&gt;Drying is a processing step applied to phosphogypsum after deferrization.&lt;SEP&gt;Drying is a process step where phosphogypsum is heated at 165°C, 180°C, and 195°C for varying durations.&lt;SEP&gt;Drying is a process applied to phosphogypsum at specific temperatures to prepare it for mortar mixture preparation.&lt;SEP&gt;Drying is a physical treatment process applied to phosphogypsum samples to prepare them for leaching or further processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792486856</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Evaporation">
  <data key="d0">Evaporation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaporation is a fundamental concentration and separation process utilized across various chemical and industrial operations to reduce solution volume, remove liquid components, and isolate target compounds. By systematically decreasing the volume of liquid streams, the technique effectively increases the concentration of dissolved substances, preparing them for subsequent recovery or processing stages. The method operates by driving off solvent, thereby facilitating the phase transition of dissolved materials from liquid to solid states and enabling the efficient recovery of solid products from filtered solutions.

In practical applications, evaporation serves multiple specialized functions depending on the feedstock and desired output. It is commonly applied to filtrates and supernatant liquids to recover ammonium sulfate as a byproduct and to extract various sulfate compounds. The process is also integral to rare earth element extraction, where it concentrates leach solutions during indirect leaching to boost initial rare earth element levels. Furthermore, evaporation is used to precisely concentrate stripping solutions to specific target volumes, such as 200 mL, and to facilitate the direct conversion of liquid potassium sulfate into its solid form. Collectively, these applications highlight evaporation as a versatile unit operation designed to concentrate solutions, isolate valuable compounds, and prepare chemical streams for downstream processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792123048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Potassium Dihydrogen Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Potassium Dihydrogen Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Potassium Dihydrogen Phosphate (KH₂PO₄) is a versatile chemical compound consistently utilized as a fundamental reactant, precursor, and primary source of phosphorus across various synthesis and preparation protocols. It is commonly procured as a dedicated phosphate source and employed in precipitation and reflux synthesis methods. Beyond inorganic material fabrication, the compound also serves as a primary component in buffer preparation.

In specialized material synthesis, Potassium Dihydrogen Phosphate is frequently combined with phosphogypsum under hydrothermal conditions to produce hydroxyapatite and hydroxyapatite nanorods. Within this process, it fulfills a dual function: acting as a primary phosphate source that supplies essential potassium and phosphate ions, while simultaneously operating as a secondary precursor to balance the calcium and phosphate ratios derived from the gypsum. Additionally, the compound is introduced into CTAB surfactant solutions, where it initiates calcium phosphate layer formation and facilitates critical electrostatic interactions. These combined applications underscore its adaptability as a key chemical reagent in both structural material synthesis and solution-based chemical processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-005</data>
