  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Freundlich Isotherm Model">
  <data key="d0">Freundlich Isotherm Model</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Freundlich Isotherm Model is a mathematical framework used to interpret multilayer adsorption data on heterogeneous surfaces.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791287728</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adsorption Capacity">
  <data key="d0">Adsorption Capacity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adsorption Capacity is a quantitative metric that measures the maximum amount of adsorbate retained per unit mass of an adsorbent, typically evaluated at equilibrium. It serves as a fundamental indicator of how much substance a given adsorbent can effectively capture, providing a standardized basis for comparative analysis across different materials and experimental conditions.

In practical applications, this metric is frequently used to quantify the uptake of specific contaminants, such as heavy metal ions and fluoride. For example, when evaluating nanohydroxyapatite (nHAp) adsorbents, Adsorption Capacity specifically quantifies the mass of fluoride retained per gram of the material. Theoretical and empirical models often represent this capacity using parameters like the Freundlich constant, which helps characterize adsorption behavior and predict uptake limits.

The value of Adsorption Capacity is influenced by environmental and operational factors, with the maximum adsorption potential for certain substances, such as fluoride, observed to improve with increasing temperature. Ultimately, Adsorption Capacity remains a critical parameter for assessing adsorbent efficiency, defining the equilibrium limit of adsorbate retention relative to the adsorbent's mass.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792317876</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pb(II)">
  <data key="d0">Pb(II)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Pb(II) is a lead ion classified as a heavy metal ion that has been extensively studied for its targeted removal from aqueous solutions. Environmental research has systematically evaluated its adsorption behavior onto various nanocomposite materials, with particular emphasis on biochar (BC) and hydroxyapatite-modified biochar (HAP-BC). As a critical target pollutant, Pb(II) serves as a benchmark for assessing the efficacy of these adsorbents in heavy metal remediation.

The adsorption process for Pb(II) is characterized by monolayer coverage principles and closely follows the Langmuir adsorption model. Experimental studies have thoroughly analyzed its adsorption kinetics, removal capacity, removal percentage, and underlying removal mechanisms. Notably, Pb(II) exhibits the highest removal capacity among all metals tested in these comparative studies. Additionally, its competitive adsorption dynamics have been rigorously evaluated across varying pH levels, demonstrating consistent affinity and stability under different environmental conditions. Collectively, these findings underscore Pb(II)'s significance in advancing efficient, model-based strategies for heavy metal ion sequestration.&lt;SEP&gt;Pb(II) is a heavy metal ion targeted for adsorption using organophosphonate-modified hydroxyapatites.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791655882</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zn(II)">
  <data key="d0">Zn(II)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Zn(II) is a zinc ion classified as a heavy metal that is frequently targeted for removal from aqueous solutions. Its adsorption behavior has been extensively investigated on biochar (BC) and hydroxyapatite-biochar nanocomposites (HAP-BC). The adsorption process on these biochar-based materials adheres to multilayer coverage principles and is effectively described by the Freundlich adsorption isotherm model. Researchers have thoroughly evaluated the adsorption kinetics, equilibrium, removal capacity, and underlying mechanisms of Zn(II) on these substrates, often comparing its performance alongside other metals.

In competitive adsorption experiments, Zn(II) is commonly studied in conjunction with Pb(II) and Cu(II). However, its removal efficiency is notably influenced by environmental and chemical factors. The presence of competing divalent cations, particularly Ca(II) and Mg(II), significantly reduces its adsorption capacity. Additionally, elevated ionic strengths have been observed to slightly diminish the overall removal capacity of Zn(II) from solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Zn(II) is a heavy metal ion targeted for adsorption using organophosphonate-modified hydroxyapatites.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791655882</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Water Treatment">
  <data key="d0">Water Treatment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Primary environmental application benefiting from the high surface area and porosity of HAP-BC.&lt;SEP&gt;Water Treatment is an industrial and environmental application field utilizing adsorbent materials for pollutant removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Water treatment is an application area where PG and its derivatives are used to retain metals and anions.&lt;SEP&gt;An application area for calcium hydroxide as a common base.&lt;SEP&gt;Water treatment is an application area for sustainable photocatalysts derived from the synthesized Hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Water treatment is an application area for sustainable photocatalysts derived from the synthesized Hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-001</data>
