<node id="FESEM">
  <data key="d0">FESEM</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Field Emission Scanning Electron Microscopy (FESEM) is an advanced imaging and microscopy technique primarily utilized to capture high-resolution images for the detailed characterization of material structures. It is extensively employed to visualize and analyze surface and nanoscale morphology, enabling researchers to observe particle morphology, size, agglomerates, and nanoparticle distribution across various synthesized materials. The technique is particularly valuable for examining specific composite and modified substances, such as the surface morphology and particle assemblies of modified biochar, the structural characteristics of HAP-BC following recycling processes, and the nanoscale features of HAp.

Beyond qualitative morphological assessment, FESEM serves a critical quantitative function by providing the necessary imagery to measure the average crystallite size of samples. Through these diverse applications, FESEM functions as a versatile analytical tool in materials science and nanotechnology, bridging the gap between structural visualization and precise dimensional analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792189124</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hexagonal Pyromorphite">
  <data key="d0">Hexagonal Pyromorphite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Hexagonal pyromorphite is a crystalline mineral compound formed on HAP-BC during the adsorption of Pb(II).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chemisorption">
  <data key="d0">Chemisorption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Chemisorption is the adsorption mechanism indicated by the pseudo-second-order kinetic model for metal ion uptake on biochars.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Electrostatic Interactions">
  <data key="d0">Electrostatic Interactions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Electrostatic interactions are the primary removal mechanisms for Cu(II) and Zn(II) by HAP-BC and for all three ions by BC.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrostatic interactions are attractive forces promoted by negative zeta potential that enhance adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrostatic interactions are the primary mechanism driving hydroxyapatite-protein binding.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrostatic Interactions are molecular forces responsible for binding macromolecules to the adsorbent surface.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791748680</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adsorption Kinetics">
  <data key="d0">Adsorption Kinetics</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A study examining the influence of time intervals on the adsorption rate of heavy metals onto biochar.&lt;SEP&gt;The study of the rate and time-dependent behavior of heavy metal ion uptake by adsorbents.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption kinetics describes the rate and mechanistic pathway of heavy metal ion uptake by BC and HAP-BC in mixed solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption kinetics refers to the rate and mechanism of protein adsorption on HAp and other biomaterials.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption kinetics describes the rate and mechanism of adsorbate uptake onto an adsorbent over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792317751</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adsorption Isotherms">
  <data key="d0">Adsorption Isotherms</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adsorption isotherms describe the equilibrium adsorption capacities and selectivity ordering of BC and HAP-BC for ternary metal solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption isotherms are experimental curves used to derive BET surface area, cumulative pore volume, and average pore width for the powders.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption Isotherms are experimental curves analyzed to determine surface area, pore volume, and mesoporosity of the powders.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption isotherms refer to the study of adsorption equilibrium at constant temperature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791655701</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pseudo-first-order Kinetic Model">
  <data key="d0">Pseudo-first-order Kinetic Model</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The pseudo-first-order kinetic model is a mathematical framework used to analyze adsorption kinetics, though it provided a poorer fit than the pseudo-second-order model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
