<node id="Hydration">
  <data key="d0">Hydration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydration refers to a multifaceted chemical process and property that manifests differently across various material science and industrial contexts. In aqueous chemistry, it denotes a specific property of magnesium (Mg(II)) ions that enhances their interaction with water molecules while simultaneously reducing competitive adsorption. In the realm of construction materials, hydration describes the chemical reaction process within cementitious systems, which is notably influenced by the addition of phosphogypsum. When applied directly to phosphogypsum, hydration functions as a processing method that alters the crystal structure and texture of the material, though this process can be hindered by phosphorus impurities. Furthermore, when applied to calcined phosphogypsum, hydration serves as a subsequent treatment that effectively reduces heavy metal concentrations.

In metallurgical applications, hydration operates as a distinct chemical process involving residual sulfides in molten slag. As these sulfides undergo hydration, their chemical stability is compromised, leading to reduced performance that subsequently degrades the overall quality of slag wool. Collectively, these descriptions illustrate that hydration encompasses diverse chemical transformations and processing techniques that significantly influence the structural, chemical, and performance characteristics of ions, cementitious composites, phosphogypsum, and slag-based materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydration is a chemical process involved in the cooking and setting of synthetic phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydration is a chemical process accelerated by increasing curing temperature in the phosphogypsum matrix.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydration is the chemical reaction between cementitious materials and water, studied in phosphogypsum-fly ash binders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792452510</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adsorption Isotherm">
  <data key="d0">Adsorption Isotherm</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adsorption isotherm is a graphical representation showing the relationship between adsorbate concentration and adsorption capacity.&lt;SEP&gt;A scientific chart used to analyze the porosity and surface area of solid materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption isotherm describes the equilibrium relationship between the amount of fluoride adsorbed and its concentration in solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption Isotherm describes the relationship between equilibrium concentration and adsorption capacity at constant temperature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792318458</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Removal Efficiency">
  <data key="d0">Removal Efficiency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Removal Efficiency is a quantitative metric expressed as a calculated percentage that represents the fraction or proportion of a target substance successfully removed or extracted from a solution, wastewater, or source material. It serves as a standardized, objective measure across various chemical and environmental engineering contexts to evaluate the effectiveness of separation and purification processes.

In water treatment and environmental remediation, Removal Efficiency specifically measures the percentage of heavy metal ions removed from wastewater or aqueous solutions, typically utilizing adsorbents. This metric can be tracked over multiple operational cycles to assess the durability and sustained performance of the treatment materials. In resource recovery and nuclear waste management applications, Removal Efficiency quantifies the successful extraction or reduction of radioactive materials, radionuclides, and rare earth elements from source materials such as phosphogypsum. In these specialized contexts, the metric is often calculated as a leaching percent to evaluate the effectiveness of specific extraction or leaching processes. Together, these applications demonstrate how Removal Efficiency functions as a versatile indicator of process success across diverse industrial and scientific domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791790264</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
