<node id="Ternary System">
  <data key="d0">Ternary System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An experimental setup involving the simultaneous presence of Pb(II), Cu(II), and Zn(II) ions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Electrostatic Repulsion">
  <data key="d0">Electrostatic Repulsion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Electrostatic Repulsion is a force that hinders heavy metal adsorption when the biochar surface carries a positive charge.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrostatic repulsion is a phenomenon affecting adsorption at lower pH levels due to positively charged adsorbent surfaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrostatic repulsion is a force that reduces the adsorption process when adsorbents are positively charged.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791655145</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Electrostatic Attraction">
  <data key="d0">Electrostatic Attraction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Electrostatic Attraction is a force that enhances heavy metal adsorption when the biochar surface acquires a negative charge.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biochar Surface">
  <data key="d0">Biochar Surface</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Biochar Surface is the active interface of BC and HAP-BC where charge-dependent adsorption interactions occur.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Optimum Condition">
  <data key="d0">Optimum Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Optimum Condition refers to the specific experimental parameter (pH = 6) that yields the highest adsorption capacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Removal Percentages">
  <data key="d0">Removal Percentages</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Removal Percentages quantify the efficiency of heavy metal ion extraction by the adsorbents over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791288852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adsorption Rate">
  <data key="d0">Adsorption Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adsorption rate is the speed at which heavy metals are adsorbed onto material surfaces over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption Rate measures the speed at which heavy metal ions are uptake by BC and HAP-BC during the initial contact period.&lt;SEP&gt;Adsorption Rate indicates how quickly fluoride is adsorbed onto nHAp, increasing with temperature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792318805</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinetic Parameters">
  <data key="d0">Kinetic Parameters</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Kinetic parameters are quantitative values including rate constants and equilibrium capacities derived from model fitting.&lt;SEP&gt;Kinetic Parameters are mathematical values derived from pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order models to describe adsorption behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Kinetic Parameters are values calculated at various heating rates, including activation energy and pre-exponential factor.</data>
