<node id="SEM Analysis">
  <data key="d0">SEM Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">SEM Analysis is a microscopic imaging and analytical technique primarily employed to examine the surface morphology, microstructure, and particle characteristics of various solid materials. This method enables the detailed observation of structural features such as particle distribution, agglomeration, coalescence, crystal architecture, and morphological differences. By providing high-resolution visual data, SEM Analysis serves as a fundamental tool for characterizing the physical properties and structural integrity of diverse samples across scientific and industrial research.

The technique is widely applied across multiple material systems to address specific analytical needs. It is used to investigate the surface morphology of biochar samples and to observe particle structure, agglomeration, and coalescence following heat treatment. In cementitious and inorganic material studies, SEM Analysis examines the binder and the interfacial regions between magnesium oxysulfate cement and phosphogypsum, as well as the morphology and microstructure of hydration products. Additionally, it is utilized to analyze the surface crystal structure and particle morphology of decomposition products, the sliced crystal structure of phosphogypsum, and the morphological differences and particle distribution of HAp and gypsum samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792363202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
