<node id="FTIR Spectroscopy">
  <data key="d0">FTIR Spectroscopy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, commonly known as FTIR spectroscopy, is a fundamental analytical and characterization technique used to analyze infrared spectra and identify molecular vibrations within diverse samples. By generating detailed spectral data, the method facilitates both qualitative and quantitative chemical analysis, enabling researchers to detect characteristic chemical bands, functional groups, and specific absorption features. The technique operates by measuring how materials interact with infrared radiation, thereby revealing their vibrational properties and modes at a molecular level.

FTIR spectroscopy is widely applied across multiple scientific disciplines to examine the chemical and structural compositions of various complex materials. In environmental and agricultural research, it is utilized to study the biodegradation and transformation processes of organic waste, such as olive-mill residues. In materials science and biomedicine, the technique serves as a critical tool for characterizing calcium phosphate materials and hydroxyapatite, where it successfully identifies distinct absorption bands corresponding to phosphate, carbonate, and hydroxyl groups. Furthermore, FTIR spectroscopy is extensively employed to analyze the functional groups and structural characteristics of proteins, often in conjunction with hydroxyapatite samples, providing comprehensive insights into biomolecular interactions and material properties.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR Spectroscopy is an analytical technique employed to confirm the chemical conversion of PPG into HAp by identifying vibrational modes.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR Spectroscopy is an analytical technique employed to confirm the chemical conversion of PPG into HAp by identifying vibrational modes.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR Spectroscopy is an analytical technique used to identify chemical bonds and molecular structures in material samples.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR Spectroscopy is a technique used to generate stacked line graphs displaying chemical composition or molecular structure data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792427006</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differential Scanning Calorimetry">
  <data key="d0">Differential Scanning Calorimetry</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Differential scanning calorimetry is a technique used alongside FTIR to study olive-mill residue composting.&lt;SEP&gt;Differential Scanning Calorimetry is used alongside thermogravimetric analysis to record thermal changes and endothermic peaks of Hydroxyapatite.&lt;SEP&gt;Differential scanning calorimetry is used alongside thermogravimetric analysis to study thermal properties during decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Differential scanning calorimetry is a technique used to observe endothermic effects at specific temperatures during dehydration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-017</data>
