<node id="Steric Hindrance">
  <data key="d0">Steric Hindrance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Steric hindrance is a phenomenon caused by high concentrations of Na(I) and Cl^- that reduces heavy metal adsorption capacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791289149</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surface Adsorption Sites">
  <data key="d0">Surface Adsorption Sites</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Surface adsorption sites on HAP-BC are competed for by Na(I) and Cl^- at high ionic strengths, reducing heavy metal uptake.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791289149</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Monovalent Ions">
  <data key="d0">Monovalent Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Monovalent ions like Na(I) and Cl^- display little competition at low concentrations but cause steric hindrance at high concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791289149</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coexisting Cations">
  <data key="d0">Coexisting Cations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Coexisting cations such as Na(I), K(I), Ca(II), and Mg(II) compete with heavy metals for adsorption sites on HAP-BC.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791289149</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equilibrium Adsorption Capacity">
  <data key="d0">Equilibrium Adsorption Capacity</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Equilibrium adsorption capacity measures the amount of metal adsorbed per gram of adsorbent at equilibrium.&lt;SEP&gt;A quantitative metric measuring the maximum amount of heavy metal ions adsorbed per gram of adsorbent.&lt;SEP&gt;A quantitative metric (Qe) measured in milligrams per gram, representing the amount of metal ions adsorbed onto the material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9e17ad262bd9835860df552d7ff475f1-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791653869</data>
