<node id="Solid-State Reaction Method">
  <data key="d0">Solid-State Reaction Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Solid-state reaction method is a synthesis route used to prepare hydroxyapatite powders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Template-Directed Method">
  <data key="d0">Template-Directed Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Template-directed method is an alternative synthesis route for preparing hydroxyapatite powders.&lt;SEP&gt;Template-directed methods are chemical techniques employed to synthesize hydroxyapatite crystals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791486667</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Micro-Emulsion Method">
  <data key="d0">Micro-Emulsion Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Micro-emulsion method is an alternative synthesis route for preparing hydroxyapatite powders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zinc Ions">
  <data key="d0">Zinc Ions</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Zinc ions (Zn 2+) are heavy ions that can be removed from wastewater using hydroxyapatite powders.&lt;SEP&gt;Zinc Ions are heavy metal ions (Zn2+) targeted for removal from waste water using the powders.&lt;SEP&gt;Zinc ions are heavy metal pollutants targeted for removal by hydroxyapatite-biochar nanocomposites in comparative adsorption studies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791656147</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Micropores">
  <data key="d0">Micropores</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Micropores are structural features in hydroxyapatite particles that influence protein adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Micropores are small voids formed during early hydration and later stages, providing conditions for water storage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Macroporous Structure">
  <data key="d0">Macroporous Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Macroporous structure is a pore architecture that forms alongside mesopores in hydroxyapatite powders after heat treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surface Topography">
  <data key="d0">Surface Topography</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Surface Topography refers to the physical texture and scale characteristics of a material's surface.&lt;SEP&gt;The three-dimensional surface structure of the sample, indicated by particle shadows.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scale Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Scale Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Scale Analysis is a procedural approach used to determine the dimensions and magnification of microscopic images.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Field of View">
  <data key="d0">Field of View</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Field of View is a measurement representing the visible spatial extent of the SEM micrograph.&lt;SEP&gt;Field of View represents the measurable width of an image, estimated at 1.5 to 2.0 micrometers based on a scale bar.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure Label C">
  <data key="d0">Figure Label C</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure Label C is a marker indicating the third panel of a multi-part scientific figure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cumulative Pore Volume">
  <data key="d0">Cumulative Pore Volume</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Cumulative pore volume is a quantitative metric derived from nitrogen adsorption isotherms, representing the total volume of pores in the synthesized powders.&lt;SEP&gt;Cumulative Pore Volume is a quantitative metric measuring pore capacity in cubic centimeters per gram, peaking at approximately 0.64 cm³/g for the Surf Sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Average Pore Width">
  <data key="d0">Average Pore Width</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Average pore width is a quantitative metric derived from nitrogen adsorption isotherms, indicating the mean width of pores across the powder samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Average Pore Width is a quantitative metric measuring pore diameter in nanometers, showing an inverse trend to surface area and pore volume.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surf Sample">
  <data key="d0">Surf Sample</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Surf Sample is an untreated material specimen exhibiting the highest surface area and pore volume among tested conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Surf sample is an untreated specimen that remains largely amorphous.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surf-900C Sample">
  <data key="d0">Surf-900C Sample</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Surf-900C Sample is a heat-treated material specimen showing drastically reduced surface area and pore volume but increased pore width.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NoSurf Sample">
  <data key="d0">NoSurf Sample</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">NoSurf Sample is an untreated material specimen with moderate surface area and low pore volume.&lt;SEP&gt;The NoSurf sample is an untreated specimen that remains largely amorphous.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NoSurf-900C Sample">
  <data key="d0">NoSurf-900C Sample</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">NoSurf-900C Sample is a heat-treated material specimen exhibiting low surface area and pore volume but the widest average pore width.&lt;SEP&gt;The NoSurf-900C sample is a treated specimen that exhibits the highest degree of crystallinity and forms distinct Hydroxyapatite phases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scherrer Equation">
  <data key="d0">Scherrer Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Scherrer Equation is a mathematical formula utilized to calculate or estimate the average crystallite size of materials based on X-ray diffraction (XRD) data. It functions by establishing an inverse relationship between the crystallite dimensions and the width or broadening of diffraction peaks. This fundamental principle allows researchers to derive nanoscale structural information directly from standard diffraction measurements, making it a cornerstone technique in materials characterization.

In practical applications, the equation is extensively employed across nanotechnology and the analysis of prepared samples to quantify particle sizes. While broadly applicable to various crystalline substances, it is specifically recognized for its utility in determining the crystallite size of specialized materials, such as hydroxyapatite powders. By consistently linking diffraction peak characteristics to physical dimensions, the Scherrer Equation remains a vital analytical tool for understanding material microstructures.&lt;SEP&gt;The Scherrer Equation is a mathematical formula used to estimate the average crystallite size from XRD data.&lt;SEP&gt;Scherrer Equation is a mathematical formula used to estimate the average crystallite size of prepared samples from XRD data.&lt;SEP&gt;The Scherrer Equation is a mathematical formula applied to XRD diffraction peaks to calculate the crystalline size of nHAp.&lt;SEP&gt;The Scherrer Equation is used to calculate HA crystallite size from X-ray diffraction data.&lt;SEP&gt;The Scherrer equation is a mathematical formula applied to X-ray diffraction data to calculate crystallite size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792426134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Crystallites">
  <data key="d0">Crystallites</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Crystallites are microscopic crystal grains within a material whose size is determined using diffraction analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330579</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Peak Broadening">
  <data key="d0">Peak Broadening</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Peak Broadening represents the widening of diffraction peaks, serving as a key variable in crystallite size calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;Represents the Full Width at Half Maximum of a diffraction peak, indicating crystal size inversely.&lt;SEP&gt;A phenomenon in XRD patterns caused by intrinsic and crystalline size effects in nanocrystals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791835638</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Full Width at Half Maximum">
  <data key="d0">Full Width at Half Maximum</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Full Width at Half Maximum is the specific measurement metric used to quantify peak broadening in diffraction patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;A measurement of diffraction peak width used in X-ray diffraction analysis to quantify peak broadening.&lt;SEP&gt;Full Width at Half Maximum (FWHM) is a measured spectral value used as an input variable in the Scherrer equation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792426483</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chart A">
  <data key="d0">Chart A</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chart A is the bottom vertical bar chart displaying BET Surface Area data for four sample conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Chart A is a filled area chart illustrating the growth of scientific publications over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Chart A is a dual-axis combination chart plotting sulfur content and desulfurization rate against time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-057&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792098242</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chart B">
  <data key="d0">Chart B</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chart B is the middle vertical bar chart displaying Cumulative Pore Volume data for four sample conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791330905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chart C">
  <data key="d0">Chart C</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chart C is the top vertical bar chart displaying Average Pore Width data for four sample conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Chart C is a horizontal bar chart illustrating the volume of publications for various phosphogypsum applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-057</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791428038</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Diagonal Hatching Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Diagonal Hatching Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Diagonal Hatching Pattern is a visual styling applied to the bars in all three charts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Powder Surf">
  <data key="d0">Powder Surf</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Powder Surf is a hydroxyapatite sample synthesized with a surfactant, exhibiting a unimodal and uniform particle size distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Powder Surf is a synthesized powder prepared with surfactant, exhibiting higher surface area and pore volume before heat treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A synthesized HA powder produced using CTAB surfactant, resulting in a pure phase without b-TCP.&lt;SEP&gt;A synthesized powder produced using surfactant, characterized by high carbonate ion adsorption and needle-like morphology.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Powder NoSurf">
  <data key="d0">Powder NoSurf</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Powder NoSurf is a hydroxyapatite sample synthesized without a surfactant, exhibiting a bimodal and less uniform particle size distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Powder NoSurf is a synthesized powder prepared without surfactant, showing different pore characteristics and phase composition compared to Powder Surf.&lt;SEP&gt;A synthesized HA powder produced without surfactant, which exhibits larger particles and minor b-TCP formation.&lt;SEP&gt;A synthesized powder produced without surfactant, exhibiting lower surface area and carbonate adsorption compared to Powder Surf.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FESEM Imaging">
  <data key="d0">FESEM Imaging</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">FESEM Imaging is a microscopic imaging technique used to evidence the presence of large particles in the powders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DLS Measurement">
  <data key="d0">DLS Measurement</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">DLS Measurement is a dynamic light scattering technique used to measure particle size distribution in the powders.&lt;SEP&gt;Dynamic light scattering data used to characterize particle size distribution in the synthesized powders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HA Phase">
  <data key="d0">HA Phase</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">HA Phase is hydroxyapatite, the predominant crystalline phase observed in all powders except for minor β-TCP peaks in specific samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="β-TCP Phase">
  <data key="d0">β-TCP Phase</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">β-TCP Phase is beta-tricalcium phosphate, a secondary crystalline phase that appears in Powder NoSurf-900C and as a shoulder in Powder NoSurf.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JCPDS 09-0432">
  <data key="d0">JCPDS 09-0432</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">JCPDS 09-0432 is a standard diffraction data card used for identifying the HA phase in XRD analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A standard diffraction data card utilized for identifying the HA phase in synthesized powders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-008</data>
