<node id="Secondary Electron Imaging">
  <data key="d0">Secondary Electron Imaging</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Secondary Electron Imaging is the SEM mode used to visualize surface topography based on secondary electron emission.&lt;SEP&gt;Secondary Electron Imaging is the standard microscopy mode used to visualize the surface topography of the phosphogypsum sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792483036</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Accelerating Voltage">
  <data key="d0">Accelerating Voltage</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Accelerating Voltage is a technical parameter set to 20kV to accelerate the electron beam during imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;Accelerating Voltage is an instrumental parameter set to 5.00 kV for the microscopy analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Accelerating Voltage is a technical parameter set to 15.0kV during the electron microscopy imaging process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792483036</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Magnification">
  <data key="d0">Magnification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnification is a fundamental instrumental and imaging parameter utilized primarily in microscopy, particularly within Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM). It functions as a technical setting that scales a captured image relative to the actual physical dimensions of the specimen, thereby determining the effective zoom level and the degree of fine detail visible in the resulting micrographs. As a variable parameter rather than a fixed constant, its specific value is adjusted according to the observational requirements of each imaging session.

Across various documented captures, Magnification has been applied at multiple distinct levels, including 1,000x, 2,000x, 30,000x, 100,000x, and 200,000x (also noted as 200KX). To ensure accurate spatial interpretation alongside these varying zoom levels, the parameter is frequently accompanied by technical annotations and scale bars, such as those measuring 1 µm and 3 µm. Together, these elements provide a comprehensive framework for interpreting the magnified visual data and maintaining precise dimensional context in scientific imaging.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792407953</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
