<node id="HA">
  <data key="d0">HA</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">HA refers to hydroxyapatite, the base material of the synthesized powders, which undergoes shrinkage and structural transformation from mesoporous to meso- plus macroporous upon heating.&lt;SEP&gt;HA is the abbreviation for Hydroxyapatite, a crystalline mineral phase.&lt;SEP&gt;HA is a categorical row label within a statistical data table.&lt;SEP&gt;HA refers to a crystalline phase identified by lattice fringes with 0.344 nm interplanar spacing and diffraction rings corresponding to (002) and (211) planes.&lt;SEP&gt;HA is the synthesized material whose particle size, morphology, and aggregation were analyzed using various microscopy and milling techniques.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-afe70f2d82515df80906bf3babd4e4eb-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792426621</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mesoporous HA Structure">
  <data key="d0">Mesoporous HA Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mesoporous HA structure is the initial structural state of the hydroxyapatite powders before heat treatment, characterized by fine pores.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Meso-Plus Macroporous Structure">
  <data key="d0">Meso-Plus Macroporous Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Meso-Plus Macroporous Structure is the resulting structural state of the powders after heating at 900°C, indicating the formation of larger pores alongside mesopores.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TEM Analysis">
  <data key="d0">TEM Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">TEM analysis is a technique used in cited studies to evidence the formation of nano-sized pores along HA rods after calcination.&lt;SEP&gt;TEM analysis is a microscopy technique used to observe the structure of HAp.&lt;SEP&gt;TEM Analysis is an imaging technique used to verify the formation of elongated Hydroxyapatite nanorods.&lt;SEP&gt;TEM Analysis is a characterization technique used to propose the formation mechanism based on evolutionary information.&lt;SEP&gt;TEM analysis is a microscopy technique used to observe the structure of HAp.&lt;SEP&gt;TEM Analysis is an imaging technique used to verify the formation of elongated Hydroxyapatite nanorods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792189124</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Particle Growth Inhibition">
  <data key="d0">Particle Growth Inhibition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Particle growth inhibition is a mechanism during powder synthesis where surfactants act as templates to restrict crystal expansion, resulting in higher surface area.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Process Temperature">
  <data key="d0">Process Temperature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Process temperature is a critical synthesis parameter that controls the surface area of hydroxyapatite crystals, independent of the surfactant type.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791331657</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CTAB Surfactant">
  <data key="d0">CTAB Surfactant</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A chemical agent used during synthesis to produce pure hydroxyapatite powders, alter particle morphology, and increase surface area.&lt;SEP&gt;CTAB Surfactant forms assemblies that interact with phosphate ions and provide nucleation sites for Hydroxyapatite growth.&lt;SEP&gt;An organic molecule mentioned in the sample name that may contribute to the detected Carbon peak.&lt;SEP&gt;CTAB Surfactant forms assemblies that interact with phosphate ions and provide nucleation sites for Hydroxyapatite growth.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792189983</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FTIR Analysis">
  <data key="d0">FTIR Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">FTIR Analysis is a spectroscopic and analytical technique primarily utilized to examine the molecular and chemical characteristics of various materials. At its core, the method identifies vibrational modes, molecular vibrations, and characteristic peaks associated with specific chemical bonds within prepared samples. By detecting these spectral signatures, the technique enables the precise identification of functional groups, such as phosphate, hydroxyl, and carbonate, particularly within calcium apatites and synthesized powders. It is frequently employed to confirm the predominant hydroxyapatite phase in synthesized materials and to support detailed conclusions regarding the chemical composition of specific samples, such as HAp-CTAB-9.

Beyond phase and composition verification, FTIR Analysis serves as a versatile tool for analyzing post-adsorption surface changes and identifying coordination reactions. It is also applied to confirm the presence of structural defects like oxygen vacancies, quantify chemical mixtures, and broadly study material properties. Through these capabilities, FTIR Analysis provides comprehensive chemical and structural characterization across diverse scientific and industrial contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR Analysis is a spectroscopic technique used to identify vibrational modes of chemical bonds in the prepared samples.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR analysis is an analytical technique used to support conclusions regarding the chemical composition of the HAp-CTAB-9 sample.&lt;SEP&gt;Analytical technique used to confirm the presence of oxygen vacancies.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR Analysis is an experimental technique used to identify chemical bonds and ions within MPG.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792301054</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vibrational Modes">
  <data key="d0">Vibrational Modes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Specific atomic movement patterns of chemical bonds identified through spectroscopic analysis, including stretching and bending modes.&lt;SEP&gt;Vibrational Modes are specific atomic bond movements, such as symmetric stretching and bending, characteristic of phosphate tetrahedra.&lt;SEP&gt;Vibrational modes such as bending, stretching, and rocking describe the infrared spectral behavior of chemical groups like nitrate and water.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791813449</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Powder Surf-900C">
  <data key="d0">Powder Surf-900C</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A heat-treated version of the surfactant-synthesized powder, showing reduced surface area and increased crystallinity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-011</data>
