<node id="Flexural Strength">
  <data key="d0">Flexural Strength</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Flexural strength is a fundamental mechanical property that quantifies a material's resistance to bending and deformation under load, frequently serving as a tensile resistance metric. It is typically evaluated using standardized testing protocols, such as one-point bending tests on prismatic samples, with results recorded in megapascals (MPa) at defined curing intervals, commonly 28 days. This property is routinely measured alongside compressive strength to characterize structural integrity and durability across a wide spectrum of traditional and composite building materials, including mortar, concrete, flooring tiles, and stabilized adobe.

In phosphogypsum-based systems, flexural strength exhibits significant variability heavily influenced by composition, processing conditions, and curing environments. The property generally improves with the use of thermally or heat-treated phosphogypsum, elevated modification temperatures, and extended drying times. Performance is further enhanced by incorporating cementitious materials, recycled waste glass, sodium hydroxide, and reinforcing fibers such as polypropene, mineral, or glass. In specific formulations like PBSLM, higher phosphogypsum content can increase flexural strength, with treated variants showing up to a 46% improvement in alpha-hemihydrate applications. Conversely, when phosphogypsum is introduced into standard mortar mixes or autoclaved brick manufacturing, flexural strength typically declines as replacement percentages rise, and the property remains notably sensitive to surface curing conditions. Despite these variations, flexural strength remains a critical benchmark for evaluating bending resistance, with mortar formulations demonstrating relative stability even as Plaster of Paris content increases from 40% to 55% and generally following compressive strength trends in mixed applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
