<node id="Civil Engineering">
  <data key="d0">Civil Engineering</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Civil engineering is the discipline that explores the application and future growth prospects of phosphogypsum in construction.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil engineering is the field where PG has various potential applications, including in mass concrete and retaining walls.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil engineering is the discipline that can benefit from interdisciplinary growth in Phosphogypsum-based materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil engineering is the construction field where phosphogypsum-based materials are developed and applied.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil engineering is a field exploring the resource utilization and development of phosphogypsum-based construction materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil engineering is a broad field where phosphogypsum-based materials are utilized for resource development.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil engineering is a field of application for phosphogypsum-based materials developed through resource utilization efforts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-049&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792403444</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AFm Phase">
  <data key="d0">AFm Phase</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">AFm phase is a mineral formation that develops when PG is added to calcium aluminate cement, reducing compressive strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791363034</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Air Curing">
  <data key="d0">Air Curing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Air curing is a treatment method applied to cement samples during strength testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Air curing is a curing condition under which mortar compressive strength decreases between 10 and 53% with PG addition.&lt;SEP&gt;Air curing is a long-term curing method applied to samples to determine compressive strength at specified ages.&lt;SEP&gt;Air curing is a standard environmental conditioning method that results in significant strength reductions with high phosphogypsum content.&lt;SEP&gt;Air curing is a conditioning process where mortar mixes are kept in air for specified durations to evaluate strength.&lt;SEP&gt;Air Curing is a curing method that yields higher mortar strengths compared to moist room curing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792503702</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Magnesium Potassium Phosphate Cement">
  <data key="d0">Magnesium Potassium Phosphate Cement</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnesium potassium phosphate cement is a material whose compressive strength is negatively impacted by PG addition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791363306</data>
