  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wang et al.">
  <data key="d0">Wang et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wang et al. is a research group that has conducted extensive investigations into the sustainable valorization of phosphogypsum and the controlled synthesis of advanced nanomaterials for environmental remediation. In their work on phosphogypsum processing, the researchers combined lime treatment with flotation techniques to effectively purify the industrial byproduct. They also documented the material's high reactivity and rapid carbonation rate, emphasizing its potential for carbon dioxide capture applications. Additionally, Wang et al. developed key technologies for the low-temperature decomposition of phosphogypsum to generate high concentrations of sulfur dioxide, successfully establishing a demonstration facility to validate this process.

Beyond phosphogypsum utilization, the group has made significant contributions to the precise synthesis of hydroxyapatite. Through microwave irradiation synthesis, they observed that crystal growth is directly proportional to both reaction time and temperature. To achieve targeted morphological control, Wang et al. successfully regulated hydroxyapatite crystal growth by incorporating a cationic surfactant, specifically hexadecyltrimethylammonium bromide. Building upon this controlled synthesis methodology, the researchers fabricated a hydroxyapatite-biochar nanocomposite engineered for the efficient removal of multiple heavy metal ions from aqueous systems. Collectively, these studies highlight Wang et al.'s broad expertise in material processing, crystal engineering, and pollution control technologies.&lt;SEP&gt;Wang et al. are researchers who investigated the strength properties of CPGFM with specific HPG and activator ratios.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers cited alongside Liu et al. regarding the application of Game Theory for weight integration.&lt;SEP&gt;Wang et al. are researchers who divided purchased inputs into renewable and nonrenewable resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792225627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mohammad Salem Al-Hwaiti">
  <data key="d0">Mohammad Salem Al-Hwaiti</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Mohammad Salem Al-Hwaiti is a researcher who successfully leached 226Ra from phosphogypsum using chemical combinations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791364578</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="226Ra">
  <data key="d0">226Ra</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">226Ra is a radioactive isotope and radionuclide that is extensively monitored in environmental and industrial settings, particularly within phosphogypsum (PG) and phosphogypsum stacks. It is consistently identified as a radioactive contaminant and impurity in PG materials, where its presence is systematically measured during radioactivity analyses of PG samples and stack fluids. Environmental research has focused heavily on characterizing the dissolution behavior of 226Ra from phosphogypsum, evaluating how the isotope migrates and interacts with surrounding media under various conditions.

The management and remediation of 226Ra have been addressed through targeted leaching techniques, with studies confirming that specific chemical and aqueous combinations, alongside organic solvent leaching methods, can effectively extract and successfully remove the isotope from PG. The environmental and regulatory significance of 226Ra is underscored by its concentration levels in phosphogypsum, which directly determine regulatory compliance for the material's agricultural application. Additionally, while the measured activity of 226Ra in phosphogypsum stack fluids remains relatively low, it is consistently noted to be slightly elevated above normal groundwater values, highlighting its ongoing relevance in environmental monitoring and waste management frameworks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792486704</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
