<node id="Tensile Strength">
  <data key="d0">Tensile Strength</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tensile strength is a fundamental mechanical property of mortar and mortar mixes, primarily utilized to evaluate material performance and structural integrity. It is quantified in megapascals (MPa), typically ranging from 0 to 4.5 MPa, and is rigorously assessed using briquette specimens alongside standardized testing protocols. This metric serves as a critical indicator of how mortar responds to compositional changes and environmental conditions during its lifecycle.

The influence of phosphogypsum (PG) substitution on tensile strength presents varying reported trends. While several observations indicate a consistent decline in strength as PG replacement percentages increase, other data suggest an initial improvement with up to 10% PG substitution, followed by a reduction at higher replacement levels. Additionally, tensile strength values are generally higher in air-cured specimens. Across different curing ages, the performance behavior of tensile strength closely mirrors that of flexural strength, demonstrating parallel trends and reinforcing its reliability as a comparative mechanical benchmark.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792502258</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Selenite Gypsum">
  <data key="d0">Selenite Gypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Selenite gypsum is a form of gypsum whose strength development and setting time are negatively affected by soluble phosphate contaminants.&lt;SEP&gt;Selenite gypsum is a mineral used as a comparison material for setting time studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Selenite gypsum is a crystalline form of gypsum interground with clinker to manufacture cement.&lt;SEP&gt;Selenite gypsum is a natural mineral used as a comparative material to phosphogypsum in cement manufacturing studies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792450981</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gypsum Hemihydrate">
  <data key="d0">Gypsum Hemihydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Gypsum hemihydrate is a material whose functionality is negatively impacted by contaminants such as soluble phosphorus and organic substances in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Gypsum Hemihydrate is another name for calcium sulfate hemihydrate, mentioned in the text regarding its positive correlation with holding time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792301205</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soluble Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Soluble Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Soluble phosphate is a contaminant that negatively impacts the strength development and setting time of selenite gypsum when present above 1.0%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365237</data>
