<node id="Bhadauria">
  <data key="d0">Bhadauria</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Bhadauria is a researcher who observed varying improvements and declines in mortar compressive strength at different PG concentrations and curing ages.&lt;SEP&gt;Bhadauria is a researcher who documented the decline in concrete workability with PG substitution.&lt;SEP&gt;Bhadauria demonstrated substantial initial setting time prolongation when replacing cement with phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A researcher who documented prolonged initial and final setting times in cement mixtures with PG substitution.&lt;SEP&gt;Bhadauria partially replaced cement in pastes in 2006 with PG to observe soundness expansion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792452663</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mun">
  <data key="d0">Mun</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Mun is a researcher who demonstrated that mortar compressive strength increases with PG concentration up to 12% before deteriorating.&lt;SEP&gt;Mun is a researcher who used milk of lime to wash raw phosphogypsum at a specific ratio.&lt;SEP&gt;Mun washed, dried, and calcined phosphogypsum to produce treated phosphogypsum for alkali-activated cement manufacturing.&lt;SEP&gt;Mun is a researcher who investigated the chemical resistance of mortars containing calcined phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Mun investigated microstructural changes in 2008, linking ettringite formation to denser microstructures.&lt;SEP&gt;Mun is a researcher who investigated phosphogypsum treatment for activating blast furnace slag in non-sintering cement production.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792484618</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hou">
  <data key="d0">Hou</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Hou is a researcher who reported that early PG addition enhances AFt development and that PG and fly ash have a beneficial synergistic impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Hou synthesized calcium sulfate whiskers from washed phosphogypsum and compared their purity to unwashed samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791772487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CeSeH Gels">
  <data key="d0">CeSeH Gels</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">CeSeH Gels are non-crystalline geopolymer system products anticipated to form from cement hydration in the presence of PG.&lt;SEP&gt;CeSeH Gels are cementitious gel formations in the mortar microstructure noted for their compact composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365515</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AFm">
  <data key="d0">AFm</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">AFm is a hydration compound produced when PG concentration reaches 5%, replacing other diffraction peaks.&lt;SEP&gt;AFm is a mineral phase produced in the mortar mixture, with its quantity dependent on the available sulphate content.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365515</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AFt">
  <data key="d0">AFt</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">AFt is a hydration product whose development is enhanced by early PG addition.&lt;SEP&gt;AFt is a hydration product in PGCM that increases with prolonged curing age.&lt;SEP&gt;AFt is a cementitious gel that interlaces with C-S-H to form a dense network structure beneficial for immobilization.&lt;SEP&gt;AFt is a hydration product whose precipitation is promoted by the incorporation of gamma alumina.&lt;SEP&gt;A needle-like hydration product formed from the reaction of tricalcium aluminate and gypsum, contributing to mortar density.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792297406</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moist Curing">
  <data key="d0">Moist Curing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Moist curing is a curing condition under which mortar compressive strength decreases between 13 and 71% with PG addition.&lt;SEP&gt;Moist curing is a hydration maintenance technique that similarly reduces strength metrics when phosphogypsum substitution increases.&lt;SEP&gt;Moist curing is a laboratory curing regime conducted in a moist room at 23°C.&lt;SEP&gt;Moist curing is a conditioning process where mortar mixes are kept in moist environments to facilitate strength development.&lt;SEP&gt;Moist Curing is a curing method that results in lower mortar strengths compared to air curing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792502258</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C2AH8">
  <data key="d0">C2AH8</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">C2AH8 is a hydration compound that predominated in PG-free samples but disappeared at 5% PG concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
