<node id="Semi-hydrated Gypsum">
  <data key="d0">Semi-hydrated Gypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Semi-hydrated Gypsum is a chemical compound formed during the Non-immersing Method, possessing a permeable and disordered microstructure.&lt;SEP&gt;Semi-hydrated Gypsum is an intermediate product formed from the dehydration of dihydrate gypsum during thermal processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792301772</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dihydrate Gypsum">
  <data key="d0">Dihydrate Gypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Dihydrate Gypsum is a calcium sulfate mineral phase and chemical compound that serves as a primary component of phosphogypsum. It represents the final crystalline product generated through the complete hydration and recrystallization of hemihydrated (semi-hydrated) gypsum. This hydration process is actively promoted by calcium ions, during which the mineral's crystals nucleate and grow on existing complexes. Within phosphogypsum-based cementitious materials (PGCM) and related composites (PGEF), Dihydrate Gypsum functions as a crucial structural element that enhances mechanical properties and contributes to the overall integrity of the material.

Despite its role in initial structural development, Dihydrate Gypsum exhibits distinct behavior during moisture loss and drying stages. When exposed to drying conditions, whether at low temperatures or elevated heat, the mineral partially dehydrates back into hemihydrate gypsum. This dissolution and subsequent dehydration are accelerated during the drying phase, and the material's dissolution rate has been observed to increase with curing age. While the initial hydration and crystallization strengthen the composite, the accelerated dissolution and partial dehydration during moisture loss can ultimately contribute to material degradation over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792301341</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydration-Recrystallization Technique">
  <data key="d0">Hydration-Recrystallization Technique</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydration-Recrystallization Technique is an innovative manufacturing process that transforms semi-hydrated gypsum into dihydrate gypsum, enhancing brick properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-016</data>
