<node id="Calcium Aluminate Sulphate Hydrate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Aluminate Sulphate Hydrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium aluminate sulphate hydrate is a hydration product whose diffraction peaks appear as PG levels increase.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C-S-H Gel">
  <data key="d0">C-S-H Gel</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">C-S-H gel is an amorphous hydration product present in large quantities in concrete mixes without PG.&lt;SEP&gt;C-S-H gel is a hydration product generated from calcium and sulfate ions that contributes to the consolidation and strength of phosphogypsum filling bodies.&lt;SEP&gt;C-S-H gel is a flocculent hydration product that forms around phosphogypsum particles at low phosphorus levels.&lt;SEP&gt;C-S-H gel is a hydration product that interlaces with ettringite to form a dense network structure.&lt;SEP&gt;C-S-H Gel is a chemical compound formed during slag activation with PG, linked to reduced chloride permeability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792453181</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydration Process">
  <data key="d0">Hydration Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydration process is the chemical reaction in concrete where PG acts as an activator and filler, consuming compounds like calcium sulfate dihydrate.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydration process is the chemical transformation where hemihydrate gypsum converts to dihydrate gypsum, reducing heavy metals.&lt;SEP&gt;The hydration process transforms PG and MPG, affecting heavy metal leaching and ecological safety.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792299784</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Waste Wood Fibre">
  <data key="d0">Waste Wood Fibre</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Waste wood fibre is added to phosphogypsum specimens to improve their thermal and acoustic insulation properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Biowashing">
  <data key="d0">Biowashing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Biowashing is a treatment process used to remove impurities and heavy metals from raw and calcined phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;A technique used to remove impurities and heavy metals from raw and calcined phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792096852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wu F">
  <data key="d0">Wu F</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wu F is a researcher who studied the semidry high target solidification/stabilization of harmful impurities in phosphogypsum and its harmless treatment technology.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Potgieter JH">
  <data key="d0">Potgieter JH</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Potgieter JH investigated chemical and physical treatments of South African phosphogypsum for use as a cement set retarder.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Purification Process">
  <data key="d0">Purification Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The purification process removes contaminants from washed phosphogypsum to produce pure phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment method whose effectiveness can be evaluated through microstructural analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Purification processes are employed to remove impurities from phosphogypsum to make it suitable for cement and plaster manufacture.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4d02158bbc770d7c6bd9657b3f3defc-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792455042</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kandil et al.">
  <data key="d0">Kandil et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Kandil et al. conducted research purifying phosphogypsum with sulfuric acid to produce an insoluble residue.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lutke et al.">
  <data key="d0">Lutke et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Lutke et al. reported that switching to sulfuric acid, increasing concentration and temperature, and decreasing the solid/liquid ratio improved leaching efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791365947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Garg et al.">
  <data key="d0">Garg et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Garg et al. used calcined phosphogypsum at 150 to 160 degrees Celsius to attempt contaminant removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Garg et al. manufactured masonry using chalk, cement, and PG, measuring strength at 7 and 28 days.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792456533</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Taher">
  <data key="d0">Taher</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Taher is a researcher whose work primarily focuses on the thermal processing and construction applications of phosphogypsum, particularly Egyptian phosphogypsum. In 2007, Taher explored the decomposition of phosphogypsum through reactions with various forms of carbon. A central component of this research involved optimizing calcination parameters, with Taher determining that 600 degrees Celsius is the optimal temperature for processing Egyptian phosphogypsum. This thermal treatment was found to reduce the total porosity of the material and decrease the concentration of water-soluble phosphates and fluorides, a chemical change directly linked to improved performance in cementitious systems. Taher has also been cited for contributions regarding the microstructure of calcium sulfate dihydrate.

Beyond material characterization, Taher extensively investigated the incorporation of both raw and calcined phosphogypsum into slag cement manufacturing. The research systematically evaluated how calcination temperatures and the initial state of the phosphogypsum influence critical cement properties, including setting time, bulk density, and compressive strength. By demonstrating that calcination reduces soluble impurities and total porosity, Taher established a clear mechanism for the observed reduction in setting times. Collectively, this body of work provides a comprehensive framework for optimizing phosphogypsum-based slag cement formulations, balancing thermal processing conditions with desired mechanical and durability outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Taher is a researcher who investigated thermal treatment of phosphogypsum for manufacturing Portland slag cement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98d8b2df45924e721bfa570f652f33e3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792484618</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
