<node id="Direct Leaching">
  <data key="d0">Direct Leaching</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Direct Leaching is a conventional extraction and recovery technique utilized to dissolve and recover rare earth elements and uranium from phosphate-based feedstocks, specifically phosphogypsum and phosphate rocks. The process employs strong inorganic acids to facilitate the direct dissolution of these target elements from the solid material. Successful implementation requires precise control over several critical operational parameters, including contact time, solid-to-liquid ratio, temperature, and agitation speed, to ensure effective mass transfer and recovery rates.

Despite its established status and ongoing investigation as a viable recovery pathway, Direct Leaching presents notable economic and practical challenges. When applied to phosphogypsum, the method is frequently characterized as costly, expensive, and largely ineffective or unfeasible. These limitations stem primarily from the inherently low concentration of rare earth elements within phosphogypsum, which diminishes the process's overall efficiency and economic viability. Consequently, while Direct Leaching remains a recognized technique for processing both phosphogypsum and phosphate rocks, its application is heavily constrained by feedstock composition and operational costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792402260</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
