<node id="Salting-Out Effect">
  <data key="d0">Salting-Out Effect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The salting-out effect is a chemical interaction that contributes to the poor solubility of calcium sulfate in hydrochloric acid, limiting extraction results.&lt;SEP&gt;The salting-out effect is a controlling factor optimized during the solvent extraction process to enhance phase separation.&lt;SEP&gt;A phenomenon where added salts reduce the solubility of target compounds, enhancing their transfer to the organic phase.&lt;SEP&gt;A phenomenon caused by the nitrate-nitric acid mixture that was found to be negligible in this system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791811818</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium Nitrate">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Nitrate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium nitrate is a hydrated salt compound formed through the reaction of calcium carbonate with nitric acid, typically utilized in aqueous solution form. In industrial applications, particularly within phosphogypsum processing, it serves multiple critical functions. It is employed in an acidic medium to facilitate the recrystallization of phosphogypsum-dehydrate and acts as a primary leachate source to extract rare earth elements. Consequently, calcium nitrate is generated as a byproduct alongside rare earth element concentrates during phosphogypsum conversion. The compound inherently contains rare earth elements and functions as a key intermediate in these processing workflows.

The physicochemical behavior of calcium nitrate during processing dictates specific operational constraints and interactions. It remains dissolved in the leach solution but precipitates at elevated temperatures, thereby establishing a strict upper limit for maximum extraction temperatures. Additionally, when the hydrated salt contacts ionic liquids, it actively contributes to water migration within the system. Beyond its role in phosphogypsum conversion, calcium nitrate serves as an alternative calcium source for the preparation of HAP, although it demonstrates a comparatively lower adsorption capacity than phosphogypsum itself.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium Nitrate is a chemical compound represented as Ca(NO3)2, likely used in synthesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b81aeff540d385f1d1eda298b44619be-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792262255</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
