<node id="Synthetic Gypsum">
  <data key="d0">Synthetic Gypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Synthetic gypsum is a manufactured and laboratory-produced variant of gypsum that functions as a construction material and an alternative starting material in various industrial and chemical processes. It is closely linked to phosphogypsum, as it is often produced from this byproduct, processed alongside it within chemical conversion flowcharts, or utilized as a purer source characterized by cleaner gypsum crystals and ions. In research and development contexts, synthetic gypsum is frequently employed as a refined benchmark or substitute in studies involving phosphogypsum derivatives.

Analytical evaluations of synthetic gypsum involve comprehensive elemental composition assessments and Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy to identify its functional groups. When compared directly against phosphogypsum in solubility studies and wet conversion processes designed for potassium sulfate production, synthetic gypsum demonstrates distinct chemical properties. Notably, FT-IR analyses indicate that synthetic gypsum lacks certain absorption bands typically observed in phosphogypsum, highlighting its superior purity and unique molecular structure. These characteristics collectively establish synthetic gypsum as a versatile, high-purity material essential for both construction applications and advanced chemical research.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405227</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
