<node id="Ardealite">
  <data key="d0">Ardealite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ardealite is a calcium phosphate sulfate mineral phase that forms when impurities are incorporated into the gypsum structure. It typically emerges as phosphate concentration increases, specifically when phosphate ion content exceeds seven percent. The mineral is chemically defined by the formula Ca2(SO4)(HPO4)·4H2O, though it has also been recorded in scientific literature using the notation CaHPO4CaSO4•4H2O, notably in a 1984 publication.

Analytically, Ardealite is characterized by specific infrared absorption bands and has been examined using infrared spectroscopy in locations such as the Cioclovina Uscata Cave. While spectral analysis effectively identifies the mineral phase, researchers note that definitively establishing its transition to brushite via this technique remains difficult. Thermally, Ardealite undergoes a multi-stage dehydration process marked by distinct endothermic peaks, ultimately decomposing during heat treatment into brushite, monetite, and bassanite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792400824</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
