<node id="Potassium Sulphate">
  <data key="d0">Potassium Sulphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Potassium Sulphate (K2SO4) is a chemical compound and salt that exists in both synthetic and commercial forms, frequently utilized as a commercial grade reference material. It is primarily produced from phosphogypsum, an industrial co-product, through conversion and mineral sequestration processes such as alkaline dissolution. The synthesis involves the reaction of sulfate and potassium ions, a process predominantly driven by potassium hydroxide and calcium sulfate. Furthermore, the compound can be generated as a stable co-product or salt precipitate following the dispersion of di-hydrated calcium sulphate or after evaporating the liquid phase subsequent to portlandite synthesis.

The material is categorized into synthetic and commercial variants, which are routinely compared against the original phosphogypsum by-product and exhibit varying trace element levels. Chemically, it contains constituents including K2O, SO3, and CaO, and its composition is rigorously examined using analytical techniques such as SEM-EDS and FTIR spectroscopy. FTIR analysis specifically identifies characteristic peaks corresponding to sulphate ions and asymmetric stretching vibrations. Both forms are consistently evaluated for their trace element contents and overall chemical makeup to ensure accuracy across synthetic and commercial applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792118643</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CO2 Mineral Sequestration">
  <data key="d0">CO2 Mineral Sequestration</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CO2 Mineral Sequestration is a process that utilizes phosphogypsum to capture carbon dioxide while producing valuable sulphates and carbonates.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 mineral sequestration is an environmental process facilitated by the preparation of high-purity gypsum from phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 Mineral Sequestration is a technique using phosphogypsum as a calcium source to capture and store carbon dioxide.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 Mineral Sequestration is a primary aim of the hydroxide conversion method, utilizing PG as a calcium source to mitigate greenhouse gas emissions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-000</data>
