<node id="Anhydrite III">
  <data key="d0">Anhydrite III</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Anhydrite III, also recognized as gamma-anhydrite, is a hexagonal crystalline phase of anhydrous calcium sulfate. It represents a fully dehydrated chemical compound that forms through the thermal processing and dehydration of gypsum, semihydrate, and phosphogypsum. The emergence of this specific calcium sulfate phase occurs within a defined thermal window, with documented formation temperatures ranging from approximately 66°C to 280°C, frequently cited around 133°C to 140°C depending on the precursor material and exact heating protocols. Structurally, it constitutes a distinct anhydrous state that develops after the initial dehydration stages of its hydrated precursors.

Upon continued thermal exposure, Anhydrite III remains stable until it reaches 428°C, at which point it undergoes a phase transformation into Anhydrite II. This temperature-dependent behavior underscores its role as an intermediate crystalline phase in the systematic dehydration sequence of calcium sulfate hydrates. As a product of controlled thermal treatment, Anhydrite III is characterized by its complete water loss and its specific hexagonal lattice structure, which persists until the specified transformation threshold is met.&lt;SEP&gt;A calcium sulfate phase tracked in the experimental data table across different temperatures and holding times.&lt;SEP&gt;A calcium sulfate phase formed from bassanite at temperatures exceeding 150°C.&lt;SEP&gt;Anhydrite III is a phase that transforms into anhydrite II at higher temperatures during the dehydration of synthetic PG.&lt;SEP&gt;Anhydrite III is a mineral phase that transforms into anhydrite II at higher temperatures than pure gypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792402587</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anhydrite II">
  <data key="d0">Anhydrite II</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Anhydrite II, also recognized as beta-anhydrite, is an insoluble calcium sulfate mineral phase that forms during the thermal dehydration process of hydrated calcium sulfates. It emerges as a distinct intermediate phase resulting from the transformation of soluble anhydrite III at elevated temperatures. Scientific observations place its stability and formation within a temperature range of approximately 250°C to 450°C, with specific transition points frequently cited between 320°C and 428°C. As a stable compound in this thermal window, it represents a critical stage in the progressive dehydration pathway of calcium sulfate minerals.

Crystallographic classifications of Anhydrite II present notable discrepancies in the literature, reflecting ongoing scientific debate regarding its exact lattice structure. While some sources definitively describe it as an orthorhombic crystalline form, others classify it as hexagonal, likely due to differing experimental conditions or polymorphic transitions during synthesis. Regardless of its precise symmetry, Anhydrite II serves as a transitional phase that ultimately transforms into standard anhydrite at significantly higher temperatures, specifically at 1184°C. This comprehensive thermal behavior underscores its role as a key high-temperature polymorph within the calcium sulfate system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792402834</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anhydrite I">
  <data key="d0">Anhydrite I</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Anhydrite I is a calcium sulfate phase formed from the transition of anhydrite II at 1184 degrees Celsius.&lt;SEP&gt;A high-temperature crystalline phase of anhydrite that forms from the transformation of anhydrite II.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-003</data>
