<node id="Frost Resistance">
  <data key="d0">Frost Resistance</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A durability characteristic of phosphogypsum-based materials that improves through the addition of mineral admixtures or reinforcing fibers.&lt;SEP&gt;An important durability indicator for building materials, evaluated through dissolution rate and strength coefficient changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Frost resistance is a durability property of phosphogypsum cement mortar that is inhibited by the addition of quicklime.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791773399</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PGCM">
  <data key="d0">PGCM</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">PGCM, an abbreviation for phosphogypsum-based cementitious material, is a composite building material extensively investigated for its durability, mechanical performance, and fundamental physicochemical characteristics. Research focuses on evaluating its compressive strength, strength coefficient, shrinkage properties, and length variation, while also analyzing its hydration behavior, mineral phase composition, and dissolution rate. The material's response to various curing conditions and the incorporation of different additives or admixtures are closely monitored to understand how these factors influence its structural integrity and baseline mechanical properties.

Beyond its intrinsic properties, PGCM is rigorously tested for its resistance to environmental stressors and weathering. Studies assess its frost resistance and freeze-thaw performance, alongside its erosion resistance and damage mechanisms under prolonged exposure. The material is subjected to dry-wet cycles and broader environmental stress tests to measure its long-term stability and degradation patterns. Collectively, these comprehensive evaluations aim to validate PGCM's viability as a durable construction material capable of withstanding diverse operational and climatic challenges.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791515306</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strength Coefficient">
  <data key="d0">Strength Coefficient</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Strength coefficient is a parameter determined for specimens after freeze-thaw and wet-dry cycles.&lt;SEP&gt;A quantitative metric measuring structural strength retention during freeze-thaw cycles.&lt;SEP&gt;Strength Coefficient is a quantitative metric used to evaluate the structural integrity of PGCM after cyclic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Strength coefficient is a measured metric reflecting the mechanical strength retention of PGCM after dry-wet cycles.&lt;SEP&gt;A metric reflecting PGCM's structural integrity that is affected by admixture dosage and freeze-thaw cycles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791515306</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
