<node id="Road Material">
  <data key="d0">Road Material</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Road material refers to construction applications where phosphogypsum is utilized for stabilization and durability.&lt;SEP&gt;Road material refers to the application of phosphogypsum in construction for road bases and pavements to promote sustainable development.&lt;SEP&gt;Road material is an infrastructure application utilizing phosphogypsum, requiring radiological and leaching assessments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791772368</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cement Content">
  <data key="d0">Cement Content</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Cement content refers to the proportion of cement in PGCM, with maximum expansion observed at 15%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aft">
  <data key="d0">Aft</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Aft is the abbreviation for ettringite, one of the main hydration products identified in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CaSO">
  <data key="d0">CaSO</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">CaSO is the abbreviation for calcium sulfate dihydrate crystal, identified as a main hydration product.&lt;SEP&gt;CaSO is a compound in phosphogypsum whose dissolution and hydration are prevented by calcium phosphate formation.&lt;SEP&gt;CaSO is a chemical compound whose intensity decreases distinctly as coal size increases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792364185</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrostatic Pressure">
  <data key="d0">Hydrostatic Pressure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrostatic pressure builds up in large pores during freeze-thaw cycles, causing capillary pore enlargement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Network Crack Phenomenon">
  <data key="d0">Network Crack Phenomenon</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Network crack phenomenon describes the interconnected microcrack structure resulting from alternating stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Penetrating Cracks">
  <data key="d0">Penetrating Cracks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Penetrating cracks are larger, longer cracks formed when independent pores connect under alternating stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Capillary Wall">
  <data key="d0">Capillary Wall</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Capillary wall is the boundary of capillary pores that experiences pressure from expanding free water.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Early Hydration Stage">
  <data key="d0">Early Hydration Stage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Early hydration stage is the initial phase of PGCM reaction where fewer floccules and more micropores form.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Later Stage">
  <data key="d0">Later Stage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Later stage refers to the period where hydration slows down, free water evaporates, and pore increase occurs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subzero Temperature Environment">
  <data key="d0">Subzero Temperature Environment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Subzero temperature environment is the condition during freeze-thaw cycles where free water freezes and pressure builds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Above-Zero Temperature Environment">
  <data key="d0">Above-Zero Temperature Environment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Above-zero temperature environment is the condition where crystal water melts slowly and pores remain expanded.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aggregates">
  <data key="d0">Aggregates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Aggregates are the surrounding particles in PGCM that are squeezed by hydration products and separated by cracks.&lt;SEP&gt;Aggregates are irregular, rounded clumps of fused smaller particles observed in the phosphogypsum sample.&lt;SEP&gt;Aggregates are clusters of primary crystallites in dried in situ ball-milled hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792425962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dry-Wet Cycle Erosion">
  <data key="d0">Dry-Wet Cycle Erosion</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Dry-wet cycle erosion is an environmental process that subjects PGCM to alternating wet and dry conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dry Process">
  <data key="d0">Dry Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Dry process is the phase where free water evaporates from pores, generating shrinkage stress.&lt;SEP&gt;The Dry Process is a method for producing phosphoric acid that heats ore in an electric furnace to produce elemental phosphorus.&lt;SEP&gt;The dry process is a manufacturing route where phosphate ore is heated in an electric furnace to produce elemental phosphorus as an intermediary.&lt;SEP&gt;Dry process is an industrial method that generates hemihydrate phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792456417</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Water Absorption And Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Water Absorption And Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Water absorption and expansion is a key characteristic of porous materials like PGCM that drives internal stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Porous Materials">
  <data key="d0">Porous Materials</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Porous materials are substances like PGCM that store free water and exhibit significant expansion upon water absorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Macroscopic Physical And Mechanical Properties">
  <data key="d0">Macroscopic Physical And Mechanical Properties</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Macroscopic physical and mechanical properties are the overall performance metrics of PGCM that deteriorate over cycles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Failure">
  <data key="d0">Material Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Material failure is the ultimate degradation state of PGCM resulting from prolonged cyclic environmental action.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Energy Spectra">
  <data key="d0">Energy Spectra</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Energy spectra are the analytical data presented in Fig. 11 representing the chemical composition of hydration products.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PG Particles">
  <data key="d0">PG Particles</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">PG particles are the base components in PGCM that become progressively wrapped by hydration products.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shrinkage Stress">
  <data key="d0">Shrinkage Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shrinkage stress is generated during the dry process when free water evaporates, causing material contraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expansion Stress">
  <data key="d0">Expansion Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Expansion stress is generated when unreacted quicklime absorbs water and expands, widening pores and microcracks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alternating Stress">
  <data key="d0">Alternating Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Alternating stress results from the cyclic action of dry and wet environments, gradually increasing crack dimensions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791516802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adhesion">
  <data key="d0">Adhesion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adhesion refers to the bonding strength between aggregates within PGCM, which is reduced by penetrating cracks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
