<node id="CPGFM">
  <data key="d0">CPGFM</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">CPGFM (Cemented Paste Filling Material) is a specialized cementitious composite designed primarily for industrial and mining applications, particularly goaf backfill and open-pit restoration. It is formulated as a slurry that incorporates phosphogypsum (PG) and high-phosphorus gypsum (HPG) as primary aggregates, combined with quicklime, cement, slag, alkaline activators, phosphorus tailings, water, and various admixtures. As a calcium phosphogypsum filling composite, its composition is engineered to optimize mechanical properties and effectively utilize solid waste materials.

The material's mechanical strength and flow characteristics are highly sensitive to the precise proportions of its components, as well as to environmental conditions and specific processing treatments. Beyond its structural utility, CPGFM serves a critical environmental function by immobilizing harmful substances, notably phosphorus and fluorine. Leaching tests demonstrate that the composite significantly reduces phosphorus concentration in water, making it a sustainable construction material that balances enhanced performance with environmental safety for widespread application in Chinese mining operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CDW">
  <data key="d0">CDW</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">CDW refers to construction demolition waste, which was analyzed alongside PG for its phosphorus leaching behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;CDW is a material whose leachate fluoride content was tracked alongside PG and CPGFM in Fig. 13.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GB 5086.1">
  <data key="d0">GB 5086.1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">GB 5086.1 is a standard detailing leaching test procedures for identifying waste characteristics for storage and disposal facilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HJ/T 299-2007">
  <data key="d0">HJ/T 299-2007</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">HJ/T 299-2007 is a standard specifying leaching test conditions to simulate the release of hazardous components from solid wastes.&lt;SEP&gt;HJ/T 299-2007 is a Chinese standard detailing the sulphuric acid and nitric acid method for extracting leaching toxicity from solid waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leaching Test">
  <data key="d0">Leaching Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Leaching test is a standardized procedure used to measure the release of major and trace elements from materials under varying pH conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Leaching test is an experimental procedure used to analyze the release mechanisms of heavy metals from PG and MPG.&lt;SEP&gt;Leaching test is an experimental procedure used to evaluate the release of heavy metals from PBSLM6 into aqueous solutions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792295471</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="F-">
  <data key="d0">F-</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">F- represents the soluble fluoride ion whose concentration in leachate is affected by pH, dissolution, and adsorption processes.&lt;SEP&gt;F- represents the fluoride ion, which is quantified using specific volumetric and pH-controlled procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;F- is an impurity found in phosphogypsum that restricts its use in building materials.&lt;SEP&gt;F- is a chemical impurity in PG that is removed during purification or calcination.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluoride ion, a chemical species tracked in the dataset with fluctuating concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-60e237a0a1045feedf76d35d654a79bd-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792453767</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C-S-H Matrix">
  <data key="d0">C-S-H Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The C-S-H matrix is a component in the CPGFM model with an assumed Ca/Si ratio of 1.5, used to simulate dissolution reactions.&lt;SEP&gt;The C-S-H matrix provides binding sites that adsorb soluble fluorine, reducing its concentration in leachate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yellow Phosphorus Slag-Based Binder">
  <data key="d0">Yellow Phosphorus Slag-Based Binder</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A composite material composed of yellow phosphorus slag, fly ash, cement clinker, and lime used as a structural binder.&lt;SEP&gt;The addition of this binder to PG causes stabilized fluorine to be re-released into the filling slurry as pH rises.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPGFM Backfill">
  <data key="d0">CPGFM Backfill</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">CPGFM backfill is a composite material engineered to immobilize phosphorus and fluorine, minimizing their environmental leaching.&lt;SEP&gt;CPGFM backfill is a cemented composite material where heavy metal ions and fluorine are immobilized through physical and chemical processes.&lt;SEP&gt;CPGFM backfill is a material used for immobilizing toxic and harmful elements through gel interlacing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Copper Ion">
  <data key="d0">Copper Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Copper ions are toxic heavy metal ions that can replace calcium ions in the C-S-H matrix through ion exchange.&lt;SEP&gt;Copper ion (Cu²⁺) is an ion that may replace calcium ions in C-S-H.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chromium Ion">
  <data key="d0">Chromium Ion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Chromium ions are toxic heavy metal ions that can replace calcium ions in the C-S-H matrix through ion exchange.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromium ion (Cr³⁺) is an ion that may replace silicon ions in C-S-H.&lt;SEP&gt;A heavy metal ion that can be replaced by iron ions to form substitutional solid solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromium ion is a heavy metal present in phosphogypsum that participates in substitutional solid solution formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792300665</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Physical Adsorption">
  <data key="d0">Physical Adsorption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical adsorption relies on molecular attraction forces, such as Van der Waals forces, to immobilize toxic metal ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical adsorption is a mechanism for pollutant stabilization that is weaker than chemical combination.&lt;SEP&gt;A proposed adsorption mechanism driven by electrostatic interactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792317345</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chemical Combination">
  <data key="d0">Chemical Combination</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Chemical combination involves the formation of stable chemical bonds between harmful ions and hydration products to immobilize contaminants.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemical combination is a mechanism for pollutant stabilization that has a stronger solidification effect than physical adsorption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SPLP Tests">
  <data key="d0">SPLP Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">SPLP tests are standardized procedures used to measure leaching concentrations of heavy metal ions in the filling body.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719530</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Secondary Release">
  <data key="d0">Secondary Release</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Secondary release refers to the risk of previously immobilized fluorine being re-released into the slurry due to pH changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chronic Poisoning">
  <data key="d0">Chronic Poisoning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Chronic poisoning is a severe health consequence for humans if heavy metal elements from PG enter the food chain.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Van der Waals Force">
  <data key="d0">Van der Waals Force</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Van der Waals force is the molecular attraction responsible for the physical adsorption of toxic ions in the backfill.&lt;SEP&gt;A molecular bond force disrupted during temperature treatment, initiating heavy metal replacement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792299784</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Xu et al.">
  <data key="d0">Xu et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Xu et al. highlighted the health risks of heavy metals from PG entering the human food chain.&lt;SEP&gt;Researchers who investigated the effects of ball milling time on phosphogypsum particle size and hardened body strength.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791773516</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chen and Wu">
  <data key="d0">Chen and Wu</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Chen and Wu described how toxic and harmful elements enter hydration products through ion exchange.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719874</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fig. 15">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 15</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 15 depicts the mechanisms of physical adsorption and chemical combination for immobilizing toxic metal ions.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 15 is a figure providing a schematic diagram of toxic metal ion immobilization by CPGFM backfill.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 15 is a visual reference illustrating crystal structures in hydrated MPG.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 15 is an SEM-EDS diagram illustrating MPG after hydration.&lt;SEP&gt;Fig. 15 is a figure showing the transformation of brushite into brushite and monetite phases at 150 °C.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792406487</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Al-F Complex">
  <data key="d0">Al-F Complex</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Al-F Complex is a chemical form that fluorine mainly exists in when pH is less than 5.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791719874</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Food Chain">
  <data key="d0">Food Chain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Food Chain is the pathway through which heavy metal elements from PG can enter the human body and cause poisoning.&lt;SEP&gt;The Food Chain is the ecological pathway for heavy metal migration and transformation.&lt;SEP&gt;The food chain is the ecological pathway through which heavy metals migrate and eventually accumulate in the human body.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792295471</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kidney Failure">
  <data key="d0">Kidney Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Kidney Failure is a severe health consequence for humans resulting from chronic heavy metal poisoning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-022</data>
