<node id="Liquid Limit">
  <data key="d0">Liquid Limit</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Liquid limit is a physical property of the adobe soil, measured at 56.41%.&lt;SEP&gt;The liquid limit of the soil determines the required amount of mixing water for the specimens.&lt;SEP&gt;A soil property measured at 56.41 percent, indicating the water content at which soil transitions to a liquid state.&lt;SEP&gt;Liquid Limit is a geotechnical parameter measuring the water content at which soil transitions from plastic to liquid state.&lt;SEP&gt;Liquid limit is a soil consistency parameter measured during Atterberg limit tests.&lt;SEP&gt;A measured parameter defining the water content at which soil transitions from a plastic to a liquid state.&lt;SEP&gt;A geotechnical parameter indicating the water content at which soil transitions from a plastic to a liquid state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792141666</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plasticity Index">
  <data key="d0">Plasticity Index</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The Plasticity Index is a fundamental soil consistency parameter that quantifies the range of water content over which a soil material remains in a plastic state. It is a calculated value derived from the liquid and plastic limits, and its determination is typically conducted through standardized Atterberg limits testing. In specific geotechnical evaluations, such as those involving adobe soil, the Plasticity Index has been recorded at 20.70 percent.

Beyond its role as a basic geotechnical indicator, the Plasticity Index serves as a key parameter reflecting a soil's resistance to water erosion. From an engineering and stabilization perspective, the Plasticity Index is generally reduced when soils are treated with additives such as cement, fly ash, and phosphogypsum, a reduction that typically indicates improved soil stability and performance. Similarly, the addition of SM has been shown to significantly improve soil characteristics by lowering its Plasticity Index. Overall, the Plasticity Index remains a critical metric for evaluating soil behavior, consistency, and the effectiveness of various ground improvement techniques.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792141245</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="A-7-5 Soil">
  <data key="d0">A-7-5 Soil</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A-7-5 Soil is the classification of the adobe soil according to AASHTO standards based on its liquid limit and plasticity index.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-000</data>
