<node id="Count Normalizer ($n$)">
  <data key="d0">Count Normalizer ($n$)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable representing the number of reflections measured, used as a normalizer in the texture coefficient calculation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791834951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Reflections ($N$)">
  <data key="d0">Total Reflections ($N$)</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable representing the total number of diffraction peaks considered in the analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791834951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Summation">
  <data key="d0">Summation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The mathematical operation summing intensity ratios for all N reflections in the texture coefficient formula.&lt;SEP&gt;Summation is a mathematical operation indicated by the Greek letter Sigma, used to aggregate values across all samples or criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ce07008b902e3e05b9cea9315e7aa04-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792160925</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nanocrystal">
  <data key="d0">Nanocrystal</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A nanoscale crystalline material that exhibits peak broadening in XRD patterns due to size effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanocrystals are microscopic crystalline structures where XRD diffraction patterns exhibit widening effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791834951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Instrumental Broadening">
  <data key="d0">Instrumental Broadening</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A component of XRD pattern widening attributed to equipment limitations rather than material properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Instrumental broadening is one of two components that distinguish XRD diffraction pattern widening.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791834951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Physical Broadening">
  <data key="d0">Physical Broadening</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A component of XRD pattern widening attributed to intrinsic crystalline size effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical broadening is the second component that distinguishes XRD diffraction pattern widening.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791834951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Doped HAp">
  <data key="d0">Doped HAp</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Doped HAp is a hydrothermally synthesized variant of hydroxyapatite with modified crystallographic properties.&lt;SEP&gt;A hydroxyapatite sample with a measured crystallite size of 2.65 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;A modified variant of synthesized HAp nanoparticles with a measured crystallite size of 1.93 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;Doped HAp is a synthesized nanoparticle variant with a crystallite size of 17.55 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;Doped HAp is a hydroxyapatite sample modified with dopant ions, exhibiting an optical band gap of 5.45 eV.&lt;SEP&gt;Doped HAp is a synthesized nanoparticle sample characterized by associated particles with no distinct geometric forms and a crystallite size of 30.89 nm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791834951</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Urea HAp">
  <data key="d0">Urea HAp</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Urea HAp is a hydrothermally synthesized variant of hydroxyapatite processed with urea.&lt;SEP&gt;A hydroxyapatite sample with a measured crystallite size of 3.76 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;A urea-modified variant of synthesized HAp nanoparticles with a measured crystallite size of 4.14 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;Urea HAp is a synthesized nanoparticle variant with a crystallite size of 43.32 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;Urea HAp is a hydroxyapatite sample modified with urea, exhibiting a reduced optical band gap of 5.13 eV.&lt;SEP&gt;Urea HAp is a synthesized nanoparticle sample with a monodispersed nanorod shape and an average length of 38.74 nm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791835059</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Naphthalene HAp">
  <data key="d0">Naphthalene HAp</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Naphthalene HAp is a hydrothermally synthesized variant of hydroxyapatite processed with naphthalene.&lt;SEP&gt;A hydroxyapatite sample with a measured crystallite size of 23.5 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;A naphthalene-modified variant of synthesized HAp nanoparticles with a measured crystallite size of 13.51 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;Naphthalene HAp is a synthesized nanoparticle variant with a crystallite size of 39.61 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;Naphthalene HAp is a hydroxyapatite sample modified with naphthalene, exhibiting an optical band gap of 5.32 eV.&lt;SEP&gt;Naphthalene HAp is a synthesized nanoparticle sample featuring a monodispersed nanorod shape and an average length of 53.23 nm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791835059</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Palmitic Acid HAp">
  <data key="d0">Palmitic Acid HAp</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Palmitic Acid HAp is a hydrothermally synthesized variant of hydroxyapatite processed with palmitic acid.&lt;SEP&gt;A hydroxyapatite sample with a measured crystallite size of 17.11 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;A palmitic acid-modified variant of synthesized HAp nanoparticles with a measured crystallite size of 3.64 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;Palmitic Acid HAp is a synthesized nanoparticle variant with a crystallite size of 43.32 nm.&lt;SEP&gt;Palmitic Acid HAp is a hydroxyapatite sample modified with palmitic acid, exhibiting the highest optical band gap of 5.58 eV.&lt;SEP&gt;Palmitic Acid HAp is a synthesized nanoparticle sample showing associated particles with no distinct geometric forms and a crystallite size of 50.79 nm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791835059</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scherrer's Method">
  <data key="d0">Scherrer's Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Scherrer's method is a technique that analyzes XRD peak widening to determine crystalline size.&lt;SEP&gt;A technique that considers only the impact of crystalline size on XRD peak widening without addressing lattice microstructure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791835059</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Williamson–Hall Method">
  <data key="d0">Williamson–Hall Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Williamson–Hall method evaluates lattice microstructure characteristics beyond crystalline size.&lt;SEP&gt;A crystallographic analysis technique that accounts for both crystallite size and microstrain in XRD peak broadening.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791835059</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FWHM">
  <data key="d0">FWHM</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">FWHM (full width at half maxima) is a measured parameter in radians used in crystalline size calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;Full Width at Half Maximum, representing the width parameter for both Gaussian and Lorentzian functions.&lt;SEP&gt;FWHM (Full Width at Half Maximum) is a parameter used in the Sahadat-Scherrer Model equation to calculate crystallite size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791835059</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scherrer's Equation">
  <data key="d0">Scherrer's Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Scherrer's equation calculates crystallite size using wavelength, shape factor, and FWHM parameters.&lt;SEP&gt;An XRD model used to calculate crystallite size.&lt;SEP&gt;Scherrer's Equation is a mathematical formula used to calculate nanoparticle average diameter from XRD results.&lt;SEP&gt;Scherrer's Equation is a mathematical formula used to calculate the average diameter of nano-hydroxyapatite crystallites from XRD data.&lt;SEP&gt;A mathematical formula used to determine the average crystallite size (length and width) of the nanopowder samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a52951853fd542b78835b677936c557b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791854421</data>
