<node id="Optimum Moisture Content">
  <data key="d0">Optimum Moisture Content</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Optimum Moisture Content is a fundamental geotechnical parameter and compaction metric that defines the ideal water percentage at which a soil material achieves its maximum dry density during compaction testing. This critical threshold is routinely measured in both standard and modified Proctor tests to determine optimal soil moisture conditions. From a structural engineering standpoint, Optimum Moisture Content represents the moisture level required to achieve maximum bearing capacity; surpassing this point leads to a significant reduction in load-bearing performance.

The value of Optimum Moisture Content is dynamic and responds predictably to changes in testing conditions and material composition. It decreases as compaction energy increases, with documented shifts from 18.3 wt% down to 17 wt% under higher energy regimes. Additionally, the parameter generally declines when various stabilizing agents or modifiers are introduced to the soil matrix, including cement, fly ash, phosphogypsum, and SM when added to raw phosphogypsum. These characteristics make Optimum Moisture Content an essential indicator for predicting soil behavior and optimizing compaction procedures in geotechnical applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792141666</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Dry Unit Weight">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Dry Unit Weight</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Maximum Dry Unit Weight is a compaction parameter measured in kN/m³ for modified and standard Proctor tests.&lt;SEP&gt;A geotechnical parameter that increases with the addition of SM to raw PG.&lt;SEP&gt;A soil density metric that increases with phosphogypsum content but decreases with fly ash content.&lt;SEP&gt;Maximum dry unit weight is a soil property that decreases as fly ash content increases due to lower specific surface gravity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-764bdde7c1b68efdbe7342cc41c2d3d4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792142217</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Preconsolidation Stress">
  <data key="d0">Preconsolidation Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Preconsolidation Stress is a geotechnical parameter measured in KPa indicating the maximum past stress on soil.&lt;SEP&gt;A stress parameter measured at 35 KPa, corresponding to a specific permeability coefficient.&lt;SEP&gt;Stress parameter that increases in E2 Mix, reflecting enhanced load-bearing resistance.&lt;SEP&gt;A geotechnical parameter indicating stress history, improved by SM addition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791936533</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Diametric Compression Strength">
  <data key="d0">Diametric Compression Strength</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Diametric Compression Strength is a mechanical property measured in MPa to assess material resistance.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical property representing tensile strength under compression, measured during diametric compression testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanical property measured for P12 Mix, showing notable values after CM addition.&lt;SEP&gt;Diametric compression strength is a mechanical property measured in MPa, showing values like 0.18 MPa at 7 days for P12 Mix.&lt;SEP&gt;A measured mechanical property used to classify mixes and assess pavement suitability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791936533</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compression Index">
  <data key="d0">Compression Index</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Compression Index is a parameter related to the compressibility of calcareous materials.&lt;SEP&gt;A consolidation parameter measuring settlement decline by compression, recorded at 0.25 for raw phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Compressibility parameter that decreases in E2 Mix, indicating reduced soil compressibility.&lt;SEP&gt;A geotechnical parameter indicating compressibility, improved by SM addition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1791936533</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Backfill">
  <data key="d0">Backfill</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Backfill is a geotechnical application where stabilized mixes are laid for construction purposes.</data>
