<node id="Oxygen Vacancies">
  <data key="d0">Oxygen Vacancies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Oxygen vacancies are structural defects that occur within the crystal lattice of materials, with their presence in hydroxyapatite (HAp) specifically inferred from spectral peak differences and EDX data. These defects are structurally characterized by a measurable reduction in spectral intensity at 3540 cm⁻¹, which correlates directly with the loss of hydroxide (OH⁻) ions from the material's framework.

Functionally, oxygen vacancies significantly modify the electronic and optical properties of the host structure. They introduce localized energy levels that act as traps for electrons and holes, thereby reducing the overall optical band gap energy. By serving as both charge traps and active adsorption sites, these vacancies directly influence charge recombination rates and facilitate efficient charge transfer processes, making them critical to the material's functional performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792189361</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surfactant Concentration">
  <data key="d0">Surfactant Concentration</data>
