<node id="PPG">
  <data key="d0">PPG</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">PPG, or polypropylene glycol, is a waste polymer that functions as a precursor and derivative source for hydroxyapatite (HAp) within the context of the referenced study. As a foundational material, it is specifically repurposed from discarded polymer waste to synthesize PPG-derived HAp samples. The selection of PPG as a precursor highlights its chemical suitability for transforming waste into valuable ceramic compounds.

Prior to its conversion, the phase composition of the PPG precursor was thoroughly analyzed to establish baseline characteristics. The material subsequently undergoes a hydrothermal synthesis process, during which it is successfully converted into hydroxyapatite. This transformation confirms PPG's efficacy as a polymer precursor, demonstrating a reliable pathway for deriving HAp through controlled thermal and chemical treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792188816</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
