<node id="Beta-tricalcium phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Beta-tricalcium phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Beta-tricalcium phosphate is a phase formed in HAP samples after calcination at high temperatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta-tricalcium phosphate is a compound formed from the partial decomposition of hydroxyapatite at 900 °C due to dehydroxylation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792001842</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ammonia solution">
  <data key="d0">Ammonia solution</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ammonia solution is an alkaline reagent added to the reaction mixture at room temperature to facilitate synthesis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792001842</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thermal analysis">
  <data key="d0">Thermal analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermal analysis is a technique used to study the partial conversion of hydroxyapatite into beta-tricalcium phosphate via dehydroxylation up to 700 °C.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conclusion">
  <data key="d0">Conclusion</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The conclusion section summarizes the synthesis process, analytical results, and structural transformations of the materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rod-like morphology">
  <data key="d0">Rod-like morphology</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rod-like morphology is a particle shape observed in dried hydroxyapatite and calcined samples at 600 °C, measuring 50–57 nm in length.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spherical morphology">
  <data key="d0">Spherical morphology</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Spherical morphology is a particle shape observed in hydroxyapatite calcined at 900 °C, with an average diameter of 54–74 nm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Porous structure">
  <data key="d0">Porous structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A porous structure forms from agglomerated particles in calcined hydroxyapatite, potentially beneficial for physiological fluid circulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dehydroxylation">
  <data key="d0">Dehydroxylation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dehydroxylation is a thermal decomposition process occurring up to 700 °C that causes the partial conversion of hydroxyapatite into beta-tricalcium phosphate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Apatite structure">
  <data key="d0">Apatite structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The apatite structure is the fundamental crystal structure retained by all hydroxyapatite samples before and after heat treatments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dried Hydroxyapatite">
  <data key="d0">Dried Hydroxyapatite</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Dried Hydroxyapatite is the uncalcined form of the synthesized material, exhibiting a desultory structure and rod-like morphology.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcined Hydroxyapatite at 600 °C">
  <data key="d0">Calcined Hydroxyapatite at 600 °C</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcined Hydroxyapatite at 600 °C is a heat-treated sample that retains a rod-like morphology and poor crystalline nature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calcined Hydroxyapatite at 900 °C">
  <data key="d0">Calcined Hydroxyapatite at 900 °C</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcined Hydroxyapatite at 900 °C is a heat-treated sample that exhibits spherical morphology, increased particle size, and partial decomposition into beta-tricalcium phosphate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Desultory Structure">
  <data key="d0">Desultory Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Desultory structure describes the irregular and non-uniform arrangement of particles observed in dried hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Agglomerated Particle">
  <data key="d0">Agglomerated Particle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Agglomerated particles refer to clusters of hydroxyapatite particles that form during drying and calcination processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pure Phase">
  <data key="d0">Pure Phase</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pure phase indicates the chemical purity of ammonium sulfate as confirmed by XRD and IR analyses.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="1 h">
  <data key="d0">1 h</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">1 h is the reaction time required for the complete preparation of HAP, as determined by XRD patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;1 h denotes the duration of the chemical reaction process at room temperature.&lt;SEP&gt;1 h is the milling time applied to samples intended for ex situ processing to ensure homogeneous mixing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792427797</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nano-HAP">
  <data key="d0">Nano-HAP</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Nano-HAP refers to the synthesized nano-sized hydroxyapatite produced from phosphogypsum waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792002021</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FTIR Spectra Analysis">
  <data key="d0">FTIR Spectra Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">FTIR Spectra Analysis is an infrared spectroscopy technique used to identify chemical groups and molecular vibrations in the samples.</data>
