<node id="Research Duration">
  <data key="d0">Research Duration</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The review presents results from more than 25 years of work on phosphogypsum at the Ecole des Mines de Saint-Etienne.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405316</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Magmatic Phosphate Rock">
  <data key="d0">Magmatic Phosphate Rock</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Magmatic phosphate rock is an igneous phosphate ore processed to produce phosphogypsum with elevated trace element concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405316</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kola Peninsula">
  <data key="d0">Kola Peninsula</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The Kola Peninsula is a geographic region supplying magmatic phosphate rock that yields phosphogypsum with high sodium oxide concentrations.&lt;SEP&gt;Kola Peninsula is a geographic region in Russia known for producing phosphate rock that yields m-PG with high sodium oxide content.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405316</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nepheline">
  <data key="d0">Nepheline</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Nepheline is a mineral compound formed from rock phosphate initials in samples originating from the Kola Peninsula.&lt;SEP&gt;Nepheline is a sodium potassium aluminum silicate mineral that constitutes the phosphate rock from the Kola Peninsula.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405316</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Huelva Phosphogypsum">
  <data key="d0">Huelva Phosphogypsum</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Huelva phosphogypsum is a regional variant where sulfuric acid from pyrite roasting introduces additional trace element impurities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405316</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thorium Nesosilicate">
  <data key="d0">Thorium Nesosilicate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Thorium nesosilicate is a highly radioactive crystalline substance within phosphogypsum that remains difficult to resolve.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792405316</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radioactivity Index">
  <data key="d0">Radioactivity Index</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The radioactivity index is a calculated metric used to evaluate the safety of phosphogypsum for construction applications.&lt;SEP&gt;A safety metric for phosphogypsum that must be evaluated for marketability.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactivity Index is a calculated metric representing the weighted sum of radioactive element concentrations in phosphogypsum samples.</data>
