<node id="FT-IR Spectra">
  <data key="d0">FT-IR Spectra</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">FT-IR spectra are recorded to evaluate the structural and morphological effects of different milling techniques on hydroxyapatite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792425962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wet Ball Milling">
  <data key="d0">Wet Ball Milling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Wet ball milling is a control process investigated to compare against in situ milling methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Wet ball milling is a configuration that consistently produces smaller particle sizes compared to dry milling.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792425962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dry Ball Milling">
  <data key="d0">Dry Ball Milling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Dry ball milling is a control process investigated to determine its effect on the stoichiometric ratio and structural integrity.&lt;SEP&gt;Dry ball milling is a mechanical processing technique previously implemented in hydroxyapatite synthesis to induce lattice strain.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792425962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="In Situ Milling">
  <data key="d0">In Situ Milling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">In situ milling is a technique applied during synthesis to create mechanical shearing effects on crystallites.&lt;SEP&gt;In situ milling is a novel technique applied during synthesis to assess its impact on particle size and material properties.&lt;SEP&gt;In Situ Milling is a processing technique that produces Hydroxyapatite samples similar in size and stoichiometric ratio to natural bone mineral.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792425962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate Peak">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate Peak</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The phosphate peak at approximately 1092 cm^-1 is used as the primary reference for normalizing IR spectra.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792425962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hydroxyl Peak">
  <data key="d0">Hydroxyl Peak</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The hydroxyl peak at 3570 cm^-1 is measured to calculate the OH to PO4 ratio from processed spectra.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792425962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Baseline Correction">
  <data key="d0">Baseline Correction</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Baseline correction is a data processing step involving linear subtraction of background spectra to isolate relevant peak amplitudes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792425962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Effects">
  <data key="d0">Structural Effects</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural effects refer to changes in the material lattice that are monitored to ensure milling processes do not alter composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792425962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Morphological Effects">
  <data key="d0">Morphological Effects</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Morphological effects refer to physical shape and surface characteristics evaluated alongside structural changes during milling.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1792426134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Milling Media">
  <data key="d0">Milling Media</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Milling media are physical tools used in milling processes to grind hydroxyapatite samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Milling media refers to physical agents such as small or large particles used during in situ milling to influence particle size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-72ff3bbf1e2b594abc3ec5c8d86e3178-chunk-006</data>
