<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Cement">
  <data key="d7">33.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Cement and Phosphogypsum encompasses a multifaceted industrial application where phosphogypsum functions as a foundational raw material, specialized additive, and partial substitute in construction chemistry and cement manufacturing. Chemically, phosphogypsum serves as a primary source of calcium oxide and a reactive material that interacts with water. In Portland cement production, it is extensively employed to stabilize the cement matrix, act as a set retarder or regulator (often replacing natural gypsum), and modulate the hydration process to optimize workability and curing characteristics. This substitution directly influences setting time and compressive strength. Processed, neutralized, or pre-treated phosphogypsum is integrated into various binder formulations, mortar mixtures, and concrete applications, where it can form a high-activity binder when mixed with cement. Conversely, the use of impure phosphogypsum is known to reduce clinker strength.

Empirical studies and industrial practices demonstrate that phosphogypsum can successfully replace up to ten percent of cement in construction mixtures while maintaining required structural properties. Additionally, it is combined with cement in specific proportions, such as a one-to-one ratio, for mixed additive formulations, and is utilized to enhance soil stabilization effectiveness. The material is also applied as an additive during the cement grinding phase. Technical documentation frequently compares the chemical and physical properties of cement and phosphogypsum in data tables to guide these precise applications.

From a sustainability and resource management perspective, cement production is recognized as a vital recycling technology and a potential future valorization method for phosphogypsum. Although phosphogypsum has been historically utilized and is actively proposed for large-scale adoption as a sustainable, resource-efficient alternative, its widespread commercial promotion is currently hindered by technical bottlenecks. To mitigate these challenges and facilitate broader industrial integration, phosphogypsum is frequently combined with other solid wastes. Ultimately, the integration of phosphogypsum into cement systems highlights its evolving role as a valuable, eco-friendly resource in modern construction chemistry and co-production processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is utilized as an alternative additive and set regulator in cement production.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is investigated as a substitute for natural gypsum in cement production.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum is studied for its application in cement production and stabilization.</data>
