<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Gypsum">
  <data key="d7">50.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Gypsum and Phosphogypsum centers on their shared chemical foundation, distinct industrial origins, and interconnected processing pathways. Spectral annotations and compositional analyses confirm that both materials are primarily composed of calcium sulfate, predominantly in dihydrate form with minor hemihydrate and anhydrite variants. Gypsum constitutes the dominant phase of phosphogypsum, accounting for approximately 90 to 96 weight percent of its mass. Within the phosphogypsum matrix, gypsum forms elongated, prismatic, and needle-like crystals that agglomerate into a lattice structure, serving as the primary host for trace elements and rare earth elements. While natural or refined calcium sulfate is classified as gypsum, phosphogypsum is an industrial byproduct and waste material generated during phosphoric acid manufacturing.

Despite their chemical similarity, the materials are distinguished by purity and application, yet they are increasingly linked through advanced recycling technologies. Novel processes, including controlled calcination, radionuclide extraction, and rare earth element recovery, are employed to recycle raw phosphogypsum. These treatments facilitate recrystallization and purification to synthesize pure gypsum, which may occasionally emerge as a secondary byproduct. Processing stacked phosphogypsum not only recovers this valuable mineral but also significantly reduces the environmental and economic demand for extracting natural gypsum.

In practical and analytical contexts, both materials exhibit versatile utility and serve as critical benchmarks. Phosphogypsum is widely utilized in soil amendments, construction, and building materials as a setting regulator and a functional replacement for natural gypsum. Conversely, pure gypsum is highly valued in cement production as a sole calcium oxide source, where it can substitute for or complement phosphogypsum. Analytically, gypsum acts as a reference standard for sulfur salvage studies, agricultural effectiveness assessments, and micrograph analyses when evaluated against phosphogypsum. Furthermore, the presence of gypsum crystals can indicate unreacted phosphogypsum in carbonation products, while phosphogypsum’s radioactivity indices are routinely evaluated relative to natural gypsum to ensure regulatory compliance.</data>
