<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Calcination">
  <data key="d7">27.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Calcination and Phosphogypsum describes a thermal treatment process applied to raw phosphogypsum to modify its physical, chemical, mineralogical, and thermodynamic properties. During this process, the texture and morphology of the raw material influence the processing dynamics, while significant dehydration, crystal structure alteration, and phase changes occur, primarily transforming the substance into anhydrite and calcium sulphate hemihydrate. This thermal conversion is extensively analyzed to understand dehydration patterns and impurity transformations, which are fundamental to optimizing the treatment.

As a critical pretreatment and waste management strategy, calcination serves as the most prevalent treatment technology for phosphogypsum, primarily aimed at purifying the material and reducing its impurity load. The process effectively eliminates volatile contaminants and removes specific hazardous components, including fluoride, organic phosphates, eutectic phosphorus, soluble phosphate (which is converted into stable forms), and various toxic elements, while also decreasing radioactivity. By mitigating the material's retardation effect on setting time and purifying its composition, calcination significantly enhances its performance for specific end uses.

The treatment activates phosphogypsum for enhanced use in fly ash-lime systems and gypsum plaster production, while simultaneously improving its mechanical properties and mitigating strength degradation within building matrices. These improvements substantially increase the material's suitability for construction applications, such as brick manufacturing, and support broader industrial recycling initiatives. Despite its demonstrated efficacy and widespread recognition as a laboratory-scale purification technology, the calcination process currently lacks large-scale industrial scalability.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum samples undergo calcination at temperatures ranging from 170°C to 950°C.</data>
