<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Tunisia">
  <data key="d7">23.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphogypsum in Tunisia represents a major industrial byproduct, accounting for approximately 6.24% of global production and reserves, with an annual generation of roughly 10.0 million metric tons. This substantial output establishes Tunisia as a primary geographic source for scientific research, where the material is frequently cited in academic literature for extensive chemical characterization. Tunisian phosphogypsum is notably characterized by very high concentrations of heavy metals, including cadmium, mercury, and zinc, alongside various associated impurities.

Despite its classification as an industrial problem due to significant stockpiling and its underutilized value, efforts have been made to valorize the material for practical applications. Tunisian phosphogypsum has been utilized in the construction sector, specifically in the production of clay bricks and cement manufacturing, as well as in papermaking. Ongoing investigations focus on developing effective management strategies and stabilization processes to address disposal challenges and enhance its industrial reuse.

The environmental footprint of phosphogypsum storage and disposal in Tunisia has been a focal point of ecological investigation. Scientific assessments have evaluated the impact of phosphogypsum stockpiles on local soil bacterial communities and wild plant species, as well as the effects of leachate on regional groundwater systems. Together, these factors underscore Tunisia’s dual role as a leading global producer of phosphogypsum and a critical reference location for understanding its chemical properties, environmental consequences, and potential for sustainable material reuse.</data>
