<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Phosphoric Acid">
  <data key="d7">43.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Phosphogypsum and Phosphoric Acid is fundamentally defined by an industrial production pathway in which the wet manufacturing process of Phosphoric Acid yields Phosphogypsum as a major solid and chemical byproduct. This generation occurs at a substantial mass ratio, producing approximately five tonnes of Phosphogypsum for every single tonne of Phosphoric Acid manufactured. Chemically, Phosphogypsum is intrinsically linked to this cycle as it inherently retains residual Phosphoric Acid and its salts as primary impurities. This residual acid adheres to gypsum crystal surfaces, exists as a water-soluble component, and contributes significantly to the material's highly acidic nature. During standard industrial treatment, this soluble acid is typically eliminated through rinsing with pure water. Additionally, during the wet-process production phase, approximately eighty percent of the cadmium content transfers to the resulting Phosphoric Acid.

Beyond its role as a manufacturing waste product, Phosphogypsum participates in a cyclical industrial dynamic with Phosphoric Acid. It serves as a viable source for Phosphoric Acid recovery through targeted chemical processing, where phosphate impurities within the Phosphogypsum can react with aqueous citric acid to regenerate Phosphoric Acid. Conversely, in certain processing stages, Phosphoric Acid is deliberately added to Phosphogypsum slurry to facilitate specific chemical reactions. Together, these interactions highlight a bidirectional relationship where Phosphoric Acid production continuously generates Phosphogypsum, while the resulting byproduct contains recoverable Phosphoric Acid that can be extracted or manipulated under controlled industrial conditions.</data>
