<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Hydroxyapatite">
  <data key="d7">25.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Hydroxyapatite and Phosphogypsum establishes a chemical synthesis and resource recovery pathway in which phosphogypsum, a calcium- and phosphorus-rich industrial byproduct of the phosphate industry, functions as the primary feedstock, precursor, and reactant for producing hydroxyapatite. Recognized as a sustainable secondary resource, this abundant waste material is systematically repurposed to synthesize various targeted forms of the compound, including nanocrystalline hydroxyapatite, nano-hydroxyapatite, and hydroxyapatite nanorods, providing a cost-effective alternative to conventional mineral sources. The transformation typically relies on hydrothermal synthesis or general chemical processing to extract essential elemental components. Specific experimental protocols involve reacting phosphogypsum waste with phosphoric acid and ammonia, while other approaches demonstrate that subjecting the material to alkaline conditions or higher pH levels can similarly facilitate the precipitation and formation of hydroxyapatite as a distinct phase.

Collectively, this relation highlights a targeted environmental and industrial strategy that valorizes phosphate industry waste into a valuable nanomaterial. By converting an abundant industrial byproduct into a precisely engineered solid compound, the process underscores a sustainable chemical conversion framework that simultaneously achieves ecological remediation and efficient material production. This synthesis pathway effectively bridges waste management with advanced materials manufacturing, ensuring that the resource recovery process remains both economically viable and environmentally responsible.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum serves as the raw material for synthesizing hydroxyapatite nanoparticles.</data>
  <data key="d9">calcium source,chemical composition,chemical conversion,conversion,extraction target,material synthesis,material valorization,pH dependency,phase identification,precursor,product formation,raw material,reactant,reactant to product,research context,source material,synthesis,synthesis input,synthesis precursor,waste utilization</data>
