<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Phosphorus">
  <data key="d7">25.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Phosphogypsum and Phosphorus encompasses significant industrial, chemical, and environmental dimensions. Phosphogypsum, an industrial byproduct, contains phosphorus as a definitive identifier and one of its constituent elements. Within the material's matrix, phosphorus is distributed heterogeneously in scattered spots and is frequently identified as a trace element, though it is simultaneously analyzed as a major chemical species and functions as both a primary component and a significant impurity depending on the application. Industrially, phosphorus acts as a harmful, acidic, and toxic impurity that compromises filler strength and reduces overall product integrity. Consequently, soluble forms are actively targeted for removal or efficient precipitation during treatment and recycling processes to ensure safe handling, proper solidification, and high-quality end products, a process proven effective as phosphorus concentration can decrease by 43% following leaching.

Environmentally and agriculturally, the relationship presents a complex duality. When phosphogypsum is stockpiled or improperly disposed of, precipitation events can facilitate the leaching and export of soluble phosphorus into adjacent waterways, raising ecological concerns such as eutrophication and altering authigenic phosphorus content at disposal sites. Conversely, from an agricultural perspective, phosphogypsum serves as a valuable direct source of phosphorus for soils, with high application rates significantly boosting available soil phosphorus. Furthermore, phosphogypsum waste stockpiles contain substantial recoverable phosphorus, positioning it as a key industrial source and a potential intervention point for mitigating global phosphorus scarcity. However, extracting this resource requires careful management, as the material's high radioactivity necessitates safe processing technologies. Ultimately, the Phosphogypsum-Phosphorus relation highlights the material's dual nature as both a hazardous waste requiring rigorous environmental management and a valuable resource with substantial agricultural, economic, and strategic potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains Phosphorus, which shows undetectable levels in the analysis.</data>
