<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Fluorine">
  <data key="d7">32.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Fluorine and Phosphogypsum characterizes fluorine as a naturally occurring, toxic, and hazardous impurity derived from processed phosphate ore that serves as a fundamental chemical component within the material. Fluorine manifests in phosphogypsum through multiple physical and chemical states, including solid or dissolved forms co-crystallized during acid attack, distinct mineral phases, and surface-adsorbed species with measurable adsorption capacities over time. It primarily exists as fluoride compounds, exhibits a strong, sharp ring structure in specific particles, and functions as a volatile species capable of transferring during processing or volatilizing as hydrogen fluoride gas, while also persisting in soluble forms. Reported concentrations of fluorine vary significantly across different studies and industrial batches, ranging from approximately 0.12% to 1.39% by weight, with some elemental analyses identifying it at up to 35.00 wt%. This variability is attributed to differences in analytical techniques, such as EDS, as well as fluctuations in processing parameters like citric acid levels and the specific origin of the phosphate ore.

As a major environmental contaminant, fluorine poses substantial ecological and public health risks, capable of migrating through soil and water pollution, atmospheric deposition, and agricultural applications, which can lead to groundwater contamination and fluorosis. Industrially, its presence as an acidic impurity adversely affects the physical properties of phosphogypsum by compromising filler strength and reducing overall structural integrity, thereby hindering its safe and effective reuse in construction materials. To mitigate these harmful effects and enable industrial application, comprehensive treatment protocols are essential. Volatile fluorine compounds are typically eliminated during calcination, soluble forms are targeted for removal during purification stages, and residual fluorine is managed through solidification techniques to prevent environmental migration. These combined measures underscore the necessity of rigorous control strategies to safely manage fluorine levels in phosphogypsum and ensure its responsible utilization.</data>
