<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Heavy Metals">
  <data key="d7">36.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Heavy Metals and Phosphogypsum characterizes heavy metals as inherent, toxic, and harmful impurities within phosphogypsum, typically occurring in trace concentrations alongside radioactive elements. These contaminants significantly alter the chemical properties of phosphogypsum, notably modifying its solubility compared to natural gypsum. Due to their high solubility and environmental availability, heavy metals readily leach, diffuse, and permeate into surrounding ecosystems when phosphogypsum is discharged, stored for extended periods, or undergoes decomposition. This migration facilitates the accumulation of contaminants in adjacent soils, their subsequent entry into nearby water bodies, and their uptake by agricultural plants, thereby posing substantial threats to ecological systems and human health.

To mitigate these environmental and health risks, the resource utilization and industrial applications of phosphogypsum must strictly adhere to established environmental standards governing heavy metal content. Chemical and physical processing methods are frequently employed to reduce heavy metal concentrations within the material. Furthermore, targeted management protocols utilize stabilization, passivation, and immobilization treatments to effectively trap contaminants and prevent their further migration into soil and water systems. The controlled application of phosphogypsum to land can also induce the passivation of heavy metals in soil solutions, thereby reducing their water-soluble toxicity and mitigating potential soil and plant contamination.

Given the persistent risks associated with contaminant transformation and movement, continuous environmental monitoring is strongly recommended. Such oversight ensures the long-term safety of agricultural practices and broader ecological preservation, balancing the industrial utility of phosphogypsum with the imperative to manage its inherent heavy metal hazards responsibly.</data>
