<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Uranium">
  <data key="d7">25.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between phosphogypsum and uranium describes the presence of uranium, primarily the U-238 radioisotope, as a naturally occurring trace radioactive impurity within the phosphogypsum matrix. During phosphate rock processing, approximately 86 percent of the uranium transfers to the phosphoric acid product, while the remaining portion is retained in the phosphogypsum byproduct. Scientific analyses have documented uranium concentrations in phosphogypsum typically ranging from 9.3 to 27.4 ppm, with specific measurements recording levels such as 14 ppm, and have mapped its distribution across various grain fractions.

Due to its inherent radioactivity, uranium in phosphogypsum poses potential radiation hazards that necessitate continuous monitoring and strict management. Monitoring efforts primarily target phosphogypsum waste piles to evaluate exposure risks for nearby workers and ensure environmental compliance, systematically tracking both total concentration and binding percentages. Furthermore, uranium may be released during environmental exposure or leaching processes, often preferentially leaching in experimental settings at concentrations around 17.29 µg/L. During these leaching events, uranium is frequently released alongside rare earth elements and cadmium.

Beyond its hazardous characteristics, uranium is recognized as a valuable associated resource. Consequently, recovery protocols are implemented to extract this element from phosphogypsum, a process that simultaneously prevents long-term environmental pollution and maximizes the material's overall utility.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains uranium and other uranium decay products.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains uranium, which exhibits varying solubility depending on leaching conditions and salinity.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains uranium, which was analyzed in stack solutions and leachates from coastal disposal sites.</data>
