<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Silicon Dioxide">
  <data key="d7">14.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphogypsum and Silicon Dioxide are chemically linked through the consistent presence of silicon dioxide as a constituent within phosphogypsum. Silicon dioxide is identified as a major and predominant component of the material's primary chemical composition, and it is found in both raw and calcined phosphogypsum samples. While it is broadly characterized as a main chemical constituent, specific quantitative analyses report a concentration of approximately 3.44%, indicating that its relative abundance may vary depending on the sample source, geological origin, or industrial processing stage.

Beyond its compositional role, silicon dioxide also functions as an impurity within phosphogypsum that notably enhances the material's sintering resistance. This dual characterization underscores its complex presence in the material, where it contributes to the baseline chemical makeup while simultaneously influencing thermal processing characteristics. Overall, silicon dioxide remains an integral chemical component of phosphogypsum, consistently present across different states and contributing to both its structural composition and functional properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains Silicon Dioxide as a minor constituent at 2.27 wt%.&lt;SEP&gt;Silicon dioxide is a component of phosphogypsum at 0.79 wt%.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains silicon dioxide as part of its initial mixture.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains a small amount of Silicon Dioxide as detected by X-ray fluorescence.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains silicon dioxide as a chemical component.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains Silicon Dioxide at highly variable concentrations depending on the geographical source.</data>
