<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Phosphorus Pentoxide">
  <data key="d7">22.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Phosphogypsum and Phosphorus Pentoxide is characterized by a complex industrial and chemical interdependence rooted in their shared production cycle. Phosphogypsum is generated as a byproduct during the extraction of phosphorus pentoxide, and the resulting material inherently retains this compound as a chemical component. Analytical data indicates that phosphorus pentoxide is present in phosphogypsum at varying concentrations, typically ranging from 0.50% to 1.56% by weight, which is generally classified as a minor constituent or low-amount presence. Despite these relatively low percentages, it constitutes a major portion of the material's overall phosphorus content and usually exceeds fluorine concentrations. Chemically, phosphorus pentoxide acts as a soluble impurity that is substituted within the phosphogypsum crystal lattice, a dual status that simultaneously defines it as a primary constituent and a contaminant.

This presence carries significant functional and environmental implications. As an impurity, phosphorus pentoxide influences material properties, such as retarder action, and is widely recognized as a major contributor to environmental pollution. Consequently, industrial processes often target its elimination or reduction to ensure the safe reuse of phosphogypsum. However, the variable concentration sometimes necessitates the addition of supplementary phosphorus sources in certain applications. Conversely, rather than being entirely discarded, the entrained phosphorus pentoxide can be effectively recovered alongside rare earth elements through sulfuric acid cycle leaching. This multifaceted interaction underscores how phosphorus pentoxide functions within phosphogypsum as both a managed impurity requiring mitigation and a recoverable resource within chemical and industrial cycles.</data>
  <data key="d9">chemical analysis,chemical component,chemical composition,chemical compound,composition,contains,contains impurity,contamination,crystal lattice substitution,impurity,limitation,phosphorus content,phosphorus source,production byproduct,retarder effect,waste composition,weight percentage</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce9090f230bcd0bfeefe92db7a576442-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-983b2c3f393a1de00521565a70363819-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1adcb38defdd04a271ddba318999ba67-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2760dc2a11fedad02d24741a978cf20d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-160a0fe95f7c82b193c6af751106ab6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d5e4f0f96b4ad61d23685552d3211225-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-010</data>
