<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Iron(III) Oxide">
  <data key="d7">11.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Iron(III) oxide and phosphogypsum describes the presence of the former within the latter's chemical composition. Phosphogypsum consistently contains iron(III) oxide, though its classification and abundance vary significantly across different sources. Some descriptions characterize iron(III) oxide as a major chemical component or a fundamental part of phosphogypsum's main composition, while others classify it as a minor constituent, an impurity, or present only in trace amounts.

Quantitative measurements further illustrate this variability, with reported concentrations ranging from 0.32% to 1.74 wt%. These discrepancies likely stem from differences in the raw materials, industrial processing conditions, or analytical methodologies used across various studies. Consequently, while iron(III) oxide is definitively a recognized constituent of phosphogypsum, its precise role and concentration within the material remain context-dependent and subject to methodological variation.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron(III) oxide is a component of phosphogypsum at 0.07 wt%.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains iron(III) oxide as a minor impurity component.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains iron(III) oxide as a chemical component.</data>
