<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Sodium Oxide">
  <data key="d7">8.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphogypsum and Sodium Oxide are linked by a compositional relationship that confirms the presence of sodium oxide within phosphogypsum. However, the provided descriptions exhibit notable discrepancies regarding its relative abundance. Several entries characterize sodium oxide as a major or primary chemical component of phosphogypsum. Conversely, other descriptions provide specific quantitative measurements, reporting concentrations of 0.01%, 0.13%, and 0.14% by weight, which objectively classify it as a minor constituent. These conflicting accounts likely stem from variations in the geological origin of the phosphogypsum samples, differences in industrial processing techniques, or the application of distinct analytical standards across the referenced datasets.

Despite these quantitative and qualitative differences, all descriptions consistently affirm the fundamental chemical association between the two substances. The synthesis of these records indicates that while sodium oxide is definitively present in phosphogypsum, its exact proportion varies significantly depending on the specific source material and measurement methodology. Consequently, the relation is best understood as a confirmed but variable compositional link, where sodium oxide functions as a trace to minor component in most quantified instances, despite occasional classifications as a major constituent.</data>
