<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Cadmium">
  <data key="d7">30.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Cadmium and Phosphogypsum characterizes cadmium as a naturally occurring, prominent heavy metal impurity and trace chemical constituent within phosphogypsum, a byproduct of phosphoric acid production that contains over sixty other elements. Scientific analyses consistently verify cadmium's presence across both raw and washed phosphogypsum variants, with concentrations typically ranging from 3.50 to 7.40 ppm and occasionally reported around 6 ± 1 ppm. The contaminant is widespread throughout the sample matrix but is typically concentrated in fine particle fractions. Chemically, cadmium dissolves readily in distilled water and concentrated acids, and its release fluctuates significantly based on initial concentrations, temperature conditions, and liquid-to-solid fractionation during leaching processes. Analytical outcomes exhibit notable variability due to differences in sampling locations, matrix composition, and detection thresholds, with some studies reporting non-detection while others note concentrations exceeding probable effect thresholds.

As a hazardous contaminant requiring continuous environmental monitoring, cadmium poses substantial ecological and agricultural risks when associated with phosphogypsum. It can accumulate in reclaimed marsh soils following long-term application as a soil amendment, readily enter the food chain through crop uptake, and volatilize into the atmosphere during steam emission processes. Furthermore, phosphogypsum-associated cadmium has been estimated to release approximately 667 tons into estuarine environments. Consequently, the association between cadmium and phosphogypsum is defined by its consistent detection as a toxic impurity, its variable but measurable concentrations, and its multifaceted potential to drive soil-crop pollution cycles, atmospheric contamination, and aquatic ecosystem impacts.</data>
