<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Calcium">
  <data key="d7">28.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Calcium and Phosphogypsum centers on calcium’s role as a primary, dominant, and widely distributed chemical constituent within the phosphogypsum matrix. Analytical measurements consistently identify calcium as a major elemental component, with specific studies reporting concentrations of approximately 38.14 ± 1.70 and 38.91 wt%, while its presence alongside sulfur accounts for over ninety percent of the material’s overall composition. This consistent abundance serves as a key identifier for confirming phosphogypsum’s chemical identity. Within the compound, calcium is frequently characterized as chemically activated, which enhances its reactivity and utility. It acts as a crucial precursor for carbonate formation and provides a substantial source for carbon dioxide mineral sequestration, while also contributing to the generation of beneficial hydration elements.

From a processing and chemical behavior standpoint, calcium can be successfully extracted from phosphogypsum using a preparative vertical-flow electrophoresis system. Its leaching characteristics are actively studied, particularly under acid rain conditions, and research indicates that calcium concentration decreases by twenty-two percent following standard leaching processes. Additionally, calcium ions within the phosphogypsum structure can undergo isomorph substitution, where they are replaced by rare earth elements. In agricultural and environmental applications, phosphogypsum functions as a significant calcium source for plant nutrition. When applied as a soil amendment, it plays a vital role in soil reclamation by displacing excess sodium and hydrogen ions, thereby facilitating soil improvement and sustainable land management. Collectively, these attributes underscore calcium’s integral chemical presence in phosphogypsum and its multifaceted value in material verification, industrial processing, and environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is derived from phosphogypsum and improves SAC ion content.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is a primary component of phosphogypsum, which is composed primarily of calcium sulfate dihydrate.</data>
