<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Quartz">
  <data key="d7">14.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphogypsum and quartz are characterized by the presence of quartz as a common impurity and minor mineral constituent within the phosphogypsum matrix. In its raw state, quartz is present in the starting phosphogypsum material alongside other phases such as aluminosilicates and is consistently identified as an impurity phase. Quantitative analyses indicate that its concentration varies across different samples, typically accounting for small amounts that range from approximately 0.9 weight percent to roughly 5 percent of the total mineralogical composition. During industrial processing, this quartz fraction is frequently retained and subsequently discarded as part of specific beneficiation operations aimed at purifying the phosphogypsum.

Despite its minor status in the raw material, the abundance and role of quartz shift significantly when phosphogypsum is utilized in downstream applications. Notably, when phosphogypsum is incorporated into construction materials, quartz becomes the dominant component; it is identified as the primary mineral phase in all analyzed brick samples containing phosphogypsum. This transition highlights how the mineralogical profile of phosphogypsum evolves from a quartz-bearing raw byproduct into a composite material where quartz dictates the foundational structure, underscoring its consistent presence and variable significance throughout the material's lifecycle.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains a significant amount of quartz as a major crystalline component or impurity.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartz is present as a minor mineralogical component in phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartz is a mineral component associated with the phosphogypsum matrix.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains quartz as a minor mineral phase.&lt;SEP&gt;Quartz is identified as a main mineral component within phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d9">chemical composition,common mineral phase,component,composition,contains impurity,impurity,impurity composition,material,matrix component,mineral composition,mineralogical composition,minor phase,primary phase</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d18c456289f64c1e16c73a0dfd6595b3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-35aef6efc801611130c5dd196fe513df-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b19ca721a4da4422ee10228d88a4c292-chunk-002</data>
