<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Sulphuric Acid">
  <data key="d7">9.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between phosphogypsum and sulphuric acid is characterized by a complex, cyclical industrial interaction involving waste generation, resource recovery, and chemical treatment. In standard industrial operations, the manufacturing process of sulphuric acid generates phosphogypsum as a significant solid waste byproduct. Conversely, phosphogypsum can also function as a raw material or feedstock in the production of sulphuric acid, highlighting a potential circular economy pathway.

Beyond production cycles, sulphuric acid plays a direct role in the processing and purification of phosphogypsum. It is actively employed in treatment processes designed to reduce impurities and purify the material, often in combination with silica. During these chemical treatments, the reaction between sulphuric acid and phosphogypsum can lead to the formation of bassanite within the phosphogypsum matrix. Additionally, specific treatment protocols involving sulphuric acid are utilized to directly produce or regenerate phosphogypsum. Overall, the interaction between these two substances encompasses both traditional waste management and advanced chemical recycling, where sulphuric acid serves as both a manufacturing byproduct generator and a critical reagent for phosphogypsum purification and mineral transformation.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains residual sulphuric acid, contributing to its highly acidic nature.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative treatment,bassanite formation,chemical process,chemical property,chemical reaction,chemical relationship,chemical treatment,impurity content,process byproduct,production byproduct,purification method,raw material,treatment</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-001fe856b0d193d82422dc8a5baedc86-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8c22d31cfad5421c936a23705a3bc6e0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1775dab65ef7445e05c5e396bcc0009b-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1792489906</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Silica">
  <data key="d7">9.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphogypsum and silica share a multifaceted relationship that encompasses both compositional and processing dimensions. Silica is naturally present in phosphogypsum as a minor constituent and impurity, typically originating from production additives or process water. Within the phosphogypsum matrix, silica manifests in various physical forms, including amorphous SiO2, quartz, and other SiO2-containing minerals. During standard industrial processing, a significant portion of this silica impurity is effectively removed through water washing.

Beyond its role as an inherent component, silica also serves a functional purpose in the processing and analysis of phosphogypsum. It is utilized as a treatment agent, frequently combined with sulphuric acid, to help reduce overall impurities in the material. Additionally, silica is employed in laboratory and analytical settings to treat phosphogypsum samples across varying pH levels, facilitating more accurate chemical assessments.&lt;SEP&gt;Silica is mixed with sulphuric acid in proposed treatment methods for phosphogypsum.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative treatment,chemical composition,chemical mixture,chemical treatment,composition,contains impurity,impurity presence,mineralogy,purification method,sample preparation,treatment</data>
