<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Calcium Hydroxide">
  <data key="d7">8.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Calcium Hydroxide and Phosphogypsum encompasses bidirectional chemical interactions and industrial processing applications. When Calcium Hydroxide is applied to Phosphogypsum, it serves as a critical modifier and treatment agent. During neutralization modification, Calcium Hydroxide alters the internal structure of Phosphogypsum and effectively removes harmful fluoride and phosphate contaminants. Specifically, during the heating of Phosphogypsum, Calcium Hydroxide reacts with freed phosphate ions to form chemically inactive compounds. Additionally, when added to calcined Phosphogypsum, it enhances the material's engineering properties by improving both setting time and compressive strength. Conversely, the addition of Phosphogypsum to Calcium Hydroxide systems promotes hydration processes, which is evidenced by the disappearance of Calcium Hydroxide diffraction peaks.

In the reverse direction, Phosphogypsum serves as a raw material for the synthesis and conversion of Calcium Hydroxide. Through specific preparation processes, particularly when treated with Sodium Hydroxide, Phosphogypsum is chemically converted into Calcium Hydroxide. This derived Calcium Hydroxide is not only a valuable byproduct but also functions as an effective agent for carbon sequestration. Together, these processes highlight the versatile chemical interplay between the two substances, where Calcium Hydroxide modifies and stabilizes Phosphogypsum for construction and environmental remediation, while Phosphogypsum can be recycled to produce Calcium Hydroxide for carbon capture applications.</data>
  <data key="d9">additive,chemical conversion,chemical interaction,chemical process,chemical reaction,conversion,neutralization,preparation,product,purification,reacts with,strength improvement,synthesis product,treated by</data>
