<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Polonium-210">
  <data key="d7">8.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphogypsum and polonium-210 share a well-documented relationship characterized by the consistent presence of this radionuclide within the industrial byproduct. Polonium-210 is a relatively long-lived radioactive component that is retained in phosphogypsum, originating from the processing of phosphate rock. During the wet phosphoric acid production process, polonium-210 partitions into the resulting material and coprecipitates with calcium sulfate, resulting in measurable concentrations within the final product.

Because of its inherent radioactivity, polonium-210 is routinely quantified during radioactive assessments of phosphogypsum. Its concentration is systematically analyzed alongside other radionuclides to evaluate the material's radiological profile and ensure proper handling and regulatory compliance.</data>
  <data key="d9">component of,composition,contains,coprecipitates in,major alpha emitter,partitioning,radioactive content,radioactive isotope,radiological analysis,radionuclide content</data>
