<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Egypt">
  <data key="d7">10.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Egypt and phosphogypsum encompasses significant industrial production, extensive scientific analysis, and notable environmental considerations. Egypt is a major producer of phosphogypsum, contributing substantially to global output with an annual production volume of 5.4 million tonnes. Given this substantial output, phosphogypsum samples originating from Egypt have been the focus of detailed chemical investigations. These studies analyze the mean weight percentages of various chemical oxides, assess overall chemical composition, and identify specific impurities. Researchers have also conducted comparative analyses of these samples across different geographic locations within Egypt to establish comprehensive compositional baselines.

In addition to chemical profiling, the material has been extensively studied regarding contamination and radiological properties. Egyptian phosphogypsum is characterized by elevated radionuclide levels, which necessitate specific pretreatment protocols prior to safe handling or utilization. Furthermore, Egypt serves as a critical geographic source for evaluating the broader radiological impact associated with both phosphogypsum and the phosphate fertilizers derived from it. Collectively, these factors establish Egypt as both a key production hub and a focal region for comprehensive environmental and chemical research on phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Egypt is a source region for Phosphogypsum, with its chemical composition documented in Table 1.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum samples from Egypt were analyzed and reported in scientific studies.</data>
  <data key="d9">chemical analysis,composition,data source,geographic origin,geographic source,geographical sampling,geographical source,high radionuclides,production,production volume,radiological study,regional comparison,sample origin,study location</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-048&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1792487639</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Spain">
  <data key="d7">12.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Phosphogypsum in Spain is defined by the country's substantial role in global production and the distinct geochemical profile of the material generated domestically. Spain stands as a major worldwide producer of phosphogypsum, accounting for approximately 10.84% of global production or reserves. The nation generates roughly 2.5 million metric tons of the byproduct annually, necessitating the management of large quantities through extensive industrial stockpiles.

The phosphogypsum produced in Spain is characterized by elevated radionuclide concentrations, prompting extensive scientific scrutiny. Samples sourced from this region have been rigorously analyzed to determine their mean weight percentages of various chemical oxides, overall chemical composition, and impurity levels. Furthermore, environmental case studies focusing on Spanish phosphogypsum stockpile sites have demonstrated that geogenic weathering serves as the dominant mechanism governing the distribution of trace pollutants within these deposits.&lt;SEP&gt;Spain exhibits significantly higher Radium-226 radioactivity in its phosphogypsum compared to other nations.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum stacks near Huelva, Spain, contribute to estuarine contamination.</data>
