<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Compressive Strength">
  <data key="d7">11.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between compressive strength and phosphogypsum is highly dependent on the specific construction material and the substitution level of phosphogypsum utilized. In general, higher concentrations or fractions of phosphogypsum tend to decrease the compressive strength of various construction materials, particularly within cement matrices and cementitious systems, where its introduction leads to a consistent decline in strength across different curing periods. For cement mortar, the impact is significant and varies according to both concentration and material composition, with replacement levels playing a critical role in determining the final mechanical properties. Similarly, in minibricks, increasing phosphogypsum content reduces mechanical strength, although the resulting values typically remain above established industry standards.

However, the effect is not uniformly negative across all material types and substitution ratios. In some cases, phosphogypsum addition can enhance or optimize compressive strength depending on the specific formulation. For instance, a 10% substitution level has been shown to optimize compressive strength before reductions occur at higher percentages. Conversely, certain traditional materials exhibit different responses; the compressive strength of adobe samples increases with the addition of phosphogypsum, and the strength of bricks actually peaks at a high phosphogypsum content of 75%, reaching 21.8 MPa. These variations highlight that while high phosphogypsum concentrations generally compromise strength in modern cementitious applications, lower or specific substitution rates can be beneficial or even optimal for other construction materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Incorporating thermally treated phosphogypsum improves the long-term compressive strength of cement mortars.&lt;SEP&gt;Compressive strength is significantly improved by specific phosphogypsum types at low replacement percentages.</data>
  <data key="d9">concentration dependency,content correlation,impact,increases,influence,material interaction,material property,mechanical property,performance enhancement,performance impact,property alteration,property change,property influence,reduction,strength reduction,type dependency</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-d1ffdf413ee3cbdda92474adf77e2c78-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e4a551024f80f822f3f35e38ec278151-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-819484fd12a30d085e4eb297904c9e2b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0549fcda95f7199440791b20bf85635-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
