<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Radionuclides">
  <data key="d7">21.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Phosphogypsum and Radionuclides characterizes the presence of naturally occurring radioactive impurities within phosphogypsum, an industrial byproduct derived from phosphate rock processing. These radionuclides, which include uranium-238 (U-238) from the U-238 decay series, radium-226 (Ra-226), potassium-40 (K-40), and thorium-232 (Th-232), are present in trace amounts and are heterogeneously distributed throughout the material. Their distribution and mode of occurrence are extensively documented, with research indicating that certain radionuclides can be carried by nanoparticles, significantly influencing their physical transport and environmental mobility. Additionally, the presence of these radioactive elements alters the chemical properties of the byproduct, causing its solubility to differ from that of natural gypsum.

The co-occurrence of radionuclides in phosphogypsum and its associated mortars presents substantial environmental and long-term health hazards. When the material is improperly stored or applied in agricultural settings, it raises critical safety concerns, particularly regarding the potential uptake of radioactive elements by crops and the risk of chronic radiation exposure to humans and ecosystems. Consequently, these inherent hazards severely limit the broader industrial and agricultural applications of phosphogypsum. Due to these persistent risks, the environmental behavior, dispersion mechanisms, and safety monitoring of radionuclides within phosphogypsum remain primary focal points of ongoing scientific research and regulatory oversight.&lt;SEP&gt;Radionuclides are naturally occurring radioactive elements retained in untreated phosphogypsum.&lt;SEP&gt;Raw phosphogypsum contains a total of 0.522 wt% radionuclides.&lt;SEP&gt;Radionuclides remain as contaminants in phosphogypsum across all treatment methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Radionuclides are chemical phases present within phosphogypsum under both acidic and basic conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains various radioactive elements derived from phosphate rocks.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains heterogeneously distributed radionuclides that contribute to radiological risks.</data>
  <data key="d9">characteristic,chemical composition,chemical phase,composition,contains,contaminant,contamination,contamination source,content,distribution,environmental concern,environmental hazard,hazard,hazardous component,health risk,impurity,limitation,persistent impurity,pollutant emission,radioactive contamination,radioactive content,radioactivity,safety parameter,solubility difference,subject of study</data>
