<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Radium">
  <data key="d7">17.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Phosphogypsum and Radium describes how phosphogypsum inherently concentrates, retains, and co-crystallizes with radium, a naturally occurring radioactive element and isotope that functions as an impurity. During industrial production and phosphate decomposition, radium is predominantly transferred from the processing stream into phosphogypsum waste, with approximately 80% of the naturally occurring radium from phosphate rock migrating into the material. Once incorporated, scientific studies estimate that roughly 89% of this radium remains securely bound within the phosphogypsum crystal structure. The distribution of radium within the material is size-dependent, demonstrating preferential retention in smaller grain size fractions.

To evaluate the material's radiological properties, radium concentration is routinely measured in phosphogypsum, serving as a key component in calculating its radioactivity index. Because radium is a radioactive component, its presence poses notable environmental risks and significantly influences leaching dynamics and broader environmental impact assessments. Consequently, managing this radioactive content requires specific removal processes alongside continuous environmental monitoring. The radioactive isotopes of radium within phosphogypsum are frequently characterized in scientific studies to evaluate their environmental behavior and develop effective mitigation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Approximately 80% of naturally occurring Radium is concentrated in Phosphogypsum during processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains radium, which poses potential water contamination risks due to phosphate fertilizer production.</data>
  <data key="d9">chemical composition,co-crystallization,composition,containment,contains,distribution,hazard,impurity,leachability,migration,preferential retention,radioactive concentration,radioactive content,radioactive element,radioactive hazard,radioactivity,radiological analysis,radionuclide content,radionuclide transfer,retention,waste composition</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0060991ac74a9675ce0293eccdc023cd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-001</data>
