<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Radon">
  <data key="d7">8.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between phosphogypsum and radon centers on phosphogypsum's role as a significant source of radon emanation. Phosphogypsum contains radon, a radioactive gas that is part of the uranium decay series, along with other associated decay products. Within the material, radon is not uniformly distributed but is notably concentrated in the fine particle fractions compared to larger granular components. This inherent composition establishes a direct physical link between the industrial byproduct and the release of radioactive gases.

Because phosphogypsum actively emits radon, particularly when accumulated in large storage stacks, it poses substantial environmental and health risks due to potential human exposure. To evaluate these environmental and health impacts, radon emanation from phosphogypsum is systematically measured across various geographic locations. Monitoring and sampling efforts have been conducted in multiple regions, including specific studies performed on samples from Cyprus, to accurately assess the public health implications and environmental footprint of these emissions.</data>
  <data key="d9">composition,emanation,emanation source,emission source,emits,environmental concern,hazard,particle distribution,radioactive association,radioactive component,radioactive gas,radioactive release,radiological hazard,radiological measurement</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1792489442</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Arsenic">
  <data key="d7">9.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Arsenic and Phosphogypsum establishes that arsenic is a naturally occurring, trace heavy metal impurity consistently present within phosphogypsum. Across multiple independent studies, arsenic has been measured as a standard chemical constituent of phosphogypsum samples. While it is fundamentally classified as a toxic and hazardous element, its presence in phosphogypsum is not uniform; rather, it tends to accumulate and become localized in specific hotspots within the material.

From an environmental perspective, the presence of arsenic in phosphogypsum poses potential ecological risks and contributes to environmental contamination, necessitating rigorous environmental monitoring. Despite these hazards, analytical data indicates that arsenic concentrations in phosphogypsum are generally lower than the rare effect concentration, suggesting that acute adverse effects are not universally triggered. Nevertheless, due to its inherent toxicity and localized accumulation, the arsenic-phosphogypsum association remains a significant factor in environmental risk assessment and material management.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains arsenic, which is classified as a toxic element.</data>
  <data key="d9">chemical constituent,composition,contains,elemental composition,hazard,hazardous component,impurity,measurement,threshold compliance,toxic element,toxic metal,trace content</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-401b567da916dc34559e0bfe5a5b367b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-fbe0ad1b5f7f65c2338dc42cd9ca4b89-chunk-045&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a5d09b48f57a4e888bb729aaf988c936-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ad247a75dda6243453b4132b71670203-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009</data>
