<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Calcium Carbonate">
  <data key="d7">11.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Calcium Carbonate and Phosphogypsum encompasses a series of industrial processes focused on synthesis, recycling, and material substitution. Phosphogypsum serves as a primary feedstock for generating calcium carbonate through multiple chemical pathways. During the thermal decomposition of phosphogypsum, calcium carbonate is co-produced alongside calcium oxide. The resulting decomposition slag can further be utilized to capture carbon dioxide, which facilitates the synthesis of calcium carbonate. In alternative conversion processes, phosphogypsum yields calcium carbonate concurrently with ammonium sulfate. These synthesis steps are generally integrated into broader preparation processes designed to extract and refine calcium carbonate from phosphogypsum waste.

Additional methods emphasize purification, targeted precipitation, and resource recovery. Phosphogypsum can be recycled to generate high-purity calcium carbonate through recrystallization and mineralization techniques. The carbonation route represents another prevalent method, where calcium carbonate is precipitated directly from phosphogypsum. When combined with digestion, this carbonation process not only forms calcium carbonate but also enables the recovery of target rare earth elements embedded within the phosphogypsum matrix. 

Beyond production, the materials exhibit functional interchangeability in specific industrial applications. Modified phosphogypsum demonstrates significant potential as a viable substitute for calcium carbonate powder in polymer composite materials. Collectively, these processes highlight a cyclical industrial relationship where phosphogypsum is transformed into valuable calcium carbonate products, while modified phosphogypsum itself can partially replace calcium carbonate in downstream manufacturing.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum undergoes carbonation to produce calcium carbonate.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum contains the total calcium that theoretically forms calcium carbonate during carbonation.&lt;SEP&gt;Phosphogypsum serves as the primary feedstock that is transformed into calcium carbonate during the carbonation process.</data>
  <data key="d9">calcium reservoir,carbon capture,carbonation,chemical conversion,decomposition product,feedstock,formation process,intermediate compound,mineralization,preparation,production,reacts to form,recycling,replacement,substitution,synthesis,synthesis product,theoretical source,transformation</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a3152ce135edc56e14c47e05535e05c7-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c2674917fb6c60e75303effeaa10768e-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-edfc4dcbd74cbf0b294f83f2034b209d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-80e3f6300e001922a5ca2a6a8ac2e0d2-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1792123906</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
