<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Copper">
  <data key="d7">8.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between copper and phosphogypsum is defined by the consistent presence of copper as a trace element within phosphogypsum matrices. Analytical assessments and tabular data confirm that copper is typically the most abundant heavy metal in phosphogypsum and frequently occurs in elevated concentrations.

During leaching processes, including exposure to distilled water and sequential leaching, copper exhibits high dissolution rates and significant liquid/solid fractionation. This behavior is driven by the weak bonding between copper and gypsum surfaces. Consequently, phosphogypsum serves as a major environmental source of copper, with an estimated 1158 tons released into the estuary.</data>
  <data key="d9">abundance,composition,contains,contamination,fractionation,leaching,presence,release,trace content,trace element analysis</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-34a3ac199714be8fd1cdff8ead90fc92-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-66f6434914b6de99019386e4bdcbc57e-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-525daf613910500054a23427717dab1f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
