<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Sodium">
  <data key="d7">8.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between phosphogypsum and sodium encompasses both compositional characteristics and environmental interactions. Phosphogypsum contains sodium as a metallic impurity and trace element, with a relatively lower mass fraction compared to its major constituent elements. Despite this lower abundance, sodium remains one of the key elements found in phosphogypsum samples and is consistently identified as a major chemical species during analytical testing. Within the material's matrix, sodium forms chemical complexes with phosphates and sulfates. Furthermore, the presence of sodium is persistent through processing, as sodium ions are not completely eliminated during the washing of phosphogypsum.

Beyond its intrinsic composition, phosphogypsum also interacts with sodium in agricultural and soil management contexts. When applied to sodic soils, the calcium introduced by phosphogypsum displaces adsorbed sodium, thereby influencing soil chemistry and contributing to remediation efforts. This dual role highlights sodium's significance both as a residual component within phosphogypsum and as a target element in soil treatment applications.</data>
