<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Jordan">
  <data key="d7">11.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Jordan and phosphogypsum is characterized by substantial industrial output and practical application within the construction sector. Jordan operates as a major global producer of phosphogypsum, generating approximately three million tons annually. The material is primarily extracted from specific domestic regions, notably Aqaba and Eshidiya, and is subsequently processed for use in building materials and construction projects.

Scientific evaluation of Jordanian phosphogypsum focuses heavily on its chemical properties and environmental safety. Samples sourced from the country are routinely analyzed and compared to determine their chemical composition and impurity levels. Additionally, Jordan serves as a primary geographic location for assessing the radiological impacts of treated phosphogypsum when integrated into construction. These studies specifically investigate radionuclide distribution and trace metal mobility, providing essential data on the material's environmental behavior and safety profile in built environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Jordan produces phosphogypsum, contributing to worldwide production.&lt;SEP&gt;Jordan is a country where the potential utilizations of phosphogypsum were reviewed.&lt;SEP&gt;Jordan is listed as the origin for phosphogypsum samples.</data>
  <data key="d9">application site,chemical analysis,environmental impact,field site,geographic origin,geographic source,high volume,origin,production,production location,radiological assessment,radiological impact,regional comparison,regional study,sample origin,sample source,study location,utilization review</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e1871f90fba8192d1aa537b2a9f4555c-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7797768798fcd1152bdc10ff73139e35-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-043</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1791725530</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Brazil">
  <data key="d7">15.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Brazil and phosphogypsum are characterized by the country's status as a major global producer, generating approximately 5.4 million tons annually, which accounts for roughly 10.62% of worldwide production and reserves. Brazilian phosphogypsum exhibits distinct physical properties, notably a generally smaller particle size compared to material from China. Industrially, the by-product is utilized as a setting retarder in Portland cement and has been investigated for agricultural applications, including fertilizer production. The chemical composition and impurity levels of samples originating from Brazil have been extensively documented and frequently used in comparative chemical analyses.

A significant focus of research on Brazilian phosphogypsum involves its radiological profile. The material has undergone detailed radiochemical characterization, with specific studies conducted in locations such as Imbituba. These investigations are part of broader scientific efforts to assess radionuclide intake and understand the radioactivity associated with the by-product. Consequently, Brazil serves as a primary geographic source for sampling and a central focus for both chemical and radiochemical evaluations of phosphogypsum, highlighting its importance in industrial, agricultural, and environmental research contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Brazil exhibits significantly lower phosphogypsum radioactivity that meets national standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Brazil is cited in literature as a location where phosphogypsum samples containing calcium sulphate hemihydrate are found.&lt;SEP&gt;Brazil is the origin of phosphogypsum samples studied by Geraldo et al.</data>
  <data key="d9">cement additive,chemical analysis,field site,geographic source,global distribution,high volume,industrial by-product,literature,lower radioactivity,occurrence,origin,particle size comparison,production,production location,production share,production volume,radiochemical analysis,regional comparison,regional focus,research sample,sample origin,sample source,study location</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-70650e5cb032fce744763706d8701e36-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-051&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4c4b1b446b8362051476593a7c1a0d7c-chunk-047&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eb86340ba0851c06e97600104fca25c9-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9c8cdabf609cc047d173d4ff8dbb5187-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b0ac8d630b9aa4d874707445e2552a09-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-28fbd4302bba090e667215c840757ff7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f3d20c90c2a00342300c9b55c3e29f23-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d11">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d12">1792408891</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Phosphogypsum" target="Poland">
  <data key="d7">4.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Poland produces approximately 1.5 million tons of phosphogypsum annually, leading to significant environmental concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;Poland is the country where the phosphogypsum samples were produced.&lt;SEP&gt;Poland has documented phosphogypsum management practices and evaluated technologically enhanced natural radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Poland accounts for 6.57% of the global distribution of phosphogypsum production or reserves.</data>
