<edge source="Hydrothermal Synthesis" target="Hydroxyapatite">
  <data key="d7">30.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Hydrothermal Synthesis is a primary and widely employed technique utilized for the production of synthetic hydroxyapatite and its various derivatives. This method relies on the precipitation and subsequent crystal growth of the material under controlled hydrothermal conditions, typically conducted at temperatures around 200 °C. By carefully regulating the reaction environment, the process consistently yields high-quality, phase-pure nanocrystalline structures with precise and well-defined morphological forms. The versatility of Hydrothermal Synthesis allows it to be tailored to produce a diverse array of hydroxyapatite architectures depending on specific synthesis parameters and additives. Documented forms include nanoparticles, nanopowders, nanorods, needlelike crystals, flaky structures, and microspheres. Furthermore, the strategic use of cationic surfactant templates enables the directed formation of nanopowders, while the technique specifically drives the formation of elongated nanorods and flaky structures. It is also highly advantageous for fabricating hydroxyapatite microspheres with specific structural properties.

Notably, Hydrothermal Synthesis demonstrates significant adaptability in material fabrication by accommodating various starting materials. It serves as an effective strategy for synthesizing hydroxyapatite from alternative and waste-derived raw materials, most notably converting waste sources such as phosphogypsum into phase-pure nanocrystalline hydroxyapatite. This capability bridges sustainable waste valorization with high-precision crystalline production. Consequently, the method is highly valued for advanced material applications, establishing itself as a robust and adaptable approach for fabricating hydroxyapatite crystals and nanostructures in both academic research and industrial settings.</data>
  <data key="d9">advantages,chemical synthesis,crystal growth,experimental process,fabrication,material production,method,methodology,nanomaterial production,nanopowder production,particle formation,phase transformation,preparation,produces,production,production technique,reaction environment,synthesis,synthesis method,synthesis technique</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-a7ec54d376b079eceb4eab9d1f23165e-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2eed3e4bb65f425c7011f91fbc1c7dd5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-20b5888df3bfef8e98cc13abb005b7f0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a6c42f76034cfa4338bd156b7179fc5-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f1fb5c243f2539cf7be6b6021b63c36f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c4a64a852097fdc910b98ed788503ce4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f5dcf59bedaa00614f1acc222b1fb7b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6825862084b162a4b16cd1037c76e3d7-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-06f299b7e723cda1f0de1a647b02b6b4-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-2196dee89de9694f28ef4eaa0e85f130-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4739e42903c95e1fb7e0ecfbca3a12df-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d4a38fcc1b0f33a17d23e7a4407a9cf9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3fd8439e859e3dd0c96328384e1761f0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54b92b2ad16a2528ff6a863329c5d403-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-176fa906dd9c8409d2d1c49b8bdf9948-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f07601cd9e687d5cc5c7d9b6b467f21e-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e992f3bad9db87c40308ea55e121bd8a-chunk-011</data>
